window.NPLEX_NOTES=window.NPLEX_NOTES||{};Object.assign(window.NPLEX_NOTES,{"GI-1":{"unit":"GI-1","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-01","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-01-01","title":"Gastrointestinal Embryology","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows the gut from a simple endodermal tube to the adult digestive tract and its glands. It covers the three gut divisions and their arteries, midgut herniation and rotation, separation of the esophagus from the airway, budding of the liver and pancreas, colonization of the gut wall by neural crest, and recanalization of the duodenum. Board questions usually describe a newborn with vomiting, drooling, a midline sac, or delayed meconium and ask which developmental step failed. They also use the gut divisions to explain blood supply and why visceral pain is felt in the epigastric, periumbilical, or suprapubic region. If you know what each step normally does, every defect follows from it.","prerequisites":["Germ layers and embryonic folding in weeks 3 and 4","Neural crest formation and migration (NE-1)","Ventral branches of the abdominal aorta","Organization of sympathetic, parasympathetic, and visceral afferent fibers (NE-9)","Peritoneum, mesenteries, and retroperitoneal organs (GI-4)"],"connections":[{"to":"Gastrointestinal blood supply (GI-5)","how":"The celiac, superior mesenteric, and inferior mesenteric territories are the foregut, midgut, and hindgut, and their junctions are the watershed zones."},{"to":"The gut in the body cavities (GI-4)","how":"Rotation and fixation of the mesenteries explain which organs end up secondarily retroperitoneal."},{"to":"Respiratory embryology (PU-1)","how":"The lung bud and trachea arise from the same foregut tube as the esophagus, so their separation defects are shared."},{"to":"Motility, digestion, and absorption (GI-6)","how":"The neural crest derived enteric plexuses are the circuits that coordinate peristalsis and secretion."},{"to":"Congenital gastrointestinal disease (GI-13-H)","how":"Atresias, malrotation, abdominal wall defects, and Hirschsprung disease are developmental failures described here."}],"terms":[{"term":"Primitive gut","def":"The endodermal tube formed when embryonic folding incorporates the dorsal part of the yolk sac in week 4."},{"term":"Vitelline duct","def":"The narrow connection between the midgut and the yolk sac, attached to the apex of the midgut loop until it normally regresses."},{"term":"Septum transversum","def":"A plate of mesoderm between the heart and the yolk stalk into which the liver grows and which contributes to the diaphragm and ventral mesentery."},{"term":"Physiologic umbilical herniation","def":"The normal temporary passage of the midgut loop into the umbilical cord from week 6 until about week 10."},{"term":"Malrotation","def":"Incomplete or abnormal rotation of the midgut loop that leaves the bowel malpositioned on a narrow mesentery."},{"term":"Midgut volvulus","def":"Twisting of the malrotated midgut around the axis of the superior mesenteric artery, causing obstruction and ischemia."},{"term":"Tracheoesophageal septum","def":"The partition formed by fusion of the tracheoesophageal ridges that separates the ventral airway from the dorsal esophagus."},{"term":"Recanalization","def":"Reopening of a gut lumen that was temporarily filled by proliferating epithelium."},{"term":"Annular pancreas","def":"A ring of pancreatic tissue around the second part of the duodenum formed by a bifid ventral pancreatic bud."},{"term":"Pancreas divisum","def":"Failure of the dorsal and ventral pancreatic ducts to fuse, so most pancreatic juice drains through the minor papilla."},{"term":"Aganglionosis","def":"Absence of myenteric and submucosal ganglion cells in a segment of bowel, the defining lesion of Hirschsprung disease."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-01-01-C01","obj":"GI-1.1","title":"Gut tube divisions, their arteries, and referred visceral pain","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"During week 4, cephalocaudal and lateral folding of the embryo draws the dorsal part of the yolk sac inside as the primitive gut. Endoderm forms the epithelial lining and the secretory cells of the liver and pancreas; the surrounding splanchnic mesoderm forms the connective tissue, smooth muscle, and visceral peritoneum. The tube is closed cranially by the oropharyngeal membrane and caudally by the cloacal membrane, and the midgut stays open to the yolk sac through the vitelline duct."},{"type":"p","text":"Each abdominal division is supplied by one unpaired ventral branch of the aorta, and these three arteries develop from the vitelline arteries that once supplied the yolk sac. Because each artery grows with its segment, the arterial borders are the embryologic borders. The foregut ends just distal to the major duodenal papilla, where the bile duct enters, and the midgut ends at the junction of the proximal two thirds and distal third of the transverse colon. The junctions receive blood from both neighbors, such as the pancreatic head through the superior and inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries. Where territories meet, as at the splenic flexure, collateral flow is thin and the wall is vulnerable to ischemia when perfusion falls (GI-5)."},{"type":"table","title":"The three gut divisions","cols":["Division","Adult derivatives (abdominal)","Artery","Parasympathetic","Pain referred to"],"rows":[["Foregut","Abdominal esophagus, stomach, duodenum to the major papilla, liver, gallbladder, pancreas","Celiac trunk","Vagus nerve","Epigastrium"],["Midgut","Duodenum distal to the papilla, jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, proximal two thirds of transverse colon","Superior mesenteric artery","Vagus nerve","Periumbilical region"],["Hindgut","Distal third of transverse colon, descending and sigmoid colon, rectum, anal canal above the pectinate line","Inferior mesenteric artery","Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2 to S4)","Suprapubic region"]],"caption":"The pharynx and thoracic esophagus are also foregut but take blood from other arteries. The spleen is supplied by the celiac trunk but is mesodermal, forming in the dorsal mesogastrium, so it is not a gut derivative."},{"type":"p","text":"Visceral pain fibers from most of the gut run back along the sympathetic splanchnic nerves to the spinal segments that supplied each division in the embryo: roughly T5 to T9 for the foregut, T10 to T11 for the midgut, and T12 to L2 for the hindgut. The exception is the distal sigmoid colon and rectum, whose pain fibers travel with the pelvic splanchnic nerves to S2 to S4. In the dorsal horn they converge on neurons that also receive input from skin of the same segments, so the brain projects the sensation onto those dermatomes. The umbilicus lies in the T10 dermatome, which is why midgut pain is periumbilical."},{"type":"p","text":"Visceral afferents are sparse and bilateral and respond to distension, ischemia, and inflammation rather than cutting. Visceral pain is therefore dull, cramping, poorly localized, felt in the midline, and often accompanied by nausea, sweating, and pallor. When inflammation reaches the parietal peritoneum, which is supplied by somatic nerves of the body wall, the pain becomes sharp and localizes over the organ."},{"type":"flow","title":"How visceral pain is referred","steps":["Distension, ischemia, or inflammation stimulates visceral afferents","Fibers travel with sympathetic splanchnic nerves","Enter the cord at the segment of that gut division","Converge on dorsal horn neurons shared with skin input","Brain localizes pain to the midline dermatome"],"caption":"Convergence of visceral and somatic input on the same spinal neurons explains why gut pain is felt on the body wall."},{"type":"example","title":"Why appendicitis pain moves","text":"Obstruction of the appendix lumen distends its wall. The appendix is midgut, so its afferents enter the cord at about T10, and the first pain is dull, periumbilical, and accompanied by nausea. Over several hours inflammation reaches the somatically innervated parietal peritoneum in the right iliac fossa, so the pain shifts to the right lower quadrant and becomes sharp, localized, and worse with movement."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-01-01-C02","obj":"GI-1.2","title":"Midgut herniation, rotation, and their failures","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The midgut forms a U shaped primary intestinal loop suspended by dorsal mesentery. The superior mesenteric artery (SMA) runs in that mesentery as the axis of the loop, and the vitelline duct attaches at its apex. The cranial limb becomes the distal duodenum, jejunum, and most of the ileum; the caudal limb becomes the distal ileum, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and proximal two thirds of the transverse colon. A small swelling on the caudal limb, the cecal bud, marks the future cecum."},{"type":"p","text":"In week 6 the loop lengthens quickly while the large liver and the mesonephric kidneys fill the small abdominal cavity. Lacking room, the loop passes into the extraembryonic coelom within the umbilical cord. This physiologic herniation is normal. While in the cord, the loop turns 90 degrees counterclockwise, viewed from the front, around the SMA. Around week 10, as liver growth slows, the mesonephros regresses, and the abdomen enlarges, the loops return. The jejunum returns first and settles on the left, and the loop completes a further 180 degrees, for 270 degrees in total. The cecum returns last, lies at first below the liver, and then descends to the right iliac fossa."},{"type":"flow","title":"Normal midgut rotation","steps":["Week 6: midgut loop herniates into the umbilical cord","First 90 degrees counterclockwise around the SMA axis","Cranial limb coils into jejunum and ileum","About week 10: loops return, jejunum first","Further 180 degrees during return, 270 degrees in total","Cecum returns last and descends to the right iliac fossa","Colonic mesenteries fuse with the posterior wall"],"caption":"Rotation viewed from the front. Fusion of the colonic mesenteries makes the ascending and descending colon secondarily retroperitoneal."},{"type":"p","text":"Normal rotation places the third part of the duodenum behind the SMA, with the duodenojejunal junction left of midline, and gives the small bowel mesentery a long diagonal root that keeps the bowel from twisting."},{"type":"p","text":"In malrotation the loop stops short of 270 degrees. With nonrotation (only the first 90 degrees) the small bowel lies on the right and the colon on the left. With incomplete rotation the cecum stays high near the midline, and peritoneal (Ladd) bands run from it across the second part of the duodenum to the right posterior wall, where they can compress the duodenum. The dangerous feature is the mesentery: it stays short, narrow, and centered on the SMA, so the whole midgut can twist around the vessel. This midgut volvulus obstructs the bowel and occludes the SMA and superior mesenteric vein, threatening ischemia from the distal duodenum to the transverse colon. It most often presents in the first month of life, though it can occur at any age, with sudden bilious vomiting, since the block lies below the papilla. A contrast study shows the duodenojejunal junction right of midline."},{"type":"p","text":"If the herniated loops fail to return, the infant has an omphalocele: bowel, and sometimes liver, in a midline sac into which the cord inserts. It often accompanies trisomies 13 and 18, Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome, and cardiac and neural tube defects."},{"type":"table","title":"Omphalocele and gastroschisis","cols":["Feature","Omphalocele","Gastroschisis"],"rows":[["Mechanism","Physiologic hernia fails to return","Lateral body wall fails to close"],["Location","Midline, through the umbilical ring","Beside the umbilicus, usually on the right"],["Covering","Sac of amnion and peritoneum","None; bowel bathed in amniotic fluid"],["Cord insertion","Into the sac","Normal, beside the defect"],["Associated anomalies","Frequent, including aneuploidy","Uncommon; bowel often thickened and inflamed"]],"caption":"Both raise maternal serum alpha fetoprotein."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A newborn's contrast study shows the duodenojejunal junction to the right of the spine. Which segments of bowel are at risk of infarction, and which are spared?","explain":"The narrow mesentery of malrotation lets the midgut twist around the SMA, so the whole SMA territory from the distal duodenum to the distal transverse colon is at risk, while the hindgut supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery is spared.","check":"Compare with the malrotation paragraph and the C01 table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-01-01-C03","obj":"GI-1.3","title":"Separation of the esophagus from the airway","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Early in week 4 the respiratory (laryngotracheal) diverticulum buds from the ventral wall of the foregut just below the pharynx. As it grows caudally, longitudinal tracheoesophageal ridges grow in from the lateral walls of the foregut and fuse in the midline to form the tracheoesophageal septum. The septum divides the tube into a ventral part, which becomes the larynx, trachea, and lung buds, and a dorsal part, which becomes the esophagus. The two remain connected only at the laryngeal inlet."},{"type":"flow","title":"Normal division of the foregut","steps":["Respiratory diverticulum buds from the ventral foregut","Tracheoesophageal ridges grow in from the lateral walls","Ridges fuse into the tracheoesophageal septum","Ventral tube becomes larynx, trachea, and lung buds","Dorsal tube becomes the esophagus"],"caption":"Airway and esophagus share one origin, so a faulty septum leaves them joined or leaves the esophagus interrupted."},{"type":"p","text":"The esophagus is short at first and lengthens quickly as the heart and lungs descend into the thorax. Its epithelium proliferates and partly fills the lumen before recanalizing, and its muscle comes from the surrounding mesenchyme: striated in the upper esophagus and smooth in the lower esophagus, with a mixed zone between. The vagus supplies both, the striated part directly and the smooth part through the enteric plexus. If the esophagus fails to lengthen enough, part of the stomach is pulled through the diaphragm as a congenital hiatal hernia."},{"type":"p","text":"When the septum deviates posteriorly, or the dorsal foregut wall is pushed forward, the division is incomplete. The result is esophageal atresia, in which the esophagus ends blindly, a tracheoesophageal fistula (TEF), an abnormal channel between the esophagus and trachea, or both. The defect occurs in about 1 in 3,000 births. In esophageal atresia, swallowed amniotic fluid cannot reach the intestine, where it is normally absorbed and returned to the mother through the placenta, so polyhydramnios is common. About half of affected infants have other anomalies, and the defect belongs to the VACTERL association: vertebral, anal, cardiac, tracheoesophageal, renal, and limb anomalies."},{"type":"table","title":"Main forms of esophageal atresia and fistula","cols":["Form","Anatomy","Key findings and their basis"],"rows":[["Atresia with distal fistula (most common, about 85 to 90%)","Blind upper pouch; lower esophagus joins the trachea near the carina","Drooling and choking or cyanosis at the first feed as the pouch overflows into the larynx; a feeding tube coils in the pouch on radiograph; gas in the stomach because air passes through the fistula; gastric acid can reflux into the lungs"],["Isolated atresia (no fistula)","Blind upper pouch; lower esophagus does not reach the airway","Drooling and polyhydramnios; gasless abdomen because no air can reach the stomach"],["Isolated fistula without atresia (H type)","Continuous esophagus with a fistula to the trachea","Coughing with feeds and recurrent aspiration pneumonia; often recognized later because swallowing is possible"]],"caption":"Gas below the diaphragm means the lower esophagus connects to the airway; a gasless abdomen means it does not."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A newborn drools, cannot pass a feeding tube, and has no gas anywhere in the abdomen on radiograph. Which form of the defect is present?","explain":"Isolated esophageal atresia without a distal fistula. Without a connection between the trachea and lower esophagus, swallowed or inhaled air cannot reach the stomach, so the bowel is gasless.","check":"Compare with the table of forms of esophageal atresia and fistula."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-01-01-C04","obj":"GI-1.4","title":"Liver, biliary tree, and pancreas","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The liver begins in weeks 3 to 4 as the hepatic diverticulum, an endodermal outgrowth from the ventral wall of the distal foregut where the duodenum will form. It grows into the septum transversum. There its endodermal cords become hepatocytes and the lining of the intrahepatic bile ducts, and they surround the vitelline and umbilical veins, which break up to form the hepatic sinusoids. Mesoderm of the septum transversum forms the connective tissue of the liver, and the ventral mesentery derived from it becomes the lesser omentum and the falciform ligament. Hepatocytes begin to secrete bile at about week 12."},{"type":"p","text":"The stalk joining the liver bud to the foregut narrows to become the bile duct, and a small ventral outgrowth from it forms the gallbladder and cystic duct. The bile duct first opens on the ventral wall of the duodenum; when the duodenum rotates to the right and becomes C shaped, its opening is carried to the dorsomedial wall, so the adult bile duct passes behind the duodenum. In biliary atresia the extrahepatic ducts are destroyed by a progressive inflammatory and fibrosing process around the time of birth, rather than by simple failure of a developmental step. Because bile cannot reach the gut, infants develop conjugated hyperbilirubinemia, dark urine, and pale stools in the first weeks, followed by biliary cirrhosis."},{"type":"p","text":"The pancreas forms from two endodermal buds of the duodenum. The dorsal bud grows into the dorsal mesentery; the ventral bud lies beside the bile duct. When the duodenum rotates, the ventral bud travels with the bile duct around the back of the duodenum and comes to lie below and behind the dorsal bud. The parenchyma fuses, and the duct systems join so that the main pancreatic duct drains with the bile duct at the major papilla. Islets develop from the same endoderm in the third month."},{"type":"flow","title":"Development of the pancreas","steps":["Dorsal and ventral buds arise from duodenal endoderm","Duodenum rotates to the right and becomes C shaped","Ventral bud and bile duct swing behind the duodenum","Ventral bud lies below and behind the dorsal bud","Parenchyma fuses and the two duct systems join","Main duct and bile duct drain at the major papilla"],"caption":"The ventral bud migrates with the bile duct, which is why the two share the major papilla."},{"type":"table","title":"Contributions of the two pancreatic buds","cols":["Bud","Parenchyma formed","Duct formed","Drains at"],"rows":[["Ventral","Uncinate process and lower part of the head","Proximal part of the main pancreatic duct","Major papilla, with the bile duct"],["Dorsal","Upper head, neck, body, and tail","Distal main duct; its proximal part persists as the accessory duct","Minor papilla (accessory duct)"]],"caption":"The adult main pancreatic duct is the whole ventral duct joined to the distal dorsal duct."},{"type":"p","text":"Two defects follow from this sequence. In annular pancreas the ventral bud is bifid; one half migrates normally while the other passes around the opposite side of the duodenum, so a ring of pancreatic tissue encircles the second part of the duodenum. The ring can narrow or obstruct the duodenum in a newborn or present later in life, and it is associated with Down syndrome and duodenal atresia. In pancreas divisum the parenchyma fuses but the ducts do not. The dorsal duct then drains most of the gland through the small minor papilla, while the ventral duct drains only the uncinate process and lower head. It is the most common congenital anomaly of the pancreas and is usually silent, but in a minority the high flow through a narrow opening is thought to raise ductal pressure and contribute to recurrent acute pancreatitis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-01-01-C05","obj":"GI-1.5","title":"Neural crest and the enteric nervous system","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The enteric nervous system comes from neural crest. Most of it arises from vagal neural crest at the level of the hindbrain, with a smaller sacral crest contribution to the hindgut. These cells enter the foregut and migrate in a cranial to caudal wave through the gut mesenchyme, reaching the distal rectum during the early fetal period. They first form the myenteric plexus between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers, and some then move inward to form the submucosal plexus."},{"type":"table","title":"Cells that control the gut wall","cols":["Component","Location","Origin","Main role"],"rows":[["Myenteric (Auerbach) plexus","Between circular and longitudinal muscle","Neural crest","Motility: peristalsis and muscle tone"],["Submucosal (Meissner) plexus","Submucosa","Neural crest","Secretion, absorption, and local blood flow"],["Interstitial cells of Cajal","Within the muscle layers","Mesenchyme, not neural crest","Pacemaker slow waves"]],"caption":"Enteric neurons include excitatory neurons that release acetylcholine and inhibitory neurons that release nitric oxide and vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP)."},{"type":"p","text":"Migration depends on signaling. Gut mesenchyme secretes glial cell line derived neurotrophic factor (GDNF), which binds the RET receptor tyrosine kinase on crest cells and drives their survival, proliferation, and migration. Endothelin 3 acting on the endothelin B receptor (EDNRB) keeps precursors dividing so the pool is large enough to colonize the whole gut, and the transcription factor SOX10 is needed for the crest cells to survive. Melanocytes also come from neural crest, so EDNRB, endothelin 3, and SOX10 mutations can combine aganglionosis with patchy depigmentation and deafness (Waardenburg syndrome type 4)."},{"type":"p","text":"Hirschsprung disease (congenital aganglionic megacolon) results when the wave stops before reaching the end of the gut. Loss of function mutations in RET are the most common identified cause. Because colonization proceeds cranial to caudal, the aganglionic segment always begins at the internal anal sphincter and extends proximally a variable distance, most often only through the rectosigmoid. It affects about 1 in 5,000 births, is about four times more common in boys, and about 10% of cases occur in children with Down syndrome."},{"type":"flow","title":"From RET mutation to megacolon","steps":["RET loss of function reduces GDNF signaling","Neural crest wave stops before the distal bowel","Distal segment lacks myenteric and submucosal ganglia","No inhibitory NO and VIP neurons, so segment cannot relax","Peristalsis stops at the narrow segment: functional obstruction","Proximal normal colon dilates and hypertrophies"],"caption":"The diseased segment is the narrow one; the dilated colon above it is normal bowel stretched by retained stool."},{"type":"p","text":"The aganglionic segment stays tonically contracted, and the rectoanal inhibitory reflex, relaxation of the internal sphincter when the rectum is distended, is absent. Newborns fail to pass meconium in the first 48 hours and develop abdominal distension and vomiting; a rectal examination may release an explosive gush of gas and stool. A contrast enema shows a narrow distal segment and a transition zone to dilated colon. Rectal suction biopsy shows no ganglion cells in the submucosa and thickened acetylcholinesterase positive nerve fibers from extrinsic nerves. Enterocolitis from stasis is the most feared complication. Acquired megacolon in Chagas disease is the microbiologic parallel: Trypanosoma cruzi destroys ganglia that had formed normally."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Could a failure of neural crest migration leave the transverse colon aganglionic while the rectum has normal ganglia?","explain":"Essentially no. Crest cells colonize the gut from cranial to caudal, so any arrest leaves the most distal bowel uncolonized. The rectum is always involved, and skip lesions are extremely rare.","check":"Compare with the description of migration and the Hirschsprung paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-01-01-C06","obj":"GI-1.6","title":"Duodenal recanalization and duodenal atresia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The duodenum forms from the end of the foregut and the beginning of the midgut, with the junction just distal to the opening of the bile duct, which is why it receives both celiac and superior mesenteric blood. As the stomach rotates, the duodenum becomes C shaped, swings to the right, and most of it becomes secondarily retroperitoneal when its mesentery fuses with the posterior wall. During the second month its epithelium proliferates so rapidly that it fills the lumen. Vacuoles then form within the epithelial plug and coalesce, and the lumen is restored by the end of the embryonic period."},{"type":"p","text":"If recanalization fails, the duodenum is completely closed (atresia) or narrowed by a web or diaphragm (stenosis). Most duodenal atresias lie in the second part just distal to the major papilla, so bile enters the obstructed segment and the vomitus is bilious; a lesion above the papilla gives nonbilious vomiting. The fetus cannot carry swallowed amniotic fluid to the absorptive intestine, so polyhydramnios is common. A plain radiograph shows the double bubble: gas in the stomach and in the dilated proximal duodenum, with no gas beyond in complete atresia. About 30% of affected infants have trisomy 21, and annular pancreas, malrotation, and cardiac defects often coexist."},{"type":"p","text":"Atresia of the jejunum and ileum has a different cause. These segments become atretic after an intrauterine vascular accident, such as volvulus or occlusion of a mesenteric branch, that leads to ischemic necrosis and resorption of a segment of bowel. They produce several dilated loops, more distension, and are not associated with Down syndrome. Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis is not a developmental failure; the pyloric muscle thickens after birth."},{"type":"table","title":"Localizing obstruction in a vomiting newborn","cols":["Condition","Onset and vomitus","Imaging","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Esophageal atresia with distal TEF","First feed; regurgitation of unchanged feed, drooling","Plain film: tube coils in the upper pouch; gas in the stomach","Faulty tracheoesophageal septum"],["Duodenal atresia","First day; usually bilious","Plain film: double bubble, no distal gas","Failed recanalization"],["Malrotation with volvulus","Often first weeks, sudden; bilious","Upper GI contrast study: duodenojejunal junction right of midline; distal gas may be present","Narrow mesentery twists around the SMA"],["Jejunoileal atresia","First day; bilious, with distension","Plain film: several dilated loops","Intrauterine vascular accident"],["Hirschsprung disease","First days; delayed meconium, then bilious vomiting","Contrast enema: narrow distal segment, transition zone, dilated colon above","Failed neural crest migration"],["Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis","About 2 to 8 weeks; projectile, nonbilious","Plain film: large gastric bubble, little distal gas","Postnatal pyloric muscle hypertrophy"]],"caption":"Bile in the vomitus places the obstruction below the major papilla; nonbilious vomiting places it above."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading a double bubble","text":"A newborn whose mother had polyhydramnios vomits bile stained fluid a few hours after the first feed. The abdomen is not distended, and the radiograph shows a double bubble with no gas beyond. Bile places the block below the major papilla. The two bubbles are the stomach and the dilated proximal duodenum, and the absence of distal gas means the obstruction is complete rather than a stenosis. Polyhydramnios shows that swallowed fluid never reached the absorptive bowel, and the flat abdomen shows the block is high. This is duodenal atresia from failed recanalization of the second part, and the infant should be examined for features of trisomy 21. If gas were present beyond the duodenum, malrotation with volvulus would have to be considered, because a twist can be partial and still strangle the entire midgut."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Each defect in this chapter is a normal step that failed, and the step tells you the anatomy, the signs, and the associations. Pathology explains the timing and pattern of each defect: failed recanalization gives duodenal atresia, while a vascular accident gives jejunoileal atresia, and failed return gives omphalocele, while failed body wall closure gives gastroschisis. Genetics and syndromes link the gut to the rest of the body, as with trisomy 21 in duodenal atresia, annular pancreas, and Hirschsprung disease, and the VACTERL association with tracheoesophageal fistula."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology and microbiology show why these structures matter after birth. The enteric plexuses are the circuits that coordinate peristalsis, and losing their inhibitory neurons, whether by failed migration or by Trypanosoma cruzi infection, produces the same functional obstruction and dilation. The limits of embryologic reasoning are real: the timing of weeks and the frequencies of associated anomalies vary between sources, and conditions such as biliary atresia and pyloric stenosis appear in newborns but are not simple failures of a developmental step."}],"summary":["Foregut, midgut, and hindgut are supplied by the celiac trunk, superior mesenteric artery, and inferior mesenteric artery; the borders lie just distal to the major papilla and at the distal third of the transverse colon.","Visceral pain travels with sympathetic afferents to the spinal segments of each division and is referred to the epigastrium, umbilicus, or suprapubic region; it localizes only when the parietal peritoneum is involved.","The midgut herniates into the cord in week 6, rotates 270 degrees counterclockwise around the SMA (90 degrees outside, 180 degrees during return), and returns by about week 10.","Malrotation leaves a narrow mesentery that can twist around the SMA, causing bilious vomiting and ischemia of the whole midgut; Ladd bands can compress the duodenum.","Omphalocele is failed return of the physiologic hernia into a sac of amnion and peritoneum, often with aneuploidy; gastroschisis is an uncovered body wall defect beside the umbilicus.","The tracheoesophageal septum separates the ventral airway from the dorsal esophagus; posterior deviation gives atresia, fistula, or both, most often a blind upper pouch with a distal fistula.","The ventral pancreatic bud rotates behind the duodenum with the bile duct; a bifid ventral bud gives annular pancreas, and failed duct fusion gives pancreas divisum with drainage through the minor papilla.","Neural crest colonizes the gut cranial to caudal under GDNF and RET signaling, so Hirschsprung disease always involves the rectum and produces a narrow aganglionic segment below a dilated normal colon.","Failed recanalization of the second part of the duodenum gives bilious vomiting, polyhydramnios, a double bubble, and a strong association with trisomy 21; jejunoileal atresia follows vascular accidents."],"sources":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Digestive System"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen: abdominal viscera, their arterial supply, innervation, and referred visceral pain"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Gastrointestinal Tract, congenital abnormalities including atresia, Hirschsprung disease, and abdominal wall defects; The Pancreas, congenital anomalies"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"General Principles of Gastrointestinal Function: Motility, Nervous Control, and Blood Circulation"}]},"GI-10":{"unit":"GI-10","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-10","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-10-01","title":"Inherited Diseases of the Liver, Pancreas, and Colon","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the single-gene diseases that most often injure the digestive organs: iron overload in hereditary hemochromatosis, copper overload in Wilson disease, alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, the two molecular routes to colorectal cancer with their inherited syndromes, and the gut and liver effects of cystic fibrosis. Board questions either name a gene or inheritance pattern and ask what follows, or describe skin, eye, joint, lung, or stool findings and ask for the gene. Each section traces one molecular defect from the normal protein to every finding it explains.","prerequisites":["Mendelian inheritance patterns, codominance, penetrance, and the Knudson two-hit model","Protein folding, endoplasmic reticulum quality control, and proteasomal degradation","Liver acinus zones and biliary excretion (GI-9)","Exocrine pancreatic secretion and fat digestion (GI-6, GI-7)","Oncogenes, tumor suppressor genes, and DNA repair"],"connections":[{"to":"Hepatic diseases and disorders (GI-13-C)","how":"Metal overload and alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency are inherited causes of chronic hepatitis, cirrhosis, and hepatocellular carcinoma."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal neoplasms (GI-13-K)","how":"The adenoma to carcinoma and mismatch repair pathways explain the behavior, location, and histology of sporadic and inherited colorectal cancers."},{"to":"Pulmonary system","how":"Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency and cystic fibrosis damage the lung through the same proteins that injure the liver and pancreas."},{"to":"Hematology","how":"Hepcidin and ferroportin control both dietary iron absorption and macrophage iron recycling, and free copper causes hemolysis in Wilson disease."},{"to":"Pancreatic disease and malabsorption (GI-13-B, GI-13-E)","how":"Cystic fibrosis is the classic inherited cause of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency and fat-soluble vitamin deficiency."}],"terms":[{"term":"Hepcidin","def":"A liver-derived peptide hormone that lowers plasma iron by binding ferroportin and causing its internalization and degradation."},{"term":"Ferroportin","def":"The only known cellular iron exporter, found on the basolateral membrane of duodenal enterocytes and on macrophages and hepatocytes."},{"term":"Transferrin saturation","def":"The percentage of transferrin iron-binding sites occupied, calculated as serum iron divided by total iron-binding capacity."},{"term":"Ceruloplasmin","def":"A copper-containing ferroxidase made by hepatocytes that carries most of the copper in plasma."},{"term":"Kayser-Fleischer ring","def":"A golden-brown ring at the edge of the cornea formed by copper deposited in Descemet membrane."},{"term":"Codominance","def":"Expression of both alleles of a gene in a heterozygote, so each allele contributes its own product."},{"term":"Loop-sheet polymerization","def":"Insertion of the reactive center loop of one misfolded serpin molecule into the beta-sheet of the next, forming chains that cannot be secreted."},{"term":"Microsatellite instability","def":"Changes in the length of short repeated DNA sequences in a tumor, caused by failed DNA mismatch repair."},{"term":"Two-hit hypothesis","def":"The principle that both alleles of a tumor suppressor gene must be inactivated before a cell loses its function."},{"term":"Hamartoma","def":"A disorganized but benign overgrowth of tissue elements normally found at that site."},{"term":"CFTR","def":"Cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator, a cAMP-regulated, ATP-gated anion channel that conducts chloride and bicarbonate."},{"term":"Meconium ileus","def":"Obstruction of the distal ileum in a newborn by thick, dehydrated meconium."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-10-01-C01","obj":"GI-10.1","title":"Hereditary hemochromatosis: low hepcidin and parenchymal iron overload","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The body has no regulated way to excrete iron. Adults lose only about 1 to 2 mg per day in shed cells and minor bleeding, so total body iron (about 3 to 4 g) is controlled at absorption. The controller is hepcidin, a peptide hormone made by hepatocytes. Hepcidin binds ferroportin on duodenal enterocytes and macrophages and causes its internalization and degradation. Low hepcidin therefore lets iron flow into plasma."},{"type":"p","text":"Hepatocytes raise hepcidin when transferrin saturation rises. HFE, an MHC class I-like protein that must pair with beta-2 microglobulin to reach the cell surface, works with transferrin receptors and the BMP6 to SMAD signaling pathway to turn on the hepcidin gene. Hereditary hemochromatosis (HH) is most often caused by homozygosity for the HFE C282Y mutation on chromosome 6p, inherited as an autosomal recessive trait and common in people of Northern European descent. Replacing cysteine 282 with tyrosine breaks a disulfide bond, so HFE cannot bind beta-2 microglobulin and stays inside the cell. The liver therefore makes too little hepcidin for the body's iron stores, and the gut keeps absorbing iron as though the person were iron deficient."},{"type":"flow","title":"From HFE mutation to organ injury","steps":["Homozygous HFE C282Y impairs hepatocyte iron sensing","Hepcidin stays inappropriately low","Ferroportin stays active on enterocytes and macrophages","Excess iron absorbed and released into plasma for years","Transferrin saturates; non-transferrin-bound iron enters parenchymal cells","Fenton reaction makes hydroxyl radicals","Lipid peroxidation, cell death, and stellate cell fibrosis"],"caption":"HH is a hormone deficiency: the defect is the hepcidin signal, not the iron transporters themselves."},{"type":"p","text":"Once transferrin is saturated, non-transferrin-bound iron enters parenchymal cells of the liver, pancreas, heart, joints, and pituitary. Free ferrous iron drives the Fenton reaction (Fe2+ plus hydrogen peroxide yields the hydroxyl radical), which peroxidizes membranes, damages mitochondria and DNA, and activates hepatic stellate cells to lay down collagen. Iron appears first as hemosiderin in periportal (zone 1) hepatocytes and stains blue with Prussian blue. Because ferroportin stays active, macrophages in spleen and marrow hold relatively little iron, the reverse of transfusional overload, in which iron is delivered to macrophages first."},{"type":"table","title":"Organ damage in hereditary hemochromatosis","cols":["Site","Mechanism","Manifestation"],"rows":[["Liver","Hepatocyte oxidative injury and stellate cell fibrosis","Hepatomegaly, micronodular cirrhosis, hepatocellular carcinoma"],["Pancreatic islets","Iron injury to beta cells, plus insulin resistance from liver disease","Diabetes mellitus"],["Skin","Increased melanin, with some dermal hemosiderin","Bronze or slate-gray pigmentation"],["Heart","Iron in cardiac myocytes","Dilated (sometimes restrictive) cardiomyopathy, arrhythmias, conduction block"],["Joints","Synovial iron and calcium pyrophosphate deposition","Arthropathy of the second and third metacarpophalangeal joints, chondrocalcinosis"],["Pituitary","Iron in gonadotrophs","Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism"]],"caption":"Cirrhosis, diabetes, and skin pigmentation form the classic triad sometimes called bronze diabetes."},{"type":"p","text":"Disease usually appears in middle age, after total body iron has reached many times normal. Women present about a decade later than men because menstruation and pregnancy remove iron. Penetrance is incomplete: many C282Y homozygotes have high iron studies but no organ damage; alcohol, viral hepatitis, and modifier genes push some toward cirrhosis. The laboratory signature is a high transferrin saturation (above about 45%), the earliest marker, with a high ferritin that reflects stored iron. Cirrhosis greatly raises the risk of hepatocellular carcinoma because hepatocytes regenerate for years under oxidative DNA damage. Iron-rich tissue also favors siderophilic bacteria such as Vibrio vulnificus, Yersinia enterocolitica, and Listeria monocytogenes."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A man with beta-thalassemia major who receives regular transfusions develops iron overload. Where does the iron first accumulate, and how does that differ from HFE hemochromatosis?","explain":"Transfused red cells are removed by macrophages, so iron loads the reticuloendothelial system first; in HH, low hepcidin keeps macrophages exporting iron, so parenchymal cells load first.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on non-transferrin-bound iron and macrophage iron."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-10-01-C02","obj":"GI-10.2","title":"Wilson disease: failed copper excretion and ceruloplasmin loading","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Copper is a cofactor for cytochrome c oxidase, superoxide dismutase, lysyl oxidase, and ceruloplasmin, but free copper is a strong oxidant. Where iron balance is set at absorption, copper balance is set at excretion: bile is the only significant route for removing excess copper. Inside the hepatocyte, the chaperone ATOX1 delivers copper to ATP7B, a P-type copper-transporting ATPase in the trans-Golgi network. ATP7B has two jobs. It pumps copper into the Golgi lumen, where it is built into apoceruloplasmin to form ceruloplasmin, and when hepatocyte copper rises it moves to vesicles near the canalicular membrane and sends copper into bile."},{"type":"p","text":"Wilson disease (hepatolenticular degeneration) is the autosomal recessive loss of ATP7B, encoded on chromosome 13q. Both jobs fail. Apoceruloplasmin is secreted without copper, is unstable, and is degraded quickly, so serum ceruloplasmin is low. Copper cannot enter bile, so it accumulates in hepatocytes, first bound to metallothionein and then free, where it generates reactive oxygen species, injures mitochondria, and produces steatosis, chronic hepatitis, and eventually cirrhosis. When hepatocyte storage is exceeded or hepatocytes die, copper that is not bound to ceruloplasmin spills into blood and deposits in other tissues."},{"type":"flow","title":"Copper accumulation in Wilson disease","steps":["Biallelic ATP7B loss in hepatocytes","No copper loading onto ceruloplasmin; no biliary copper excretion","Low serum ceruloplasmin; copper builds up in the liver","Oxidative hepatocyte injury: steatosis, hepatitis, cirrhosis","Free copper released into blood","Deposits in cornea, basal ganglia, kidney; red cells hemolyze"],"caption":"The liver is injured first; the eye, brain, and kidney are injured by copper that overflows from it."},{"type":"table","title":"Wilson disease findings and their mechanisms","cols":["Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Low serum ceruloplasmin (below about 20 mg/dL)","Copper-free apoceruloplasmin is degraded rapidly"],["Usually low total serum copper but high non-ceruloplasmin copper","Ceruloplasmin normally carries most plasma copper; the unbound fraction rises"],["High 24-hour urine copper","Unbound copper is filtered by the glomerulus"],["High hepatic copper content on biopsy","Copper cannot leave the hepatocyte in bile"],["Kayser-Fleischer rings","Copper in Descemet membrane at the corneal periphery"],["Tremor, dystonia, dysarthria, parkinsonism, psychiatric change","Copper injury to the lenticular nucleus (putamen and globus pallidus)"],["Coombs-negative hemolytic anemia","Free copper released from dying hepatocytes oxidizes red cell membranes"],["Proximal tubular dysfunction (Fanconi pattern)","Copper deposition in renal tubules"]],"caption":"Total body copper is high even though serum ceruloplasmin and total serum copper are low."},{"type":"p","text":"Presentation depends on age. Children and adolescents usually present with liver disease, ranging from asymptomatic transaminase elevation to chronic hepatitis, cirrhosis, or acute liver failure with Coombs-negative hemolysis. Young adults more often present with movement disorders or psychiatric change, because copper has had time to reach the brain. Kayser-Fleischer rings are found in nearly all patients with neurologic disease but in only about half of those with liver disease alone, so their absence does not exclude the diagnosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Two distinctions matter. Ceruloplasmin is an acute phase reactant, so inflammation can raise it into the normal range, and it is also low in protein-losing states, severe liver failure of any cause, and some heterozygous carriers; no single value is diagnostic. Menkes disease is the mirror image: X-linked loss of ATP7A, the enterocyte copper exporter, blocks absorption and causes copper deficiency with kinky hair, connective tissue weakness, and neurodegeneration."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-10-01-C03","obj":"GI-10.3","title":"Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency: a toxic protein in the liver and too little in the lung","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Alpha-1 antitrypsin (AAT) is a serine protease inhibitor (serpin) encoded by SERPINA1 on chromosome 14q. Hepatocytes make most of it and secrete it into plasma, where it forms the major part of the alpha-1 globulin band on serum protein electrophoresis. Its main target is neutrophil elastase in the lung. AAT acts as bait: elastase cleaves its reactive center loop, the inhibitor changes shape, and the enzyme is trapped in an inactive complex. Alleles are codominant and named by electrophoretic mobility: M is normal, S is mildly deficient, Z is severely deficient, and null alleles make no protein."},{"type":"p","text":"The Z allele (Glu342Lys) destabilizes the protein during folding. The reactive loop of one Z molecule inserts into the beta-sheet of the next, a process called loop-sheet polymerization, and the chains cannot leave the endoplasmic reticulum (ER). Most Z protein is retained, so PiZZ homozygotes have serum levels of only about 10 to 15% of normal. One mutation therefore causes two different diseases by two different mechanisms."},{"type":"flow","title":"One mutation, two mechanisms","steps":["SERPINA1 Z allele (Glu342Lys) misfolds","Z protein polymerizes in the hepatocyte ER","Liver: retained polymers cause ER stress and hepatocyte death","Regeneration and fibrosis lead to cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma","Lung: little AAT reaches plasma and alveoli","Unopposed neutrophil elastase destroys alveolar walls","Panacinar emphysema, worst at the lung bases"],"caption":"Liver disease is a toxic gain of function; lung disease is a loss of function."},{"type":"p","text":"In the liver, retained polymers trigger ER stress, mitochondrial injury, and apoptosis, and the surviving hepatocytes regenerate under chronic injury, which leads to fibrosis, cirrhosis, and hepatocellular carcinoma. The polymers appear as round eosinophilic globules in periportal hepatocytes that are PAS-positive and diastase-resistant. PAS stains carbohydrate, and diastase digests glycogen; a glycoprotein such as AAT keeps its PAS stain after diastase, which separates these globules from glycogen. Liver disease can appear as neonatal cholestatic hepatitis in a minority of PiZZ infants or as cirrhosis in adults, and its severity varies with how efficiently each person degrades the retained polymers."},{"type":"p","text":"In the lung, too little AAT leaves neutrophil elastase free to digest elastin. Damage involves the whole acinus (panacinar emphysema) and is worst in the lower lobes, probably because they receive more blood flow and therefore more neutrophils. This differs from smoking-related centriacinar emphysema, which favors the upper lobes. Smoking accelerates AAT deficiency greatly: oxidants convert methionine 358 in the reactive loop to methionine sulfoxide, inactivating the remaining inhibitor, and smoke recruits more neutrophils. Smokers with PiZZ often develop emphysema in their 30s or 40s."},{"type":"example","title":"Predicting disease from genotype","text":"Compare three genotypes. PiZZ: about 10 to 15% of normal serum AAT, with polymers retained in hepatocytes, so both cirrhosis and emphysema are possible. Null/null: no AAT is made at all, so serum levels are lowest and emphysema is severe, but nothing accumulates in the ER, so the liver is spared. PiMZ: the M allele is secreted normally and the Z allele is partly retained, giving intermediate serum levels (roughly half to two-thirds of normal) with little lung risk in nonsmokers and only a modestly raised risk of liver disease when another insult is present. The rule that follows: liver disease requires a retained, misfolded protein, while lung disease requires only a low serum level."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-10-01-C04","obj":"GI-10.4","title":"Two molecular pathways to colorectal cancer","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Colonic epithelium renews every few days from stem cells at the crypt base, where Wnt signaling keeps cells proliferating. As cells move up the crypt, Wnt signaling falls and beta-catenin, the transcriptional effector of the pathway, is destroyed by a complex that includes APC, axin, and glycogen synthase kinase 3 beta. This complex phosphorylates beta-catenin and marks it for ubiquitination and proteasomal degradation, so surface cells stop dividing."},{"type":"h","text":"Chromosomal instability: the adenoma to carcinoma sequence"},{"type":"p","text":"Most sporadic colorectal cancers follow this route. Loss of both APC alleles (chromosome 5q) is the gatekeeping event: beta-catenin escapes destruction, enters the nucleus, and with TCF transcription factors switches on growth genes such as MYC and cyclin D1, producing a small adenoma. An activating KRAS point mutation, which needs only one allele, impairs GTP hydrolysis, holding the GTPase in its active GTP-bound state and giving growth-factor-independent signaling, so the adenoma enlarges. Loss of chromosome 18q genes such as SMAD4 removes growth inhibition by TGF-beta. Loss of TP53 (chromosome 17p) comes late: without p53, cells with DNA damage neither arrest in G1 nor undergo apoptosis, and the adenoma becomes an invasive carcinoma. The order is typical rather than fixed; the accumulation of hits matters most, and the sequence usually takes about a decade."},{"type":"flow","title":"Adenoma to carcinoma sequence","steps":["Normal colonic mucosa","APC loss: beta-catenin accumulates, early adenoma","KRAS activation: adenoma enlarges","SMAD4 and other 18q losses","TP53 loss: no G1 arrest or apoptosis","Invasive adenocarcinoma"],"caption":"Tumor suppressors (APC, SMAD4, TP53) need two hits; the oncogene KRAS needs one."},{"type":"h","text":"Microsatellite instability and Lynch syndrome"},{"type":"p","text":"DNA polymerase slips on microsatellites, short tandem repeats such as runs of CA, creating small insertion or deletion loops. Mismatch repair proteins correct these errors: MSH2 with MSH6 recognizes the mismatch, and MLH1 with PMS2 directs excision and resynthesis. When repair fails, repeat lengths change throughout the genome, producing microsatellite instability (MSI) and frameshift mutations in growth genes that contain repeats, such as TGFBR2 and BAX. Lynch syndrome (hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer) is an autosomal dominant germline mutation in MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, or PMS2 (or an EPCAM deletion that silences MSH2). The germline mutation is the first hit, and a somatic second hit in any cell removes repair. Patients have few adenomas, but each progresses quickly, so cancers appear early (often in the 40s). Endometrial cancer is the most common extracolonic tumor, followed by ovarian, gastric, small bowel, urothelial, and hepatobiliary cancers. Most MSI colorectal cancers, however, are sporadic and arise when the MLH1 promoter is silenced by hypermethylation, usually in serrated lesions with BRAF V600E mutations."},{"type":"table","title":"Comparing the two pathways","cols":["Feature","Chromosomal instability","Mismatch repair (MSI)"],"rows":[["Share of colorectal cancer","Most sporadic cancers","About 15%; Lynch syndrome is a small fraction"],["Key genes","APC, KRAS, SMAD4, TP53","MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2"],["Inherited form","FAP (germline APC)","Lynch syndrome (germline mismatch repair gene)"],["Genome","Aneuploid, gains and losses of chromosomes","Near-diploid, many small frameshift mutations"],["Typical site","Left colon and rectum","Right colon"],["Histology","Usual gland-forming adenocarcinoma","Mucinous or poorly differentiated, many lymphocytes"]],"caption":"Both pathways end in adenocarcinoma, but they differ in genes, genome pattern, location, and histology."},{"type":"example","title":"Is this MSI tumor inherited or sporadic?","text":"A right-sided colon cancer shows microsatellite instability and, on immunohistochemistry, loss of MLH1 and PMS2 staining. PMS2 is lost too because PMS2 is unstable without its partner MLH1, so the primary defect is MLH1. MLH1 loss can be germline (Lynch) or sporadic (promoter hypermethylation). If the tumor carries BRAF V600E and the MLH1 promoter is methylated, the pattern fits the sporadic serrated pathway. If neither is present, a germline MLH1 mutation is likely. Loss of MSH2 with MSH6, by contrast, points to a germline MSH2 (or EPCAM) defect."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-10-01-C05","obj":"GI-10.5","title":"Inherited polyposis syndromes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Polyposis syndromes are distinguished by the gene involved, the inheritance pattern, and above all the type of polyp. An adenoma is a dysplastic neoplasm of the glandular epithelium and is itself a precursor of carcinoma. A hamartoma is a disorganized overgrowth of normal tissue elements; it is not dysplastic, yet the syndromes that produce hamartomas still raise cancer risk."},{"type":"p","text":"Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) is an autosomal dominant germline mutation in APC on chromosome 5q, with about a quarter of cases arising de novo. Every colonic cell already carries one mutant allele, so a single somatic hit in any crypt starts an adenoma. That explains why hundreds to thousands of adenomas carpet the colon from adolescence and why colorectal cancer is nearly certain by about age 40 to 50 if the colon remains. Duodenal and periampullary adenomas and congenital hypertrophy of the retinal pigment epithelium are also common. Attenuated FAP, from mutations at the ends of the gene, produces fewer than 100 adenomas and later cancer."},{"type":"p","text":"Gardner syndrome is FAP with prominent extracolonic growths: osteomas of the skull and mandible, desmoid tumors, epidermoid cysts, and dental anomalies such as supernumerary or impacted teeth. Turcot syndrome is colorectal polyposis with a primary brain tumor; medulloblastoma accompanies APC mutations, and glioblastoma accompanies mismatch repair defects."},{"type":"p","text":"MUTYH-associated polyposis is autosomal recessive. Oxidized guanine (8-oxoguanine) pairs with adenine during replication, and the MUTYH glycosylase removes that adenine as part of base excision repair. Without both copies of MUTYH, G:C to T:A transversions accumulate, including in APC and KRAS, producing tens to a few hundred adenomas and a high cancer risk. Because both alleles must be lost, parents are usually unaffected carriers and siblings share the risk."},{"type":"p","text":"Peutz-Jeghers syndrome is autosomal dominant loss of STK11 (LKB1), a kinase that activates AMP-activated protein kinase and so restrains mTOR-driven growth. Hamartomatous polyps occur mainly in the small intestine and have a characteristic branching core of smooth muscle covered by normal mucosa. Large polyps lead the bowel into intussusception or bleed. Melanin pigmentation appears in childhood on the lips, buccal mucosa, and skin of the hands and feet. Cancer risk rises in the colon, stomach, small bowel, pancreas, breast, ovary, and testis."},{"type":"table","title":"Inherited polyposis syndromes","cols":["Syndrome","Gene and inheritance","Polyps","Distinguishing features"],"rows":[["FAP","APC; autosomal dominant","Hundreds to thousands of adenomas","Near-certain colorectal cancer; retinal pigment epithelium hypertrophy"],["Gardner","APC; autosomal dominant","Numerous adenomas","Osteomas, desmoids, epidermoid cysts, extra teeth"],["Turcot","APC or mismatch repair genes","Adenomas","Medulloblastoma (APC) or glioblastoma (mismatch repair)"],["MUTYH-associated","MUTYH; autosomal recessive","Tens to hundreds of adenomas","Base excision repair defect; unaffected parents"],["Peutz-Jeghers","STK11; autosomal dominant","Hamartomas, mainly small bowel","Mucocutaneous pigmentation, intussusception, many cancer sites"]],"caption":"Lynch syndrome is not a polyposis syndrome: it produces few adenomas (see C04)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 45-year-old has about 60 colonic adenomas. Neither parent had polyps, but his sister has a similar number. Which syndrome fits best?","explain":"MUTYH-associated polyposis: an attenuated adenoma count with affected siblings and unaffected parents fits autosomal recessive inheritance, whereas FAP is dominant.","check":"Compare with the MUTYH paragraph and the inheritance column of the table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-10-01-C06","obj":"GI-10.6","title":"Cystic fibrosis in the gut, pancreas, and liver","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"CFTR, encoded on chromosome 7q, is an ATP-binding cassette protein that works as an anion channel. Protein kinase A, activated by cAMP, phosphorylates its regulatory domain, and ATP binding at its nucleotide-binding domains opens the pore to chloride and bicarbonate. In secretory epithelia (pancreatic ducts, cholangiocytes, intestinal crypts, and airways), apical anion secretion draws sodium across the tight junctions and water follows, keeping secretions fluid. In pancreatic ducts, CFTR also supports chloride and bicarbonate exchange that produces the bicarbonate-rich juice that neutralizes gastric acid."},{"type":"p","text":"Cystic fibrosis (CF) is autosomal recessive and most common in people of Northern European descent, with a carrier frequency of about 1 in 25. The most common mutation, F508del, removes a phenylalanine and causes misfolding; ER quality control sends the protein for proteasomal degradation, so little CFTR reaches the apical membrane. Other mutation classes block synthesis (nonsense mutations), gating, or conductance, but all reduce anion and water secretion, leaving secretions thick, dehydrated, and acidic."},{"type":"flow","title":"From CFTR mutation to fat-soluble vitamin deficiency","steps":["F508del CFTR misfolds and is degraded","Little CFTR at the apical duct membrane","Chloride and bicarbonate secretion fall; less water follows","Thick, acidic secretions plug pancreatic ductules","Acini are destroyed and replaced by fibrosis and fat","Lipase deficit causes steatorrhea","Vitamin A, D, E, and K malabsorption"],"caption":"Pancreatic destruction begins before birth, so most patients have exocrine insufficiency from infancy."},{"type":"table","title":"Gastrointestinal effects of cystic fibrosis","cols":["Site","Mechanism","Manifestation"],"rows":[["Fetal ileum","Dehydrated, protein-rich meconium sticks to the mucosa","Meconium ileus at birth; perforation can cause meconium peritonitis"],["Exocrine pancreas","Ductal plugging, acinar destruction, fibrosis","Steatorrhea, failure to thrive, high immunoreactive trypsinogen on newborn screening"],["Duodenal lumen","Low bicarbonate leaves the pH low, inactivating enzymes and precipitating bile acids","Worsens fat malabsorption"],["Endocrine pancreas","Islets damaged later by surrounding fibrosis","CF-related diabetes in adolescence and adulthood"],["Intrahepatic bile ducts","Thick bile plugs ductules, causing local inflammation and fibrosis","Focal biliary cirrhosis; in a minority, multilobular cirrhosis with portal hypertension"],["Gallbladder","Concentrated, sluggish bile","Small gallbladder, gallstones"]],"caption":"Every lesion traces back to too little water in a secretion that depends on CFTR."},{"type":"p","text":"Fat-soluble vitamin deficiency follows directly from fat malabsorption, because vitamins A, D, E, and K are absorbed within micelles with dietary fat. Vitamin A deficiency causes night blindness and xerophthalmia; vitamin D deficiency causes rickets or osteomalacia; vitamin E deficiency causes hemolytic anemia and a spinocerebellar neuropathy; vitamin K deficiency lowers factors II, VII, IX, and X and prolongs the prothrombin time. Protein malabsorption can cause hypoalbuminemia and edema in infants."},{"type":"p","text":"The sweat duct shows CFTR working in the opposite direction. There it reabsorbs chloride from the lumen, and sodium follows, so normal sweat reaching the skin is dilute. Without CFTR, chloride and sodium stay in the duct and sweat is salty; a sweat chloride of about 60 mmol/L or more supports the diagnosis. The same channel defect explains the lung disease, and congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens causes male infertility."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why is sweat chloride high in CF when the defect reduces chloride movement in both the pancreas and the sweat duct?","explain":"In the pancreatic duct CFTR secretes chloride into the lumen, but in the sweat duct it reabsorbs chloride from the lumen; losing reabsorption leaves chloride in the sweat.","check":"Compare with the first and last paragraphs of this section."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"These diseases share a logic that crosses disciplines. Biochemistry defines the faulty protein: a hormone signal (HFE and hepcidin), a metal pump (ATP7B), a misfolded inhibitor (AAT), a degradation complex or repair enzyme (APC, mismatch repair, MUTYH), or an ion channel (CFTR). Cell biology then decides the kind of injury. Misfolding in the ER produces toxic retention in AAT deficiency but degradation and loss of function with F508del CFTR, and free iron and copper injure through the same oxidant chemistry. Anatomy and histology explain where damage appears: periportal hepatocytes, the lenticular nucleus, Descemet membrane, pancreatic ductules, and colonic crypts."},{"type":"p","text":"Genetics predicts who is affected and how quickly. Recessive loss-of-function diseases (HH, Wilson, CF, MUTYH) require two mutant germline alleles; dominant cancer syndromes (FAP, Lynch, Peutz-Jeghers) carry one hit in every cell, so one somatic event starts a tumor. Codominance explains the graded AAT levels, and incomplete penetrance in HH shows that genotype alone does not guarantee disease. Pathology links these defects to common clinical pictures (cirrhosis, hepatocellular carcinoma, emphysema, malabsorption, and colon cancer) that also arise from nongenetic causes, so the gene must be inferred from the full pattern of findings."}],"summary":["HFE C282Y hemochromatosis is autosomal recessive; low hepcidin leaves ferroportin active, so iron absorption continues despite overload.","Free iron injures parenchymal cells by the Fenton reaction, causing cirrhosis, hepatocellular carcinoma, diabetes, skin pigmentation, cardiomyopathy, arthropathy, and hypogonadism.","Wilson disease is autosomal recessive ATP7B loss: copper is neither loaded onto ceruloplasmin nor excreted in bile, so ceruloplasmin is low while body copper is high.","Overflow copper produces Kayser-Fleischer rings, basal ganglia degeneration, Coombs-negative hemolysis, and high urinary copper.","PiZZ alpha-1 antitrypsin polymerizes in the hepatocyte ER, forming PAS-positive, diastase-resistant globules and cirrhosis, while low serum levels cause lower-lobe panacinar emphysema.","The chromosomal instability pathway proceeds through APC loss, KRAS activation, and TP53 loss; FAP is its germline form.","Mismatch repair failure causes microsatellite instability; Lynch syndrome is the autosomal dominant germline form, while most sporadic MSI cancers come from MLH1 promoter methylation.","FAP (autosomal dominant APC) produces hundreds of adenomas; MUTYH polyposis is autosomal recessive; Peutz-Jeghers (STK11) produces hamartomas with mucocutaneous pigmentation.","CFTR loss reduces chloride, bicarbonate, and water secretion, causing meconium ileus, pancreatic insufficiency with steatorrhea and vitamin A, D, E, and K deficiency, and focal biliary cirrhosis."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Liver and Gallbladder: metabolic and inherited liver disease"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Gastrointestinal Tract: colonic polyps and neoplastic disease"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Genetic Disorders; Neoplasia"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Micronutrients: Minerals (iron and copper); DNA repair"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Pancreatic ductal secretion and intestinal absorption of iron"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Liver Disease; Disorders of the Exocrine Pancreas; Neoplasia"}]},"GI-11":{"unit":"GI-11","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-11","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-11-01","title":"Immune Responses in Gastrointestinal Disease","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"The gut must ignore food and trillions of resident bacteria while still stopping pathogens, and it does this with a specialized mucosal immune system built around M cells, secretory IgA, and regulatory T cells. This chapter explains that normal balance and then the diseases that follow when it fails: celiac disease, Crohn disease, autoimmune gastritis, autoimmune hepatitis, primary biliary cholangitis, gastric MALT lymphoma, and the immune injury and serology of hepatitis B. Board questions usually name a cell, antibody, HLA type, or serologic pattern and ask you to explain the lesion it produces, so the emphasis here is on mechanism.","prerequisites":["Antibody classes, class switching, and complement activation","CD4 helper subsets (Th1, Th2, Th17, regulatory T cells) and CD8 cytotoxic T cell killing","MHC class I and class II antigen presentation","Hypersensitivity types I to IV","Histology of the small intestine, gastric body and antrum, and liver lobule (GI-1 to GI-3)"],"connections":[{"to":"Digestion and absorption (GI-6, GI-7)","how":"Loss of proximal villi in celiac disease and terminal ileal disease in Crohn disease explain which nutrients are malabsorbed."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal microbiology (GI-9)","how":"Helicobacter pylori, Giardia, and hepatitis B are pathogens whose disease is shaped as much by the host response as by the organism."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal genetics (GI-10)","how":"HLA-DQ2 and DQ8 in celiac disease and NOD2 in Crohn disease show how immune genes set disease risk."},{"to":"Renal pathology and vasculitis","how":"Hepatitis B immune complexes cause membranous nephropathy and polyarteritis nodosa, linking liver infection to the kidney and medium-sized arteries."},{"to":"Hematology","how":"Autoimmune gastritis causes pernicious anemia, and celiac disease causes iron and folate deficiency anemia."}],"terms":[{"term":"M cell","def":"A specialized epithelial cell over Peyer patches that transports intact luminal antigen to underlying immune cells."},{"term":"Polymeric Ig receptor (pIgR)","def":"An epithelial receptor that binds J chain-containing IgA and IgM on the basolateral surface and carries them into the lumen."},{"term":"Secretory component","def":"The fragment of pIgR that stays bound to IgA after cleavage and protects it from luminal proteases."},{"term":"Oral tolerance","def":"Reduced systemic immune responsiveness to an antigen that was first encountered through the gut."},{"term":"Tissue transglutaminase (tTG)","def":"A calcium-dependent enzyme that deamidates gliadin in celiac disease and is the target of its IgA autoantibodies."},{"term":"NOD2","def":"A cytosolic pattern recognition receptor for muramyl dipeptide from bacterial peptidoglycan whose variants raise Crohn disease risk."},{"term":"Transmural inflammation","def":"Inflammation that extends through every layer of the bowel wall from mucosa to serosa."},{"term":"Interface hepatitis","def":"Lymphocyte and plasma cell inflammation that crosses the limiting plate between portal tracts and the hepatocytes around them."},{"term":"Marginal zone lymphoma","def":"A low-grade B cell lymphoma derived from post-germinal center marginal zone B cells; the extranodal form is MALT lymphoma."},{"term":"Type III hypersensitivity","def":"Tissue injury caused by deposited antigen-antibody complexes that activate complement and recruit leukocytes."},{"term":"Window period","def":"The interval in acute hepatitis B after HBsAg disappears and before anti-HBs is detectable, when IgM anti-HBc is the only marker."},{"term":"Anti-HBc","def":"Antibody to the hepatitis B core antigen, which forms only after natural infection because the vaccine contains no core protein."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-11-01-C01","obj":"GI-11.1","title":"Mucosal immunity, secretory IgA, oral tolerance, and selective IgA deficiency","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The intestinal mucosa is the largest surface of the body exposed to foreign antigen, separated from food and trillions of commensal bacteria by a single layer of epithelium. Antigen is sampled and lymphocytes primed at organized inductive sites, and effector cells act diffusely in the lamina propria and epithelium."},{"type":"h","text":"Antigen sampling by M cells"},{"type":"p","text":"Peyer patches are aggregated lymphoid follicles in the mucosa and submucosa, most numerous in the terminal ileum. The follicle-associated epithelium over them contains M (microfold) cells, which have short irregular microfolds instead of a brush border, little overlying mucus, and a basolateral pocket holding dendritic cells and lymphocytes. M cells transcytose bacteria, viruses, and particles into this pocket with little degradation, and dendritic cells carry the antigen into the follicle and T cell zones. Several pathogens use M cells as an entry route, including Salmonella, Shigella, and Yersinia."},{"type":"h","text":"Making and delivering secretory IgA"},{"type":"p","text":"In the Peyer patch, helper T cells (through CD40 ligand), TGF-beta, and retinoic acid made by gut dendritic cells switch activated B cells to IgA. Retinoic acid also induces the gut homing molecules alpha4beta7 integrin and CCR9, so these cells leave through lymph and blood and return to the lamina propria as plasma cells. There they secrete dimeric IgA, two monomers joined by a J chain. More IgA is made each day than all other antibody classes combined, most of it in the gut."},{"type":"flow","title":"From antigen sampling to secretory IgA","steps":["M cell carries luminal antigen to dome dendritic cells","Dendritic cells prime T cells, which help B cells","TGF-beta, retinoic acid, and CD40 ligand switch B cells to IgA","IgA B cells home to the lamina propria","Plasma cells secrete dimeric IgA with J chain","pIgR carries the dimer across the enterocyte","pIgR is cleaved; secretory component stays attached"],"caption":"Inductive sites prime B cells; effector plasma cells sit beneath the whole epithelium."},{"type":"p","text":"The polymeric Ig receptor (pIgR) on the basolateral surface of enterocytes binds J chain-containing IgA, carries it across the cell in vesicles, and is cleaved at the apical surface. The bound fragment, secretory component, protects IgA from proteases and anchors it in mucus. Secretory IgA acts by immune exclusion: it agglutinates microbes, blocks adhesins, and neutralizes toxins. Because IgA activates the classical complement pathway poorly, this defense causes little inflammation."},{"type":"h","text":"Oral tolerance"},{"type":"p","text":"Feeding a protein normally reduces later systemic responses to it, a state called oral tolerance. Lamina propria CD103+ dendritic cells carry food antigen to mesenteric lymph nodes and present it with TGF-beta and retinoic acid, which convert naive CD4 T cells into FoxP3+ regulatory T cells that suppress through IL-10 and TGF-beta. Presentation without costimulation causes anergy, and very high doses can delete responding clones. Breakdown of these controls contributes to celiac disease and inflammatory bowel disease."},{"type":"h","text":"Selective IgA deficiency"},{"type":"p","text":"Selective IgA deficiency (serum IgA below about 7 mg/dL with normal IgG and IgM) is the most common primary immunodeficiency and reflects failed differentiation of B cells into IgA plasma cells. Most patients have no symptoms because pIgR also transports pentameric IgM, which carries a J chain, into secretions. A minority have recurrent sinopulmonary infections and giardiasis, and the defect is associated with atopy, autoimmune disease, and celiac disease. Some patients form IgG or IgE antibodies against IgA and can have anaphylaxis when given blood products that contain IgA."},{"type":"stop","predict":"If a person lacked pIgR entirely, would IgM still reach the gut lumen as it does in selective IgA deficiency?","explain":"No. Selective IgA deficiency is compensated because pIgR still moves J chain-containing pentameric IgM into secretions. Without pIgR, neither dimeric IgA nor pentameric IgM is actively transported, so mucosal antibody defense would fail even with normal plasma cells.","check":"The pIgR paragraph and the selective IgA deficiency paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-11-01-C02","obj":"GI-11.2","title":"Celiac disease immunopathogenesis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Celiac disease is an immune-mediated enteropathy of the small intestine triggered by gluten in genetically susceptible people. The harmful fraction is gliadin from wheat, along with related proteins in barley and rye. Gliadin is rich in proline and glutamine, so gastric, pancreatic, and brush border proteases cannot cut it completely, and long peptides reach the lamina propria intact."},{"type":"h","text":"The enzyme and the HLA molecule"},{"type":"p","text":"Tissue transglutaminase (tTG, transglutaminase 2) is a calcium-dependent enzyme released in the lamina propria. It deamidates selected glutamine residues in gliadin to glutamate, adding negative charge. HLA-DQ2 and HLA-DQ8 have peptide-binding pockets that favor negatively charged residues, so deamidated gliadin binds them tightly and is presented strongly to CD4 T cells. About 90% to 95% of patients carry DQ2 and most of the rest carry DQ8. These alleles are necessary but not sufficient, since about a third of people of European ancestry carry DQ2 and most never develop disease; their absence makes celiac disease very unlikely."},{"type":"flow","title":"From gluten to villous atrophy","steps":["Gliadin peptides resist digestion and reach the lamina propria","tTG deamidates glutamine to glutamate","HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 presents deamidated gliadin","Gliadin-specific CD4 Th1 cells release IFN-gamma","Stressed enterocytes express IL-15 and MICA","Intraepithelial CD8 T cells kill enterocytes via NKG2D","Villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, intraepithelial lymphocytosis"],"caption":"An adaptive CD4 response and an innate-like epithelial response act together."},{"type":"p","text":"Two arms injure the mucosa. Gliadin-specific CD4 Th1 cells release IFN-gamma, which drives mucosal inflammation and epithelial injury. At the same time, stressed enterocytes express IL-15 and the stress ligand MICA, which activate intraepithelial CD8 T cells through the NKG2D receptor to kill enterocytes. Crypts become hyperplastic as they try to replace lost enterocytes, and when loss outpaces replacement, villi flatten. The lesion is worst in the duodenum and proximal jejunum, where gluten load is highest."},{"type":"h","text":"Why antibodies form against a self enzyme"},{"type":"p","text":"tTG cross-links gliadin peptides to itself. A B cell whose receptor recognizes tTG internalizes these complexes and presents gliadin peptides on DQ2 or DQ8, so gliadin-specific CD4 T cells give it help. This explains IgA autoantibodies to tTG that depend on gluten intake and fall when gluten is removed. The endomysial antigen detected in the anti-endomysial test is tTG itself, and antibodies to deamidated gliadin peptides also appear."},{"type":"table","title":"Serologic and genetic markers in celiac disease","cols":["Test","What it detects","Use and limitation"],"rows":[["IgA anti-tTG","IgA against tissue transglutaminase","Most sensitive screening antibody; requires normal IgA and current gluten intake"],["IgA anti-endomysial","IgA binding tTG in connective tissue around smooth muscle","Very specific; read by immunofluorescence"],["IgG anti-deamidated gliadin peptide or IgG anti-tTG","IgG class antibodies","Used when the patient is IgA deficient"],["Total serum IgA","IgA level","Detects IgA deficiency, which makes IgA tests falsely negative"],["HLA-DQ2 and DQ8 typing","Susceptibility alleles","Negative result nearly excludes disease; positive result does not confirm it"]],"caption":"Duodenal biopsy showing villous atrophy confirms the diagnosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Proximal mucosal loss explains the manifestations: iron deficiency (iron is absorbed in the duodenum), folate deficiency, steatorrhea with loss of fat-soluble vitamins and calcium leading to osteopenia, and secondary lactose intolerance from brush border loss. Dermatitis herpetiformis is an itchy vesicular rash with granular IgA in the dermal papillae, linked to antibodies against epidermal transglutaminase. Long-standing disease raises the risk of enteropathy-associated T cell lymphoma."},{"type":"example","title":"A negative screen that is not reassuring","text":"A 28-year-old woman with iron deficiency anemia, bloating, and recurrent sinusitis eats wheat daily. IgA anti-tTG is negative. Before accepting this, ask whether she can make IgA. Recurrent sinusitis suggests selective IgA deficiency, which is about 10 times more common in celiac disease than in the general population. Total IgA is undetectable, so the IgA test could not be positive. IgG anti-deamidated gliadin peptide is strongly positive, she carries HLA-DQ2, and duodenal biopsy shows villous atrophy with intraepithelial lymphocytes. Iron deficiency fits duodenal damage, where most dietary iron is absorbed."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-11-01-C03","obj":"GI-11.3","title":"NOD2, commensal bacteria, and transmural granulomatous inflammation in Crohn disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Crohn disease is a chronic inflammatory bowel disease in which a genetically susceptible host mounts a sustained, poorly regulated response to its own intestinal bacteria. Genes, a defective barrier, altered flora, and environmental factors such as smoking all contribute, but the NOD2 story shows the core mechanism most clearly."},{"type":"h","text":"What NOD2 normally does"},{"type":"p","text":"NOD2 is a cytosolic pattern recognition receptor that senses muramyl dipeptide, a fragment of peptidoglycan found in gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria. It is expressed in monocytes, macrophages, dendritic cells, and the Paneth cells at the base of small intestinal crypts. Binding activates NF-kappa-B and MAP kinase signaling through the kinase RIPK2, supports Paneth cell secretion of antimicrobial alpha-defensins, and triggers autophagy of intracellular bacteria together with ATG16L1. The common risk variants reduce NOD2 function, risk is higher with two variant alleles than with one, and variants are linked to ileal disease."},{"type":"h","text":"Why weak sensing causes strong inflammation"},{"type":"p","text":"With defective NOD2, Paneth cells secrete fewer defensins and macrophages clear internalized bacteria poorly, so commensals penetrate the mucosa and persist. Persistent bacterial antigen keeps dendritic cells and macrophages making IL-12 and IL-23. IL-12 drives Th1 cells that secrete IFN-gamma, and IL-23 sustains Th17 cells that secrete IL-17 and IL-22. Variants in IL23R, ATG16L1, and IRGM also influence risk, supporting this model: the disease reflects failed bacterial clearance followed by a compensatory, uncontrolled adaptive response."},{"type":"flow","title":"From NOD2 variant to Crohn lesion","steps":["NOD2 variant weakens sensing of muramyl dipeptide","Fewer Paneth cell defensins; poor bacterial autophagy","Commensals cross the mucosa and persist","Dendritic cells and macrophages secrete IL-12 and IL-23","Th1 (IFN-gamma) and Th17 (IL-17) responses expand","Activated macrophages release TNF and form granulomas","Transmural inflammation, fissures, fibrosis"],"caption":"Loss of innate bacterial control leads to excessive cell-mediated immunity."},{"type":"p","text":"IFN-gamma activates macrophages, which secrete TNF and aggregate as epithelioid cells into noncaseating granulomas, found in roughly a third of resected specimens. Th17 cytokines recruit neutrophils that form crypt abscesses and ulcers. Because this cell-mediated response is not confined to the mucosa, it extends through the full thickness of the wall."},{"type":"table","title":"Crohn features explained by mechanism","cols":["Feature","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Fistulas, abscesses, perforation","Transmural inflammation tunnels through the wall into bowel, bladder, vagina, or skin"],["Strictures and obstruction","Chronic injury and TGF-beta-driven fibrosis thicken the wall"],["Skip lesions and cobblestone mucosa","Patchy foci of inflammation; linear ulcers separate edematous mucosa"],["Creeping fat","Serosal inflammation causes mesenteric fat to wrap the bowel"],["Terminal ileal predominance","Dense lymphoid tissue and Paneth cells where NOD2 activity matters most"],["B12 and bile acid malabsorption","Loss or resection of the terminal ileum, where both are absorbed"],["Noncaseating granulomas","IFN-gamma-activated macrophages; caseation suggests tuberculosis instead"]],"caption":"Ulcerative colitis, by contrast, is mucosal, continuous from the rectum, and lacks granulomas."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why would a loss-of-function variant in an innate immune sensor lead to more intestinal inflammation rather than less?","explain":"NOD2 is needed to clear bacteria early through defensins and autophagy. When it fails, bacteria persist in the mucosa and keep antigen-presenting cells making IL-12 and IL-23, so Th1 and Th17 responses grow and cause chronic transmural, granulomatous injury.","check":"The paragraph on why weak sensing causes strong inflammation."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-11-01-C04","obj":"GI-11.4","title":"Autoantibodies in autoimmune gastritis, autoimmune hepatitis, and primary biliary cholangitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"In these three diseases, tissue injury is mainly T cell mediated, and the autoantibodies chiefly mark loss of tolerance to a specific self antigen. Knowing each antibody's target tells you which cell is attacked, where the damage occurs, and which laboratory values change."},{"type":"table","title":"Autoimmune gastrointestinal diseases and their autoantibodies","cols":["Disease","Autoantibodies","Target","Cells injured","Typical laboratory pattern"],"rows":[["Autoimmune gastritis","Anti-parietal cell; anti-intrinsic factor","H+/K+-ATPase; intrinsic factor","Parietal cells of body and fundus","Achlorhydria, high gastrin, low pepsinogen I, low B12"],["Autoimmune hepatitis type 1","ANA; anti-smooth muscle","Nuclear antigens; F-actin","Hepatocytes","High aminotransferases, high IgG"],["Autoimmune hepatitis type 2","Anti-LKM1 (often with anti-LC1)","Cytochrome P450 2D6","Hepatocytes","High aminotransferases, high IgG; usually children and adolescents"],["Primary biliary cholangitis","Antimitochondrial (AMA)","E2 subunit of the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex","Small intrahepatic bile duct epithelium","High alkaline phosphatase and GGT, high IgM"]],"caption":"The target cell predicts whether the pattern is hepatocellular or cholestatic."},{"type":"h","text":"Autoimmune gastritis"},{"type":"p","text":"CD4 T cells attack the H+/K+-ATPase of parietal cells, which are confined to the oxyntic mucosa of the body and fundus, so the antrum is spared. Anti-parietal cell antibodies are sensitive but also occur in some healthy older adults, while anti-intrinsic factor antibodies are less sensitive but highly specific. Some anti-intrinsic factor antibodies block B12 binding to intrinsic factor, and others block uptake of the complex in the ileum. Loss of acid removes the normal feedback inhibition of antral G cells, so gastrin rises and drives enterochromaffin-like cell hyperplasia, which can progress to gastric neuroendocrine tumors. B12 deficiency (pernicious anemia, with megaloblastic anemia and subacute combined degeneration) appears only after years because hepatic B12 stores are large. The disease clusters with autoimmune thyroiditis and type 1 diabetes."},{"type":"h","text":"Autoimmune hepatitis"},{"type":"p","text":"Here T cells attack hepatocytes, producing interface hepatitis rich in plasma cells. Because hepatocytes are the target, aminotransferases rise far more than alkaline phosphatase, and polyclonal IgG is elevated. Type 1, the most common form, occurs mainly in women at any age with ANA and anti-smooth muscle antibodies. Type 2 affects mainly children and adolescents and is defined by anti-liver kidney microsomal type 1 (anti-LKM1) antibodies against cytochrome P450 2D6."},{"type":"h","text":"Primary biliary cholangitis"},{"type":"p","text":"Primary biliary cholangitis occurs mainly in middle-aged women. AMA, present in about 90% to 95% of patients, targets the E2 component of the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex on the inner mitochondrial membrane. T cells destroy small interlobular bile ducts, sometimes with granulomas around them (the florid duct lesion). Bile flow fails, so alkaline phosphatase and GGT rise, retained bile constituents cause pruritus, cholesterol rises with xanthomas, and fat-soluble vitamins are malabsorbed. Progressive duct loss leads to biliary cirrhosis. Associations include Sjogren syndrome, systemic sclerosis, and autoimmune thyroiditis. Primary sclerosing cholangitis differs: it scars large ducts, is linked to ulcerative colitis, and is AMA negative."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-11-01-C05","obj":"GI-11.5","title":"Helicobacter pylori and gastric MALT lymphoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT) lymphoma is an extranodal marginal zone B cell lymphoma. It is a low-grade tumor of small B cells that arises where chronic antigen stimulation has built lymphoid tissue, such as the stomach in H. pylori infection, the salivary gland in Sjogren syndrome, and the thyroid in Hashimoto thyroiditis. The stomach is the most common site, and most gastric cases are associated with H. pylori."},{"type":"h","text":"Acquired lymphoid tissue and antigen-driven growth"},{"type":"p","text":"Normal gastric mucosa has almost no organized lymphoid tissue. Chronic H. pylori gastritis creates lymphoid follicles with germinal centers in the mucosa. B cells in these follicles proliferate in response to CD40 ligand and cytokines from H. pylori-specific CD4 T cells. The tumor B cells themselves usually do not recognize H. pylori; their immunoglobulin often binds self antigens, and their growth depends on help from the bacterium-specific T cells nearby. Years of driven proliferation favor acquired genetic changes, and one clone eventually outgrows the rest."},{"type":"p","text":"Histologically, small centrocyte-like and monocytoid B cells (CD20 positive, CD5 and CD10 negative) expand the marginal zone and invade gastric glands to form lymphoepithelial lesions. The clonal proliferation can be shown by light chain restriction or immunoglobulin gene rearrangement."},{"type":"flow","title":"From H. pylori gastritis to MALT lymphoma","steps":["Chronic H. pylori infection inflames the gastric mucosa","Lymphoid follicles form in previously follicle-poor mucosa","H. pylori-specific T cells provide CD40 ligand and cytokines","Polyclonal B cells proliferate continuously","A monoclonal marginal zone clone emerges","Early tumor still needs T cell help","t(11;18) or transformation makes growth independent"],"caption":"Regression after eradication is possible only while the clone remains antigen dependent."},{"type":"h","text":"Why early lesions regress"},{"type":"p","text":"Eradicating H. pylori removes the antigen that keeps the specific T cells active. Without CD40 ligand and cytokine help, an early antigen-dependent clone stops proliferating and its cells undergo apoptosis, so many localized, H. pylori-positive tumors regress over months. Response is unlikely when the clone has become autonomous. The t(11;18)(q21;q21) translocation fuses API2 (BIRC3) to MALT1, and the fusion protein activates NF-kappa-B continuously, replacing the survival signal that T cell help once supplied; t(1;14) involving BCL10 and t(14;18) involving MALT1 act the same way. Invasion beyond the submucosa, nodal spread, and transformation to diffuse large B cell lymphoma also predict failure."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two patients have localized H. pylori-positive gastric MALT lymphoma, and only one tumor carries t(11;18). Which tumor is more likely to regress after the organism is eradicated?","explain":"The tumor without t(11;18). It still depends on H. pylori-driven T cell help for NF-kappa-B activation and survival. The API2-MALT1 fusion activates NF-kappa-B by itself, so removing the bacterium leaves the clone's growth signal intact.","check":"The paragraph on why early lesions regress."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-11-01-C06","obj":"GI-11.6","title":"Immune-mediated injury in hepatitis B and its immune complex diseases","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hepatitis B virus (HBV) is a partially double-stranded DNA hepadnavirus that replicates in hepatocytes without killing them. Two observations show this: during the immune tolerant phase of perinatally acquired infection, patients have very high viral DNA with normal aminotransferases, and immunosuppressed patients carry large viral loads with little inflammation. Liver damage in HBV infection is therefore a product of the host response."},{"type":"h","text":"CD8 T cells cause the hepatitis"},{"type":"p","text":"Infected hepatocytes process viral proteins and display peptides on MHC class I. Virus-specific CD8 cytotoxic T cells recognize them and kill the cells through perforin and granzymes and through Fas ligand, producing apoptotic acidophil bodies and aminotransferase release. The same T cells secrete IFN-gamma and TNF, which suppress viral replication in many hepatocytes without killing them. Anti-HBs neutralizes free virions and limits spread. In acute infection, viral DNA peaks weeks before aminotransferases, because injury begins only when the T cell response arrives."},{"type":"flow","title":"How the immune response injures the liver in hepatitis B","steps":["HBV replicates in hepatocytes without cytopathic effect","Viral peptides appear on MHC class I","Virus-specific CD8 T cells recognize infected hepatocytes","Perforin, granzymes, and Fas ligand cause apoptosis","Aminotransferases rise; acidophil bodies form","IFN-gamma and TNF suppress replication noncytolytically"],"caption":"The strength of the T cell response decides whether infection clears, persists, or becomes fulminant."},{"type":"p","text":"Outcome depends on the strength of this response. A vigorous, broad response clears the virus, and an excessive one causes fulminant hepatitis. A weak response allows chronic infection, in which exhausted T cells (expressing inhibitory receptors such as PD-1) kill hepatocytes slowly, driving cycles of injury, regeneration, fibrosis, and eventually cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma. About 90% of perinatal infections become chronic, partly because neonatal T cell responses are immature and transplacental HBeAg may promote tolerance, while fewer than 5% of adult infections do. When immunosuppression is withdrawn, T cell recovery can cause a hepatitis flare."},{"type":"h","text":"Immune complex disease"},{"type":"p","text":"Infected hepatocytes release HBsAg in large excess as noninfectious subviral particles, so circulating antigen-antibody complexes form with antigen in excess. These small soluble complexes escape clearance by phagocytes, deposit in vessel walls and glomeruli, and activate complement, a type III hypersensitivity reaction. A serum sickness-like prodrome of fever, rash, and arthralgia can precede jaundice in acute infection, and complement levels may fall."},{"type":"table","title":"Hepatitis B immune complex diseases","cols":["Feature","Polyarteritis nodosa","Membranous nephropathy"],"rows":[["Site of deposits","Walls of medium-sized muscular arteries","Subepithelial side of the glomerular basement membrane"],["Antigen implicated","HBsAg complexes","HBV antigens; HBeAg prominent in many studies"],["Mechanism of injury","Complement and neutrophils cause segmental transmural fibrinoid necrosis","Membrane attack complex injures podocytes with little leukocyte influx"],["Manifestations","Renal, mesenteric, nerve, and skin infarcts; hypertension; microaneurysms","Nephrotic syndrome"],["Distinguishing points","Usually spares pulmonary arteries; no glomerulonephritis; ANCA negative","More common in children in endemic regions"]],"caption":"Both are type III hypersensitivity, but the location of the deposits determines the lesion."},{"type":"p","text":"In polyarteritis nodosa, lesions of different ages often coexist along an artery, and the pulmonary arteries and glomerular capillaries are usually spared because they are not medium-sized systemic muscular arteries. In membranous nephropathy, subepithelial deposits lie beyond the basement membrane, out of reach of circulating leukocytes, so complement injures podocytes and causes heavy proteinuria without much inflammation."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-11-01-C07","obj":"GI-11.7","title":"Hepatitis B vaccine response and serology","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The hepatitis B vaccine contains recombinant HBsAg produced in yeast and combined with an adjuvant, most often an aluminum salt. It contains no viral DNA, no core protein, and nothing that can replicate. The vaccinated person therefore makes anti-HBs, a neutralizing IgG antibody against the surface antigen, but never anti-HBc. Anti-HBc forms only when HBV replicates in hepatocytes and produces core protein, so it marks natural infection, past or present. A level of anti-HBs of at least 10 mIU/mL after the full series is taken as evidence of protection."},{"type":"h","text":"What each marker means"},{"type":"p","text":"HBsAg means current infection, acute or chronic, and persistence beyond 6 months defines chronic infection. IgM anti-HBc marks acute infection (and can reappear in flares of chronic disease), while IgG anti-HBc persists for life after any infection. Anti-HBs marks immunity, from either recovery or vaccination. HBeAg indicates active replication and high infectivity, and anti-HBe usually indicates lower replication. Anti-HBc is not protective, so it signals exposure rather than immunity."},{"type":"table","title":"Hepatitis B serologic patterns","cols":["State","HBsAg","Anti-HBc","Anti-HBs"],"rows":[["Susceptible, never infected or vaccinated","Negative","Negative","Negative"],["Immune from vaccination","Negative","Negative","Positive"],["Immune from resolved infection","Negative","Positive (IgG)","Positive"],["Acute infection","Positive","Positive (IgM)","Negative"],["Window period","Negative","Positive (IgM)","Negative"],["Chronic infection","Positive for over 6 months","Positive (IgG)","Negative"]],"caption":"Anti-HBc separates infection from vaccination; anti-HBs alone means vaccine."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading two serology panels","text":"Two nurses have positive anti-HBs and negative HBsAg. Nurse A is anti-HBc negative; nurse B is IgG anti-HBc positive. Both are immune, but for different reasons. Nurse A has never been infected, because without core protein replicating in her liver she could not form anti-HBc, so her anti-HBs came from vaccination. Nurse B was infected and recovered: IgG anti-HBc records the past infection, and anti-HBs shows the virus was cleared. If nurse B had instead shown IgM anti-HBc with both HBsAg and anti-HBs negative, she would be in the window period of acute infection, not immune."},{"type":"h","text":"Exceptions that change interpretation"},{"type":"p","text":"Anti-HBs falls below detectable levels years after vaccination in many people, but memory B cells persist and a booster or exposure produces a rapid anamnestic rise. HBsAg from the vaccine itself can be detected briefly after a dose. Passively acquired antibody also misleads: hepatitis B immune globulin gives anti-HBs without vaccination, and infants of infected mothers can carry maternal IgG anti-HBc for many months, whereas IgM does not cross the placenta. Isolated anti-HBc may mean resolved infection with waned anti-HBs, the window period, occult infection, or a false positive."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Every disease in this chapter is a failure of the balance described in the first section. Anatomy and histology locate the damage: Peyer patches and Paneth cells explain ileal Crohn disease, parietal cell distribution explains why autoimmune gastritis spares the antrum, and the duodenal site of iron absorption explains iron deficiency in celiac disease. Immunology supplies the effector: CD4 Th1 and CD8 T cells in celiac disease, Th1 and Th17 cells in Crohn disease, T cell attack with marker autoantibodies in the autoimmune diseases, CD8 cytotoxicity and immune complexes in hepatitis B, and T cell help that sustains early MALT lymphoma."},{"type":"p","text":"Microbiology and pathology meet where host response decides the lesion: commensals in Crohn disease, H. pylori in MALT lymphoma, and HBV, whose injury tracks the T cell response rather than viral load. Laboratory findings follow the target, with tTG antibodies for celiac disease, a hepatocellular pattern in autoimmune hepatitis, a cholestatic pattern in primary biliary cholangitis, and anti-HBc as the record of true infection. Two limits matter: IgA-based tests fail in IgA deficiency, and susceptibility genes such as HLA-DQ2 and NOD2 raise risk without determining disease."}],"summary":["M cells over Peyer patches sample antigen; TGF-beta and retinoic acid switch B cells to IgA, and pIgR transports dimeric IgA, leaving secretory component attached.","Secretory IgA excludes microbes without inflammation; oral tolerance relies on regulatory T cells, anergy, and deletion.","Selective IgA deficiency is the most common primary immunodeficiency; secretory IgM compensates, and anti-IgA antibodies can cause transfusion anaphylaxis.","In celiac disease, tTG deamidates gliadin so HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 presents it; CD4 Th1 cells and IL-15-activated intraepithelial CD8 T cells flatten villi.","IgA anti-tTG and anti-endomysial antibodies arise through gliadin-specific T cell help to tTG-specific B cells; check total IgA.","Defective NOD2 lets commensals persist, driving IL-12 and IL-23, Th1 and Th17 responses, transmural inflammation, and noncaseating granulomas in Crohn disease.","Autoimmune gastritis targets the H+/K+-ATPase and intrinsic factor; autoimmune hepatitis has ANA and anti-smooth muscle (type 1) or anti-LKM1 (type 2); PBC has AMA against PDC-E2.","Early gastric MALT lymphoma depends on H. pylori-specific T cell help and can regress after eradication; t(11;18) makes it independent.","HBV is not cytopathic; CD8 T cells cause hepatocyte injury, and HBV antigen immune complexes cause polyarteritis nodosa and membranous nephropathy.","The vaccine is HBsAg only, so anti-HBs alone means vaccination and anti-HBs with anti-HBc means recovery from infection."],"sources":[{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Specialized immunity at epithelial barriers: immunity in the gastrointestinal system"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Immunologic tolerance and autoimmunity; congenital and acquired immunodeficiencies"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Gastrointestinal Tract: celiac disease, inflammatory bowel disease, gastritis, and gastric lymphoma"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Liver and Gallbladder: viral hepatitis, autoimmune hepatitis, and primary biliary cholangitis"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg Medical Microbiology","where":"Hepatitis viruses"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Gastrointestinal and liver disease"}]},"GI-12":{"unit":"GI-12","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-12","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-12-01","title":"Gastrointestinal Pathogens","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the bacteria, viruses, and parasites that infect the stomach, intestine, and liver, organized by mechanism of disease: a toxin already present in food, a toxin that switches on intestinal secretion, a cytotoxin that kills colonocytes, invasion of the mucosa, destruction of villus cells, or immune injury to hepatocytes. The mechanism predicts the clinical picture, including how quickly illness begins, whether stool is watery, fatty, or bloody, and which complications follow. Board questions usually supply an exposure, an incubation period, a stool finding, or a laboratory clue and ask for the organism, its virulence factor, or the test that detects active infection.","prerequisites":["Small intestinal and colonic epithelium, crypts, villi, and Peyer patches (GI histology)","Intestinal electrolyte transport: CFTR chloride secretion and sodium-coupled absorption (GI-7)","G protein signaling: Gs, adenylyl cyclase, cAMP, guanylyl cyclase, and cGMP","Bacterial structure, gram staining, AB toxin architecture, and secretion systems","Virus classification by genome type, envelope, and replication strategy"],"connections":[{"to":"Gut immunity (GI-11)","how":"Secretory IgA, Th2 responses, and CD4 T cell counts explain why IgA deficiency favors Giardia, advanced HIV favors the coccidia, and corticosteroids unleash Strongyloides."},{"to":"Inflammatory and peptic disease (GI-13-G)","how":"Helicobacter pylori is the leading cause of chronic gastritis and peptic ulceration through its effects on gastrin and acid secretion."},{"to":"Infectious GI diseases (GI-13-L)","how":"The syndromes described there (dysentery, traveler's diarrhea, liver abscess, acute hepatitis) are the clinical expression of the organism biology taught here."},{"to":"GI neoplasms (GI-13-K)","how":"Chronic hepatitis B and C lead to hepatocellular carcinoma, and H. pylori leads to gastric adenocarcinoma and gastric MALT lymphoma."},{"to":"Renal and hematologic pathology","how":"Shiga toxin injures glomerular endothelium, producing the hemolytic uremic syndrome triad of hemolysis, thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney injury."}],"terms":[{"term":"Preformed toxin","def":"A toxin produced in food before ingestion, so illness begins within hours and does not require the organism to infect the host."},{"term":"Enterotoxin","def":"A toxin acting on intestinal epithelium to cause fluid and electrolyte secretion, usually without killing the cell."},{"term":"AB toxin","def":"A toxin with a binding subunit that attaches to a host receptor and an active subunit that enters the cell and modifies a target protein."},{"term":"ADP-ribosylation","def":"Enzymatic transfer of an ADP-ribose group from NAD+ onto a host protein, changing that protein's activity."},{"term":"Dysentery","def":"Frequent small-volume stools with blood and mucus, cramping, and tenesmus, caused by inflammatory destruction of colonic mucosa."},{"term":"Infectious dose","def":"The number of organisms required to establish infection, set largely by how well the organism survives gastric acid."},{"term":"M cell","def":"A specialized epithelial cell overlying Peyer patches that samples luminal particles and delivers them to underlying lymphoid tissue."},{"term":"Pseudomembrane","def":"An adherent plaque of fibrin, mucus, neutrophils, and necrotic epithelium on injured colonic mucosa, characteristic of Clostridioides difficile colitis."},{"term":"Hemolytic uremic syndrome","def":"Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney injury caused by endothelial injury and microvascular thrombi."},{"term":"Covalently closed circular DNA","def":"The stable episomal form of the hepatitis B genome in the hepatocyte nucleus that serves as template for all viral transcripts."},{"term":"Acid-fast oocyst","def":"The thick-walled infective stage of coccidian parasites, which retains carbol fuchsin on modified acid-fast staining of stool."},{"term":"Autoinfection","def":"Completion of the Strongyloides life cycle within a single host, as larvae mature in the bowel and reinvade without leaving the body."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-12-01-C01","obj":"GI-12.1","title":"Toxin-mediated diarrhea and vomiting","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Toxin-mediated illness divides into three mechanisms, and the incubation period separates them. A toxin already present in food acts as soon as it is absorbed, so vomiting begins within about 1 to 6 hours. A secretory enterotoxin requires the organism to reach the small intestine and multiply, so watery diarrhea appears after about a day. A cytotoxin kills colonocytes and produces inflammatory colitis over several days."},{"type":"p","text":"Staphylococcus aureus and the emetic form of Bacillus cereus illness both follow ingestion of heat-stable toxin, so reheating the food does not protect: the bacteria die and the toxin survives. Staphylococcal enterotoxins stimulate vagal afferents in the gut, and vomiting is driven through those afferents rather than by mucosal destruction, so illness is brief, usually under 24 hours, with little fever. Cereulide, the emetic toxin of B. cereus, acts the same way and is classically linked to rice dishes, while a separate heat-labile B. cereus enterotoxin produced in the intestine causes watery diarrhea about 8 to 16 hours after exposure."},{"type":"h","text":"Secretory toxins act through cyclic nucleotides"},{"type":"p","text":"Vibrio cholerae is a curved, motile, oxidase-positive gram-negative rod that is readily killed by gastric acid, so a large inoculum or hypochlorhydria favors infection, and it does not invade. Cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates the alpha subunit of Gs, blocking its GTPase activity so that adenylyl cyclase remains active. Rising cAMP activates protein kinase A, which opens CFTR in crypt cells and inhibits brush border sodium and chloride absorption, so chloride moves into the lumen and sodium and water follow. The stool is isotonic, voluminous, and free of blood or leukocytes, the rice-water appearance. Glucose-coupled sodium absorption through SGLT1 is untouched, which is the physiologic basis of oral rehydration."},{"type":"p","text":"Enterotoxigenic Escherichia coli, the usual cause of traveler's diarrhea, makes a heat-labile toxin that works like cholera toxin and raises cAMP, and a heat-stable toxin that activates guanylyl cyclase C, the receptor for the endogenous peptide guanylin, raising cGMP. Both second messengers open the same chloride channel, so the stool is watery for the same reason."},{"type":"flow","title":"Cholera toxin to isotonic fluid loss","steps":["B subunit binds GM1 ganglioside on the enterocyte","A subunit ADP-ribosylates Gs alpha","Adenylyl cyclase stays active, cAMP rises","Protein kinase A opens CFTR, blocks Na+ absorption","Chloride secretion draws Na+ and water into the lumen","Isotonic rice-water stool, hypovolemia, metabolic acidosis"],"caption":"The epithelium stays intact, so stool contains no blood or leukocytes and recovery is complete once secretion stops."},{"type":"p","text":"Clostridioides difficile is a spore-forming anaerobic gram-positive rod whose spores resist drying and alcohol. Disease requires two conditions: loss of the colonic flora that normally suppress germination, typically after antibiotic exposure, and a strain carrying toxin genes. Toxins A and B glucosylate Rho family GTPases and collapse the actin cytoskeleton, so colonocytes round up, tight junctions open, and cells die, while interleukin 8 recruits neutrophils and plaques of fibrin, mucus, and necrotic debris form pseudomembranes. Tests detect the toxin gene by nucleic acid amplification or the toxin itself by immunoassay, and they are meaningful only with diarrhea, since a positive gene test without diarrhea reflects colonization."},{"type":"table","title":"Toxin-mediated gastrointestinal illness","cols":["Organism","Toxin and mechanism","Typical exposure","Onset and stool"],"rows":[["Staphylococcus aureus","Preformed heat-stable enterotoxin, vagal emetic pathway","Protein-rich foods held unrefrigerated","About 1 to 6 hours, vomiting, little fever"],["Bacillus cereus, emetic","Preformed heat-stable cereulide","Rice dishes","About 1 to 6 hours, vomiting"],["Bacillus cereus, diarrheal","Heat-labile enterotoxin made in the gut","Meats, sauces, vegetables","About 8 to 16 hours, watery"],["Vibrio cholerae","Cholera toxin raises cAMP","Contaminated water, shellfish","Massive isotonic rice-water stool"],["Enterotoxigenic E. coli","Heat-labile raises cAMP, heat-stable raises cGMP","Food and water during travel","Watery, afebrile, over days"],["Clostridioides difficile","Toxins A and B kill colonocytes","Recent antibiotics, health care","Inflammatory diarrhea, pseudomembranes"]],"caption":"Incubation time separates preformed toxin from toxin made in the host, and only the cytotoxins destroy epithelium."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-12-01-C02","obj":"GI-12.2","title":"Invasive enteric bacteria: Shigella, Salmonella, and Campylobacter","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Invasive organisms enter and destroy mucosa, so the host responds with fever, cramping, and stool containing blood and leukocytes. Fecal leukocytes or a positive fecal lactoferrin therefore point toward this group and away from the secretory toxins. The three classic invaders are separated by laboratory phenotype, by the dose required, and by their characteristic complications."},{"type":"p","text":"Shigella species are nonmotile, lactose nonfermenting gram-negative rods that do not produce hydrogen sulfide. They tolerate gastric acid well, so as few as about 10 to 100 organisms can infect, which explains person-to-person spread in households and child care settings. Shigella crosses the colonic epithelium through M cells, escapes its vacuole into the cytoplasm, and polymerizes host actin to drive itself into adjacent cells, spreading laterally while shielded from antibody. Infected macrophages die by pyroptosis after inflammasome activation, releasing interleukin 1 beta and amplifying inflammation. The result is superficial ulceration of the distal colon with dysentery, while bacteremia is uncommon. Shigella dysenteriae type 1 also produces Shiga toxin and can therefore cause hemolytic uremic syndrome."},{"type":"p","text":"Salmonella species are motile and produce hydrogen sulfide, which blackens the butt of triple sugar iron agar, and they are acid sensitive, so a much larger dose is required and reduced gastric acidity raises risk. A type III secretion system injects effectors that cause membrane ruffling and bacterial uptake, and the organism then survives inside a modified vacuole in macrophages. Nontyphoidal serotypes from poultry, eggs, and reptiles cause gastroenteritis that can be followed by bacteremia and seeding of bone, a pattern seen in sickle cell disease. Salmonella Typhi carries a Vi capsule, is restricted to humans, and spreads from intestinal lymphoid tissue through lymphatics and blood to liver, spleen, and marrow, producing enteric fever with rising fever, abdominal pain, rose spots, and ulceration over Peyer patches. It can persist in the gallbladder, especially with gallstones, creating a chronic carrier state."},{"type":"p","text":"Campylobacter jejuni is a comma-shaped or S-shaped, motile, oxidase-positive gram-negative rod that is microaerophilic and grows at 42 degrees C (107.6 degrees F), a property used for selective isolation. Acquired from undercooked poultry and unpasteurized milk, it invades the ileum and colon and causes fever, periumbilical cramping that can mimic appendicitis, and bloody diarrhea. Its lipooligosaccharide carries sugar structures resembling peripheral nerve gangliosides, so antibodies against the organism can cross-react with axonal membranes and produce Guillain-Barre syndrome by molecular mimicry, usually one to three weeks after the diarrhea. Reactive arthritis is a second postinfectious complication, most often in HLA-B27 positive people."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing the classic invasive enteric bacteria","cols":["Feature","Shigella","Salmonella","Campylobacter jejuni"],"rows":[["Motility","Nonmotile","Motile","Motile, single polar flagellum"],["Hydrogen sulfide","Not produced","Produced","Not an enteric fermenter"],["Shape or growth clue","Straight rod","Straight rod","Comma or S shaped, grows at 42 degrees C (107.6 degrees F)"],["Infectious dose","Very low, about 10 to 100","High, acid sensitive","Intermediate"],["Usual exposure","Person to person, child care","Poultry, eggs, reptiles","Undercooked poultry, raw milk"],["Depth reached","Distal colon mucosa, rarely bacteremic","Mucosa then macrophages, can be bacteremic","Ileum and colon mucosa"],["Classic complication","Dysentery, HUS with S. dysenteriae type 1","Osteomyelitis, gallbladder carriage with S. Typhi","Guillain-Barre syndrome, reactive arthritis"]],"caption":"Motility and hydrogen sulfide separate Shigella from Salmonella on one agar slant, while shape and growth temperature identify Campylobacter."},{"type":"flow","title":"How Shigella produces dysentery with few organisms","steps":["Acid tolerance lets a small dose reach the colon","Uptake through M cells over colonic lymphoid tissue","Escape from the vacuole into the cytoplasm","Actin-based motility carries it cell to cell","Macrophage pyroptosis releases interleukin 1 beta","Neutrophilic ulceration, blood and mucus in small stools"],"caption":"Intracellular spread keeps the organism away from antibody, so mucosal destruction is extensive while bloodstream invasion is rare."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-12-01-C03","obj":"GI-12.3","title":"Yersinia enterocolitica, enterohemorrhagic E. coli, and Listeria monocytogenes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Yersinia enterocolitica is a gram-negative coccobacillus with bipolar staining that multiplies near 4 degrees C (39.2 degrees F), so refrigeration does not protect and cold enrichment is used to recover it. Exposure is classically undercooked pork or raw milk. It invades the terminal ileum through M cells and multiplies in mesenteric lymph nodes, so fever, right lower quadrant pain, and tender nodes mimic appendicitis, a picture called pseudoappendicitis. Growth depends on iron acquisition, so iron overload and chelation raise the risk of severe infection."},{"type":"p","text":"Enterohemorrhagic Escherichia coli, usually serotype O157:H7, comes from undercooked ground beef and contaminated produce, and is recognized because it does not ferment sorbitol. It adheres to colonic epithelium with intimin and effaces the microvilli without invading, so fever is often absent and fecal leukocytes modest even when stool is grossly bloody. Its Shiga toxin, encoded by a lysogenic bacteriophage, binds globotriaosylceramide (Gb3) and cleaves one adenine from the 28S ribosomal RNA of the 60S subunit, halting protein synthesis and killing the cell. Gb3 sits on colonic and glomerular endothelium, so absorbed toxin injures the kidney: damaged endothelium nucleates platelet microthrombi, red cells fragment passing them, platelets are consumed, and filtration falls. The resulting hemolytic uremic syndrome appears about 5 to 10 days after the diarrhea begins."},{"type":"flow","title":"From Shiga toxin to hemolytic uremic syndrome","steps":["Adherent EHEC effaces colonic microvilli without invading","Shiga toxin damages colonic vessels, stool turns bloody","Absorbed toxin binds Gb3 on glomerular endothelium","Depurination of 28S rRNA stops protein synthesis","Injured endothelium triggers platelet microthrombi","Schistocytes, thrombocytopenia, rising creatinine"],"caption":"Kidney injury comes from circulating toxin, not bacteria, so blood cultures are negative while the film is abnormal."},{"type":"p","text":"Listeria monocytogenes is a facultatively intracellular, catalase-positive gram-positive rod with tumbling motility at about 25 degrees C (77 degrees F). It grows in the cold, so refrigerated deli meats and soft cheeses are classic sources. Internalins trigger uptake by enterocytes, listeriolysin O perforates the vacuolar membrane so the organism reaches the cytoplasm, and ActA nucleates host actin into a tail that pushes it into the neighboring cell. Because it never crosses an extracellular space, antibody is largely ineffective and cell-mediated immunity is required. Healthy adults usually have a self-limited febrile gastroenteritis, but in neonates, pregnancy, and impaired T cell function it crosses the placenta or blood brain barrier, causing fetal infection, fetal loss, and meningitis."},{"type":"table","title":"Three distal small bowel pathogens compared","cols":["Feature","Yersinia enterocolitica","EHEC O157:H7","Listeria monocytogenes"],"rows":[["Gram reaction","Gram-negative coccobacillus","Gram-negative rod","Gram-positive rod"],["Growth clue","Grows near 4 degrees C (39.2 degrees F)","Does not ferment sorbitol","Cold growth, tumbling at 25 degrees C (77 degrees F)"],["Key virulence factor","M cell invasion, iron acquisition","Intimin plus phage-encoded Shiga toxin","Listeriolysin O and ActA"],["Mucosal behavior","Invades ileum and nodes","Adheres and effaces, no invasion","Invades, spreads cell to cell"],["Fever and stool","Fever, inflammatory stool","Often afebrile, bloody","Fever, mild gastroenteritis"],["Characteristic outcome","Pseudoappendicitis","Hemolytic uremic syndrome","Meningitis, fetal infection"]],"caption":"Yersinia and Listeria grow at refrigeration temperature; mucosal behavior and the compartment reached set each syndrome."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: two causes of bloody diarrhea","text":"A 4 year old has 4 days of bloody diarrhea with little fever, then becomes pale with a creatinine of 1.8 mg/dL, platelets of 60,000 per microliter, and schistocytes on the film. Another child has fever to 39 degrees C (102.2 degrees F) and small bloody stools full of leukocytes. The first is toxin acting at a distance: EHEC adheres without invading, so fever is slight, and absorbed Shiga toxin injures glomerular endothelium days later. The second is invasion, with Shigella destroying colonic epithelium and driving a febrile neutrophilic response. Shiga toxin also comes from Shigella dysenteriae type 1, so invasion and fever make the distinction, not the toxin."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-12-01-C04","obj":"GI-12.4","title":"Viral enteritis: rotavirus, norovirus, and the enteroviruses","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"These enteric viruses are all nonenveloped, and that single structural fact explains their epidemiology. Without a lipid envelope they survive gastric acid, bile, drying on surfaces, and alcohol-based disinfection, so they spread efficiently by the fecal-oral route. They injure the small intestine rather than the colon, so stool is watery and contains neither blood nor leukocytes."},{"type":"p","text":"Rotavirus is a reovirus with a segmented double-stranded RNA genome and a triple-layered capsid. It infects mature enterocytes at the villus tips while sparing crypt cells, which produces diarrhea by two mechanisms at once. Loss of absorptive cells removes the brush border disaccharidases, so sugars remain in the lumen and hold water osmotically, while the surviving crypt cells continue to secrete. The viral protein NSP4 acts as an enterotoxin, raising intracellular calcium to stimulate chloride secretion and also activating enteric nerves. Disease is most severe between about 6 and 24 months of age, after maternal antibody wanes, and the combination of vomiting with large-volume stool makes dehydration the main risk. Reinfection occurs because immunity is partial, but later episodes are milder."},{"type":"flow","title":"Rotavirus injury to the villus","steps":["Virus infects mature enterocytes at villus tips","Absorptive surface and disaccharidases are lost","Unabsorbed sugars retain water in the lumen","Crypt cells survive and keep secreting","NSP4 raises intracellular calcium and chloride secretion","Osmotic plus secretory watery diarrhea with vomiting"],"caption":"Two mechanisms operate together, which is why diarrhea continues even when oral intake is reduced."},{"type":"p","text":"Norovirus is a calicivirus with a positive-sense single-stranded RNA genome. A few dozen particles can infect, shedding is enormous, and the virus persists on surfaces and in foods such as shellfish and uncooked produce, so outbreaks sweep through cruise ships, dormitories, and long term care facilities. Attachment depends on histo-blood group antigens on gut epithelium, and people lacking a functional FUT2 secretor enzyme resist the common strains. Immunity is strain specific and short lived, so reinfection is the rule. Illness lasts about 1 to 3 days with vomiting at least as prominent as diarrhea, reflecting both small bowel injury and delayed gastric emptying."},{"type":"p","text":"Coxsackieviruses and echoviruses are picornaviruses with positive-sense single-stranded RNA genomes, transmitted fecal-orally with a peak in summer and early fall. For the gastrointestinal tract they matter mainly as a route of entry: they replicate in the pharynx and intestinal lymphoid tissue, then enter the bloodstream and seed other organs, causing aseptic meningitis, myocarditis and pericarditis, hand foot and mouth disease, herpangina, and pleurodynia. Diarrhea, when present, is mild."},{"type":"table","title":"Viral causes of enteritis","cols":["Virus","Family and genome","Who is affected","Mechanism and course"],"rows":[["Rotavirus","Reovirus, segmented double-stranded RNA","Infants about 6 to 24 months","Villus tip cell loss plus NSP4 enterotoxin, several days of watery stool"],["Norovirus","Calicivirus, single-stranded RNA","All ages, closed-setting outbreaks","Very low infectious dose, brief strain-specific immunity, 1 to 3 days"],["Coxsackievirus, echovirus","Picornavirus, single-stranded RNA","Children, summer and fall","Gut is the entry site, then viremia to meninges, heart, skin"]],"caption":"All are nonenveloped and resist acid and disinfection, but only rotavirus and norovirus are major diarrheal pathogens."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does rotavirus diarrhea persist for days even after the virus is controlled, and why does it worsen with milk feeding?","explain":"The villus tip cells carrying the disaccharidases must be replaced by cells migrating up from the crypts, which takes days. Until then lactose is not hydrolyzed, remains in the lumen, and holds water osmotically.","check":"The villus injury flow and the mechanism column of the viral table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-12-01-C05","obj":"GI-12.5","title":"The hepatitis viruses: genomes, transmission, and chronicity","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Five unrelated viruses share one target, the hepatocyte, and therefore produce a similar acute illness: malaise, anorexia, nausea, right upper quadrant discomfort, then jaundice with dark urine, raised aminotransferases, and a tender liver. The decisive biology is which of them can persist. Hepatocyte injury is largely immune mediated, since cytotoxic CD8 T cells kill hepatocytes displaying viral peptides, so a vigorous response clears the virus and causes a symptomatic icteric illness while a weak response permits persistence with milder initial symptoms. That is why perinatal hepatitis B infection, acquired when the immune system tolerates the virus, is usually silent and almost always chronic, whereas adult infection is usually symptomatic and usually resolves."},{"type":"p","text":"Hepatitis A virus is a nonenveloped picornavirus with a positive-sense single-stranded RNA genome, so it survives in water and shellfish and spreads fecal-orally. It never becomes chronic, and severity rises with age at infection: young children are often asymptomatic while adults have a longer icteric illness. Hepatitis E virus is also nonenveloped, single-stranded RNA, and waterborne, and it behaves like hepatitis A with two differences: mortality is high in the third trimester of pregnancy, and it can persist in organ transplant recipients and others with suppressed T cell function."},{"type":"p","text":"Hepatitis B virus is a hepadnavirus with a partially double-stranded circular DNA genome and an envelope carrying surface antigen. After entry the genome is repaired in the nucleus into covalently closed circular DNA, a stable episome transcribed into pregenomic RNA, and the viral polymerase then reverse transcribes that RNA back into DNA inside new capsids, so a DNA virus depends on reverse transcription. The episome is long lived and can remain in hepatocytes after apparent recovery, so infection can reactivate when immunity falls. Viral DNA fragments also integrate into host chromosomes, which contributes to carcinogenesis, so hepatocellular carcinoma can arise even without cirrhosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Hepatitis D virus is a defective agent with a small circular RNA genome and a single protein, delta antigen. It replicates using host RNA polymerase but cannot assemble an infectious particle unless hepatitis B supplies surface antigen as an envelope, so it occurs only with hepatitis B. Hepatitis C virus is an enveloped flavivirus whose RNA-dependent RNA polymerase lacks proofreading, so each person carries a swarm of related variants. Hypervariable envelope regions change faster than neutralizing antibody can be produced, which explains both the absence of a vaccine and persistence in most infections, with slow progression through fibrosis to cirrhosis and then a rising risk of carcinoma."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why hepatitis B persists","steps":["Virion delivers partial DNA genome to the hepatocyte nucleus","Genome completed into covalently closed circular DNA","Transcription yields pregenomic RNA and viral proteins","Reverse transcriptase copies pregenomic RNA into DNA","Episomal template is not degraded or cleared","Reactivation possible whenever T cell control weakens"],"caption":"The stable nuclear episome, not continued cell entry, is what makes elimination difficult and reactivation possible."},{"type":"table","title":"Comparing the hepatitis viruses","cols":["Virus","Family and genome","Transmission","Chronic infection","Distinctive point"],"rows":[["HAV","Picornavirus, single-stranded RNA, nonenveloped","Fecal-oral, water and shellfish","Never","Worse with increasing age at infection"],["HBV","Hepadnavirus, partially double-stranded DNA, enveloped","Blood, sexual, perinatal","Yes, about 90% perinatal, under 5% adult","Reverse transcription, stable episome, carcinoma without cirrhosis"],["HCV","Flavivirus, single-stranded RNA, enveloped","Blood","Yes, in most infections","Envelope variation, no vaccine"],["HDV","Deltavirus, circular single-stranded RNA, defective","Blood and sexual, only with HBV","Yes, with HBV","Requires hepatitis B surface antigen"],["HEV","Hepevirus, single-stranded RNA, nonenveloped","Fecal-oral water, zoonotic meat","Only with suppressed immunity","High mortality in third trimester"]],"caption":"The enveloped bloodborne viruses are the ones that persist, with hepatitis E the exception in immunosuppressed hosts. Hepatitis B serology is developed in GI-11."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-12-01-C06","obj":"GI-12.6","title":"Helicobacter pylori: survival in acid and detection of active infection","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Helicobacter pylori is a curved or spiral, flagellated, microaerophilic gram-negative rod that lives in the mucus layer over gastric epithelium rather than in the lumen. It is urease, catalase, and oxidase positive. Urease hydrolyzes urea into ammonia and carbon dioxide, and the ammonia buffers acid in the immediate surroundings, which allows the organism to cross the acidic lumen and establish itself. Flagella drive it through viscous mucus toward the epithelial surface, where adhesins such as BabA bind blood group antigens. Two further factors drive injury: the vacuolating cytotoxin VacA, which forms anion channels in host membranes and impairs T cell responses, and CagA, injected by a type IV secretion system, which disrupts the cytoskeleton and tight junctions and induces interleukin 8, recruiting neutrophils."},{"type":"p","text":"Where the gastritis sits determines the disease. Antral-predominant gastritis destroys somatostatin-producing D cells, and because somatostatin normally restrains gastrin release from G cells, losing it raises gastrin and parietal cell acid output. Excess acid reaching the duodenum causes gastric metaplasia there, which H. pylori colonizes, and acid plus inflammation produces a duodenal ulcer. Body-predominant gastritis has the opposite effect: inflammation of acid-producing mucosa leads to atrophy and hypochlorhydria, and chronic inflammation, atrophy, intestinal metaplasia, and dysplasia lead to gastric adenocarcinoma. Persistent antigenic stimulation of gastric lymphoid tissue can also drive a clonal B cell proliferation, a gastric MALT lymphoma that may regress when the organism is eliminated."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Antral gastritis to duodenal ulcer","steps":["H. pylori colonizes antral mucus, protected by urease-derived ammonia","Antral inflammation destroys somatostatin-producing D cells","Loss of somatostatin inhibition raises gastrin from G cells","Gastrin drives parietal cell acid secretion","Acid load causes duodenal gastric metaplasia, which is colonized","Further inflammation sustains colonization and ulceration"],"caption":"The loop sustains itself because the acid produced by the infection creates new surface for the organism to colonize."},{"type":"p","text":"Detection separates active infection from past exposure. The urea breath test exploits urease directly: labeled urea is swallowed, living organisms split it, and labeled carbon dioxide appears in the breath. The stool antigen test detects antigen being shed. Both indicate current infection and can serve as tests of cure. Endoscopic biopsy allows a rapid urease test, histology, and culture, and histology adds information about atrophy, metaplasia, and lymphoid proliferation. Anything that suppresses the organism lowers urease activity and antigen shedding, so proton pump inhibitors, bismuth, and antibiotics cause false negative results and are withheld before testing. Serum antibody persists long after the organism is gone, so serology indicates exposure and cannot confirm eradication."},{"type":"table","title":"Tests for Helicobacter pylori","cols":["Test","What it detects","Active infection only","Main limitation"],"rows":[["Urea breath test","Urease activity of live organisms","Yes","False negative with acid suppression or antimicrobials"],["Stool antigen","Shed bacterial antigen","Yes","Same drug effect"],["Biopsy rapid urease test","Urease in tissue","Yes","Needs endoscopy, sampling error"],["Biopsy histology","Organisms plus mucosal changes","Yes","Needs endoscopy, observer dependent"],["Serum antibody","IgG against H. pylori","No","Stays positive after cure"]],"caption":"Four of the five tests depend on live organisms or their products, so acid suppression and antimicrobials must be accounted for."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A treated patient returns 8 weeks later: serology is still positive but the stool antigen test is negative. Was the infection eliminated?","explain":"Most likely yes. Antibody persists for months to years, so serology says nothing about current status, while a negative stool antigen, with drugs stopped beforehand, means antigen is no longer being shed.","check":"The stool antigen and serology rows of the test table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-12-01-C07","obj":"GI-12.7","title":"Intestinal parasites: protozoa, coccidia, and Strongyloides","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Noninvasive parasites blunt the absorptive surface and cause fatty nonbloody stool, invasive ones ulcerate the colon and can reach the liver, and the coccidia live in epithelial cells, so their severity tracks CD4 T cell immunity."},{"type":"p","text":"Giardia duodenalis is swallowed as a cyst from untreated surface water, and the cyst wall resists usual chlorination. Trophozoites attach to the duodenal and jejunal brush border with a ventral adhesive disc without entering the cell, and attachment with the inflammatory response shortens microvilli and lowers disaccharidase activity, so absorption fails and stool is greasy. Secretory IgA is central to clearance, so IgA deficiency brings recurrent infection, and intermittent shedding makes stool antigen or nucleic acid testing better than microscopy."},{"type":"p","text":"Entamoeba histolytica trophozoites bind colonic epithelium through a galactose specific lectin, kill cells with amoebapores, and digest matrix with proteases. Lateral extension is easier than downward penetration, so the ulcer is narrow at the surface and wide beneath, the flask-shaped ulcer of the cecum and ascending colon. Trophozoites with ingested red cells mark invasive disease and separate E. histolytica from the identical nonpathogenic Entamoeba dispar. Organisms entering mesenteric venules reach the liver by the portal vein and form a single, usually right sided abscess."},{"type":"p","text":"The coccidia share acid-fast oocysts in stool. Cryptosporidium oocysts resist chlorine and are infectious when shed, so they survive in treated water; the organism sits inside an enterocyte but outside its cytoplasm, and illness is self-limited in a normal host but profuse at low CD4 counts. Cyclospora oocysts need days outside the host to become infectious, so direct spread does not occur, and Cystoisospora belli causes prolonged diarrhea in advanced HIV infection."},{"type":"p","text":"Strongyloides stercoralis filariform larvae penetrate intact skin, travel in blood to the lungs, ascend the airways, and are swallowed, maturing into parthenogenetic females in duodenal mucosa. Some of the rhabditiform larvae released into the lumen mature into infective filariform larvae and reinvade through the bowel wall or perianal skin. That autoinfection sustains infection for decades, with intermittent eosinophilia and the migratory rash called larva currens. A Th2 response with eosinophils and IgE normally holds the burden down; corticosteroids suppress it and HTLV-1 shifts the response away from Th2, so larval numbers rise into hyperinfection, migrating larvae carry enteric bacteria into the blood, and eosinophilia often disappears."},{"type":"flow","title":"Strongyloides autoinfection and hyperinfection","steps":["Filariform larvae penetrate skin from soil","Blood carries larvae to lungs, then they are swallowed","Adult females mature in duodenal mucosa, laying eggs","Rhabditiform larvae enter the lumen","Some become filariform in the gut and reinvade","Loss of the Th2 response allows dissemination"],"caption":"Autoinfection explains decades of persistence; loss of eosinophil control explains hyperinfection."},{"type":"table","title":"Intestinal parasites by mechanism","cols":["Organism","Infective stage and exposure","Mechanism","Stool and clinical clue"],"rows":[["Giardia duodenalis","Cyst in untreated water","Brush border attachment, no invasion","Greasy nonbloody stool, worse with IgA deficiency"],["Entamoeba histolytica","Cyst in food or water","Lectin, amoebapores, proteases","Bloody stool, flask ulcers, liver abscess"],["Cryptosporidium","Chlorine-resistant oocyst","Intracellular, extracytoplasmic","Watery, prolonged with low CD4 count"],["Cyclospora cayetanensis","Oocyst on fresh produce","Small bowel enterocytes","Relapsing watery stool, no direct spread"],["Cystoisospora belli","Large acid-fast oocyst","Small bowel enterocytes","Chronic diarrhea in advanced HIV"],["Strongyloides stercoralis","Filariform larva through skin","Autoinfection within one host","Eosinophilia, larva currens, hyperinfection"]],"caption":"Acid-fast oocysts group the coccidia, invasion separates Entamoeba from Giardia, skin penetration marks Strongyloides."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: fatty stool versus bloody stool","text":"Two travelers return with diarrhea. One has three weeks of greasy stools with bloating, no blood and no fever. The other has cramping, small bloody stools, and a tender liver with a single right lobe lesion. The first is Giardia, which attaches to the brush border and blunts microvilli, so fat absorption fails while the mucosa stays intact. The second is Entamoeba histolytica, which lyses colonic epithelium and enters portal venules."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Microbiology joins physiology at the transport protein. Cholera toxin and the heat-labile toxin of enterotoxigenic E. coli raise cAMP, the heat-stable toxin raises cGMP, and rotavirus NSP4 raises intracellular calcium, yet all three converge on chloride secretion (through CFTR for the cyclic nucleotides, through calcium-activated chloride channels for NSP4) with sodium and water following, which is why the stool is isotonic and why glucose-coupled sodium absorption still works and makes oral rehydration effective. Histology explains the rest of the stool findings: injury confined to villus tips removes disaccharidases and produces osmotic diarrhea, injury to colonic epithelium produces blood and leukocytes, and attachment without invasion gives bloody stool with little fever, as in enterohemorrhagic E. coli."},{"type":"p","text":"Immunology explains who becomes severely ill and which complications occur. Secretory IgA restrains Giardia, CD4 T cells restrain the coccidia, cell-mediated immunity restrains Listeria because it never leaves the cytoplasm, and a Th2 response with eosinophils restrains Strongyloides, so corticosteroids convert a quiet infection into hyperinfection. Immune mechanisms also cause damage: cytotoxic T cells rather than the virus kill hepatocytes in viral hepatitis, antibody raised against Campylobacter lipooligosaccharide cross-reacts with nerve gangliosides in Guillain-Barre syndrome, and chronic antigenic stimulation by H. pylori drives gastric MALT lymphoma. The limits of this reasoning should be stated plainly: incubation periods and infectious doses overlap between organisms, Shiga toxin is produced by both Shigella dysenteriae type 1 and enterohemorrhagic E. coli, and several agents can produce the same stool pattern, so a single clue rarely identifies an organism without the exposure history and a confirmatory test."}],"summary":["Onset within about 1 to 6 hours with vomiting means toxin was present in the food; a day or more means the organism had to colonize and secrete toxin in the host.","Cholera toxin and the heat-labile toxin of ETEC raise cAMP, the heat-stable toxin raises cGMP, and both open CFTR, giving isotonic watery stool with an intact mucosa.","C. difficile toxins A and B glucosylate Rho GTPases, collapsing the cytoskeleton and producing pseudomembranes; a toxin gene test without diarrhea indicates colonization only.","Shigella is nonmotile, makes no hydrogen sulfide, and infects at about 10 to 100 organisms by spreading cell to cell with host actin; Salmonella is motile, makes hydrogen sulfide, and needs a large dose because it is acid sensitive.","Campylobacter jejuni is comma shaped and grows at 42 degrees C (107.6 degrees F), and ganglioside mimicry links it to Guillain-Barre syndrome.","EHEC adheres without invading, so fever is slight, while absorbed Shiga toxin depurinates 28S rRNA at Gb3-bearing glomerular endothelium and causes hemolytic uremic syndrome.","Listeria uses listeriolysin O and ActA to spread cell to cell, so control requires cell-mediated immunity and disease strikes neonates, pregnancy, and T cell deficiency.","Rotavirus destroys villus tip cells and adds the NSP4 enterotoxin, so diarrhea is both osmotic and secretory; norovirus infects at a very low dose with brief strain-specific immunity.","Hepatitis A and E are nonenveloped and fecal-oral and do not persist in healthy hosts; hepatitis B persists through its nuclear episome and reverse transcription, hepatitis C through envelope variation, and hepatitis D only where hepatitis B supplies surface antigen.","H. pylori urease buffers acid and drives antral gastritis that raises gastrin and causes duodenal ulcer; urea breath and stool antigen tests detect active infection while serology cannot confirm cure."],"sources":[{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Enteric Gram-Negative Rods (Enterobacteriaceae)"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Hepatitis Viruses"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Medical Parasitology, intestinal protozoa and nematodes"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Gastrointestinal Tract, infectious enterocolitis; Liver and Gallbladder, viral hepatitis"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Immunity to Microbes"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Gastrointestinal Disease and Liver Disease"}]},"GI-13-A":{"unit":"GI-13-A","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-01","title":"Salivary Gland Disease: Parotitis and Salivary Stones","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains the three common causes of a swollen salivary gland: mumps parotitis, acute bacterial parotitis, and an obstructing salivary stone. Each follows from normal gland structure and function. Mumps is a blood-borne viral infection of ductal tissue that also reaches the testis, pancreas, and meninges. Bacterial parotitis is an ascending infection that takes hold when salivary flow falls. Stones form where saliva is thick and drains slowly, which is why they favor the submandibular gland. Board questions give the patient, the side, the duct finding, the timing of pain, and the amylase and lipase, and ask you to name the process and predict its complications.","prerequisites":["Histology of serous and mucous acini and the salivary duct system","Anatomy of the parotid region, submandibular triangle, and floor of the mouth","Parasympathetic control of salivation through CN VII and CN IX","Structure and replication of enveloped negative-sense RNA viruses","Acute inflammation with neutrophils and pus versus mononuclear (viral) inflammation"],"connections":[{"to":"Exocrine pancreas and acute pancreatitis","how":"Amylase comes from both the salivary glands and the pancreas, so lipase is needed to tell parotitis from pancreatitis."},{"to":"Male reproductive system","how":"Mumps orchitis shows how swelling inside the unyielding tunica albuginea causes tubular necrosis and testicular atrophy."},{"to":"Central nervous system infections","how":"Mumps is a classic cause of aseptic (viral) meningitis with a lymphocytic cerebrospinal fluid."},{"to":"Immunology and autoimmune disease","how":"Sjogren syndrome destroys salivary acini, lowers flow, and so predisposes to ascending bacterial parotitis, while vaccination has made mumps uncommon."},{"to":"Oral microbiology","how":"The organisms of bacterial parotitis and secondary sialadenitis are oral and skin flora that ascend the duct when flushing stops."}],"terms":[{"term":"Parotitis","def":"Inflammation of the parotid gland, from viral infection, bacterial infection, or obstruction."},{"term":"Sialadenitis","def":"Inflammation of any salivary gland, often from infection that ascends a duct when salivary flow is reduced or blocked."},{"term":"Sialolithiasis","def":"Formation of a calcified stone (sialolith) inside a salivary gland or its duct."},{"term":"Stensen duct","def":"The parotid duct, which crosses the masseter, pierces the buccinator, and opens opposite the upper second molar."},{"term":"Wharton duct","def":"The submandibular duct, which runs forward and upward through the floor of the mouth to open at the sublingual papilla beside the lingual frenulum."},{"term":"Serous acinus","def":"A cluster of secretory cells that produce a watery, protein-rich fluid containing enzymes such as salivary amylase."},{"term":"Mucous acinus","def":"A cluster of secretory cells that produce mucins, which make saliva thick and viscous."},{"term":"Hemagglutinin-neuraminidase (HN)","def":"The mumps envelope protein that binds sialic acid on host cells and cleaves it to release new virions."},{"term":"Salivary (S-type) amylase","def":"The amylase isoenzyme made by salivary glands; it is measured together with pancreatic amylase in total serum amylase."},{"term":"Orchitis","def":"Inflammation of the testis; in mumps it is caused by viral spread through the blood after puberty."},{"term":"Aseptic meningitis","def":"Meningitis with a lymphocytic cerebrospinal fluid and no bacteria on Gram stain or culture, usually caused by a virus."},{"term":"Salivary colic","def":"Painful swelling of an obstructed gland that begins when eating stimulates secretion and settles as secretion slows."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-01-C01","obj":"GI-13-A.1","title":"Mumps parotitis versus acute bacterial parotitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The parotid is the largest salivary gland and is purely serous, so its saliva is watery and rich in salivary amylase. It lies over the mandibular ramus in a dense fascial capsule. Steady salivary flow protects it by flushing Stensen duct and delivering lysozyme, lactoferrin, and secretory IgA."},{"type":"h","text":"Mumps parotitis: a blood-borne viral infection"},{"type":"p","text":"Mumps virus is an enveloped, nonsegmented, negative-sense single-stranded RNA paramyxovirus with one serotype. Its hemagglutinin-neuraminidase (HN) protein binds sialic acid on host cells and its fusion (F) protein merges the envelope with the cell membrane. Spread by droplets, it replicates in upper airway epithelium and regional lymph nodes, and a viremia then seeds glands and meninges after an incubation of about 16 to 18 days."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of mumps","steps":["Droplets reach upper airway epithelium","Replication in epithelium and regional lymph nodes","Viremia seeds glands and meninges","Ductal infection with edema and lymphocytes","Parotitis, orchitis, pancreatitis, aseptic meningitis"],"caption":"One viremia explains why mumps can involve salivary glands, gonads, pancreas, and meninges."},{"type":"p","text":"Infected ductal epithelium degenerates and the interstitium fills with edema, lymphocytes, plasma cells, and macrophages. There is no neutrophilic exudate, so the duct opening may be red but expresses no pus. Swelling stretches the capsule, causing pain on chewing and earache. It is bilateral in most cases, though it often begins on one side. Injured acini release salivary amylase into the blood, so total serum amylase rises while lipase, made only by the pancreas, stays normal unless the pancreas is also infected."},{"type":"p","text":"Orchitis affects about 20 to 30% of infected postpubertal males, usually one testis; edema inside the unyielding tunica albuginea compresses seminiferous tubules, causing pressure necrosis and later atrophy, although sterility is rare. Mumps also causes oophoritis, pancreatitis, aseptic meningitis with a lymphocytic cerebrospinal fluid (sometimes without parotitis), and usually unilateral sensorineural hearing loss."},{"type":"h","text":"Acute bacterial parotitis: an ascending infection"},{"type":"p","text":"Bacterial parotitis is an ascending infection: when salivary flow falls, oral bacteria climb Stensen duct. Staphylococcus aureus is the most common isolate; streptococci and anaerobes also occur. Every major risk factor lowers flow or blocks the duct: dehydration, older age, the postoperative period, anticholinergic and diuretic drugs, Sjogren syndrome, poor oral hygiene, and a duct stone. Neutrophils fill ducts and acini with pus, which gland massage pushes out of the duct opening. The unilateral gland is hot and very tender in its tight capsule, with fever and neutrophilic leukocytosis. An abscess can spread into the deep neck spaces."},{"type":"table","title":"Mumps parotitis compared with acute bacterial parotitis","cols":["Feature","Mumps parotitis","Acute bacterial parotitis"],"rows":[["Cause","Mumps virus (paramyxovirus)","Staphylococcus aureus most often"],["Typical patient","Unvaccinated child or young adult","Dehydrated, older, or postoperative"],["Side","Often bilateral","Usually unilateral"],["Inflammation","Lymphocytes, plasma cells","Neutrophils (pus)"],["Duct opening","May be red, no pus","Pus on gland massage"],["Complications","Orchitis, pancreatitis, aseptic meningitis, deafness","Abscess, deep neck spread"]],"caption":"Pus at the duct in a low-flow host points to bacteria; bilateral swelling with gonadal, pancreatic, or meningeal disease points to mumps."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: amylase versus lipase in parotid swelling","text":"An unvaccinated 19-year-old has fever of 38.6 °C (101.5 °F), bilateral preauricular swelling, and mild epigastric discomfort. Amylase is 410 U/L (reference about 30 to 110 U/L); lipase is 40 U/L (reference about 10 to 60 U/L). Step 1: total amylase includes salivary and pancreatic isoenzymes, and inflamed parotid acini leak the salivary one. Step 2: lipase is pancreatic and normal, so pancreatitis is not supported; lipase above about three times its upper limit would suggest it. Step 3: amylase cannot separate viral from bacterial parotitis; the host, side, and duct finding do."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A dehydrated 78-year-old on an anticholinergic drug develops one hot, tender parotid after surgery. What will gland massage produce?","explain":"Low flow stops duct flushing, so oral bacteria, most often Staphylococcus aureus, ascend and cause neutrophilic inflammation; pus exits the duct.","check":"The bacterial parotitis subsection."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-01-C02","obj":"GI-13-A.2","title":"Why salivary stones favor the submandibular gland, and what obstruction causes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The submandibular gland is mixed, mostly serous with some mucous acini, and supplies most resting saliva. It wraps around the posterior border of the mylohyoid, so its deep part lies in the floor of the mouth. Wharton duct, about 5 cm long, runs forward and upward from there, crossed by the lingual nerve, to open at the sublingual papilla beside the lingual frenulum."},{"type":"table","title":"The major salivary glands and their stone risk","cols":["Gland","Acini","Duct","Parasympathetic ganglion","Share of stones"],"rows":[["Parotid","Purely serous","Stensen: roughly horizontal","Otic (CN IX)","About 5 to 10%"],["Submandibular","Mixed, mostly serous","Wharton: rising, bends at mylohyoid","Submandibular (CN VII)","About 80 to 90%"],["Sublingual","Mostly mucous","Many small ducts","Submandibular (CN VII)","Rare"]],"caption":"The gland with thicker saliva and an uphill duct forms most stones."},{"type":"p","text":"A stone (sialolith) starts as an organic nidus of mucin, shed cells, or debris on which calcium phosphate, mostly hydroxyapatite, precipitates in layers. Two kinds of factors favor the submandibular gland. Chemically, its saliva has more mucin and so is more viscous, it is more alkaline, which lowers calcium phosphate solubility, and it carries more calcium than parotid saliva. Mechanically, Wharton duct rises against gravity when upright, bends sharply around the mylohyoid, and ends at a narrow orifice, so slow flow gives crystals time to grow. Parotid stones are fewer and more often radiolucent; most submandibular stones are radiopaque."},{"type":"flow","title":"How a submandibular stone forms","steps":["Viscous, alkaline, calcium-rich saliva","Slow flow up a rising, angled duct","Mucin and debris form a nidus","Calcium phosphate layers deposit","Stone blocks Wharton duct"],"caption":"Chemistry supplies the material and duct mechanics supply the time."},{"type":"p","text":"Obstruction hurts when secretion is stimulated. Between meals, slow secretion seeps past a partial block. Food triggers a parasympathetic surge: acetylcholine on M3 receptors raises acinar Ca2+ and drives chloride and water secretion. The acini secrete against the blocked duct, pressure rises, and the encapsulated gland swells painfully within minutes of starting a meal (salivary colic), then settles as secretion slows. Swelling is unilateral, an anterior stone may be felt in the floor of the mouth, and blood tests are normal."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Obstruction and infection feed each other","steps":["Stone slows salivary flow","Stagnant saliva stops flushing the duct","Oral bacteria ascend","Inflammation and duct scarring","Narrower duct, slower flow"],"caption":"Stasis invites infection, and the scarring it leaves worsens stasis."},{"type":"p","text":"Stagnant saliva lets bacteria, most often Staphylococcus aureus, ascend and cause secondary bacterial sialadenitis: constant pain, fever, redness, and pus at the papilla. Repeated episodes cause chronic sialadenitis with duct dilation, fibrosis, and acinar atrophy, leaving a firm gland that can mimic a tumor."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: predicting the course of a duct stone","text":"A 45-year-old notes swelling below the left mandible within minutes of starting lunch that fades over an hour. Step 1: meal-linked pain means secretion against an obstruction. Step 2: the site is the submandibular gland, the usual stone site. Step 3: predict a tender gland at meals, a stone palpable in the floor of the mouth, normal blood counts, and little flow from the left papilla. Step 4: constant pain with fever of 38.5 °C (101.3 °F) and pus at the papilla would mean secondary bacterial sialadenitis."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a Wharton duct stone hurt most at the start of a meal rather than during sleep?","explain":"Eating triggers a parasympathetic surge of secretion against the blocked duct, so pressure rises in the encapsulated gland; during sleep secretion is minimal.","check":"The paragraph on salivary colic."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and histology set the pattern of disease. The serous parotid sends a thin, amylase-rich saliva along a roughly horizontal duct, while the mixed submandibular gland sends thicker, calcium-rich saliva up a rising, angled duct, which is why stones favor the submandibular gland. The dense parotid capsule explains the pain of both kinds of parotitis, and the facial nerve inside the gland is usually spared by infection. Physiology links the conditions: salivary flow is a host defense, so anything that lowers it (dehydration, anticholinergic drugs, Sjogren syndrome, or a stone) opens the duct to ascending bacteria."},{"type":"p","text":"Microbiology and pathology separate the causes. Mumps reaches the gland through the blood and provokes mononuclear inflammation, so it is often bilateral and spreads to the testis, pancreas, meninges, and cochlea; bacteria reach the gland up the duct and provoke neutrophilic inflammation, so the disease is usually unilateral with pus at the duct. These are patterns, not rules: mumps can begin on one side, other viruses can cause parotitis, and amylase rises in any parotitis, so the duct finding and the host carry more weight than any single test."}],"summary":["The parotid is purely serous and drains by Stensen duct opposite the upper second molar; the submandibular gland is mixed and drains by Wharton duct at the sublingual papilla.","Mumps is an enveloped negative-sense RNA paramyxovirus that spreads by droplets and reaches glands and meninges through viremia.","Mumps parotitis is often bilateral, with mononuclear inflammation and no pus at the duct.","Parotitis raises total serum amylase from the salivary isoenzyme; a normal lipase means the pancreas is spared.","Mumps orchitis after puberty is usually unilateral; swelling inside the tunica albuginea causes tubular necrosis and atrophy, but sterility is rare.","Acute bacterial parotitis, most often Staphylococcus aureus, ascends Stensen duct when salivary flow falls, as in dehydrated, older, or postoperative patients.","Pus expressed from the duct on gland massage indicates bacterial infection.","About 80 to 90% of salivary stones form in the submandibular gland because its saliva is thicker, more alkaline, and richer in calcium, and its duct rises against gravity.","A duct stone causes painful swelling at meals that settles afterward; constant pain with fever means secondary bacterial sialadenitis."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Head and Neck, section on salivary glands (sialadenitis, mumps, sialolithiasis)"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Paramyxoviruses and Rubella Virus, section on mumps virus"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head, parotid region and oral region (salivary glands)"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Secretory Functions of the Alimentary Tract, secretion of saliva"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Organs Associated with the Digestive Tract, salivary glands"}]},"GI-13-B":{"unit":"GI-13-B","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-02","title":"Pancreatic Disease: Acute and Chronic Pancreatitis","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains pancreatitis as a failure of the safeguards that keep digestive enzymes inactive until they reach the duodenum. In acute pancreatitis, trypsin switches on inside the gland and the pancreas digests itself, which explains the pain, the enzyme rise, fat necrosis with hypocalcemia, retroperitoneal hemorrhage, and complications that range from a pseudocyst to shock and ARDS. In chronic pancreatitis, repeated injury replaces acini and islets with fibrous tissue, so the gland can no longer digest fat or control glucose. Board questions give a cause, a lab pattern, or a complication and ask you to name the mechanism behind it.","prerequisites":["Histology of pancreatic acini, ducts, and islets of Langerhans","Activation of pancreatic zymogens by enteropeptidase and trypsin in the duodenum","Retroperitoneal position of the pancreas and its relation to the stomach, duodenum, splenic vessels, and common bile duct","Necrosis types, especially enzymatic fat necrosis, and granulation tissue in repair","Digestion and absorption of fat, micelles, and fat-soluble vitamins"],"connections":[{"to":"Biliary disease and gallstones","how":"A gallstone lodged at the ampulla of Vater blocks pancreatic outflow and is the leading cause of acute pancreatitis."},{"to":"Endocrine pancreas and diabetes","how":"Destruction of islets in chronic pancreatitis causes pancreatogenic (type 3c) diabetes with loss of both insulin and glucagon."},{"to":"Calcium and parathyroid physiology","how":"Hypercalcemia can trigger pancreatitis, and pancreatitis can lower serum calcium by trapping it in fat necrosis."},{"to":"Respiratory pathology","how":"Circulating enzymes and cytokines injure the alveolar capillary membrane and surfactant, producing ARDS."},{"to":"Genetics and cystic fibrosis","how":"PRSS1, SPINK1, and CFTR mutations show how single genes control the balance between trypsin activation and protection."}],"terms":[{"term":"Zymogen","def":"An inactive enzyme precursor, such as trypsinogen, that becomes active only after a peptide is cleaved off."},{"term":"Enteropeptidase","def":"A duodenal brush border enzyme (also called enterokinase) that normally converts trypsinogen to trypsin."},{"term":"SPINK1","def":"Serine protease inhibitor Kazal type 1, an acinar protein that binds and blocks small amounts of trypsin activated inside the pancreas."},{"term":"PRSS1","def":"The gene for cationic trypsinogen; gain-of-function mutations cause autosomal dominant hereditary pancreatitis."},{"term":"Enzymatic fat necrosis","def":"Death of adipose tissue in which lipase releases fatty acids that combine with calcium to form chalky calcium soaps."},{"term":"Saponification","def":"The combination of free fatty acids with calcium to form insoluble calcium soaps in necrotic fat."},{"term":"Pseudocyst","def":"A collection of pancreatic fluid walled off by granulation and fibrous tissue, with no epithelial lining, that persists beyond about 4 weeks."},{"term":"Walled-off necrosis","def":"A mature, encapsulated collection of necrotic pancreatic or peripancreatic tissue that forms after necrotizing pancreatitis."},{"term":"Cullen sign","def":"Bruising around the umbilicus from retroperitoneal blood tracking forward to the anterior abdominal wall."},{"term":"Grey Turner sign","def":"Bruising of the flanks from retroperitoneal blood tracking laterally to the subcutaneous tissue."},{"term":"Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency","def":"Inadequate delivery of pancreatic enzymes and bicarbonate to the duodenum, causing maldigestion, mainly of fat."},{"term":"Pancreatic stellate cell","def":"A periacinar cell that, when activated by injury and TGF-beta, becomes a myofibroblast and lays down collagen."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-02-C01","obj":"GI-13-B.1","title":"Premature trypsin activation and the causes of acute pancreatitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Acute pancreatitis is acute, usually reversible inflammation of the pancreas caused by digestive enzymes that become active inside the gland instead of in the duodenum. Acinar cells make large amounts of proteases, phospholipase, and lipase, so the gland depends on several layers of protection to stay intact."},{"type":"table","title":"How the pancreas normally avoids digesting itself","cols":["Safeguard","How it protects","How it fails"],"rows":[["Zymogen synthesis","Proteases and phospholipase A2 are made as inactive precursors; enteropeptidase activates trypsinogen only in the duodenum","Lysosomal cathepsin B activates trypsinogen inside the acinar cell"],["Separate compartments","Zymogen granules are kept apart from lysosomes","A sustained rise in acinar Ca2+ lets granules and lysosomes fuse"],["SPINK1","Binds and inhibits small amounts of active trypsin","Loss-of-function mutation or an activation load that exceeds it"],["Trypsin autolysis","Trypsin cleaves and inactivates itself at a site that includes arginine 122","PRSS1 mutation removes or shields the cleavage site"],["Rapid duct flow","CFTR-driven bicarbonate-rich fluid flushes zymogens into the duodenum","Duct obstruction, protein plugs, or CFTR defects"]],"caption":"Every cause of pancreatitis defeats one or more safeguards."},{"type":"p","text":"Trypsin is the pivot because it activates the other zymogens and more trypsinogen, so a small amount of trypsin inside the cell amplifies itself. Each activated enzyme then explains a lesion. Proteases cause proteolytic necrosis of acini. Elastase dissolves the elastic fibers of vessel walls, producing hemorrhage. Phospholipase A2 converts lecithin to lysolecithin, which damages cell membranes. Lipase hydrolyzes triglyceride in peripancreatic fat, producing fat necrosis. Kallikrein generates bradykinin, which dilates vessels and makes them leak. Injured acinar cells also release TNF, IL-1, IL-6, and chemokines that recruit neutrophils and spread inflammation."},{"type":"flow","title":"From insult to autodigestion","steps":["Insult: duct obstruction, alcohol, hypercalcemia, triglycerides, trauma","Sustained Ca2+ rise in the acinar cell","Zymogen granules fuse with lysosomes","Cathepsin B converts trypsinogen to trypsin","Trypsin activates more trypsinogen and the other zymogens","Proteolysis, fat necrosis, vessel injury, and hemorrhage","Cytokines recruit neutrophils and spread inflammation systemically"],"caption":"Most causes converge on trypsin activation inside the acinar cell."},{"type":"h","text":"Causes"},{"type":"p","text":"Gallstones and alcohol account for most cases in Western populations. A gallstone usually causes pancreatitis while passing, so small stones are the dangerous ones, and the patient is often a middle-aged woman with a raised ALT. Alcohol causes pancreatitis in only a small minority of heavy drinkers, usually after years of heavy use, which suggests that cofactors such as smoking and genetic variants are needed."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of acute pancreatitis and how they act","cols":["Cause","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Gallstones","Stone at the ampulla obstructs the pancreatic duct and raises duct pressure"],["Alcohol","Protein-rich viscous juice forms plugs; acetaldehyde and fatty acid ethyl esters injure acini; sphincter of Oddi dysfunction"],["Hypertriglyceridemia, usually above about 1,000 mg/dL (11.3 mmol/L)","Lipase releases toxic free fatty acids in pancreatic capillaries; chylomicrons slow capillary flow"],["Hypercalcemia (for example, primary hyperparathyroidism)","Raised Ca2+ promotes trypsinogen activation inside acini and stabilizes trypsin"],["ERCP","Instrumentation and contrast injection injure the papilla and raise duct pressure"],["Mumps (also coxsackie B virus and CMV)","Viral infection and inflammation of acinar and duct cells"],["Blunt trauma","Epigastric blow crushes the gland against the vertebral column and tears ducts"],["Drugs: thiazides, azathioprine, valproate, didanosine, L-asparaginase, sulfonamides","Mostly idiosyncratic or toxic; thiazides may act partly by raising calcium and triglycerides"]],"caption":"Less common: hereditary mutations, IgG4-related autoimmune disease, scorpion venom, idiopathic."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A child has recurrent attacks of acute pancreatitis and her father had the same. Testing shows a PRSS1 mutation that removes the trypsin autolysis site. Which safeguard has failed, and why does an ordinary small activation event become an attack?","explain":"Trypsin that activates inside the gland can no longer cleave and inactivate itself, so the small amount SPINK1 cannot neutralize persists and keeps activating more trypsinogen and the other zymogens.","check":"Compare with the safeguards table and the flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-02-C02","obj":"GI-13-B.2","title":"Interpreting the findings of acute pancreatitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The diagnosis rests on two of three criteria: typical abdominal pain, serum lipase (or amylase) above 3 times the upper limit of normal, and typical findings on imaging. Each criterion follows from where the pancreas lies and what it releases when injured."},{"type":"p","text":"The pancreas lies in the retroperitoneum across about L1 to L2, behind the stomach and lesser sac. Its pain is sudden, severe, constant, and epigastric, and it bores through to the back because inflamed tissue and leaked enzymes irritate the posterior abdominal wall and the celiac plexus. Pain often eases when the patient sits and leans forward. Nausea, vomiting, and ileus follow as inflammation inhibits nearby bowel, sometimes leaving a dilated jejunal loop beside the gland (sentinel loop) on plain film. Tachycardia and fever, often above 38 °C (100.4 °F), reflect systemic inflammation rather than infection in the first days."},{"type":"table","title":"Lipase versus amylase","cols":["Feature","Lipase","Amylase"],"rows":[["Source","Almost only the pancreas","Pancreas and salivary glands"],["Rise and fall","Rises within hours; stays high about 8 to 14 days","Rises within hours; often normal by 3 to 5 days"],["Specificity","Higher","Lower: parotitis, bowel perforation or ischemia, ruptured ectopic pregnancy, macroamylasemia, renal failure"],["Pitfalls","Can rise modestly in renal failure","May be falsely normal with marked hypertriglyceridemia and in alcohol-related acute-on-chronic disease"]],"caption":"Lipase is preferred, especially for late presentation. Neither level predicts severity."},{"type":"p","text":"Other laboratory findings point to cause and severity. An ALT above about 3 times normal suggests a gallstone cause. Leukocytosis and hyperglycemia reflect inflammation, stress hormones, and islet injury. A rising hematocrit and BUN mean hemoconcentration from fluid loss into the retroperitoneum and predict a worse course."},{"type":"h","text":"Fat necrosis and hypocalcemia"},{"type":"flow","title":"How fat necrosis lowers serum calcium","steps":["Activated lipase reaches peripancreatic, omental, and mesenteric fat","Triglyceride is hydrolyzed to free fatty acids","Fatty acids bind Ca2+ to form calcium soaps","Chalky white foci; basophilic calcium in ghostly fat cells","Serum calcium falls; below about 8 mg/dL signals severe disease"],"caption":"Calcium is trapped in necrotic fat (saponification)."},{"type":"p","text":"Hypocalcemia is a marker of severe disease because the amount of calcium trapped reflects the amount of fat necrosis. Hypoalbuminemia lowers total calcium without changing ionized calcium, and low magnesium in alcohol use impairs PTH secretion and action, so both can add to the low value. True ionized hypocalcemia raises neuronal excitability, causing perioral tingling, Chvostek and Trousseau signs, and a prolonged QT interval."},{"type":"example","title":"Is this calcium really low?","text":"On day 2 a patient has total calcium 7.0 mg/dL (reference about 8.5 to 10.5) and albumin 2.4 g/dL (reference about 3.5 to 5.0). The usual correction adds 0.8 mg/dL of calcium for each 1 g/dL that albumin is below 4.0: 7.0 plus 0.8 times 1.6 gives about 8.3 mg/dL. So much of the low total reflects low albumin, not lost calcium. The correction is unreliable in acute illness, however, so ionized calcium is measured directly (reference about 4.6 to 5.3 mg/dL, or 1.15 to 1.33 mmol/L). If it is low and the patient has tingling and a long QT, the fall is real, mostly from calcium trapped in fat necrosis, and the attack is severe."},{"type":"h","text":"Cullen and Grey Turner signs"},{"type":"p","text":"When elastase and other enzymes erode vessel walls, hemorrhagic exudate dissects through retroperitoneal tissue planes. Blood spreading from the anterior pararenal space, where the pancreas lies, into the posterior pararenal space and out to the flank subcutaneous tissue produces Grey Turner sign; blood reaching the umbilicus, usually along the falciform ligament, produces Cullen sign. These bruises appear a day or more after onset, occur in only a small minority of patients, and indicate hemorrhagic necrotizing pancreatitis with a poor prognosis. They are not specific, because any retroperitoneal bleed, such as a ruptured aortic aneurysm, can cause them. Enzyme-rich exudate can also cross the diaphragm and produce a left pleural effusion with high amylase."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-02-C03","obj":"GI-13-B.3","title":"Local and systemic complications of acute pancreatitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Most attacks (about 80% to 90%) are interstitial edematous pancreatitis: the gland swells, the attack is mild, and it resolves within a week. A minority become necrotizing pancreatitis, with nonperfused pancreatic or peripancreatic tissue on contrast imaging. Deaths cluster in two phases. In the first week, systemic inflammation drives organ failure; after that, local complications of necrosis, especially infection, dominate. Severity is graded by organ failure: no organ failure and no complications is mild, transient organ failure (under 48 hours) or a local or systemic complication is moderately severe, and organ failure persisting beyond 48 hours is severe."},{"type":"h","text":"Fluid collections and the pseudocyst"},{"type":"table","title":"Pancreatic fluid collections","cols":["Collection","Arises from","Timing","Wall and contents"],"rows":[["Acute peripancreatic fluid collection","Interstitial pancreatitis","Under 4 weeks","No wall; fluid only; most resorb"],["Pseudocyst","Interstitial pancreatitis with a duct leak","Over 4 weeks","Granulation then fibrous tissue, no epithelium; enzyme-rich fluid"],["Acute necrotic collection","Necrotizing pancreatitis","Under 4 weeks","No wall; fluid and necrotic debris"],["Walled-off necrosis","Necrotizing pancreatitis","Over 4 weeks","Mature wall; semisolid necrotic debris"]],"caption":"Collections are classified by whether necrosis is present and whether a wall has matured, which takes about 4 weeks."},{"type":"p","text":"A pseudocyst forms when a disrupted duct leaks pancreatic juice, often into the lesser sac. The surrounding tissue walls it off, first with granulation tissue and then with fibrous tissue, so the lining contains no epithelium. That absent lining separates it from true cysts and cystic neoplasms, such as serous cystadenoma, mucinous cystic neoplasm, and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasm, which are lined by epithelium. Pseudocyst fluid has a very high amylase because it is pancreatic juice. A large pseudocyst can compress the stomach or duodenum (early satiety, vomiting) or the common bile duct (jaundice); it can become infected, rupture into the peritoneum (pancreatic ascites), or bleed when enzymes erode the splenic artery into a pseudoaneurysm. Inflammation of the adjacent splenic vein can cause thrombosis, with gastric varices from left-sided portal hypertension."},{"type":"p","text":"Necrotic tissue has no blood supply, so circulating neutrophils cannot reach it, while gut ischemia and ileus let enteric bacteria such as Escherichia coli, Klebsiella, and enterococci cross the bowel wall. Infected necrosis usually appears in the second to fourth week as new fever, rising leukocytosis, and gas bubbles within a collection on CT; it is the main cause of late death. The older term pancreatic abscess described a walled-off collection of pus."},{"type":"h","text":"Systemic complications"},{"type":"flow","title":"From pancreatic inflammation to organ failure","steps":["Enzymes, cytokines, and kallikrein products enter the circulation","Systemic inflammatory response; endothelial activation","Capillary leak and vasodilation throughout the body","Lungs, kidneys, and circulation fail together","Early death from multiorgan failure in the first week"],"caption":"Organ failure in the first week is driven by systemic inflammation, not infection."},{"type":"table","title":"Systemic complications and their mechanisms","cols":["Complication","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Hypovolemic shock","Massive fluid loss into the inflamed retroperitoneum, capillary leak, vomiting, hemorrhage, and bradykinin vasodilation"],["ARDS","Phospholipase A2 degrades surfactant lecithin; cytokines and neutrophils damage the alveolar capillary membrane"],["DIC","Tissue factor from injured tissue and circulating trypsin activate coagulation, consuming platelets and factors"],["Acute kidney injury","Hypoperfusion from hypovolemia plus inflammatory tubular injury"],["Hypocalcemia and hyperglycemia","Calcium soaps in fat necrosis; stress hormones and islet injury"]],"caption":"Each systemic complication follows from enzymes or cytokines reaching the blood or from fluid lost into the retroperitoneum."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Five weeks after a mild attack of interstitial pancreatitis, imaging shows a round, thick-walled fluid collection behind the stomach. What lines its wall, and what would its fluid amylase be?","explain":"It is a pseudocyst: leaked pancreatic juice walled off by granulation and fibrous tissue without epithelium, so the fluid amylase is very high.","check":"Compare with the fluid collections table and the paragraph on the pseudocyst."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-02-C04","obj":"GI-13-B.4","title":"Chronic pancreatitis, exocrine insufficiency, and diabetes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Chronic pancreatitis is progressive, irreversible inflammation in which fibrous tissue replaces acini and eventually islets, and the ducts become irregularly narrowed and dilated. Irreversibility is the key distinction from acute pancreatitis, although repeated acute attacks can lead to it through repeated rounds of necrosis and scarring. Histology shows interlobular fibrosis, atrophic acini, dilated ducts containing protein plugs and calcified stones, and clusters of islets that survive among the scar until late."},{"type":"cycle","title":"The injury and fibrosis cycle","steps":["Injury: alcohol, duct obstruction, or a genetic defect","Acinar necrosis and cytokine release, including TGF-beta","Pancreatic stellate cells become collagen-producing myofibroblasts","Fibrosis replaces acini and strictures the ducts","Protein plugs calcify; duct pressure and ischemia rise","Further acinar injury"],"caption":"Each round of injury leaves scar that invites more injury."},{"type":"p","text":"Calcifications form when protein plugs in the ducts precipitate calcium carbonate. On imaging they are highly specific for chronic pancreatitis, especially alcohol-related disease. Dilated ducts alternating with strictures give the chain of lakes appearance of the main duct."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of chronic pancreatitis","cols":["Cause","Mechanism and features"],"rows":[["Alcohol","Most common in adults; protein plugs and toxic metabolites; smoking adds risk"],["Cystic fibrosis (CFTR)","Poor Cl- and bicarbonate secretion leaves thick juice that plugs ducts; exocrine failure often from infancy; milder variants cause adult disease"],["Ductal obstruction","Tumor, stricture after trauma or necrosis, or ampullary stenosis; fibrosis upstream of the block"],["PRSS1 gain of function","Trypsin resists autolysis or activates too readily; autosomal dominant, high penetrance; onset in childhood; high cancer risk"],["SPINK1 loss of function","Less inhibition of trypsin inside acini; often acts as a modifier together with other risk factors"],["Other","Autoimmune (IgG4-related) pancreatitis, hypercalcemia, hypertriglyceridemia, idiopathic"]],"caption":"Genetic causes defeat the same trypsin safeguards that fail in acute pancreatitis."},{"type":"h","text":"Exocrine insufficiency"},{"type":"p","text":"The pancreas has a large reserve, so maldigestion appears only after about 90% of exocrine function is lost. Fat suffers first because lipase has little backup, while pepsin and brush border enzymes partly cover protein and starch. Lost bicarbonate leaves the duodenum acidic, which inactivates the remaining lipase and precipitates bile acids, so micelles form poorly. The result is steatorrhea (bulky, pale, foul, floating stools), weight loss, and deficiency of the fat-soluble vitamins: A (night blindness), D (osteomalacia and osteoporosis), E (neuropathy and ataxia), and K (bleeding with a prolonged prothrombin time). Proteases also free vitamin B12 from haptocorrin, so B12 absorption falls, though clinical deficiency is uncommon. Serum lipase and amylase are often normal late in disease because little acinar tissue remains, so fecal elastase is used to show insufficiency."},{"type":"example","title":"Pancreatic versus mucosal malabsorption","text":"Two adults have steatorrhea. Patient A has pancreatic calcifications, low fecal elastase, and normal D-xylose absorption. Patient B has normal fecal elastase and low urinary D-xylose. D-xylose is a sugar absorbed by intact mucosa without pancreatic enzymes, so a normal result means the mucosa works and the problem is digestion: patient A has exocrine insufficiency from chronic pancreatitis. In patient B, the mucosa itself fails to absorb, which points to a mucosal disease such as celiac disease."},{"type":"h","text":"Pancreatogenic diabetes"},{"type":"p","text":"As fibrosis advances, islets are destroyed, causing pancreatogenic (type 3c) diabetes, which usually appears later than exocrine insufficiency. Because alpha and beta cells are lost together, insulin deficiency comes with poor glucagon counterregulation, so glucose swings widely and hypoglycemia is common, while ketoacidosis is less frequent than in type 1 diabetes. Only a minority show the classic triad of calcification, steatorrhea, and diabetes. Chronic pancreatitis also raises the risk of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with longstanding alcohol-related chronic pancreatitis has normal serum lipase, steatorrhea, and easy bruising. Why is the lipase normal, and what explains the bruising?","explain":"Little acinar tissue remains to release lipase into the blood. Fat maldigestion prevents absorption of vitamin K, so clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X are not carboxylated and the prothrombin time rises.","check":"See exocrine insufficiency."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and histology explain where the disease shows itself. The retroperitoneal position sends pain to the back and lets hemorrhage track to the flanks and umbilicus, the shared outlet with the bile duct at the ampulla explains gallstone pancreatitis, and the neighboring splenic vessels, stomach, and bile duct explain pseudocyst complications. Acinar cells built for exporting protein, with zymogen granules and CFTR-driven duct flow, explain both the safeguards and how they fail."},{"type":"p","text":"Biochemistry and pathology tie the findings together. Lipase causes fat necrosis and hypocalcemia, elastase causes hemorrhage, phospholipase A2 damages membranes and surfactant, and cytokines turn a local process into systemic shock, ARDS, and DIC. Genetics shows the same balance from the other side, since PRSS1, SPINK1, and CFTR mutations each lower the threshold for trypsin activation. Limits matter: enzyme levels confirm the diagnosis but do not measure severity, and late chronic disease can have normal enzymes."}],"summary":["Acute pancreatitis begins when trypsin is activated inside acinar cells, usually by cathepsin B after zymogen granules fuse with lysosomes, and then activates the other zymogens.","Gallstones and alcohol cause most cases; hypertriglyceridemia above about 1,000 mg/dL, hypercalcemia, ERCP, mumps, trauma, and drugs such as thiazides are other causes.","Diagnosis needs two of three: epigastric pain radiating to the back, lipase or amylase above 3 times normal, and typical imaging; lipase is more specific and stays high longer.","Lipase releases fatty acids that bind calcium as soaps in fat necrosis, so hypocalcemia marks severe disease.","Cullen (periumbilical) and Grey Turner (flank) bruising mean retroperitoneal hemorrhage from necrotizing pancreatitis.","A pseudocyst is enzyme-rich fluid walled off by granulation and fibrous tissue without epithelium after about 4 weeks; walled-off necrosis contains necrotic debris.","Early deaths come from systemic inflammation with shock, ARDS, and DIC; later deaths come mainly from infected necrosis.","Chronic pancreatitis is irreversible fibrosis with calcified duct plugs, caused by alcohol, cystic fibrosis, duct obstruction, and PRSS1 or SPINK1 mutations.","Exocrine insufficiency appears after about 90% loss and causes steatorrhea and deficiency of vitamins A, D, E, and K.","Islet loss causes type 3c diabetes with both insulin and glucagon deficiency, so hypoglycemia is common."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Pancreas, sections on acute pancreatitis, chronic pancreatitis, and pseudocysts"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the Exocrine Pancreas"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Secretory Functions of the Alimentary Tract, pancreatic secretion"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Pancreatic and Salivary Glands"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen, pancreas and retroperitoneum"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Organs Associated with the Digestive Tract, pancreas"}]},"GI-13-C":{"unit":"GI-13-C","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-03","title":"Cholestasis, Cirrhosis, and Noninfectious Liver Disease","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the noninfectious diseases of the liver: obstructed bile flow, immune destruction of bile ducts, inherited disorders of bilirubin handling, alcohol, metabolic fatty liver, autoimmune and drug hepatitis, and the cirrhosis and portal hypertension in which many of them end. Board questions usually give a set of liver tests, a few physical signs, or a biopsy description and ask you to name the process, locate the defect, or predict the next complication. Each finding in this chapter traces back to one lost hepatocyte or bile duct function, or to raised resistance in the portal circulation, so learning the mechanism lets you work out findings you have not memorized.","prerequisites":["Liver lobule and acinus, zones 1 to 3, sinusoids, and the space of Disse (GI-5)","Bilirubin formation, conjugation, and excretion, and the meaning of direct and indirect bilirubin (GI-9)","Hepatic synthetic functions: albumin, clotting factors, and the urea cycle (GI-9)","Portal venous anatomy and the portosystemic anastomoses","Renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system and baroreceptor control of renal blood flow"],"connections":[{"to":"Viral hepatitis (GI-12)","how":"Chronic hepatitis B and C are leading causes of the cirrhosis whose mechanism and findings are taught here."},{"to":"Esophageal and gastric bleeding (GI-13-J)","how":"Portal hypertension opens left gastric to esophageal collaterals, and variceal rupture is a major cause of death in cirrhosis."},{"to":"Liver tumors (GI-13-K)","how":"Cirrhosis of any cause, and MASH even before cirrhosis, is the main soil for hepatocellular carcinoma, while primary sclerosing cholangitis predisposes to cholangiocarcinoma."},{"to":"Inflammatory bowel disease (GI-13-G)","how":"About 70% of patients with primary sclerosing cholangitis have inflammatory bowel disease, usually ulcerative colitis."},{"to":"Endocrine and metabolic disease","how":"Insulin resistance and obesity drive metabolic fatty liver disease, and altered hepatic steroid clearance explains the feminizing signs of cirrhosis."}],"terms":[{"term":"Cholestasis","def":"Impaired formation or flow of bile, so that bile constituents such as conjugated bilirubin and bile acids accumulate in blood and tissues."},{"term":"Space of Disse","def":"The narrow perisinusoidal space between the fenestrated sinusoidal endothelium and the hepatocyte surface, where hepatic stellate cells reside."},{"term":"Hepatic stellate cell","def":"A perisinusoidal cell that stores vitamin A when quiescent and becomes a collagen-producing myofibroblast when activated by injury."},{"term":"Regenerative nodule","def":"A rounded island of proliferating hepatocytes, without normal lobular architecture, enclosed by fibrous septa in cirrhosis."},{"term":"Portal hypertension","def":"A rise in the portal pressure gradient above normal, defined clinically as a hepatic venous pressure gradient above 5 mmHg."},{"term":"Effective arterial blood volume","def":"The part of the circulating volume that fills the arterial tree and is sensed by baroreceptors, which can be low even when total body fluid is high."},{"term":"Asterixis","def":"A flapping tremor of the outstretched, dorsiflexed hands caused by brief lapses of postural muscle tone, typical of metabolic encephalopathy."},{"term":"Interface hepatitis","def":"Inflammation that crosses the limiting plate from the portal tract into the lobule and kills periportal hepatocytes."},{"term":"Mallory-Denk body","def":"An eosinophilic cytoplasmic aggregate of damaged keratin intermediate filaments bound to ubiquitin, seen in injured hepatocytes."},{"term":"UGT1A1","def":"Uridine diphosphate glucuronosyltransferase 1A1, the hepatocyte enzyme that conjugates bilirubin with glucuronic acid."},{"term":"MRP2","def":"Multidrug resistance-associated protein 2, the ATP-dependent canalicular pump that secretes conjugated bilirubin into bile."},{"term":"Kernicterus","def":"Bilirubin encephalopathy caused by deposition of free unconjugated bilirubin in the basal ganglia and brainstem nuclei."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-03-C01","obj":"GI-13-C.1","title":"Intrahepatic and extrahepatic cholestasis and the cholestatic profile","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Cholestasis is impaired formation or flow of bile. Bile formation begins at the canalicular membrane of the hepatocyte, where ATP-dependent pumps secrete bile salts (the bile salt export pump, BSEP) and conjugated bilirubin (MRP2) into the canaliculus. Bile then drains through ductules and intrahepatic ducts into the extrahepatic ducts. A failure anywhere along this path produces a similar biochemical picture, so the task is to locate it."},{"type":"p","text":"Intrahepatic cholestasis arises inside the liver, from failure of hepatocyte secretion or loss of small ducts. Estrogens and anabolic steroids inhibit canalicular transport, and drugs such as chlorpromazine and amoxicillin-clavulanate cause idiosyncratic cholestatic injury. Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy appears in the third trimester, when high levels of estrogen and progesterone metabolites impair bile salt export in susceptible women, causing pruritus of the palms and soles and raised serum bile acids. Total parenteral nutrition (TPN) causes cholestasis, especially in infants, because absent enteral feeding removes the cholecystokinin signal that drives bile flow and soybean lipid phytosterols impair bile acid signaling. Sepsis cytokines downregulate the same transporters, and primary biliary cholangitis destroys small ducts (C02). Extrahepatic obstruction blocks the large ducts, and pressure above the block dilates them, which ultrasound shows; intrahepatic cholestasis leaves duct caliber normal."},{"type":"table","title":"Locating cholestasis","cols":["Level","Examples","Ducts on ultrasound","Clue"],"rows":[["Hepatocyte secretion","Estrogens, anabolic steroids, pregnancy, TPN, sepsis","Normal","Timing with a drug, third trimester, or illness"],["Small intrahepatic ducts","Primary biliary cholangitis","Normal","Middle-aged woman, antimitochondrial antibody"],["Large extrahepatic ducts","Common bile duct stone, carcinoma of the pancreatic head or ampulla, cholangiocarcinoma, stricture","Dilated above the block","Colicky pain and fever with a stone; painless jaundice with a palpable gallbladder with carcinoma"]],"caption":"Intrahepatic cholestasis leaves ducts normal in caliber; extrahepatic obstruction dilates the ducts upstream of the block."},{"type":"p","text":"The laboratory pattern follows from what is retained. Conjugated bilirubin is water soluble, so it is filtered and darkens the urine, while no bilirubin reaches the gut to form urobilinogen and stercobilin, so stools turn pale. Retained bile acids induce alkaline phosphatase (ALP) on canalicular and cholangiocyte membranes and release it into blood, so ALP rises out of proportion to the aminotransferases. ALP also comes from bone, placenta, and intestine; gamma-glutamyl transferase (GGT) rises in hepatobiliary but not bone disease, so a high GGT confirms a hepatic source. Retained bile acids and other pruritogens cause pruritus. Cholesterol that cannot be excreted in bile raises serum lipids, including the abnormal lipoprotein X, and deposits in skin as xanthomas and xanthelasma. Without bile salts in the gut, fat and vitamins A, D, E, and K are poorly absorbed, and the prolonged prothrombin time of vitamin K deficiency corrects with parenteral vitamin K, unlike that of hepatocellular failure (C04)."},{"type":"example","title":"Is a high ALP cholestatic, and where is the block?","text":"A 52-year-old woman has pruritus. Total bilirubin is 3.4 mg/dL (reference about 0.3 to 1.2) with direct bilirubin 2.6 mg/dL, ALP 480 U/L (about 40 to 130), GGT 310 U/L (about 5 to 50), and ALT 70 U/L (about 7 to 40). Direct bilirubin dominates, so the defect lies after conjugation. ALP is nearly four times its upper limit while ALT is only mildly raised, a cholestatic rather than hepatocellular pattern, and the high GGT shows the ALP is hepatic; a growing adolescent or a patient with Paget disease of bone would have a high ALP with a normal GGT. Ultrasound then sets the level: dilated ducts mean extrahepatic obstruction, normal ducts an intrahepatic cause. With normal ducts in a middle-aged woman, an antimitochondrial antibody would support primary biliary cholangitis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-03-C02","obj":"GI-13-C.2","title":"Primary biliary cholangitis versus primary sclerosing cholangitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Both diseases are chronic immune-mediated destruction of bile ducts that causes cholestasis, progressive fibrosis, and eventually biliary cirrhosis. They differ in which ducts are attacked, how they are injured, and who is affected."},{"type":"p","text":"Primary biliary cholangitis (PBC, formerly primary biliary cirrhosis) is an autoimmune disease, about 90% in women, usually diagnosed between about 40 and 60 years of age. T cells destroy the small interlobular bile ducts of the portal tracts. The characteristic florid duct lesion is a dense lymphocytic and plasma cell infiltrate, often with noncaseating granulomas, around a damaged duct. The marker is antimitochondrial antibody (AMA), present in about 95% of patients and directed against the E2 subunit of the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex. Every cell has this antigen, but dying biliary epithelial cells keep it in an intact, immunogenic form, which helps explain why the attack centers on ducts. Serum IgM is often raised. As ducts disappear (ductopenia), retained bile acids injure hepatocytes and fibrosis spreads from the portal tracts over years. Fatigue and pruritus come early, with xanthelasma from high cholesterol and osteoporosis later. PBC accompanies other autoimmune diseases, especially Sjogren syndrome, autoimmune thyroiditis, and systemic sclerosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC) is inflammation and obliterative fibrosis of intrahepatic and extrahepatic ducts. Men are affected about twice as often as women, usually in their 30s and 40s, and about 70% have inflammatory bowel disease, mostly ulcerative colitis. The cause is not settled; a leading idea is that gut-derived lymphocytes or bacterial products reach the biliary tree through the portal blood. Concentric onion-skin fibrosis around ducts eventually leaves a fibrous cord where a duct was. Because large ducts are involved, short strictures alternate with dilated segments, giving a beaded pattern on cholangiography. Atypical perinuclear antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody (p-ANCA) is common but nonspecific, and AMA is absent. Chronic biliary inflammation is premalignant: lifetime risk of cholangiocarcinoma is roughly 10 to 15%, and gallbladder cancer and, with colitis, colorectal cancer risks also rise."},{"type":"table","title":"PBC and PSC compared","cols":["Feature","Primary biliary cholangitis","Primary sclerosing cholangitis"],"rows":[["Typical patient","Woman, usually about 40 to 60 years","Man, usually in his 30s or 40s"],["Ducts","Small intrahepatic (interlobular) ducts","Intrahepatic and extrahepatic ducts, small and large"],["Histology","Lymphocytic, granulomatous duct destruction (florid duct lesion)","Concentric onion-skin periductal fibrosis, fibrous obliteration"],["Cholangiography","Large ducts normal","Beaded ducts from strictures and dilations"],["Antibody","Antimitochondrial antibody (about 95%); raised IgM","p-ANCA in many; AMA negative"],["Associations","Sjogren syndrome, autoimmune thyroiditis, systemic sclerosis","Ulcerative colitis (about 70% have inflammatory bowel disease)"],["Main cancer risk","Hepatocellular carcinoma once cirrhotic","Cholangiocarcinoma; also gallbladder and colorectal carcinoma"]],"caption":"PBC is an autoantibody-marked disease of small ducts in women; PSC is a fibrosing disease of large and small ducts in men with colitis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-03-C03","obj":"GI-13-C.3","title":"How cirrhosis forms: stellate cells, the space of Disse, and its causes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hepatocytes face sinusoidal blood across the space of Disse. The sinusoidal endothelium is fenestrated and lacks a true basement membrane, and the space holds only a sparse, low-density matrix (mainly type IV collagen), so plasma proteins and solutes reach hepatocyte microvilli freely. Hepatic stellate cells (Ito cells) lie in this space, store vitamin A as retinyl esters in lipid droplets, and are normally quiescent."},{"type":"p","text":"Chronic injury of any cause kills hepatocytes and releases reactive oxygen species, lipid peroxides, and damage signals. Kupffer cells (resident macrophages) and injured cells release transforming growth factor beta (TGF-beta), platelet-derived growth factor (PDGF), tumor necrosis factor, and interleukin-1. Stellate cells lose their vitamin A and become myofibroblasts that proliferate (driven by PDGF), contract (driven by endothelin-1), and deposit type I and type III collagen (driven mainly by TGF-beta). This dense matrix fills the space of Disse, and the endothelium loses its fenestrations and builds a basement membrane, called capillarization of the sinusoids. Exchange between blood and hepatocytes is restricted, so function declines even before much liver is lost."},{"type":"flow","title":"From chronic injury to cirrhosis","steps":["Chronic hepatocyte injury releases reactive oxygen species and damage signals","Kupffer cells secrete TGF-beta, PDGF, and TNF","Stellate cells lose vitamin A and become myofibroblasts","Type I and III collagen fills the space of Disse","Sinusoids lose fenestrations (capillarization)","Bridging septa surround regenerative nodules","Portal resistance rises and hepatocyte exchange fails"],"caption":"Stellate cell activation links injury of any cause to the common end stage of fibrosis and nodules."},{"type":"p","text":"Cirrhosis is diffuse fibrosis with bridging septa that link portal tracts with each other and with central veins, surrounding regenerative nodules of hepatocytes that have lost lobular architecture. Nodules are micronodular (under about 3 mm), classically in alcohol, or macronodular, often after viral hepatitis, but the pattern changes with time and does not reliably identify the cause. Vascular channels in the septa carry blood past hepatocytes, and fibrosis, distorted vessels, and contracting stellate cells raise resistance to portal flow (C10). Fibrosis can partly regress if injury stops, but established nodular architecture rarely returns to normal, and cirrhosis of any cause raises the risk of hepatocellular carcinoma."},{"type":"table","title":"Main causes of cirrhosis","cols":["Category","Examples","Main injury"],"rows":[["Alcohol","Alcohol-related liver disease","Acetaldehyde, NADH excess, oxidative stress (C07)"],["Viral","Chronic hepatitis B (with or without D) and C","Immune killing of infected hepatocytes"],["Metabolic fatty liver","MASLD and MASH","Lipotoxicity from insulin resistance (C08)"],["Inherited metabolic","Hereditary hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","Iron or copper catalyzed free radicals; misfolded protein retained in the endoplasmic reticulum"],["Biliary and autoimmune","PBC, PSC, chronic large duct obstruction, autoimmune hepatitis","Bile acid toxicity; immune hepatocyte injury"],["Vascular","Chronic right heart failure, Budd-Chiari syndrome","Zone 3 congestion and hypoxia"]],"caption":"Alcohol, chronic viral hepatitis, and metabolic fatty liver disease account for most cirrhosis; many cases once called cryptogenic are burned-out MASH."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-03-C04","obj":"GI-13-C.4","title":"Findings of cirrhosis: hepatocellular failure and portal hypertension","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Cirrhosis produces two families of findings. Hepatocellular failure reflects lost hepatocyte mass and poor exchange in the hepatocytes that remain; portal hypertension reflects raised resistance to portal flow (C10). Compensated cirrhosis may show neither, and decompensation is marked by jaundice, ascites, variceal bleeding, or encephalopathy."},{"type":"p","text":"Hepatocytes make most clotting factors, including fibrinogen and factors II, V, VII, IX, X, and XI. Factor VII has the shortest half-life (about 4 to 6 hours), so the prothrombin time (PT) and international normalized ratio (INR) rise early in failure, and vitamin K cannot correct them because the defect is synthesis, not carboxylation. Factor VIII, made largely by sinusoidal endothelium, stays normal or high, which separates liver failure from disseminated intravascular coagulation. Because protein C, protein S, and antithrombin also fall, hemostasis is rebalanced rather than simply impaired, and thrombosis, including portal vein thrombosis, still occurs."},{"type":"table","title":"Hepatocellular failure explained by lost function","cols":["Finding","Lost function or cause"],"rows":[["Jaundice (mixed hyperbilirubinemia)","Failed uptake, conjugation, and canalicular excretion of bilirubin"],["High PT and INR not corrected by vitamin K","Failed synthesis of clotting factors"],["Hypoalbuminemia, edema","Failed albumin synthesis (half-life about 3 weeks, so a marker of chronic failure) lowers oncotic pressure"],["Spider angiomas, palmar erythema, gynecomastia, testicular atrophy","Less hepatic estrogen clearance, more peripheral aromatization of shunted androstenedione, and higher SHBG binding of testosterone"],["Hepatic encephalopathy, asterixis, fetor hepaticus","Ammonia and other gut-derived toxins escape the urea cycle and pass through shunts"],["Hypoglycemia (advanced failure)","Loss of glycogen stores and gluconeogenesis"]],"caption":"Each sign of hepatocellular failure is a missing hepatocyte product or a substance the hepatocyte no longer clears."},{"type":"p","text":"Hepatic encephalopathy is reversible brain dysfunction caused mainly by ammonia. Colonic bacterial urease and enterocyte glutaminase generate ammonia, normally converted to urea by periportal hepatocytes. In cirrhosis, ammonia bypasses the failing liver through shunts and reaches astrocytes, which lack a urea cycle and fix it as glutamine. Glutamine is osmotically active, so astrocytes swell and neurotransmission fails; increased GABA-ergic tone and systemic inflammation contribute. The precipitants follow from the chemistry: bleeding adds a protein load, alkalosis converts NH4+ to diffusible NH3, hypokalemia increases renal ammonia production, and infection, constipation, and sedatives worsen it. Signs progress from disturbed sleep and confusion to coma, with asterixis, a flapping tremor from brief lapses of postural tone."},{"type":"flow","title":"Ammonia and hepatic encephalopathy","steps":["Gut bacteria and enterocytes produce ammonia","Portosystemic shunts and failed urea cycle let ammonia pass","Astrocytes convert ammonia to glutamine","Glutamine draws water; astrocytes swell","Confusion, asterixis, and coma"],"caption":"Bleeding, alkalosis, hypokalemia, infection, and sedatives all push this sequence forward."},{"type":"p","text":"Portal hypertension adds ascites, splenomegaly with thrombocytopenia from platelet sequestration, esophageal varices, and caput medusae, discussed by mechanism in C10."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two patients have an INR of 2.0. One has an obstructing common bile duct stone and the other has cirrhosis. Predict each INR after parenteral vitamin K.","explain":"The obstructed patient corrects, because hepatocytes are healthy and only lack vitamin K, which needs bile salts for absorption. The cirrhotic patient does not, because the hepatocytes cannot make enough factor protein to carboxylate.","check":"The coagulation paragraph here and the fat-soluble vitamin sentence in C01."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-03-C05","obj":"GI-13-C.5","title":"Hepatorenal and hepatopulmonary syndromes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Portal hypertension increases vasodilator production in the splanchnic bed, chiefly nitric oxide (from endothelial shear stress and from gut bacterial products reaching the portal blood), along with carbon monoxide and endocannabinoids. Splanchnic arterioles dilate, systemic vascular resistance and arterial pressure fall, and cardiac output rises in a hyperdynamic circulation."},{"type":"p","text":"Hepatorenal syndrome is functional kidney failure in advanced cirrhosis, usually with ascites. Splanchnic vasodilation lowers effective arterial blood volume even though total body sodium and water are increased. Baroreceptors respond by activating the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, sympathetic nerves, and nonosmotic release of antidiuretic hormone. These defend blood pressure but constrict the renal vessels, and as disease advances, intrarenal vasodilator prostaglandins can no longer offset them. GFR falls while the tubules stay intact and retain sodium avidly, so urine sodium is very low (often under 10 mEq/L), fractional excretion of sodium is under 1%, the sediment is bland, and proteinuria is minimal; retained water causes dilutional hyponatremia. Renal histology is normal, and such kidneys work when transplanted into a recipient with a healthy liver, which proves the failure is hemodynamic. Unlike prerenal azotemia, function does not recover with volume expansion. Triggers reduce renal perfusion further: spontaneous bacterial peritonitis, gastrointestinal bleeding, large-volume paracentesis, overdiuresis, and nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs."},{"type":"flow","title":"Hepatorenal syndrome","steps":["Portal hypertension raises splanchnic nitric oxide","Splanchnic vasodilation lowers effective arterial blood volume","RAAS, sympathetic nerves, and ADH are activated","Renal vessels constrict and GFR falls","Kidneys retain sodium; urine sodium under 10 mEq/L","Kidney failure with normal renal histology"],"caption":"The kidney behaves as if the patient were hypovolemic, although total body fluid is increased."},{"type":"p","text":"Hepatopulmonary syndrome is hypoxemia caused by intrapulmonary vascular dilation in a patient with liver disease or portal hypertension. Excess pulmonary nitric oxide, from endothelin-1 acting on endothelin B receptors of pulmonary endothelium and from intravascular macrophages, widens capillaries from a normal diameter of about 8 to 15 micrometers to as much as several hundred. Oxygen cannot diffuse to red cells in the center of these wide vessels, rapid transit shortens contact time, and ventilation-perfusion mismatch and some true shunting add to the defect, widening the alveolar-arterial gradient. The dilations lie mainly in the lung bases, so standing increases flow through them: dyspnea worsens upright (platypnea) and arterial oxygen falls upright (orthodeoxia). Contrast echocardiography shows bubbles reaching the left heart only after about three or more beats, because they pass through the lung rather than an intracardiac shunt. Breathing 100% oxygen raises PaO2 because a high alveolar pressure drives oxygen farther across the vessel. Portopulmonary hypertension is the opposite lesion: vasoconstriction and remodeling raise pulmonary arterial pressure."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A man with cirrhosis and ascites has a rising creatinine, urine sodium of 5 mEq/L, and a bland sediment. He takes ibuprofen for back pain. Predict the effect on his GFR.","explain":"GFR falls further. In hepatorenal physiology, renal prostaglandins are the main local vasodilators opposing angiotensin II and sympathetic constriction; blocking cyclooxygenase removes them, so renal vasoconstriction becomes unopposed.","check":"The hepatorenal paragraph and flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-03-C06","obj":"GI-13-C.6","title":"Gilbert syndrome and Crigler-Najjar syndromes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Unconjugated bilirubin from heme breakdown is lipid soluble and travels in plasma bound to albumin. In the hepatocyte endoplasmic reticulum, UGT1A1 adds glucuronic acid to make water-soluble bilirubin glucuronides, which MRP2 secretes into bile. A UGT1A1 defect therefore raises unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin with normal liver enzymes and no bilirubin in the urine, because albumin-bound unconjugated bilirubin is not filtered. How much enzyme activity remains decides the severity."},{"type":"p","text":"Gilbert syndrome affects roughly 5 to 10% of many populations. Most affected people are homozygous for a promoter variant, an extra TA repeat in the TATA box (UGT1A1*28), that lowers transcription and leaves about 30% of normal activity. Bilirubin is normal or mildly raised, generally under about 3 mg/dL, and rises with fasting, intercurrent illness, exertion, or stress, so transient scleral icterus appears during a cold or a missed meal. The diagnosis rests on a mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia with normal aminotransferases and ALP and no hemolysis (normal hemoglobin, reticulocyte count, haptoglobin, and lactate dehydrogenase). It causes no liver injury and does not shorten life, although drugs cleared by UGT1A1, such as the active metabolite of irinotecan, are glucuronidated more slowly."},{"type":"p","text":"Crigler-Najjar syndrome is rare, autosomal recessive, and caused by coding mutations in UGT1A1. In type I, activity is absent, unconjugated bilirubin reaches about 20 to 50 mg/dL in the newborn, and bile is nearly colorless because little conjugate forms. Bilirubin that exceeds albumin binding circulates free, crosses the blood-brain barrier, and deposits in the basal ganglia (especially the globus pallidus), hippocampus, cerebellum, and brainstem nuclei, causing kernicterus. In type II (Arias syndrome), residual activity of under about 10% keeps bilirubin near 6 to 20 mg/dL, and kernicterus is uncommon. Phenobarbital induces UGT1A1 transcription through the constitutive androstane receptor; it lowers bilirubin by about 25% or more in type II, where some enzyme exists to induce, and does nothing in type I, so the response distinguishes them."},{"type":"table","title":"Inherited unconjugated hyperbilirubinemias","cols":["Feature","Gilbert syndrome","Crigler-Najjar type I","Crigler-Najjar type II"],"rows":[["UGT1A1 activity","About 30% (promoter variant)","Absent","Under about 10%"],["Usual bilirubin","Under about 3 mg/dL, rising with fasting or illness","About 20 to 50 mg/dL","About 6 to 20 mg/dL"],["Onset","Adolescence or adulthood, intermittent","First days of life","Infancy or childhood"],["Kernicterus","No","Yes, without intervention","Uncommon"],["Response to phenobarbital","Bilirubin falls","None","Falls by about 25% or more"],["Outlook","Benign","Severe","Usually compatible with normal life"]],"caption":"All three raise unconjugated bilirubin with normal liver enzymes; residual enzyme activity sets severity and the response to enzyme induction."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-03-C07","obj":"GI-13-C.7","title":"Alcohol-related liver disease and the AST to ALT ratio","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Alcohol-related liver disease progresses through three overlapping lesions: steatosis, alcoholic hepatitis, and cirrhosis. Steatosis develops in most heavy drinkers and resolves within weeks of abstinence, but only a minority progress, with risk shaped by amount and duration of drinking, female sex, genetic variants such as PNPLA3, obesity, and coexisting viral hepatitis."},{"type":"p","text":"Ethanol is oxidized by alcohol dehydrogenase to acetaldehyde and by aldehyde dehydrogenase to acetate, and both steps reduce NAD+ to NADH. The high NADH to NAD+ ratio inhibits fatty acid beta-oxidation, favors glycerol 3-phosphate (the triglyceride backbone), and shifts pyruvate to lactate, which blocks gluconeogenesis and raises lactate and uric acid. Fatty acids are therefore stored as triglyceride in large droplets, first in zone 3. Acetaldehyde forms protein adducts and damages microtubules, which impairs lipoprotein secretion; inducible CYP2E1 generates reactive oxygen species; and increased gut permeability delivers endotoxin to Kupffer cells, which release TNF."},{"type":"flow","title":"Ethanol metabolism to fatty liver","steps":["Alcohol and aldehyde dehydrogenases oxidize ethanol","NADH to NAD+ ratio rises","Beta-oxidation falls; glycerol 3-phosphate rises","Fatty acids are esterified to triglyceride","Macrovesicular steatosis, first in zone 3"],"caption":"Excess NADH redirects hepatocyte metabolism from burning fat to storing it."},{"type":"p","text":"Alcoholic hepatitis follows heavy, usually prolonged drinking and presents with fever, tender hepatomegaly, jaundice, and neutrophilic leukocytosis. Histology shows hepatocyte ballooning (swollen cells with cleared cytoplasm from a damaged cytoskeleton), Mallory-Denk bodies (aggregates of keratins 8 and 18 bound to ubiquitin), neutrophils clustered around injured hepatocytes, and pericellular and perisinusoidal fibrosis in zone 3. Mallory-Denk bodies are characteristic but not specific, also appearing in MASH, Wilson disease, and chronic cholestasis. Repeated injury activates stellate cells, and cirrhosis, classically micronodular, follows."},{"type":"p","text":"Aminotransferases are only modestly raised, usually under about 300 U/L even in severe alcoholic hepatitis, with AST more than twice ALT. Two mechanisms explain the ratio. Alcohol injures mitochondria, which hold a separate mitochondrial isoenzyme of AST but little ALT. Both enzymes need pyridoxal 5'-phosphate (vitamin B6), and the deficiency common in heavy drinkers lowers ALT activity more than AST. Aminotransferases in the thousands point to another process, such as acetaminophen injury, ischemia, or viral hepatitis. GGT is often high from enzyme induction, and macrocytosis is common."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-03-C08","obj":"GI-13-C.8","title":"Metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) is hepatic steatosis, fat in at least 5% of hepatocytes, in a person with at least one cardiometabolic risk factor (overweight or central obesity, raised glucose or type 2 diabetes, hypertension, high triglycerides, or low HDL cholesterol) and no other cause, such as significant alcohol use. The 2023 nomenclature replaced nonalcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) and nonalcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH) with MASLD and MASH. It is the most common chronic liver disease, affecting roughly a quarter to a third of adults worldwide."},{"type":"p","text":"Insulin resistance fills the liver with fat from several sources. In resistant adipose tissue, insulin fails to suppress lipolysis, so free fatty acids flow to the liver. Compensatory hyperinsulinemia, through sterol regulatory element-binding protein 1c (SREBP-1c), keeps hepatic de novo lipogenesis running even while insulin fails to suppress glucose output, a pattern called selective hepatic insulin resistance. Dietary fructose, which enters glycolysis below the phosphofructokinase-1 control point, adds lipogenic substrate. Export of triglyceride as VLDL cannot keep pace, so fat accumulates."},{"type":"p","text":"Stored triglyceride is relatively inert; the injury comes from lipotoxic intermediates (free fatty acids, diacylglycerols, ceramides, and free cholesterol) that cause endoplasmic reticulum stress, mitochondrial production of reactive oxygen species, and hepatocyte apoptosis. Less adiponectin and more TNF from visceral fat, and bacterial products from the gut, add inflammation and activate stellate cells."},{"type":"p","text":"Simple steatosis is fat without significant inflammation or hepatocyte injury and rarely progresses. Steatohepatitis (MASH) adds lobular inflammation and hepatocyte ballooning, often with Mallory-Denk bodies and zone 3 pericellular fibrosis, so its histology can look like alcoholic hepatitis; the drinking history separates them. Fibrosis stage, not the amount of fat, best predicts liver-related death, though cardiovascular disease is the most common cause of death overall. MASH can progress to cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma, and carcinoma sometimes arises before cirrhosis. Aminotransferases are often normal or mildly raised with ALT above AST, the reverse of alcohol, until advanced fibrosis, when the ratio rises. Imaging detects fat but cannot separate steatosis from MASH; biopsy remains the reference standard."},{"type":"table","title":"Simple steatosis versus MASH","cols":["Feature","Simple steatosis","MASH"],"rows":[["Histology","Macrovesicular fat only","Fat plus lobular inflammation and ballooning, often zone 3 fibrosis"],["Hepatocyte injury","Absent or minimal","Present, from lipotoxicity"],["Aminotransferases","Often normal","Mildly raised, ALT above AST"],["Course","Rarely progresses","Can progress to cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma"]],"caption":"Fat alone is the substrate; inflammation, ballooning, and fibrosis mark the lesion that progresses."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-03-C09","obj":"GI-13-C.9","title":"Autoimmune hepatitis versus drug and toxin hepatitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Autoimmune hepatitis is chronic hepatitis in which T cells, poorly restrained by regulatory T cells, attack hepatocyte antigens. Women are affected about three to four times as often as men, often when young, and the disease associates with HLA-DR3 and HLA-DR4 and with other autoimmune diseases. The hallmarks are hypergammaglobulinemia with raised IgG, circulating autoantibodies, and interface hepatitis, in which lymphocytes and abundant plasma cells cross the limiting plate and kill periportal hepatocytes. Type 1, the common adult form, carries antinuclear antibody (ANA) and anti-smooth muscle (anti-actin) antibody; type 2, mainly in children, carries anti-liver-kidney microsomal 1 antibody, directed at CYP2D6. Aminotransferases rise more than ALP, a hepatocellular pattern. It ranges from silent to acute hepatitis, can progress to cirrhosis, and is diagnosed only after viral hepatitis, drugs, and Wilson disease are excluded."},{"type":"p","text":"Drug-induced liver injury is intrinsic or idiosyncratic. Intrinsic injury is dose dependent, predictable, and rapid, as with acetaminophen. Idiosyncratic injury affects few exposed people, tracks dose poorly, and appears after weeks to months, as with isoniazid. Where the toxic metabolite forms decides which zone dies."},{"type":"p","text":"At therapeutic doses most acetaminophen is glucuronidated or sulfated, and about 5 to 10% is oxidized, mainly by CYP2E1, to N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine (NAPQI), which glutathione conjugates at once. In overdose, conjugation saturates and more NAPQI forms; once glutathione falls to about 30% of normal, free NAPQI binds cysteine residues of mitochondrial proteins, causing oxidative stress, mitochondrial permeability transition, and necrosis. Zone 3 dies first because CYP2E1 is concentrated there and glutathione and oxygen are lowest. Aminotransferases climb into the thousands, peaking at about 72 to 96 hours. Chronic alcohol use (which induces CYP2E1 and depletes glutathione) and fasting lower the toxic dose. Supplying cysteine for glutathione synthesis (the basis of N-acetylcysteine) prevents injury, which confirms the mechanism."},{"type":"p","text":"Isoniazid is acetylated by N-acetyltransferase 2 (NAT2) to acetylisoniazid, then hydrolyzed to acetylhydrazine; direct hydrolysis yields hydrazine. Both are oxidized, partly by CYP2E1, to reactive intermediates. About 10 to 20% of people taking it develop mild, transient aminotransferase rises, while clinical hepatitis affects up to about 1%, more often with older age, daily alcohol use, and concurrent rifampin; most recent studies find higher risk in slow acetylators. Histology resembles acute viral hepatitis, with diffuse lobular injury and, in severe cases, bridging or submassive necrosis."},{"type":"table","title":"Autoimmune, acetaminophen, and isoniazid hepatitis","cols":["Feature","Autoimmune hepatitis","Acetaminophen","Isoniazid"],"rows":[["Mechanism","T cell attack on hepatocytes","Intrinsic: NAPQI after glutathione depletion","Idiosyncratic: reactive hydrazine metabolites"],["Timing","Chronic, may flare acutely","Within 1 to 3 days of overdose","Usually within the first months of use"],["Histology","Interface hepatitis with plasma cells","Zone 3 (centrilobular) coagulative necrosis","Acute hepatitis pattern, diffuse lobular injury"],["Key labs","High IgG, ANA, anti-smooth muscle or anti-LKM1 antibody","Aminotransferases often over 1,000 U/L","Hepatocellular pattern: ALT and AST rise more than ALP"],["Risk factors","Female sex, HLA-DR3 and DR4","Chronic alcohol use, fasting","Older age, alcohol, rifampin, slow acetylation"]],"caption":"Autoantibodies and plasma cells point to autoimmunity; dose, timing, and the zone of necrosis point to a drug."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-03-C10","obj":"GI-13-C.10","title":"Portal hypertension: site of obstruction and its consequences","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Portal venous pressure is normally about 5 to 10 mmHg, and the hepatic venous pressure gradient (HVPG, wedged minus free hepatic venous pressure, an estimate of sinusoidal pressure) is about 1 to 5 mmHg. Portal hypertension is an HVPG above 5 mmHg; varices and ascites appear at about 10 mmHg or more, and varices can bleed above about 12 mmHg. The pressure gradient equals flow times resistance, and cirrhosis raises both: resistance from fibrosis, nodules, and contracting stellate cells, and inflow from splanchnic vasodilation (C05)."},{"type":"p","text":"The site of obstruction predicts the liver and the ascites, because ascites needs raised sinusoidal pressure, which forces fluid into the space of Disse faster than lymphatics can return it. Portal vein thrombosis (prehepatic) leaves sinusoidal pressure normal, so splenomegaly and varices develop with a normal liver and little ascites. Schistosoma mansoni and S. japonicum eggs lodge in portal venules and provoke granulomas and periportal pipestem fibrosis, a presinusoidal intrahepatic block that spares hepatocyte function. Hepatic vein thrombosis (Budd-Chiari syndrome, often from polycythemia vera or another hypercoagulable state), right heart failure, and constrictive pericarditis are posthepatic: they congest zone 3, giving a tender, enlarged nutmeg liver and ascites. In Budd-Chiari syndrome the caudate lobe enlarges because its veins drain directly into the inferior vena cava, while raised jugular venous pressure points to a cardiac cause."},{"type":"table","title":"Portal hypertension by site","cols":["Site","Examples","Liver","Ascites"],"rows":[["Prehepatic","Portal vein thrombosis","Normal","Little or none"],["Intrahepatic, presinusoidal","Schistosomiasis","Periportal fibrosis, function preserved","Less prominent"],["Intrahepatic, sinusoidal","Cirrhosis (most common cause)","Fibrosis and nodules","Yes, low protein"],["Posthepatic","Budd-Chiari syndrome, right heart failure, constrictive pericarditis","Enlarged, congested nutmeg liver","Yes, high protein"]],"caption":"Ascites tracks sinusoidal pressure, so it is prominent with sinusoidal and posthepatic blocks."},{"type":"p","text":"Raised pressure reroutes blood through portosystemic collaterals: left gastric to esophageal veins (esophageal varices), paraumbilical to superficial epigastric veins (caput medusae), and superior to middle and inferior rectal veins (anorectal varices). The congested spleen enlarges and sequesters platelets. Ascites from any portal hypertensive cause has a serum-ascites albumin gradient (SAAG) of 1.1 g/dL or more."},{"type":"example","title":"Using ascitic fluid to locate the block","text":"Two patients have ascites with a SAAG of 1.8 g/dL. Ascitic total protein is 1.2 g/dL in patient A and 3.6 g/dL in patient B. A SAAG of 1.1 g/dL or more means portal hypertension in both. In cirrhosis, capillarized sinusoids hold back protein, so the fluid is protein-poor (under 2.5 g/dL), which fits patient A. In posthepatic congestion the sinusoids are still fenestrated, so protein-rich lymph leaks out (2.5 g/dL or more), which fits patient B. Raised jugular venous pressure would point B toward the heart; normal neck veins with an enlarged caudate lobe would point to Budd-Chiari syndrome. A SAAG under 1.1 g/dL would instead suggest a peritoneal cause such as carcinomatosis or tuberculosis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-03-C11","obj":"GI-13-C.11","title":"Dubin-Johnson and Rotor syndromes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Conjugated bilirubin leaves the hepatocyte across the canalicular membrane through MRP2, the rate-limiting step of bilirubin excretion. Some conjugate is also exported to sinusoidal blood by MRP3 on the basolateral membrane and taken back up by downstream hepatocytes through OATP1B1 and OATP1B3, a shuttle that spreads the excretory load across the lobule. Dubin-Johnson and Rotor syndromes each break one part of this circuit, so both raise conjugated bilirubin without hepatocyte injury, and because conjugated bilirubin is filtered, both darken the urine."},{"type":"p","text":"Dubin-Johnson syndrome is autosomal recessive loss of functional MRP2 (gene ABCC2). Conjugated bilirubin cannot enter bile and refluxes into blood through MRP3, giving a mainly conjugated hyperbilirubinemia, usually about 2 to 5 mg/dL, that worsens in pregnancy and with estrogen-containing contraceptives, which further reduce canalicular excretion. Bile acids use a different pump, BSEP, so their secretion is normal: there is no pruritus, and ALP, GGT, and aminotransferases are normal. A dark pigment, thought to derive from retained anionic metabolites such as those of epinephrine, collects in lysosomes of centrilobular hepatocytes and turns the liver black. Total urinary coproporphyrin is normal, but over about 80% is isomer I instead of the usual minority."},{"type":"p","text":"Rotor syndrome is autosomal recessive combined deficiency of OATP1B1 and OATP1B3 (genes SLCO1B1 and SLCO1B3). Conjugated bilirubin exported to blood by MRP3 cannot be taken back up, so it accumulates in plasma. Canalicular secretion is intact and no pigment is stored, so the liver is normal in color. Total urinary coproporphyrin is raised, with isomer I a smaller majority. Both syndromes are benign."},{"type":"table","title":"Dubin-Johnson and Rotor syndromes compared","cols":["Feature","Dubin-Johnson syndrome","Rotor syndrome"],"rows":[["Defect","Canalicular secretion (MRP2, ABCC2)","Sinusoidal reuptake (OATP1B1 and OATP1B3)"],["Bilirubin","Mainly conjugated, worse with pregnancy or estrogen","Mainly conjugated"],["Liver","Black, lysosomal pigment in centrilobular hepatocytes","Normal color"],["Urinary coproporphyrin","Total normal; over about 80% isomer I","Total raised; isomer I a smaller majority"],["ALP, GGT, bile acids","Normal","Normal"],["Course","Benign","Benign, often milder"]],"caption":"Normal ALP, GGT, and bile acids separate both from cholestasis; liver color and coproporphyrin pattern separate them from each other."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a patient with Dubin-Johnson syndrome have dark urine but no itching, while a patient with a common bile duct stone has both?","explain":"Both have filtered conjugated bilirubin, which darkens the urine. In Dubin-Johnson syndrome only MRP2 fails, so BSEP still secretes bile acids and they do not accumulate. A duct stone blocks every bile constituent, so bile acids back up into blood and cause pruritus.","check":"The transporter paragraph here and the laboratory paragraph in C01."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and physiology set the pattern of disease. The lobule's zonation explains why alcohol, acetaminophen, and venous congestion injure zone 3 first, where CYP2E1 is concentrated and oxygen is lowest, while interface hepatitis begins at the periportal limiting plate. The space of Disse explains why stellate cell collagen impairs function before nodules form, and the portal circulation explains why fibrosis anywhere along it produces varices, splenomegaly, and ascites, with the site of block predicting liver appearance and ascitic protein. Biochemistry explains the bilirubin disorders, because the step that fails (conjugation by UGT1A1, canalicular export by MRP2, or reuptake by OATP1B1 and OATP1B3) predicts which fraction rises and whether bile acids follow."},{"type":"p","text":"In immunology, antimitochondrial antibody, p-ANCA, ANA, and anti-smooth muscle antibody each point to a distinct immune process, but none is diagnostic alone, and drug injury and viral hepatitis must be excluded. Cirrhosis is a common end stage whose consequences extend to the kidney, lung, brain, and endocrine system through vasodilation, shunting, and lost hepatocyte function. Laboratory ratios such as AST to ALT are probabilistic clues rather than proof, and histologic features such as Mallory-Denk bodies overlap among alcohol, MASH, and cholestasis, so each finding is read with the history."}],"summary":["Intrahepatic cholestasis (drugs, pregnancy, TPN, sepsis, PBC) leaves ducts normal in caliber; extrahepatic obstruction dilates ducts above the block.","Cholestasis raises conjugated bilirubin, ALP, and GGT, and causes pruritus, pale stools, dark urine, xanthomas, and vitamin K-correctable PT prolongation.","PBC destroys small intrahepatic ducts in middle-aged women with antimitochondrial antibody; PSC fibroses large and small ducts with beading, ulcerative colitis, and cholangiocarcinoma risk.","Activated stellate cells lay collagen in the space of Disse, capillarize sinusoids, and with bridging septa and regenerative nodules create cirrhosis.","Hepatocellular failure causes mixed jaundice, vitamin K-resistant coagulopathy, hypoalbuminemia, hyperestrogenism, and ammonia-driven encephalopathy with asterixis.","Splanchnic nitric oxide lowers effective arterial volume, causing hepatorenal syndrome with low urine sodium and normal kidneys; pulmonary capillary dilation causes hepatopulmonary hypoxemia with orthodeoxia.","Gilbert and Crigler-Najjar syndromes raise unconjugated bilirubin from reduced or absent UGT1A1; phenobarbital response separates type II from type I.","Alcohol produces steatosis, then hepatitis with ballooning, Mallory-Denk bodies, and neutrophils, then cirrhosis, with AST more than twice ALT; MASLD usually shows ALT above AST.","Autoimmune hepatitis shows high IgG and plasma cell interface hepatitis; acetaminophen causes zone 3 necrosis after glutathione depletion; isoniazid hepatitis is idiosyncratic.","Portal hypertension is prehepatic, intrahepatic, or posthepatic; Dubin-Johnson (MRP2, black liver) and Rotor (OATP1B, normal liver) raise conjugated bilirubin with normal ALP and bile acids."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Liver and Gallbladder"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Liver Disease"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Liver"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Hepatobiliary Function"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Heme degradation and bilirubin metabolism; ethanol metabolism"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Organs Associated with the Digestive Tract, liver"}]},"GI-13-D":{"unit":"GI-13-D","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-04","title":"Gallbladder Disease: Gallstones, Cholecystitis, and Their Complications","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains why gallstones form, why they come in three chemical types, and why the disease a stone causes depends on where it lodges. It covers acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis, chronic cholecystitis with porcelain gallbladder and its link to adenocarcinoma, and the complications of a moving stone: biliary colic, choledocholithiasis, ascending cholangitis, gallstone pancreatitis, gallstone ileus, and Mirizzi syndrome. Board questions give the pain pattern, temperature, white cell count, bilirubin, alkaline phosphatase, and lipase and ask you to place the stone. If you know which duct is blocked and whether bacteria are present, each pattern follows from the anatomy.","prerequisites":["Anatomy of the gallbladder, cystic duct, common hepatic duct, common bile duct, and ampulla of Vater","Bile composition and enterohepatic circulation of bile acids (GI-6)","Heme breakdown, bilirubin conjugation, and the conjugated versus unconjugated distinction","Cholecystokinin release and its actions on the gallbladder and sphincter of Oddi","Acute and chronic inflammation, dystrophic calcification, and metaplasia"],"connections":[{"to":"Hepatic disease and jaundice (GI-13-C)","how":"A duct stone causes conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with a cholestatic enzyme pattern, which must be separated from hepatocellular and hemolytic jaundice."},{"to":"Exocrine pancreas and acute pancreatitis (GI-13-F)","how":"A stone at the ampulla blocks pancreatic drainage, making gallstones a leading cause of acute pancreatitis."},{"to":"Hematology: hemolytic anemias","how":"Chronic hemolysis in sickle cell disease and hereditary spherocytosis raises the bilirubin load and produces black pigment stones, often in young patients."},{"to":"Microbiology: Enterobacteriaceae and liver flukes","how":"Enteric gram-negative rods infect obstructed bile, and flukes such as Clonorchis sinensis seed brown pigment stones in the ducts."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal neoplasia (GI-13-K)","how":"Long-standing stones and porcelain gallbladder are the main risk factors for gallbladder adenocarcinoma."}],"terms":[{"term":"Cholelithiasis","def":"The presence of stones in the gallbladder."},{"term":"Choledocholithiasis","def":"The presence of stones in the common bile duct, either passed from the gallbladder or formed in the duct."},{"term":"Cholesterol saturation","def":"The ratio of cholesterol in bile to the amount that bile salts and phospholipid can hold in solution; above 1, bile is supersaturated and crystals can form."},{"term":"Mixed micelle","def":"A water-soluble aggregate of bile salts and phospholipid (mainly lecithin) that carries cholesterol in bile."},{"term":"Biliary sludge","def":"A gel of mucin, cholesterol crystals, and calcium bilirubinate that forms in a stagnant gallbladder and can precede stones."},{"term":"Calcium bilirubinate","def":"The insoluble calcium salt of unconjugated bilirubin that forms the bulk of pigment stones."},{"term":"Biliary colic","def":"Steady visceral pain from transient cystic duct obstruction, lasting less than about 6 hours, without inflammation."},{"term":"Murphy sign","def":"Arrest of deep inspiration when the examiner presses below the right costal margin and the inflamed gallbladder descends against the fingers."},{"term":"Charcot triad","def":"Fever, jaundice, and right upper quadrant pain, the classic presentation of ascending cholangitis."},{"term":"Porcelain gallbladder","def":"A gallbladder whose scarred wall is calcified, typically after chronic cholecystitis."},{"term":"Cholecystoenteric fistula","def":"An abnormal channel between the gallbladder and the bowel, most often the duodenum, formed when a stone erodes through adherent inflamed walls."},{"term":"Hartmann pouch","def":"A bulge at the neck of the gallbladder where stones often impact."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-04-C01","obj":"GI-13-D.1","title":"Acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Between meals the gallbladder stores hepatic bile and concentrates it, typically about fivefold, by absorbing Na+, Cl-, and water through its simple columnar epithelium. When fatty acids and amino acids enter the duodenum, I cells release cholecystokinin (CCK), which contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi. The wall has mucosa, a thin smooth muscle layer, perimuscular connective tissue, and serosa, with no muscularis mucosae or submucosa, and it is supplied mainly by the cystic artery."},{"type":"p","text":"About 90% of acute cholecystitis is calculous, meaning it begins when a stone impacts in the cystic duct or gallbladder neck and stays there. Obstruction alone does not explain the inflammation, because the first injury is chemical: mucosal phospholipases convert lecithin in the trapped bile into lysolecithin, which damages the protective mucus layer and exposes the epithelium to the detergent action of concentrated bile salts, while prostaglandins released in the wall amplify the inflammation. Rising pressure in the distended organ compromises wall perfusion. Bacterial infection with enteric organisms such as Escherichia coli, Klebsiella, and enterococci usually follows rather than starts the process."},{"type":"flow","title":"Acute calculous cholecystitis","steps":["Stone impacts in cystic duct or gallbladder neck","Trapped bile distends the gallbladder","Phospholipase converts lecithin to toxic lysolecithin","Mucus barrier lost; prostaglandins drive wall inflammation","Rising pressure reduces blood flow to the wall","Enteric bacteria secondarily infect the ischemic wall"],"caption":"Chemical injury and ischemia come first; infection is a later step that drives empyema, gangrene, and perforation."},{"type":"p","text":"Each finding has a mechanism. Pain starts as poorly localized epigastric visceral pain carried by afferents entering the cord at about T6 to T9, then localizes to the right upper quadrant once the inflamed serosa touches parietal peritoneum, and it lasts longer than about 6 hours. It may be referred to the right scapula, or to the right shoulder when the diaphragm is irritated (phrenic nerve, C3 to C5). Cytokines such as IL-1, IL-6, and TNF produce fever, often above 38 °C (100.4 °F), and a neutrophilic leukocytosis, commonly about 12,000 to 15,000/µL. In the Murphy sign, deep inspiration pushes the inflamed gallbladder down onto the examiner's fingers and the patient stops inhaling. Bilirubin and alkaline phosphatase (ALP) are normal or only mildly raised from local edema; marked jaundice points to a duct stone or Mirizzi syndrome."},{"type":"p","text":"Imaging findings follow the same logic. Ultrasound shows the stone, a thickened wall, and pericholecystic fluid. In cholescintigraphy (HIDA scan), hepatocytes excrete a radiotracer into bile that reaches the duodenum but never fills the gallbladder because the cystic duct is blocked. Complications include empyema, gangrene, perforation with abscess, and emphysematous cholecystitis from gas-forming organisms such as Clostridium species, seen more often in people with diabetes."},{"type":"table","title":"Calculous versus acalculous cholecystitis","cols":["Feature","Calculous","Acalculous"],"rows":[["Share of cases","About 90%","About 5 to 10%"],["Trigger","Persistent cystic duct impaction","Stasis and ischemia without a stone"],["Typical patient","Ambulatory adult with known stones","Critically ill: sepsis, shock, burns, major trauma or surgery, prolonged fasting or TPN, mechanical ventilation"],["Mechanism","Lysolecithin, prostaglandins, distension, then infection","No CCK stimulus, so bile stagnates and concentrates; hypoperfusion injures the wall"],["Course","Often settles if the stone disimpacts","Gangrene and perforation more frequent; mortality higher"]],"caption":"Acalculous disease is suspected when a critically ill patient develops fever, leukocytosis, or unexplained sepsis with a distended, thick-walled gallbladder and no stones."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with acute cholecystitis has a total bilirubin of 6 mg/dL and an ALP three times normal. Is the cystic duct stone enough to explain this?","explain":"No. A cystic duct stone isolates only the gallbladder, while hepatic bile still drains through the common hepatic and common bile ducts, so marked cholestasis requires obstruction of the main duct: a common duct stone, cholangitis, or Mirizzi compression.","check":"The laboratory paragraph above and the location table in C04."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-04-C02","obj":"GI-13-D.2","title":"Chronic cholecystitis, porcelain gallbladder, and gallbladder adenocarcinoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Chronic cholecystitis is almost always associated with gallstones. It may follow repeated bouts of acute cholecystitis or develop gradually without a clear acute episode, and supersaturated bile itself probably contributes to mucosal injury. Bacteria, usually E. coli and enterococci, are cultured from the bile in only about one third of cases, so infection is not the main driver."},{"type":"p","text":"The pathology reflects long-term injury and repair. The wall is thickened by fibrosis and a mononuclear (lymphocyte and plasma cell) infiltrate, and the gallbladder may be contracted. Outpouchings of mucosal epithelium push through the muscle layer as Rokitansky-Aschoff sinuses. Because the scarred wall contracts and empties poorly, patients describe recurrent biliary colic or vague postprandial right upper quadrant or epigastric discomfort rather than the fever and leukocytosis of acute disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Porcelain gallbladder is dystrophic calcification of this chronically inflamed, scarred wall. Dystrophic means calcium is deposited in damaged tissue while serum calcium is normal, the same process that calcifies old granulomas or scarred heart valves. On a plain radiograph or CT, the calcium outlines the gallbladder as a rim or stippled shell, and the organ often barely functions."},{"type":"flow","title":"From stones to gallbladder adenocarcinoma","steps":["Long-standing stones irritate the mucosa","Repeated inflammation and repair","Fibrosis and mural thickening","Dystrophic calcification: porcelain gallbladder","Metaplasia, then dysplasia, in chronically injured epithelium","Invasive adenocarcinoma"],"caption":"Calcification and malignant change are separate outcomes of the same chronic injury; porcelain gallbladder marks that injury rather than causing cancer by itself."},{"type":"p","text":"Gallbladder adenocarcinoma is the most common malignancy of the extrahepatic biliary tract. It is more common in women and in older adults, with high rates in some Native American, Chilean, and northern Indian populations, and most patients have gallstones. Chronic irritation drives a metaplasia, dysplasia, and carcinoma sequence. Porcelain gallbladder raises the risk, although modern series report a lower risk than older studies, and selective (incomplete) mucosal calcification appears riskier than diffuse calcification of the muscle layer. Other recognized risks include an anomalous pancreaticobiliary duct junction, which allows pancreatic juice to reflux into the bile, and chronic Salmonella Typhi carriage."},{"type":"p","text":"The tumor is usually silent until advanced, because early disease produces few symptoms or mimics ordinary biliary pain. The thin wall without a submucosa, and the hepatic surface that lies against the liver without serosa, allow early direct invasion of the liver. Spread to the cystic and hepatic hilar nodes and invasion of the common hepatic duct then cause obstructive jaundice, weight loss, and a right upper quadrant mass, which is why prognosis is poor."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-04-C03","obj":"GI-13-D.3","title":"Cholesterol, black pigment, and brown pigment stones","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hepatic bile carries three lipids. Bile salts are secreted by the bile salt export pump (BSEP, ABCB11), phosphatidylcholine (lecithin) by the MDR3 flippase (ABCB4), and cholesterol by the ABCG5/ABCG8 transporter. Cholesterol is insoluble in water and stays dissolved only inside mixed micelles of bile salts and lecithin, or in lecithin vesicles. In Western countries about 80% of stones are cholesterol stones."},{"type":"p","text":"Cholesterol stones need four conditions acting together. First, bile becomes supersaturated because the liver secretes too much cholesterol or too few bile salts or phospholipid. Second, excess cholesterol leaves unstable vesicles that fuse and nucleate cholesterol monohydrate crystals. Third, gallbladder mucin forms a gel that traps the crystals. Fourth, gallbladder hypomotility keeps them in place long enough to grow. Supersaturation alone is common and harmless if the gallbladder empties well, which is why stasis matters so much."},{"type":"flow","title":"Cholesterol stone formation","steps":["More cholesterol or fewer bile salts and lecithin secreted","Bile becomes supersaturated with cholesterol","Unstable vesicles nucleate cholesterol monohydrate crystals","Mucin gel traps crystals as sludge","Hypomotile gallbladder retains them","Crystals aggregate into stones"],"caption":"Supersaturation, nucleation, mucus hypersecretion, and stasis must coincide; any one alone rarely makes a stone."},{"type":"table","title":"Cholesterol stone risk factors and their mechanisms","cols":["Risk factor","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Female sex, estrogen, oral contraceptives","Estrogen increases hepatic cholesterol uptake and synthesis and its secretion into bile"],["Pregnancy","Estrogen raises biliary cholesterol; progesterone relaxes smooth muscle and slows emptying"],["Obesity and insulin resistance","Increased hepatic cholesterol synthesis and biliary secretion"],["Rapid weight loss, prolonged fasting, TPN","Mobilized cholesterol enters bile; little fat intake means little CCK, so the gallbladder stagnates"],["Older age","Biliary cholesterol secretion rises and the bile acid pool shrinks"],["Fibrates","Inhibit cholesterol 7-alpha-hydroxylase, so less cholesterol becomes bile acid and more is secreted unchanged"],["Ancestry (some Native American and Mexican American groups) and ABCG8 variants","Inherited increase in biliary cholesterol secretion"]],"caption":"Every risk factor either raises cholesterol saturation, lowers bile salt or phospholipid output, or slows gallbladder emptying."},{"type":"p","text":"Pigment stones are made of calcium bilirubinate. Normal bile contains bilirubin mostly as water-soluble glucuronides, with only a little unconjugated bilirubin. Anything that raises unconjugated bilirubin in bile lets it bind calcium and precipitate."},{"type":"p","text":"Black pigment stones form in sterile gallbladder bile when the bilirubin load is high, as in chronic hemolysis (sickle cell disease, hereditary spherocytosis, thalassemia, mechanical heart valves) and cirrhosis. Gallstones in a child or young adult should prompt a search for hemolysis. Brown pigment stones form in infected ducts, including intrahepatic ducts. Bacterial beta-glucuronidase from E. coli and other enteric organisms deconjugates bilirubin glucuronides, and bacterial phospholipase releases fatty acids from lecithin, which form calcium soaps. Biliary parasites such as Clonorchis sinensis and Ascaris lumbricoides predispose to infection, so brown stones are more common in East Asia, and they can form in the ducts after cholecystectomy."},{"type":"table","title":"The three stone types","cols":["Feature","Cholesterol","Black pigment","Brown pigment"],"rows":[["Main content","Cholesterol monohydrate","Polymerized calcium bilirubinate","Calcium bilirubinate with calcium soaps of fatty acids"],["Where formed","Gallbladder","Gallbladder (sterile bile)","Bile ducts, including intrahepatic"],["Key cause","Supersaturation plus stasis","Hemolysis or cirrhosis","Infection or parasites"],["Appearance","Pale yellow, hard, often faceted","Small, black, often numerous","Soft, greasy, brown"],["On plain radiograph","Usually radiolucent; about 10 to 20% radiopaque","About 50 to 75% radiopaque","Usually radiolucent"]],"caption":"Black stones follow blood breakdown; brown stones follow bacteria in the ducts."},{"type":"p","text":"Ileal disease or resection, as in Crohn disease, also raises gallstone risk. Robbins groups it with pigment stones, because unabsorbed bile acids reaching the colon solubilize unconjugated bilirubin and increase its enterohepatic return; review books often list it under cholesterol stones, because a smaller bile salt pool favors supersaturation."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-04-C04","obj":"GI-13-D.4","title":"Where the stone lodges decides the disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Most gallstones never cause symptoms; when they do, ask which duct is blocked and whether bacteria are present. A cystic duct block isolates only the gallbladder, so bilirubin stays normal. A common bile duct block stops all bile flow, so conjugated bilirubin and ALP rise. Infection behind an obstruction adds fever and the risk of sepsis."},{"type":"table","title":"Gallstone complications by location","cols":["Stone location","Condition","Mechanism","Key findings"],"rows":[["Cystic duct, transient","Biliary colic","Gallbladder contracts against a stone after a fatty meal; no inflammation","Steady (not colicky) pain under about 6 hours; normal labs"],["Cystic duct, persistent","Acute cholecystitis","Chemical then bacterial inflammation (C01)","Pain over 6 hours, fever, Murphy sign, leukocytosis"],["Common bile duct","Choledocholithiasis","Whole biliary tree obstructed","Jaundice, high conjugated bilirubin, ALP, and GGT"],["Common bile duct plus infection","Ascending cholangitis","Infected stagnant bile under pressure","Charcot triad; Reynolds pentad adds hypotension and confusion"],["Ampulla of Vater","Gallstone pancreatitis","Pancreatic outflow blocked","Epigastric pain radiating to the back; lipase over 3 times normal"],["Bowel, via fistula","Gallstone ileus","Stone erodes into duodenum, lodges in terminal ileum","Small bowel obstruction, air in the biliary tree"]],"caption":"Each added finding moves the diagnosis: pain alone, pain with fever, pain with jaundice, then all three."},{"type":"p","text":"Duct stones usually pass from the gallbladder, although brown stones can form in the duct itself. Water-soluble conjugated bilirubin spills into urine and darkens it, little reaches the gut, so stools pale, and retained bile salts cause pruritus. Prolonged obstruction impairs vitamin K absorption and prolongs the prothrombin time. Aminotransferases may spike briefly after acute obstruction, unlike the sustained rise of hepatitis. A scarred, stone-filled gallbladder rarely distends, so a palpable nontender gallbladder with jaundice (Courvoisier sign) suggests a tumor of the pancreatic head or distal bile duct instead."},{"type":"flow","title":"Ascending cholangitis","steps":["Stone obstructs the common bile duct","Bile stagnates; intraductal pressure rises","Enteric bacteria ascend and multiply","Pressure drives bacteria and endotoxin into the blood","Bacteremia: fever, jaundice, right upper quadrant pain","Septic shock, confusion, or liver abscess"],"caption":"Obstruction, infection, and pressure together; usual organisms are E. coli, Klebsiella, Enterococcus, and anaerobes such as Bacteroides."},{"type":"p","text":"Gallstone pancreatitis follows small stones or sludge that pass the cystic duct and obstruct the ampulla, where the bile and pancreatic ducts usually share a channel, so small stones are riskier than large ones. Raised pancreatic duct pressure lets trypsinogen activate within acinar cells, starting autodigestion. An early rise in ALT favors a biliary over an alcoholic cause."},{"type":"p","text":"Gallstone ileus, despite its name, is a mechanical obstruction, typically in older women. Chronic inflammation fuses the gallbladder to the duodenum, and a large stone (usually over about 2.5 cm) erodes through as a cholecystoduodenal fistula, then lodges where the small bowel is narrowest, the terminal ileum near the ileocecal valve. Air entering through the fistula outlines the bile ducts (pneumobilia); a stone impacted in the duodenum or pylorus instead causes gastric outlet obstruction (Bouveret syndrome)."},{"type":"example","title":"Placing the stone from the numbers","text":"A 58-year-old woman has 2 days of right upper quadrant pain. Temperature is 39.2 °C (102.6 °F), WBC 17,000/µL, total bilirubin 4.8 mg/dL (reference about 0.3 to 1.2), mostly conjugated, ALP 380 U/L (reference about 40 to 130), lipase normal. Step 1: days of pain with fever and leukocytosis means inflammation, not colic. Step 2: conjugated bilirubin and ALP several times normal mean the main duct is obstructed, which a cystic duct stone cannot do. Step 3: obstruction plus fever and pain completes Charcot triad, so the answer is ascending cholangitis from choledocholithiasis. Step 4: normal lipase argues against pancreatitis. Added hypotension and confusion would make it Reynolds pentad."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does gallstone ileus produce air in the bile ducts, while choledocholithiasis does not?","explain":"The fistula opens the gallbladder to the gas-filled duodenum, so air refluxes into the ducts; in choledocholithiasis the sphincter of Oddi and the stone keep bowel gas out.","check":"The gallstone ileus paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-04-C05","obj":"GI-13-D.5","title":"Mirizzi syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The cystic duct joins the common hepatic duct to form the common bile duct. The cystic duct, common hepatic duct, and inferior surface of the liver bound the hepatocystic triangle (Calot triangle), which usually contains the cystic artery. In some people the cystic duct runs parallel to the common hepatic duct for a distance or joins it low, so the two share a wall and a common connective tissue sheath."},{"type":"p","text":"Mirizzi syndrome occurs when a stone impacted in the gallbladder neck (Hartmann pouch) or cystic duct, together with the inflammation around it, compresses the common hepatic duct from outside. No stone needs to be inside the main duct, yet hepatic bile cannot pass, so the patient develops the same conjugated hyperbilirubinemia, raised ALP and GGT, dark urine, and pale stools as in choledocholithiasis, often with the pain and fever of cholecystitis. Over time, pressure necrosis can erode the duct wall and create a cholecystobiliary (cholecystocholedochal) fistula, and the condition is graded by whether such a fistula has formed and how much of the duct wall it involves."},{"type":"flow","title":"Mirizzi syndrome","steps":["Large stone impacts in Hartmann pouch or cystic duct","Stone and surrounding inflammation press on common hepatic duct","Extrinsic narrowing blocks hepatic bile flow","Ducts above dilate; common bile duct below stays normal","Conjugated hyperbilirubinemia and cholestatic enzyme pattern","Continued pressure may erode into the duct as a fistula"],"caption":"The obstruction lies outside the duct, which explains why the duct below the compression is normal."},{"type":"p","text":"On imaging, the ducts above the compression are dilated, the common bile duct below is normal, and the stone sits in the gallbladder neck rather than in the duct. Because an extrinsic narrowing at the hepatic hilum can look like a tumor, Mirizzi syndrome is an important mimic of cholangiocarcinoma and gallbladder carcinoma. Stagnant bile above the narrowing can also become infected and cause cholangitis."},{"type":"table","title":"Telling Mirizzi syndrome from its look-alikes","cols":["Feature","Acute cholecystitis","Choledocholithiasis","Mirizzi syndrome"],"rows":[["Stone position","Cystic duct or neck","Inside the common bile duct","Neck or cystic duct, pressing on common hepatic duct"],["Jaundice","Absent or mild","Present","Present"],["Ducts above obstruction","Not dilated","Dilated, including common bile duct above the stone","Intrahepatic and common hepatic ducts dilated"],["Common bile duct below","Normal","Contains the stone","Normal caliber"],["Gallbladder inflammation","Yes","Not required","Usually yes"]],"caption":"Mirizzi combines the stone position of cholecystitis with the laboratory pattern of a duct stone."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Biochemistry sets the stone type: cholesterol stones reflect the balance of ABC transporters, bile acid synthesis by 7-alpha-hydroxylase, and the micelle, while pigment stones reflect heme turnover and whether bilirubin stays conjugated. Hematology and microbiology decide which pigment stone forms, black with hemolysis and brown with enteric bacteria or flukes. Physiology explains timing, because CCK released by a fatty meal contracts the gallbladder against a stone and fasting removes that stimulus and breeds sludge."},{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy then decides the clinical picture: cystic duct, common bile duct, ampulla, Calot triangle, and the duodenum next to the gallbladder predict colic, cholecystitis, jaundice, pancreatitis, Mirizzi syndrome, and gallstone ileus. Pathology supplies the outcomes of chemical injury, ischemia, fibrosis, dystrophic calcification, and carcinoma. These patterns have limits. Labs overlap, a stone can pass before testing and leave only a transient enzyme rise, and acalculous disease reminds you that stasis and ischemia can inflame the gallbladder without any stone."}],"summary":["Cholesterol stones need supersaturated bile, crystal nucleation, trapping mucin, and a hypomotile gallbladder; estrogen, pregnancy, obesity, rapid weight loss, fasting, and fibrates act on one or more of these.","Black pigment stones form in sterile gallbladder bile with hemolysis or cirrhosis; brown pigment stones form in infected ducts through bacterial beta-glucuronidase.","Acute calculous cholecystitis begins as chemical injury from lysolecithin and prostaglandins behind an impacted cystic duct stone, with ischemia and secondary infection following.","Pain over about 6 hours with fever, leukocytosis, and Murphy sign means cholecystitis; the same pain under 6 hours with normal labs means biliary colic.","Acalculous cholecystitis arises from stasis and hypoperfusion in critically ill patients and more often progresses to gangrene and perforation.","Chronic cholecystitis causes a fibrotic wall with Rokitansky-Aschoff sinuses; dystrophic calcification produces porcelain gallbladder, which together with long-standing stones is linked to gallbladder adenocarcinoma.","A cystic duct stone leaves bilirubin normal; a common bile duct stone raises conjugated bilirubin and ALP, and with infection produces Charcot triad or Reynolds pentad.","Small stones at the ampulla cause gallstone pancreatitis; a large stone through a cholecystoduodenal fistula causes gallstone ileus at the terminal ileum with pneumobilia.","Mirizzi syndrome is extrinsic compression of the common hepatic duct by a stone in the gallbladder neck or cystic duct, giving obstructive jaundice with a normal common bile duct below."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Liver and Gallbladder, section on disorders of the gallbladder (cholelithiasis, cholecystitis, carcinoma)"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Secretory Functions of the Alimentary Tract, section on secretion of bile by the liver and functions of the biliary tree"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Cholesterol, Lipoprotein, and Steroid Metabolism, section on bile acids and cholelithiasis"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen, section on the biliary ducts and gallbladder"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Organs Associated with the Digestive Tract, section on the gallbladder"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Enteric Gram-Negative Rods (Enterobacteriaceae)"}]},"GI-13-E":{"unit":"GI-13-E","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-05","title":"Deficiency and Malabsorption Conditions","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains why nutrients fail to reach the blood when the stomach stops making acid, when the small intestinal mucosa is damaged, when digestive enzymes are missing, when bowel is removed, and when bacteria colonize a stagnant loop. Each condition is a break at a predictable step: luminal digestion, brush border digestion, mucosal uptake, or ileal recycling of bile acids and vitamin B12. Board questions give the stool, the anemia, the vitamin pattern, and one or two tests (D-xylose, fecal elastase, breath hydrogen, gastrin, folate) and ask you to name the step that failed. If you can place a disorder on that sequence, its findings follow.","prerequisites":["Gastric parietal, chief, G, D, and ECL cells and the control of acid secretion","Luminal and brush border digestion of carbohydrate, protein, and fat (GI-6)","Micelle formation, enterohepatic circulation of bile acids, and sites of nutrient absorption (GI-7)","Iron, folate, and vitamin B12 metabolism and the megaloblastic anemias","MHC class II antigen presentation and CD4 and CD8 T cell effector functions"],"connections":[{"to":"Immune responses in GI disease (GI-11)","how":"Celiac disease and autoimmune gastritis are T cell driven diseases whose antibodies serve as diagnostic markers rather than the main cause of injury."},{"to":"Exocrine pancreas and chronic pancreatitis (GI-13-B)","how":"Loss of pancreatic enzyme output is the main luminal cause of steatorrhea and is separated from mucosal disease by fecal elastase and D-xylose."},{"to":"Hematology","how":"The type of anemia (microcytic from iron, macrocytic from B12 or folate) points to the segment of bowel or the gastric function that has failed."},{"to":"Renal pathology","how":"Fat malabsorption with an intact colon raises oxalate absorption and produces calcium oxalate kidney stones."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal neoplasia","how":"Hypergastrinemia drives ECL cell carcinoid tumors, and chronic celiac inflammation raises the risk of enteropathy-associated T cell lymphoma."}],"terms":[{"term":"Achlorhydria","def":"Absence of hydrochloric acid secretion by the stomach, usually from loss of parietal cells."},{"term":"Hypergastrinemia","def":"A raised serum gastrin level, appropriate when gastric acid is absent and inappropriate when it comes from a gastrinoma."},{"term":"Enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cell","def":"A histamine-secreting neuroendocrine cell of the gastric body and fundus whose growth is stimulated by gastrin."},{"term":"Malabsorption","def":"Defective digestion, mucosal uptake, or transport of nutrients, with loss of those nutrients in the stool."},{"term":"Steatorrhea","def":"Excess fat in the stool, classically more than about 7 g per day on a diet containing about 100 g of fat."},{"term":"Villous atrophy","def":"Shortening and flattening of small intestinal villi that reduces absorptive surface area."},{"term":"Intraepithelial lymphocyte (IEL)","def":"A T lymphocyte, mostly CD8 positive, that sits between enterocytes above the basement membrane."},{"term":"D-xylose absorption test","def":"Measurement of blood or urine xylose after an oral dose of this pentose, which is absorbed without digestion and so tests mucosal uptake."},{"term":"Fecal elastase","def":"A pancreatic protease that survives intestinal transit; a low stool level indicates reduced pancreatic exocrine output."},{"term":"Enteropeptidase","def":"A duodenal brush border protease, also called enterokinase, that converts trypsinogen to trypsin."},{"term":"Stool osmotic gap","def":"Measured stool osmolality (taken as about 290 mOsm/kg) minus twice the sum of stool Na+ and K+; a large gap means unabsorbed solute is holding water."},{"term":"Enteric hyperoxaluria","def":"Increased colonic absorption and urinary excretion of oxalate caused by fat malabsorption."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-05-C01","obj":"GI-13-E.1","title":"Achlorhydria: causes and predictable consequences","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Parietal cells in the oxyntic mucosa of the gastric body and fundus secrete H+ through the H+/K+ ATPase and also secrete intrinsic factor. Gastrin from antral G cells stimulates acid secretion, mostly indirectly by making ECL cells release histamine. Acid limits its own stimulus: when antral pH falls below about 3, D cells release somatostatin, which suppresses gastrin. Achlorhydria therefore removes a brake as well as an acid."},{"type":"p","text":"Autoimmune atrophic gastritis is the classic cause. CD4 T cells directed against the H+/K+ ATPase destroy parietal cells in the body and fundus, while the antrum, which has no parietal cells, is spared. Antibodies to parietal cells and to intrinsic factor are markers of the process. Chief cells are lost along with parietal cells, so serum pepsinogen I falls. Chronic H. pylori infection that spreads from the antrum into the body (pangastritis) causes multifocal atrophy with intestinal metaplasia and hypochlorhydria. Because the antrum is also inflamed, gastrin rises less than in autoimmune disease. Gastrectomy removes parietal cells directly; a distal resection also removes the antrum and its G cells, so gastrin does not rise."},{"type":"flow","title":"Autoimmune gastritis to gastric carcinoid","steps":["T cells destroy parietal cells in body and fundus","Acid secretion fails; antral pH stays high","D cells release little somatostatin","Antral G cells secrete gastrin without restraint","Gastrin stimulates ECL cell hyperplasia","ECL hyperplasia can progress to type 1 gastric carcinoid"],"caption":"The antrum is spared, so the loss of acid feedback produces sustained hypergastrinemia, which is trophic to ECL cells."},{"type":"p","text":"Gastrin is a growth factor for ECL cells. Sustained hypergastrinemia produces linear and then nodular ECL cell hyperplasia, which can progress to type 1 gastric neuroendocrine (carcinoid) tumors, usually small and multiple. Atrophic gastritis with intestinal metaplasia also raises the risk of gastric adenocarcinoma. A high gastrin level means something different depending on gastric pH: with achlorhydria it is an appropriate response to absent acid, while with very low pH it suggests autonomous secretion by a gastrinoma."},{"type":"p","text":"The nutrient consequences follow from what acid does. Dietary nonheme iron is mostly ferric (Fe3+), which precipitates at neutral pH. Acid keeps it soluble and favors its reduction to ferrous iron (Fe2+) by duodenal cytochrome b, the form carried by DMT1, so achlorhydria causes iron deficiency. Acid and pepsin release vitamin B12 from food proteins so it can bind haptocorrin and later intrinsic factor. Achlorhydria alone causes food-bound B12 malabsorption; autoimmune gastritis adds loss of intrinsic factor, producing pernicious anemia. Acid also kills swallowed organisms, so its loss permits bacterial overgrowth in the stomach and proximal small bowel and increases susceptibility to enteric infection."},{"type":"table","title":"Consequences of achlorhydria","cols":["Finding","Mechanism","Most marked in"],"rows":[["Hypergastrinemia","No acid-driven somatostatin brake on antral G cells","Autoimmune gastritis (antrum spared)"],["ECL hyperplasia, type 1 carcinoid","Gastrin is trophic to ECL cells","Autoimmune gastritis"],["Low pepsinogen I","Chief cells lost with oxyntic mucosa","Body-predominant atrophy"],["Iron deficiency (microcytic)","Fe3+ not solubilized and reduced","Any cause"],["B12 deficiency (macrocytic)","B12 not freed from food; intrinsic factor lost in autoimmune disease","Autoimmune gastritis, total gastrectomy"],["Bacterial overgrowth, enteric infection","Loss of the gastric acid barrier","Any cause"]],"caption":"Distal gastrectomy removes the antrum, so it causes the nutrient findings without hypergastrinemia."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two patients have fasting gastrin levels many times normal. One has a gastric pH of 7, the other a pH of 1.5 with multiple duodenal ulcers. Which patient has ECL hyperplasia as a response to the gastrin, and which has a gastrin-secreting tumor?","explain":"Both can have ECL hyperplasia, because gastrin is trophic whatever its source. The patient with pH 7 has achlorhydria and appropriate gastrin release from intact antral G cells. The patient with pH 1.5 has inappropriate gastrin with acid hypersecretion, which points to a gastrinoma.","check":"The paragraph on interpreting gastrin against gastric pH."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-05-C02","obj":"GI-13-E.2","title":"Celiac disease: mucosal lesion and its consequences","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Celiac disease (gluten-sensitive enteropathy) is an immune-mediated enteropathy triggered by gluten proteins in wheat, barley, and rye in people who carry HLA-DQ2 or HLA-DQ8. Gliadin peptides are rich in proline and glutamine, which makes them resistant to gastric, pancreatic, and brush border proteases, so long fragments reach the lamina propria."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of celiac disease","steps":["Protease-resistant gliadin peptides cross the epithelium","Tissue transglutaminase deamidates glutamine to glutamate","Negatively charged peptides bind HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 tightly","Gluten-specific CD4 T cells release interferon gamma","IL-15 activates CD8 intraepithelial lymphocytes to kill enterocytes","Villous atrophy with crypt hyperplasia"],"caption":"Deamidation by tissue transglutaminase is the step that makes gluten strongly immunogenic in genetically susceptible people."},{"type":"p","text":"B cells that recognize tissue transglutaminase complexed with gliadin receive help from gluten-specific T cells, which explains why the disease produces IgA antibodies against a self enzyme. IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase and IgA antiendomysial antibodies (endomysial antigen is tissue transglutaminase) fall when gluten is removed. Selective IgA deficiency is more common in celiac disease and makes IgA tests falsely negative, so total IgA is measured."},{"type":"p","text":"The biopsy shows three linked changes. Intraepithelial lymphocytes increase first and are the most sensitive finding. Enterocytes are killed faster than they are replaced, so villi shorten and flatten (villous atrophy), and the crypts lengthen and show more mitoses (crypt hyperplasia) as they try to restore the epithelium. The lamina propria fills with plasma cells and lymphocytes. Injury is greatest in the distal duodenum and proximal jejunum, where gluten arrives at the highest concentration, and lessens distally."},{"type":"p","text":"That distribution explains the deficiencies. Iron, folate, and calcium are absorbed mainly in the duodenum and proximal jejunum, so iron deficiency anemia that does not respond to oral iron is a common presentation, often without diarrhea. Folate deficiency can make the anemia mixed, and calcium and vitamin D malabsorption cause osteopenia. B12, absorbed in the terminal ileum, is usually spared. Lost surface area causes diarrhea, steatorrhea, weight loss, bloating, and failure to thrive in children, and loss of tip enzymes causes secondary lactase deficiency. D-xylose absorption is low."},{"type":"table","title":"Extraintestinal findings of celiac disease","cols":["Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Dermatitis herpetiformis","IgA against epidermal transglutaminase deposits granularly in dermal papillae; neutrophil microabscesses and subepidermal vesicles that itch intensely on elbows, knees, and buttocks"],["Enteropathy-associated T cell lymphoma","Chronically stimulated intraepithelial T cells undergo clonal expansion; suspect it when symptoms return despite strict gluten avoidance"],["Small bowel adenocarcinoma","Chronic mucosal injury and repair"],["Associated autoimmune disease","Shared HLA risk: type 1 diabetes, autoimmune thyroiditis"]],"caption":"Dermatitis herpetiformis is the skin expression of gluten sensitivity and shares the transglutaminase-directed IgA response of the gut disease."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-05-C03","obj":"GI-13-E.3","title":"Pancreatic enzyme deficiency versus mucosal disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Absorption needs three phases in sequence. In the luminal phase, pancreatic enzymes and bile acids break down and solubilize nutrients. In the mucosal phase, brush border enzymes finish digestion and enterocytes take up the products. In the postabsorptive phase, chylomicrons and other products leave through lymph and blood. Malabsorption is classified by the phase that fails, and two tests separate the first two phases."},{"type":"p","text":"Pancreatic exocrine insufficiency (chronic pancreatitis, cystic fibrosis, ductal obstruction by tumor, or pancreatic resection) is a luminal phase failure. Lipase has almost no backup, so fat malabsorption appears once lipase output falls to roughly 10% of normal, producing bulky, greasy, foul-smelling stools, weight loss, and deficiencies of vitamins A, D, E, and K. Protein malabsorption (azotorrhea) accompanies it. Carbohydrate digestion is better preserved because salivary amylase and brush border enzymes take over. Pancreatic proteases normally free B12 from haptocorrin, so B12 absorption can also be impaired."},{"type":"p","text":"Fecal elastase tests the pancreas: it is secreted by acinar cells and is not degraded in transit, so a low stool level means low pancreatic output. D-xylose tests the mucosa: it is a pentose that needs no digestion and is absorbed in the proximal small bowel, so after an oral dose its blood and urine levels reflect mucosal uptake. It is normal in pancreatic insufficiency and low in mucosal disease such as celiac disease. A low result can also come from bacterial overgrowth (bacteria consume xylose), slow gastric emptying, or reduced renal excretion."},{"type":"table","title":"Separating luminal from mucosal malabsorption","cols":["Test","Pancreatic insufficiency","Mucosal disease (celiac)","Bacterial overgrowth"],"rows":[["Fecal fat","High","High","High"],["Fecal elastase","Low","Usually normal","Normal"],["D-xylose absorption","Normal","Low","Low, corrects after bacteria are cleared"],["Small bowel biopsy","Normal","Villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, IELs","Normal or patchy injury"],["Iron and folate","Usually normal","Low","Folate normal or high"]],"caption":"Steatorrhea shows that fat is lost; fecal elastase and D-xylose show where the process failed."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: reading steatorrhea","text":"A man with years of heavy alcohol use has weight loss and greasy stools. Fecal fat is 25 g per day (steatorrhea is above about 7 g). Fecal elastase is low and D-xylose absorption is normal. Step 1: fat is malabsorbed. Step 2: normal D-xylose means enterocytes absorb a sugar that needs no digestion, so the mucosa works. Step 3: low elastase means acinar output is reduced. The failure is luminal, from chronic pancreatitis. If instead D-xylose were low and elastase normal, the mucosa would be at fault, and a duodenal biopsy would look for celiac disease."},{"type":"p","text":"Enteropeptidase shows why one brush border enzyme can control all pancreatic proteolysis. Pancreatic proteases are secreted as inactive zymogens so they cannot digest the gland. Enteropeptidase on the duodenal brush border cleaves the activation peptide from trypsinogen, and trypsin then activates more trypsinogen and the other zymogens: chymotrypsinogen, proelastase, the procarboxypeptidases, prophospholipase A2, and procolipase. Without enteropeptidase the cascade never starts, even though the pancreas secretes normal amounts of zymogen."},{"type":"p","text":"Congenital enteropeptidase deficiency, a rare autosomal recessive disorder, presents in infancy with diarrhea, failure to thrive, and hypoproteinemia with edema from protein malabsorption. The pancreas and the mucosa are structurally normal, and D-xylose absorption is normal."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-05-C04","obj":"GI-13-E.4","title":"Lactase deficiency and osmotic diarrhea","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Lactase (lactase-phlorizin hydrolase) is a brush border enzyme that splits lactose into glucose and galactose, which enterocytes then absorb through SGLT1. It is most active in the jejunum, is expressed most strongly on mature enterocytes toward the villus tips, and has the lowest activity of the disaccharidases, so it is the first to become rate limiting when it falls."},{"type":"p","text":"Primary (adult-type) lactase deficiency, also called lactase nonpersistence, is a genetically programmed fall in lactase expression after weaning. It is the usual state of most adults worldwide and is especially common in people of East Asian, West African, and Native American ancestry, while lactase persistence is common in people of northern European ancestry. The villi and the other disaccharidases are normal, so the biopsy is normal and D-xylose absorption is normal. Secondary lactase deficiency follows injury to the villus tips, as after viral enteritis such as rotavirus, giardiasis, or celiac disease, and recovers as the mucosa heals. Congenital lactase deficiency, present from birth, is rare."},{"type":"flow","title":"From undigested lactose to symptoms","steps":["Lactose reaches the distal bowel undigested","Unabsorbed sugar holds water in the lumen","Colonic bacteria ferment lactose","Short-chain fatty acids acidify the stool","Hydrogen, methane, and carbon dioxide distend the colon","Hydrogen is absorbed into blood and exhaled"],"caption":"Osmotic water retention causes diarrhea; fermentation causes gas, bloating, cramps, flatulence, and the breath test signal."},{"type":"p","text":"Unabsorbed lactose is osmotically active, so water stays in the lumen and stool volume rises. Colonic fermentation salvages part of the sugar as short-chain fatty acids, but the gas and acid cause bloating, cramping, and flatulence within about 30 minutes to a few hours of eating dairy. Symptoms depend on dose: many people with nonpersistence tolerate small amounts of lactose."},{"type":"p","text":"The stool chemistry shows the mechanism. The stool osmotic gap, calculated as 290 minus 2 times the sum of stool Na+ and K+ (in mOsm/kg), is large in osmotic diarrhea (commonly above about 100 to 125) because an unmeasured solute holds water. In secretory diarrhea it is small (below about 50) because electrolytes account for the osmolality. Stool pH is low, often below about 6, from fermentation acids. Because the cause is a substance the patient eats, diarrhea stops with fasting, whereas secretory diarrhea continues."},{"type":"p","text":"The hydrogen breath test measures exhaled hydrogen after a lactose load; human cells make no hydrogen, so a rise of about 20 parts per million or more over baseline means bacteria fermented lactose that the small bowel failed to absorb. False negatives occur when the colonic flora produces methane instead of hydrogen or has been suppressed. Lactase deficiency does not cause steatorrhea, weight loss, or vitamin deficiency on its own; those findings point to an underlying mucosal disease with secondary lactase loss."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with chronic watery diarrhea is admitted and given nothing by mouth for 48 hours. The diarrhea stops. Stool Na+ was 30 and K+ was 40 mmol/L before fasting. Is this osmotic or secretory diarrhea?","explain":"Osmotic. The gap is 290 minus 2 times 70, which is 150 mOsm/kg, showing an unmeasured solute. Diarrhea that stops when intake stops is driven by something ingested, such as lactose. Secretory diarrhea from toxins or hormones has a small gap and continues during fasting.","check":"The stool osmotic gap and fasting paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-05-C05","obj":"GI-13-E.5","title":"Short bowel syndrome and the consequences of ileal resection","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Short bowel syndrome is malabsorption caused by loss of functional small intestinal length, usually after extensive resection for Crohn disease, mesenteric ischemia, volvulus, or trauma in adults and necrotizing enterocolitis or congenital anomalies in children. It is commonly defined as less than about 200 cm of remaining small bowel. Its consequences depend on how much bowel remains, which segment was removed, and whether the colon is still in continuity."},{"type":"table","title":"What each segment contributes","cols":["Segment","Normal role","Effect of loss"],"rows":[["Jejunum","Most digestion and nutrient absorption; leaky epithelium, limited water salvage","Ileum can adapt and take over nutrient absorption"],["Terminal ileum","B12 and intrinsic factor uptake by cubilin; Na+-dependent bile acid uptake; tight epithelium salvages Na+ and water; L cells release PYY and GLP-1 to slow transit","B12 deficiency, bile acid loss, faster transit; no other segment replaces these functions"],["Ileocecal valve","Slows transit; limits reflux of colonic bacteria","Bacterial overgrowth, faster transit"],["Colon","Absorbs water and Na+; ferments unabsorbed carbohydrate to short-chain fatty acids","High-output stoma, dehydration, hypomagnesemia"]],"caption":"Losing the ileum has worse long-term consequences than losing an equal length of jejunum."},{"type":"p","text":"Diarrhea has several causes: less surface area, faster transit, unabsorbed solute, and, after massive resection, transient gastric acid hypersecretion that inactivates pancreatic lipase in the duodenum. Steatorrhea causes deficiencies of vitamins A, D, E, and K and binds calcium and magnesium in the lumen."},{"type":"p","text":"Ileal resection breaks the enterohepatic circulation, which normally returns about 95% of secreted bile acids. After a short resection (commonly quoted as under about 100 cm), the liver increases synthesis enough to maintain micelles, but bile acids spilling into the colon stimulate Cl- and water secretion, causing watery bile acid diarrhea with little steatorrhea. After a longer resection, losses exceed hepatic synthesis, the bile acid pool shrinks, micelles fail, and steatorrhea dominates. Removing more than about 60 cm of terminal ileum usually causes B12 deficiency, which appears only after years because the liver stores several years of B12. A depleted bile acid pool also makes bile supersaturated with cholesterol and raises the risk of gallstones."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: short versus long ileal resection","text":"Patient A had 50 cm of terminal ileum removed; patient B had 150 cm. Both have diarrhea. In patient A, the liver replaces lost bile acids, micelles form, and fat is absorbed, but the bile acids that reach the colon cause secretory diarrhea, so fecal fat is near normal. In patient B, synthesis cannot keep pace, micelles fail, and fecal fat is high; unabsorbed long-chain fatty acids reaching the colon add to the diarrhea. B12 deficiency is expected in patient B and may occur in patient A, since the cutoff of about 60 cm is approximate."},{"type":"p","text":"Enteric hyperoxaluria explains the kidney stones. Normally dietary oxalate binds calcium in the lumen to form insoluble calcium oxalate, which is excreted in stool. When fat is malabsorbed, fatty acids bind calcium as soaps, leaving oxalate soluble, and bile acids and fatty acids also make the colonic mucosa more permeable to it. The colon absorbs the free oxalate, the kidney excretes it, and calcium oxalate stones form, made more likely by low urine volume, citrate, and magnesium from diarrhea. Patients with an end jejunostomy and no colon do not develop enteric hyperoxaluria."},{"type":"flow","title":"Ileal resection to calcium oxalate stones","steps":["Terminal ileum lost; bile acids not reabsorbed","Bile acid pool shrinks and fat is malabsorbed","Luminal fatty acids bind calcium as soaps","Dietary oxalate stays free and soluble","Colon absorbs the free oxalate","Urinary oxalate rises; calcium oxalate stones form"],"caption":"The colon must be present, because it is the site of excess oxalate absorption."},{"type":"p","text":"Over months to about two years the remaining bowel adapts: villi lengthen, crypts deepen, and the lumen dilates, driven by luminal nutrients and trophic hormones such as GLP-2. Adaptation is greatest in the ileum, which is another reason that preserving ileum matters."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-05-C06","obj":"GI-13-E.6","title":"Blind loop syndrome and small intestinal bacterial overgrowth","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The proximal small bowel normally holds relatively few bacteria, commonly quoted as fewer than about 10 to the 3rd to 10 to the 4th organisms per mL, compared with about 10 to the 11th or more per gram in the colon. Gastric acid, the migrating motor complex that sweeps the lumen between meals, the ileocecal valve, secretory IgA, and bile keep it that way. Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth (SIBO) follows when stasis or loss of these defenses lets colonic-type flora, including anaerobes such as Bacteroides and Clostridium species, multiply proximally."},{"type":"p","text":"A blind loop is a segment that food bypasses, so it does not empty. Classic examples after gastric surgery are the afferent (duodenal) limb of a Billroth II gastrojejunostomy and other surgically created loops. Strictures, jejunal diverticula, fistulas, motility disorders such as scleroderma and diabetic neuropathy, resection of the ileocecal valve, and achlorhydria cause the same overgrowth."},{"type":"flow","title":"How bacterial overgrowth causes malabsorption","steps":["Stasis lets anaerobes multiply in the small bowel","Bacterial hydrolases deconjugate bile acids","Free bile acids are absorbed early and fail to form micelles","Fat and fat-soluble vitamins are malabsorbed (steatorrhea)","Bacteria take up B12 before it reaches the ileum","Bacteria synthesize folate, which the host absorbs"],"caption":"Two vitamins move in opposite directions: B12 falls while folate stays normal or rises."},{"type":"p","text":"Conjugation with glycine or taurine lowers the pKa of bile acids, so they stay ionized, cannot cross the jejunal membrane, and remain in the lumen to form micelles until the ileal transporter reclaims them. Bacterial bile salt hydrolases remove the glycine or taurine. The unconjugated bile acids are absorbed passively in the jejunum, so their luminal concentration falls below that needed for micelles, and fat digestion products are not solubilized. Free bile acids also injure enterocytes, causing patchy villus damage and loss of brush border enzymes such as lactase."},{"type":"p","text":"Anaerobes take up B12, including B12 bound to intrinsic factor, so less reaches the ileal receptor and a macrocytic megaloblastic anemia can develop. Intrinsic factor secretion and the ileum are normal, which separates this from pernicious anemia and ileal resection. Because the bacteria synthesize folate, serum folate is normal or raised, a key clue. Bacterial fermentation of carbohydrate produces gas, bloating, and an early rise in breath hydrogen after a glucose or lactulose load, and bacteria also consume D-xylose, so its absorption is low but corrects when the bacteria are cleared."},{"type":"table","title":"Sorting causes of B12 deficiency","cols":["Cause","Intrinsic factor","Gastrin","Folate","Corrects B12 absorption"],"rows":[["Pernicious anemia","Absent (anti-intrinsic factor antibodies)","High","Normal","Giving intrinsic factor"],["Bacterial overgrowth (blind loop)","Normal","Normal","Normal or high","Clearing the bacteria"],["Terminal ileal resection or disease","Normal","Normal","Normal","Nothing; the receptor is gone"]],"caption":"The last column follows the logic of the classic Schilling test, now rarely available, which boards may still use to frame these distinctions."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with a Billroth II gastrojejunostomy has steatorrhea, macrocytic anemia, low serum B12, and a high serum folate. Why does folate rise while B12 falls?","explain":"Anaerobes in the stagnant afferent loop take up B12 before it reaches the ileum but synthesize folate, which the jejunum absorbs. Deconjugated bile acids are absorbed too early to form micelles, causing the steatorrhea.","check":"The flow and the B12 paragraph in this section."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and physiology organize this chapter. Acid comes from the gastric body, iron and folate are absorbed proximally, and B12 and bile acids are reclaimed in the terminal ileum, so the location of disease predicts the deficiency: microcytic anemia points to the stomach or duodenum, macrocytic anemia with low B12 points to the stomach, the ileum, or bacteria, and folate separates bacteria (high) from the others. Biochemistry explains why a single step can fail completely, as with enteropeptidase starting the zymogen cascade or bile acid conjugation setting where bile acids are absorbed."},{"type":"p","text":"Immunology and pathology explain chronic risk. Autoimmune gastritis and celiac disease are T cell diseases with antibodies used as markers, and both carry neoplastic risk from chronic stimulation: gastrin-driven carcinoid in the stomach and T cell lymphoma in the small bowel. Microbiology enters through the acid barrier and stasis, and renal pathology through oxalate. The tests have limits: D-xylose depends on gastric emptying and renal function, IgA serology fails in IgA deficiency, and breath tests miss methane producers, so each result is read with the whole picture."}],"summary":["Achlorhydria removes the acid-somatostatin brake on G cells; with the antrum spared, gastrin rises and drives ECL hyperplasia and type 1 carcinoid.","Loss of acid impairs iron solubility and release of food-bound B12; autoimmune gastritis adds intrinsic factor loss and pernicious anemia.","In celiac disease, tissue transglutaminase deamidates gliadin so it binds HLA-DQ2 or DQ8, causing intraepithelial lymphocytosis, villous atrophy, and crypt hyperplasia worst in the distal duodenum and proximal jejunum.","Celiac disease presents with iron deficiency refractory to oral iron, dermatitis herpetiformis (IgA in dermal papillae), and risk of enteropathy-associated T cell lymphoma.","Steatorrhea with low fecal elastase and normal D-xylose means pancreatic insufficiency; low D-xylose means mucosal disease or bacterial overgrowth.","Enteropeptidase starts the zymogen cascade, so its deficiency blocks all protease activation and causes infant protein malabsorption with edema.","Lactase deficiency causes osmotic diarrhea with a large stool osmotic gap, acidic stool, a positive hydrogen breath test, and relief with fasting; villi are normal in the primary form.","Terminal ileal loss causes B12 deficiency and bile acid loss: bile acid diarrhea after short resections, steatorrhea after long ones.","Fat malabsorption with an intact colon frees oxalate from calcium, causing enteric hyperoxaluria and calcium oxalate stones.","Blind loop overgrowth deconjugates bile acids (steatorrhea) and consumes B12 while making folate, giving low B12 with normal or high folate."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Gastrointestinal Tract, sections on chronic gastritis, malabsorption and diarrhea, celiac disease, and lactase deficiency"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Digestion and Absorption in the Gastrointestinal Tract; Secretory Functions of the Alimentary Tract"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Gastric function; Nutrient digestion and absorption; Vitamin and mineral absorption"},{"book":"Lange: Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Gastrointestinal Disease, sections on malabsorption"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Specialized immunity at epithelial barriers, section on celiac disease"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Digestion of dietary proteins, activation of pancreatic zymogens"}]},"GI-13-F":{"unit":"GI-13-F","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-06","title":"Obstructive and Motility Disorders of the Gastrointestinal Tract","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers conditions that stop contents from moving through the gut, from the pharynx to the rectum. Some are failures of nerves or muscle (achalasia, adynamic ileus, Hirschsprung disease, gastroparesis), and some are physical blocks (hernias, intussusception, volvulus, adhesions). Board questions usually describe a patient by age, history, and one or two signs, such as a bird-beak esophagus, currant jelly stool, or an empty rectal vault, and ask for the mechanism or the expected finding. If you can say whether the problem is neural, muscular, or mechanical, and whether the blood supply is threatened, you can reason through most of these questions.","prerequisites":["Layers of the gut wall and the location of the myenteric and submucosal plexuses (GI-2)","Enteric neurotransmitters: acetylcholine and substance P (excitatory), nitric oxide and VIP (inhibitory) (GI-6)","Peristalsis, receptive relaxation, and the migrating motor complex (GI-6)","Midgut rotation and neural crest migration (GI-1)","Inguinal canal, femoral canal, and esophageal hiatus anatomy (GI-4)"],"connections":[{"to":"Gastrointestinal embryology (GI-1)","how":"Hirschsprung disease and midgut volvulus with malrotation are failures of neural crest migration and gut rotation."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal pathogens (GI-12)","how":"Trypanosoma cruzi destroys the enteric plexus, and Clostridioides difficile colitis can progress to toxic megacolon."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal vascular diseases (GI-13-J)","how":"Strangulation and volvulus cause bowel infarction by the same ischemic pathway as mesenteric vascular occlusion."},{"to":"Inflammatory gastrointestinal diseases (GI-13-G)","how":"Ulcerative colitis is the classic setting of toxic megacolon, and reflux is the main consequence of a sliding hiatal hernia."},{"to":"Renal and acid-base physiology","how":"Vomiting and fluid sequestration in obstruction cause hypovolemia, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis, and hypokalemia itself worsens ileus."}],"terms":[{"term":"Myenteric (Auerbach) plexus","def":"The network of enteric ganglia between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers that controls motility."},{"term":"Achalasia","def":"Failure of the lower esophageal sphincter to relax with swallowing, together with loss of peristalsis in the esophageal body."},{"term":"Adynamic (paralytic) ileus","def":"Failure of propulsion through the intestine without any physical blockage of the lumen."},{"term":"Mechanical obstruction","def":"A physical block inside, within the wall of, or outside the bowel that prevents passage of contents."},{"term":"Transition point","def":"The site on imaging where dilated bowel above a mechanical obstruction changes to collapsed bowel below it."},{"term":"Incarceration","def":"A hernia whose contents are trapped and cannot be returned to the abdomen."},{"term":"Strangulation","def":"An incarcerated or twisted segment whose blood supply is compromised, leading to ischemia and infarction."},{"term":"Closed-loop obstruction","def":"A segment blocked at both ends, as in volvulus, so pressure rises quickly and threatens its blood supply."},{"term":"Intussusception","def":"Telescoping of a proximal segment of bowel (the intussusceptum) into the adjacent distal segment (the intussuscipiens)."},{"term":"Volvulus","def":"Twisting of a loop of bowel around the axis of its mesentery, obstructing both the lumen and the vessels."},{"term":"False diverticulum","def":"An outpouching made of mucosa and submucosa only, without the muscularis propria."},{"term":"Visceral hypersensitivity","def":"A lowered threshold for perceiving gut distension or contraction as painful, central to irritable bowel syndrome."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-06-C01","obj":"GI-13-F.1","title":"Achalasia: loss of inhibitory neurons in the esophagus","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The lower esophageal sphincter (LES) is a zone of tonically contracted smooth muscle at the gastroesophageal (GE) junction. Its resting tone comes from the muscle itself and from excitatory cholinergic neurons. When a person swallows, vagal fibers activate inhibitory neurons of the myenteric plexus, which release nitric oxide (NO) and vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP). These relax the LES within seconds and also produce the timed wave of inhibition that lets peristalsis travel down the smooth muscle esophagus in order."},{"type":"p","text":"Achalasia is the selective loss of these inhibitory neurons. In primary (idiopathic) achalasia the myenteric ganglia show inflammation, mainly T lymphocytes, and fibrosis; an autoimmune response, possibly triggered by a viral infection in a genetically susceptible person, is the leading hypothesis. Excitatory cholinergic input is relatively spared, so it is left unopposed. The LES has normal or raised resting pressure and relaxes incompletely or not at all, and the distal esophagus loses peristalsis because the inhibitory wave that sequences contraction is gone."},{"type":"flow","title":"From neuron loss to the bird-beak esophagus","steps":["Inflammation destroys NO and VIP neurons of the myenteric plexus","Excitatory cholinergic tone is left unopposed","LES fails to relax on swallowing","Esophageal body loses ordered peristalsis","Food and fluid pool above a closed LES","Esophagus dilates and tapers to a narrow beak"],"caption":"The obstruction is functional: the lumen at the LES is anatomically normal but does not open."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the problem is a sphincter that will not open rather than a narrowed lumen, solids and liquids are both held up from the start. Patients also describe regurgitation of undigested food, chest discomfort, nocturnal cough or aspiration, and gradual weight loss. A barium swallow shows a dilated esophagus, often with an air-fluid level, tapering smoothly to a bird beak at the GE junction; a chest film may show a widened mediastinum and a small or absent gastric air bubble. Esophageal manometry is the defining test, showing incomplete LES relaxation with absent peristalsis. Long-standing stasis and chronic mucosal irritation raise the risk of squamous cell carcinoma."},{"type":"p","text":"Secondary achalasia has the same physiology from a known cause. In Chagas disease, Trypanosoma cruzi, transmitted by triatomine bugs in Latin America, destroys enteric ganglia over years and can produce megaesophagus, megacolon, and cardiomyopathy in the same patient. A tumor at the GE junction can infiltrate the plexus and mimic the picture (pseudoachalasia), which is suspected in an older patient with rapid weight loss and a short history."},{"type":"table","title":"Achalasia compared with other causes of esophageal dysphagia","cols":["Feature","Achalasia","Systemic sclerosis","Mechanical narrowing (stricture, cancer)"],"rows":[["Basic defect","Loss of inhibitory neurons","Smooth muscle atrophy and fibrosis","Narrowed lumen"],["LES pressure","Normal or high, poor relaxation","Low","Not the cause"],["Peristalsis","Absent in body","Weak or absent distally","Preserved"],["Dysphagia","Solids and liquids from onset","Solids and liquids, with reflux","Solids first, then liquids"],["Classic image","Bird beak","Dilated, patulous LES, reflux","Irregular or fixed narrowing"]],"caption":"Motility disorders affect solids and liquids together; a fixed narrowing blocks solids first."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Manometry shows absent distal peristalsis in two patients. One has an LES that will not relax; the other has a very weak LES and severe heartburn. Which has lost neurons and which has lost muscle?","explain":"The first has achalasia: muscle is present but its inhibitory innervation is gone, so tone persists and relaxation fails. The second has systemic sclerosis: fibrosis replaces the smooth muscle itself, so the LES cannot generate tone and acid refluxes freely.","check":"The comparison table and the second paragraph of this section."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-06-C02","obj":"GI-13-F.2","title":"Adynamic ileus compared with mechanical obstruction","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Moving contents along the intestine needs three things: intact enteric neurons, excitable smooth muscle, and a balance of autonomic input in which parasympathetic activity promotes and sympathetic activity inhibits motility. Obstruction results either when propulsion fails with an open lumen (adynamic, or paralytic, ileus) or when the lumen is physically blocked (mechanical obstruction)."},{"type":"p","text":"Adynamic ileus is a global failure of motility. After abdominal surgery, spinal and prevertebral sympathetic reflexes inhibit the enteric plexus, and handling of the bowel activates macrophages in the muscularis that release inflammatory mediators, including NO and prostaglandins, which suppress contraction. Peritonitis acts through the same inflammatory route. Hypokalemia hyperpolarizes smooth muscle cells, moving the membrane away from threshold so slow waves fail to trigger spikes. Opioids bind mu receptors on enteric neurons and reduce acetylcholine release, so propulsion stops while nonpropulsive segmental tone may increase. Because no segment is blocked, gas spreads through small and large bowel, the abdomen is distended but pain is mild, and bowel sounds are reduced or absent."},{"type":"p","text":"In mechanical obstruction the bowel above the block is still innervated and contracts harder against it, producing cramping (colicky) pain and high-pitched, tinkling sounds as fluid and gas are forced through a narrowed segment. Below the block the bowel empties and collapses, which creates the transition point on imaging. In adults, adhesions from previous surgery are the most common cause of small bowel obstruction, followed by hernias; colorectal cancer is the most common cause of large bowel obstruction. Fecal impaction blocks the lumen from within, and a gallstone lodged at the ileocecal valve (gallstone ileus) is mechanical despite its name."},{"type":"table","title":"Adynamic ileus and mechanical obstruction","cols":["Feature","Adynamic ileus","Mechanical obstruction"],"rows":[["Defect","Propulsion fails; lumen open","Lumen blocked; propulsion intact early"],["Typical causes","Surgery, peritonitis, hypokalemia, opioids","Adhesions, hernias, tumors, impaction, volvulus"],["Pain","Mild, diffuse","Colicky, waves with peristalsis"],["Bowel sounds","Absent or reduced","High-pitched early; quiet late"],["Imaging","Gas in small and large bowel, no transition point","Dilated loops above a transition point, little gas below"]],"caption":"A late mechanical obstruction can fall silent as the bowel fatigues, so the transition point is the more reliable distinction."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why prolonged mechanical obstruction becomes dangerous","steps":["Gas and secretions accumulate above the block","Distension raises wall tension","Mucosal perfusion falls; secretion exceeds absorption","Vomiting and fluid sequestration cause hypovolemia","Stasis allows bacterial overgrowth and translocation","Ischemia progresses to necrosis and perforation"],"caption":"By the law of Laplace, wall tension rises with radius, so the most dilated segment is most at risk."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: electrolytes after repeated vomiting","text":"A patient with a high small bowel obstruction vomits for two days. Gastric HCl is lost, so bicarbonate generated by parietal cells stays in the blood: metabolic alkalosis with low chloride. Volume loss activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. Aldosterone drives Na+ reabsorption in the collecting duct in exchange for K+ and H+, so urinary K+ loss causes hypokalemia, and the urine can be acidic despite systemic alkalosis (paradoxical aciduria). The hypokalemia then hyperpolarizes intestinal smooth muscle and adds an adynamic component to the mechanical block."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-06-C03","obj":"GI-13-F.3","title":"Hiatal hernias and strangulation of abdominal wall hernias","blocks":[{"type":"h","text":"Sliding and paraesophageal hiatal hernias"},{"type":"p","text":"The esophagus passes through the esophageal hiatus, a sling formed by the right crus of the diaphragm, and is held in place by the phrenoesophageal ligament. Normally a short segment of the LES sits below the diaphragm, where intra-abdominal pressure and the crural sling both add to its barrier against reflux. A hiatal hernia forms when the hiatus widens and the ligament stretches, which becomes more likely with age, obesity, and anything that raises intra-abdominal pressure."},{"type":"p","text":"In a sliding hernia, by far the most common type, the GE junction and cardia move up into the thorax. The LES is separated from the crural sling and loses its intra-abdominal segment, so the antireflux barrier weakens and gastroesophageal reflux follows. In a paraesophageal hernia the GE junction stays anchored below the diaphragm, so reflux is less prominent, but the fundus rolls up through the hiatus beside the esophagus. That herniated stomach can twist (gastric volvulus), become trapped, bleed, or strangulate."},{"type":"table","title":"Sliding and paraesophageal hiatal hernias","cols":["Feature","Sliding","Paraesophageal"],"rows":[["GE junction","Moves above the diaphragm","Stays in normal position"],["What herniates","GE junction and cardia","Fundus, beside the esophagus"],["Frequency","Most hiatal hernias","Uncommon"],["Main consequence","Reflux","Incarceration, gastric volvulus, strangulation"]],"caption":"Mixed hernias combine an elevated GE junction with herniated fundus."},{"type":"h","text":"From incarceration to strangulation"},{"type":"p","text":"An abdominal wall hernia is reducible when its contents can be returned, incarcerated when they are trapped, and strangulated when trapped contents lose their blood supply. The order of events follows vessel pressures. Mesenteric veins have thin walls and low pressure, so the tight hernia neck compresses them first while arterial blood still flows in. Blood is trapped, capillary hydrostatic pressure rises, and fluid leaks into the wall and lumen. The segment swells, which tightens the neck further, until tissue pressure exceeds arterial pressure and inflow stops."},{"type":"flow","title":"Strangulation of an incarcerated hernia","steps":["Bowel trapped in a narrow hernia neck","Thin-walled veins compressed; arterial inflow continues","Venous congestion and edema swell the segment","Swelling tightens the neck; arterial inflow stops","Hemorrhagic infarction of the trapped bowel","Perforation, peritonitis, and sepsis"],"caption":"Venous obstruction comes before arterial obstruction, so strangulated bowel is dark, swollen, and hemorrhagic."},{"type":"p","text":"Strangulation changes the picture from intermittent colic to constant, severe pain with a tense, tender, often discolored hernia that cannot be reduced, followed by fever, tachycardia, leukocytosis, and lactic acidosis as the bowel dies and bacteria cross the damaged wall. Femoral hernias carry the highest risk among groin hernias because the femoral ring is narrow and bounded by unyielding structures: the inguinal ligament in front, the lacunar ligament medially, and the pectineal ligament behind."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does strangulated bowel look dark red and swollen rather than pale?","explain":"The veins close first while arteries still pump blood in, so the segment fills with blood and edema before inflow finally stops. The result is a hemorrhagic (red) infarct, not a pale one.","check":"The strangulation flow and the paragraph before it."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-06-C04","obj":"GI-13-F.4","title":"Intussusception and volvulus","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Both cause mechanical obstruction and threaten the blood supply, because the mesentery and its vessels are dragged into or twisted with the bowel."},{"type":"h","text":"Intussusception"},{"type":"p","text":"Peristalsis grips a mass in the bowel wall, the lead point, and pulls it with its segment and mesentery into the bowel just ahead. The usual site is ileocolic, with terminal ileum entering the cecum. It is the most common cause of intestinal obstruction in children about 6 months to 3 years old. In most children the lead point is lymphoid hyperplasia of Peyer patches after a viral infection, especially adenovirus; a Meckel diverticulum, polyp, lymphoma, or the bowel wall hemorrhage of IgA vasculitis can also serve. In adults a definable lead point, usually a tumor, is present in most cases."},{"type":"p","text":"The trapped mesentery is compressed, so venous and lymphatic outflow fail first, the wall becomes edematous, and congested mucosa sheds blood mixed with mucus, the currant jelly stool. This is a sign of mucosal ischemia and tends to appear late. Typical findings are bouts of severe colicky pain with the legs drawn up and calm periods between, a sausage-shaped mass, often in the right upper quadrant, and a target sign on ultrasound from concentric rings of bowel wall."},{"type":"h","text":"Volvulus"},{"type":"p","text":"In volvulus a loop rotates around its mesenteric axis. This creates a closed-loop obstruction and kinks the mesenteric vessels at the same time, so ischemia develops early. A twist needs a mobile loop on a narrow mesenteric base. In older adults that is a long, redundant sigmoid colon, favored by chronic constipation; imaging shows a hugely dilated loop shaped like a coffee bean. In infants it is malrotation: the midgut fails to complete its 270 degree counterclockwise rotation around the superior mesenteric artery, leaving the small bowel on a narrow pedicle and often with peritoneal (Ladd) bands crossing the duodenum. Midgut volvulus can infarct the whole territory of the superior mesenteric artery."},{"type":"table","title":"Intussusception and volvulus","cols":["Feature","Intussusception","Volvulus"],"rows":[["Mechanism","Segment telescopes into the next","Loop twists on its mesentery"],["Typical patient","Child about 6 months to 3 years","Older adult (sigmoid); infant with malrotation (midgut)"],["Usual site","Ileocolic","Sigmoid colon; midgut"],["Predisposition","Lead point: Peyer patches, Meckel diverticulum, polyp, tumor","Redundant sigmoid, constipation; malrotation"],["Classic findings","Colicky pain, sausage mass, currant jelly stool, target sign","Distension, coffee bean sign; bilious vomiting in an infant"]],"caption":"Bilious vomiting in a newborn suggests obstruction beyond the ampulla, and malrotation with volvulus must be considered first."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-06-C05","obj":"GI-13-F.5","title":"Causes of megacolon: Hirschsprung disease, Chagas disease, and toxic megacolon","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Megacolon means marked dilation of the colon. The three classic causes reach it by different routes: absence of neurons from birth, destruction of neurons later in life, or acute paralysis of inflamed muscle."},{"type":"h","text":"Hirschsprung disease"},{"type":"p","text":"Enteric neurons come from vagal neural crest cells that migrate down the gut from cranial to caudal between about weeks 5 and 12 of development. If migration stops early, the distal bowel has no ganglion cells in either the myenteric or the submucosal plexus. Because migration proceeds in one direction, the aganglionic segment always includes the rectum and extends upward as one continuous segment, most often confined to the rectosigmoid. Loss-of-function mutations in RET, the tyrosine kinase receptor in the glial cell line derived neurotrophic factor pathway, are the main genetic cause, and the disease is more common in boys and in Down syndrome."},{"type":"flow","title":"Hirschsprung disease from embryology to presentation","steps":["Neural crest cells fail to reach the distal bowel","Rectum and adjoining segment lack ganglion cells","No NO and VIP release; segment cannot relax","Aganglionic segment acts as a functional obstruction","Normal colon above it dilates","Delayed meconium, distension, empty rectal vault"],"caption":"The narrow segment is the diseased part; the dilated colon above it is normally innervated."},{"type":"p","text":"A newborn fails to pass meconium within 48 hours and develops abdominal distension and bilious vomiting. On rectal examination the vault is empty and tight, and withdrawing the finger often releases explosive gas and stool. Anorectal manometry shows loss of the rectoanal inhibitory reflex, the internal sphincter relaxation that normally follows rectal distension. Rectal suction biopsy confirms the diagnosis by showing no ganglion cells, with hypertrophied nerve trunks and increased acetylcholinesterase staining. Stasis predisposes to enterocolitis."},{"type":"h","text":"Chagas disease and toxic megacolon"},{"type":"p","text":"In chronic Chagas disease, Trypanosoma cruzi infection destroys enteric ganglia, so the colon and esophagus lose inhibitory control in adult life; a patient from Latin America with megacolon, megaesophagus, and dilated cardiomyopathy fits this pattern. Toxic megacolon is different: severe colitis, classically ulcerative colitis or Clostridioides difficile colitis, extends transmurally into the muscularis propria. Inflammatory NO and neuronal injury paralyze the muscle, the colon dilates (commonly defined as more than about 6 cm), and systemic toxicity follows with fever, tachycardia, and leukocytosis. The thinned wall can perforate. Hypokalemia and opioid or anticholinergic drugs that reduce motility can precipitate it."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of megacolon","cols":["Cause","Mechanism","Classic picture"],"rows":[["Hirschsprung disease","Congenital absence of ganglion cells, rectum upward","Newborn, delayed meconium, empty rectal vault"],["Chagas disease","Acquired destruction of enteric ganglia by T. cruzi","Adult from Latin America; also megaesophagus, cardiomyopathy"],["Toxic megacolon","Transmural inflammation paralyzes colonic muscle","Ulcerative or C. difficile colitis, fever, tachycardia, risk of perforation"]],"caption":"Two are neural (absent or destroyed ganglia); one is inflammatory paralysis of muscle."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A contrast enema in a newborn shows a narrow rectum and a widely dilated sigmoid. Which segment would a biopsy show to be abnormal?","explain":"The narrow rectum. It lacks ganglion cells and cannot relax, so it obstructs; the dilated colon above has normal ganglia and is distended only because contents cannot pass.","check":"The Hirschsprung flow and its caption."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-06-C06","obj":"GI-13-F.6","title":"Zenker diverticulum","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A true diverticulum contains all layers of the wall, including the muscularis propria, as a Meckel diverticulum does. A false diverticulum is mucosa and submucosa pushed out through a gap in the muscle. Pulsion diverticula are false and form where pressure inside the lumen is high and the muscle wall has a weak spot; traction diverticula are true and form where scarring outside the wall pulls it outward."},{"type":"p","text":"The inferior pharyngeal constrictor has two parts: the oblique thyropharyngeus above and the horizontal cricopharyngeus below, which forms most of the upper esophageal sphincter. Between them on the posterior wall lies a triangle of thin muscle (Killian triangle). Zenker diverticulum is a pulsion, false diverticulum that herniates through this weak area, just above the cricopharyngeus."},{"type":"p","text":"The driving problem is cricopharyngeal dysfunction. With age the cricopharyngeus becomes fibrotic and less compliant, so it does not open fully when the pharynx contracts. Pressure in the hypopharynx rises with every swallow, and over years mucosa and submucosa bulge backward through the weak triangle, usually in adults over about 60."},{"type":"flow","title":"Formation of a Zenker diverticulum","steps":["Cricopharyngeus becomes fibrotic and opens incompletely","Hypopharyngeal pressure rises with each swallow","Mucosa and submucosa bulge through Killian triangle","Pouch enlarges and retains food","Pouch contents regurgitate, decay, or are aspirated"],"caption":"The pouch lies posteriorly above the upper esophageal sphincter, so it fills before the bolus can enter the esophagus."},{"type":"p","text":"The symptoms follow from a pouch that fills during swallowing and empties later. Dysphagia is oropharyngeal, felt high in the neck. Food that sat in the pouch returns undigested, sometimes hours after a meal, and bacterial breakdown of retained food causes halitosis. Patients may notice gurgling in the neck or a soft neck mass, and spillover into the larynx, especially when lying down, causes cough and aspiration pneumonia. A barium swallow shows the posterior pouch. Endoscopy carries a risk of perforation because the scope can enter the pouch rather than the esophagus."},{"type":"table","title":"Esophageal diverticula by location","cols":["Type","Location","Mechanism","Wall"],"rows":[["Zenker","Pharyngoesophageal junction, posterior","Pulsion from cricopharyngeal dysfunction","False"],["Traction (midesophageal)","Near the carina","Pulled out by fibrosis of nearby lymph nodes, as after tuberculosis or histoplasmosis","True"],["Epiphrenic","Just above the LES","Pulsion from distal motility disorders such as achalasia","False"]],"caption":"Pulsion diverticula are false; traction diverticula are true."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-06-C07","obj":"GI-13-F.7","title":"Gastroparesis and irritable bowel syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Both are motility disorders without a physical block. Irritable bowel syndrome (IBS), unlike gastroparesis, has no structural or biochemical marker and is defined by symptoms."},{"type":"h","text":"Gastroparesis"},{"type":"p","text":"Normal gastric emptying depends on vagally mediated relaxation of the fundus to accept a meal, rhythmic antral contractions paced by slow waves from the interstitial cells of Cajal (ICC) that grind solids, and coordinated opening of the pylorus. Gastroparesis is delayed emptying of solids without mechanical obstruction. Diabetes mellitus is a leading cause, most often type 1 of long duration. Autonomic neuropathy damages vagal fibers, the stomach loses ICC and myenteric neurons that make neuronal NO synthase, and antral contractions weaken while the pylorus fails to relax. Acute hyperglycemia itself also slows emptying. Other causes include vagotomy, systemic sclerosis, and drugs that slow motility such as opioids, and many cases are idiopathic."},{"type":"p","text":"Food stays in the stomach, so patients have early satiety, postprandial fullness, bloating, nausea, and vomiting of food eaten hours earlier; retained material can form a bezoar. In a person with diabetes, absorption of a meal no longer matches the timing of insulin, producing erratic glucose levels with unexpected hypoglycemia. Diagnosis requires excluding obstruction by endoscopy and then demonstrating delayed emptying of a solid meal, usually by scintigraphy."},{"type":"h","text":"Irritable bowel syndrome"},{"type":"p","text":"IBS is a disorder of gut-brain interaction. Its core is visceral hypersensitivity, in which normal distension and contraction are felt as pain, together with altered motility and abnormal central processing of visceral signals. Contributors include changes in the gut microbiota, low-grade mucosal immune activation with mast cells near nerve endings, altered serotonin signaling from enterochromaffin cells, and psychological stress. A subset begins after acute infectious gastroenteritis (post-infectious IBS). It is more common in women."},{"type":"p","text":"The Rome IV definition is recurrent abdominal pain, on average at least one day a week for three months, with at least two of: relation to defecation, a change in stool frequency, or a change in stool form, and onset at least six months before diagnosis. Subtypes (constipation, diarrhea, mixed, or unclassified) follow the dominant stool form. Because IBS leaves the bowel structurally normal, weight loss, bleeding, or anemia indicates another disease."},{"type":"table","title":"Gastroparesis and IBS","cols":["Feature","Gastroparesis","Irritable bowel syndrome"],"rows":[["Site","Stomach","Small bowel and colon, central processing"],["Mechanism","Vagal neuropathy, loss of ICC and NO neurons","Visceral hypersensitivity, altered motility, gut-brain axis"],["Key symptoms","Early satiety, vomiting of old food, bloating","Pain linked to defecation, altered stool form or frequency"],["Objective test","Delayed gastric emptying of solids","None; diagnosis by criteria after exclusion"],["Features suggesting another disease","Mechanical outlet obstruction on endoscopy","Weight loss, rectal bleeding, anemia, nocturnal symptoms, onset after about 50"]],"caption":"Only gastroparesis has a measurable transit delay."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Most conditions in this chapter come down to one question: is the enteric nervous system missing, damaged, or overridden, or is the lumen physically closed? Embryology explains the congenital forms, with failed neural crest migration in Hirschsprung disease and incomplete midgut rotation in malrotation. Microbiology adds Trypanosoma cruzi, which removes ganglia in the esophagus and colon, and Clostridioides difficile, which can paralyze an inflamed colon. Immunology underlies the ganglionitis of primary achalasia and the colitis behind toxic megacolon. Physiology explains why the same loss of NO and VIP neurons produces a sphincter that will not open in the esophagus and a segment that will not relax in the rectum."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology ties the mechanical disorders to ischemia. Hernias, intussusception, and volvulus all compress or twist the mesentery, and because veins close before arteries, the result is a swollen, hemorrhagic infarct that can perforate. Systemic consequences link to renal and acid-base physiology: vomiting, sequestration, and aldosterone produce hypovolemia, hypokalemia, and alkalosis, and the hypokalemia feeds back to slow the bowel further. The limits of the pattern matter too: a late mechanical obstruction can be silent like an ileus, and IBS is a diagnosis made only after structural disease has been excluded."}],"summary":["Achalasia is loss of inhibitory NO and VIP myenteric neurons, so the LES fails to relax and peristalsis is lost; dysphagia to solids and liquids, bird-beak swallow, Chagas disease as a secondary cause.","Systemic sclerosis also abolishes distal peristalsis but leaves a weak LES with reflux, because muscle, not nerve, is lost.","Adynamic ileus is global loss of propulsion (surgery, peritonitis, hypokalemia, opioids) with absent sounds and diffuse gas; mechanical obstruction has colicky pain, high-pitched sounds, and a transition point.","Sliding hiatal hernias lift the GE junction and cause reflux; paraesophageal hernias leave it in place and risk volvulus and strangulation.","Strangulation follows venous compression, edema, then arterial occlusion, producing a hemorrhagic infarct; femoral hernias carry the highest risk.","Intussusception is usually ileocolic in children about 6 months to 3 years with a lead point, colicky pain, a sausage mass, and currant jelly stool.","Volvulus is twisting on the mesentery: sigmoid in older constipated adults (coffee bean sign), midgut with malrotation in infants (bilious vomiting).","Hirschsprung disease is congenital absence of ganglion cells beginning at the rectum; the narrow segment is diseased and the dilated colon above is normal.","Zenker diverticulum is a false pulsion diverticulum through Killian triangle above a poorly opening cricopharyngeus, causing regurgitation of undigested food and halitosis.","Diabetic gastroparesis delays gastric emptying through vagal neuropathy and ICC loss; IBS is pain linked to defecation with altered stool form and no structural disease."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Gastrointestinal Tract (esophagus, small and large intestine, obstruction)"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"General Principles of Gastrointestinal Function; Physiology of Gastrointestinal Disorders"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Gastric and intestinal motility and the enteric nervous system"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Digestive System, rotation of the midgut; neural crest derivatives"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen, inguinal and femoral regions and the esophageal hiatus; Neck, pharynx"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Gastrointestinal Disease"}]},"GI-13-G":{"unit":"GI-13-G","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-07","title":"Inflammatory Gastrointestinal Diseases","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter explains the inflammatory diseases of the digestive tube from the esophagus to the anus: reflux and its complications, the three main forms of esophagitis, acute and chronic gastritis, peptic ulcer, esophageal tears and rupture, appendicitis, diverticular disease, noninfectious enteritis and colitis, inflammatory bowel disease, and common anorectal disorders. Nearly every one follows the same logic. Injury wins when an aggressive force (acid, pepsin, bile, drugs, pressure, bacteria, or the immune system) outweighs mucosal defense or blood supply. Board questions give you the site, the depth of injury, the timing of pain, and a biopsy or imaging finding, and ask you to name the process and predict its complication.","prerequisites":["Layers of the gut wall: mucosa, muscularis mucosae, submucosa, muscularis propria, serosa or adventitia","Regulation of gastric acid by gastrin, histamine, acetylcholine, and somatostatin","Prostaglandin synthesis by cyclooxygenase and its mucosal protective effects","Visceral versus somatic pain pathways and dermatomes T5 to T12","Acute inflammation, granulomatous inflammation, metaplasia, and dysplasia"],"connections":[{"to":"Gastrointestinal neoplasia","how":"Barrett esophagus, H. pylori gastritis, autoimmune gastritis, and long-standing colitis are the inflammatory precursors of adenocarcinoma, MALT lymphoma, and gastric neuroendocrine tumors."},{"to":"Hematology","how":"Autoimmune gastritis and ileal Crohn disease cause vitamin B12 deficiency and megaloblastic anemia, while chronic ulcer or NSAID blood loss causes iron deficiency."},{"to":"Immunology","how":"Eosinophilic esophagitis is a Th2 allergic disease, Crohn disease a Th1 and Th17 granulomatous disease, and autoimmune gastritis an organ-specific autoimmune disease."},{"to":"Renal and hepatobiliary systems","how":"Fat malabsorption in ileal Crohn disease causes calcium oxalate kidney stones and gallstones, and ulcerative colitis is the main association of primary sclerosing cholangitis."},{"to":"Gross anatomy of the abdomen","how":"Artery position explains which ulcers bleed, peritoneal innervation explains appendicitis pain, and the dentate line explains which anal lesions hurt."}],"terms":[{"term":"Erosion","def":"A loss of mucosa that stops above the muscularis mucosae and so heals without a scar."},{"term":"Ulcer","def":"A defect that extends through the muscularis mucosae into the submucosa or deeper and heals by fibrosis."},{"term":"Metaplasia","def":"Reversible replacement of one mature cell type by another that better tolerates a chronic stress, such as columnar for squamous epithelium in Barrett esophagus."},{"term":"Dysplasia","def":"Disordered growth with cytologic atypia that has not invaded the basement membrane; it is the direct precursor of carcinoma."},{"term":"Transmural inflammation","def":"Inflammation through the full thickness of the bowel wall, the feature that allows fistulas and strictures in Crohn disease."},{"term":"False diverticulum","def":"An outpouching of mucosa and submucosa only, pushed through a gap in the muscularis propria."},{"term":"Transient LES relaxation","def":"A vagally mediated relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter, not triggered by swallowing, that is the main moment reflux occurs."},{"term":"Achlorhydria","def":"Absence of gastric hydrochloric acid secretion, as after parietal cell loss in autoimmune gastritis."},{"term":"Crypt abscess","def":"A collection of neutrophils within the lumen of a colonic crypt, typical of active ulcerative colitis."},{"term":"Pneumoperitoneum","def":"Free gas in the peritoneal cavity, seen under the diaphragm on an upright film after perforation of a hollow viscus."},{"term":"Dentate line","def":"The pectinate line of the anal canal, which marks the change from visceral (painless) to somatic (painful) sensory innervation and holds the openings of the anal glands."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-07-C01","obj":"GI-13-G.1","title":"Appendicitis: obstruction, ischemia, and migrating pain","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The appendix is a narrow blind tube arising from the cecum, with a wall rich in lymphoid tissue. Because it has one opening, anything that blocks the lumen turns it into a closed sac. In adults the usual obstruction is a fecalith (a hardened concretion of stool); in children and adolescents it is often hyperplasia of submucosal lymphoid follicles after a viral or bacterial infection. Tumors, such as an appendiceal neuroendocrine tumor, and parasites are rare causes."},{"type":"p","text":"Behind the obstruction, mucosa keeps secreting mucus and bacteria keep multiplying, so intraluminal pressure rises. Pressure first collapses thin-walled veins, which causes congestion and edema, and then exceeds arterial inflow, which makes the wall ischemic. Ischemic mucosa loses its barrier, and colonic flora such as Escherichia coli and Bacteroides fragilis invade the wall. Neutrophils infiltrating the muscularis propria are the histologic criterion for acute appendicitis. If the process continues, the wall becomes gangrenous and perforates, usually after about 24 to 48 hours, releasing pus to form a periappendiceal abscess or diffuse peritonitis."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of acute appendicitis","steps":["Fecalith or lymphoid hyperplasia blocks the lumen","Trapped mucus and bacteria raise intraluminal pressure","Venous outflow fails, then arterial inflow fails","Bacteria invade the ischemic wall; neutrophils fill muscularis","Serosa inflames the parietal peritoneum","Gangrene, perforation, abscess, or peritonitis"],"caption":"Rising pressure in a closed lumen turns obstruction into ischemia, and ischemia lets bacteria cross the wall."},{"type":"h","text":"Why the pain moves"},{"type":"p","text":"Early pain comes from distension of the appendix. Its visceral afferents travel with sympathetic fibers to spinal segment T10, the same segment that supplies the skin around the umbilicus, so the brain refers dull, poorly localized pain to the periumbilical region. Distension also triggers nausea, vomiting, and anorexia through visceral reflexes. Within hours, usually within the first day, inflammation reaches the serosa and touches the parietal peritoneum, which has somatic innervation from the lower intercostal and first lumbar nerves. Pain then becomes sharp and localized at McBurney point, one third of the way from the anterior superior iliac spine to the umbilicus, with tenderness, guarding, and rebound."},{"type":"table","title":"Findings in appendicitis explained by mechanism","cols":["Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Periumbilical pain, then right lower quadrant pain","Visceral T10 referral, then somatic parietal peritoneal irritation"],["Rovsing sign (left lower quadrant pressure hurts on the right)","Left-sided pressure shifts bowel contents and peritoneum toward the inflamed right side"],["Psoas sign (pain on hip extension)","A retrocecal appendix lies on and irritates the psoas muscle"],["Obturator sign (pain on internal rotation of the flexed hip)","A pelvic appendix irritates the obturator internus"],["Low-grade fever, about 38 °C (100.4 °F), and neutrophilic leukocytosis","Cytokines (IL-1, IL-6, TNF) from the inflamed wall"],["Sudden spread of pain with rigidity","Perforation and generalized peritonitis"]],"caption":"Atypical positions (retrocecal, pelvic, or displaced upward in late pregnancy) shift where the parietal peritoneum is irritated, which explains atypical pain sites."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with 6 hours of vague pain around the umbilicus and vomiting has no right lower quadrant tenderness. Does that rule out appendicitis?","explain":"No. In early appendicitis only the visceral afferents are stimulated, so pain is referred to the T10 dermatome. Localized tenderness appears only when inflammation reaches the somatically innervated parietal peritoneum.","check":"The paragraph on why the pain moves."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-07-C02","obj":"GI-13-G.2","title":"Barrett esophagus: metaplasia to adenocarcinoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The normal esophagus is lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium that meets the columnar gastric mucosa at the squamocolumnar junction, the Z line, near the gastroesophageal junction. Squamous epithelium resists abrasion but not acid, pepsin, and bile. Barrett esophagus is the replacement of distal squamous epithelium by intestinal-type columnar epithelium containing goblet cells, which is intestinal metaplasia. It is an adaptation: columnar cells that secrete mucus survive chronic reflux better than the squamous cells they replace."},{"type":"p","text":"The metaplastic cells most likely arise from progenitor cells at the gastroesophageal junction or in esophageal gland ducts that are reprogrammed by repeated injury, with transcription factors such as CDX2 switching on an intestinal program. Endoscopy shows salmon-pink, velvety tongues of mucosa extending up from the gastroesophageal junction into pale squamous mucosa. Segments of 3 cm or more are called long-segment Barrett esophagus and carry higher risk. Biopsy shows columnar epithelium with goblet cells, whose clear mucin vacuoles stain blue with Alcian blue at acid pH."},{"type":"h","text":"The dysplasia to adenocarcinoma sequence"},{"type":"p","text":"Metaplasia itself is not cancer, but the new epithelium proliferates under constant injury and accumulates genetic damage. Early events include loss of CDKN2A (p16); TP53 mutation, aneuploidy, and genomic instability are closely linked to progression to high-grade dysplasia and cancer. Morphologically this appears as low-grade dysplasia, then high-grade dysplasia, then invasive adenocarcinoma of the distal esophagus. Most people with Barrett esophagus never develop cancer, yet their risk is many times that of the general population, and high-grade dysplasia carries a substantial short-term risk of invasion."},{"type":"flow","title":"Reflux to adenocarcinoma","steps":["Chronic acid, pepsin, and bile reflux","Squamous injury: reflux esophagitis","Intestinal metaplasia with goblet cells (Barrett)","Low-grade dysplasia","High-grade dysplasia","Invasive adenocarcinoma of the distal esophagus"],"caption":"Each step is a histologic stage; only dysplasia and carcinoma are neoplastic."},{"type":"table","title":"Esophageal adenocarcinoma versus squamous cell carcinoma","cols":["Feature","Adenocarcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma"],"rows":[["Precursor","Barrett esophagus","Squamous dysplasia"],["Site","Distal third","Middle third most often"],["Main risks","Chronic GERD, obesity, white men, smoking","Smoking, alcohol, hot beverages, nitrosamines, achalasia, caustic injury"]],"caption":"Barrett esophagus explains why adenocarcinoma is distal and why reflux and obesity are its risk factors."},{"type":"p","text":"Risk factors for Barrett esophagus are those of long-standing reflux: duration of GERD, central obesity, male sex, white race, older age, and smoking. Many patients have only ordinary heartburn, and some have few symptoms, because the metaplastic lining is less sensitive to acid than squamous mucosa."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-07-C03","obj":"GI-13-G.3","title":"Diverticulosis and diverticulitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A true diverticulum contains all layers of the wall, as in Meckel diverticulum. Colonic diverticula are false diverticula, also called pseudodiverticula: mucosa and submucosa herniate through the muscularis propria and are covered only by serosa. They form at points of weakness where the vasa recta, the small straight arteries supplying the mucosa, pierce the circular muscle, usually in rows between the mesenteric and antimesenteric taeniae."},{"type":"h","text":"Why pressure builds in the sigmoid"},{"type":"p","text":"A low-fiber diet produces small, hard stools. To move them, the colon uses vigorous segmental contractions that close off short chambers, and the pressure inside each chamber rises steeply. The law of Laplace for a cylinder (pressure equals wall tension divided by radius) shows that, for the same wall tension, the narrowest segment develops the highest pressure, and the sigmoid has the smallest diameter. Age-related changes in collagen and elastin weaken the wall further, which is why prevalence rises sharply after 60 years and why connective tissue disorders such as Ehlers-Danlos syndrome predispose. In Western populations about 90% or more of diverticula are left-sided, mostly sigmoid; right-sided disease is more common in Asian populations."},{"type":"table","title":"Diverticulosis versus diverticulitis","cols":["Feature","Diverticulosis","Diverticular bleeding","Diverticulitis"],"rows":[["Mechanism","Uncomplicated outpouchings","A vas rectum stretched over the dome thins and ruptures into the lumen","Neck obstructed by stool; inflammation and microperforation"],["Pain","Usually none","None","Left lower quadrant pain and tenderness"],["Systemic signs","None","Hypovolemia if bleeding is brisk","Fever, neutrophilic leukocytosis"],["Typical finding","Incidental on colonoscopy or imaging","Sudden painless hematochezia, often large and self-limited","Wall thickening and pericolic fat stranding on CT"],["Complications","Bleeding or diverticulitis","Recurrent bleeding","Abscess, perforation with peritonitis, fistula, stricture"]],"caption":"Bleeding is arterial and painless because it comes from a ruptured vessel without inflammation; pain and fever mean inflammation."},{"type":"p","text":"Diverticular bleeding is arterial because the vas rectum, draped over the dome and covered only by mucosa, thins until it ruptures. Inflammation is absent, so there is no pain or fever. Diverticulitis starts when stool obstructs the neck, the trapped sac becomes inflamed, and microperforation lets bacteria into pericolic fat. Because the sigmoid is involved, pain sits in the left lower quadrant, which is why diverticulitis is sometimes called left-sided appendicitis."},{"type":"p","text":"When an inflamed segment adheres to a neighboring organ and erodes into it, a fistula forms. A colovesical fistula, the most common, carries gas and stool into the bladder, causing pneumaturia, fecaluria, and recurrent polymicrobial urinary infection; it is more common in men, because in women the uterus lies between the sigmoid and the bladder. Repeated attacks cause fibrosis and stricture with obstruction."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-07-C04","obj":"GI-13-G.4","title":"Noninfectious enteritis and colitis: radiation, NSAIDs, and microscopic colitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Not every inflamed bowel is infected or part of inflammatory bowel disease. Radiation, drugs, and immune reactions to luminal antigens injure the mucosa by their own mechanisms, and each leaves a recognizable pattern."},{"type":"h","text":"Radiation enteritis and proctitis"},{"type":"p","text":"Ionizing radiation damages DNA most in rapidly dividing cells, and crypt stem cells renew the intestinal epithelium every few days. Within days to weeks of pelvic or abdominal radiation, crypt cells die, villi shorten, and the surface fails to absorb, causing diarrhea, cramping, and tenesmus. This acute phase usually resolves as surviving stem cells repopulate. Months to years later, a chronic phase appears because radiation also injures small arteries, producing obliterative endarteritis with intimal thickening. Chronic ischemia leads to submucosal fibrosis, strictures with obstruction, ulcers, and fragile mucosal telangiectasias that bleed, most often in the rectum."},{"type":"h","text":"NSAID enteropathy and colopathy"},{"type":"p","text":"NSAIDs injure the small intestine and colon as well as the stomach. Loss of cyclooxygenase-derived prostaglandins reduces mucus, blood flow, and epithelial repair, and the drugs also uncouple mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation in enterocytes and increase mucosal permeability. Bile and bacteria then enter the injured mucosa and cause erosions and ulcers. The result is occult blood loss with iron deficiency anemia, protein loss, and, after repeated healing, thin circumferential fibrous webs called diaphragm strictures, which are characteristic of NSAID injury."},{"type":"h","text":"Microscopic colitis"},{"type":"p","text":"Microscopic colitis causes chronic, watery, nonbloody diarrhea, often with urgency and nocturnal stools, in middle-aged and older adults, more often women. The colon looks normal at colonoscopy, so the diagnosis requires biopsy. It is thought to be an abnormal mucosal immune response to luminal antigens, linked to drugs (NSAIDs, proton pump inhibitors, SSRIs) and to autoimmune conditions such as celiac disease and autoimmune thyroid disease. Inflammation and the thickened subepithelial layer impair sodium and water absorption, so diarrhea is secretory in character and persists with fasting in many patients."},{"type":"table","title":"Two forms of microscopic colitis","cols":["Feature","Collagenous colitis","Lymphocytic colitis"],"rows":[["Defining lesion","Thick subepithelial collagen band, more than about 10 µm (normal about 3 to 5 µm)","Intraepithelial lymphocytes above about 20 per 100 surface epithelial cells"],["Shared features","Lamina propria lymphocytes and plasma cells; surface epithelial damage","Same"],["Endoscopy","Normal or near normal","Normal or near normal"],["Typical patient","Older woman with chronic watery diarrhea","Similar; association with celiac disease is notable"]],"caption":"Biopsy is the only way to make the diagnosis because the mucosa looks normal."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 68-year-old woman taking an NSAID and a proton pump inhibitor has 3 months of watery diarrhea without blood, and colonoscopy is normal. What is the most likely finding on random colon biopsies, and why does the colon look normal?","explain":"A thick subepithelial collagen band or excess intraepithelial lymphocytes, indicating microscopic colitis. The disease is confined to the epithelium and lamina propria, without ulcers or erosions, so it changes absorption but not the visible surface.","check":"The microscopic colitis paragraph and table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-07-C05","obj":"GI-13-G.5","title":"Peptic ulcer disease: gastric versus duodenal","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A peptic ulcer forms when acid and pepsin overcome the mucosal defenses: the mucus and bicarbonate layer, tight junctions, rapid epithelial restitution, and a rich mucosal blood flow that removes back-diffusing acid. Prostaglandins E2 and I2, made by cyclooxygenase, sustain mucus, bicarbonate, and blood flow, and PGE2 also restrains parietal cell acid secretion. Most ulcers are caused by Helicobacter pylori or NSAIDs, and the two act differently."},{"type":"p","text":"H. pylori is a curved, flagellated, urease-positive gram-negative rod that lives under the mucus layer. Urease splits urea into ammonia, which buffers acid around the organism and also injures epithelium, while the CagA and VacA proteins drive inflammation. Antral inflammation reduces the number and output of somatostatin-secreting D cells, so G cells lose their brake, gastrin rises, and parietal cells in the healthy body secrete more acid. That acid load reaches the duodenum, which responds with gastric metaplasia that H. pylori can then colonize, producing a duodenal ulcer. NSAIDs act mainly by inhibiting COX-1, which removes prostaglandin protection, and their direct topical injury adds to this; they cause gastric ulcers more often than duodenal ones."},{"type":"flow","title":"Antral H. pylori and duodenal ulcer","steps":["H. pylori colonizes the antrum","Inflammation reduces somatostatin from D cells","G cells released from inhibition: gastrin rises","Parietal cells in the spared body secrete more acid","Acid load causes duodenal gastric metaplasia and ulcer"],"caption":"Antral infection raises acid; diffuse body infection instead lowers acid and favors gastric ulcer and cancer."},{"type":"p","text":"Zollinger-Ellison syndrome is caused by a gastrinoma, usually in the duodenum or pancreas and sometimes part of MEN1. Autonomous gastrin causes parietal cell hyperplasia and enormous acid output, giving multiple or refractory ulcers, ulcers in unusual sites such as the distal duodenum and jejunum, and diarrhea because acid inactivates pancreatic lipase and injures the small bowel mucosa."},{"type":"table","title":"Gastric versus duodenal ulcer","cols":["Feature","Gastric ulcer","Duodenal ulcer"],"rows":[["Usual site","Lesser curvature, near the antrum and body junction","First part of the duodenum, within a few centimeters of the pylorus"],["Dominant problem","Weak mucosal defense; acid normal or low","Excess acid delivery"],["Main causes","H. pylori, NSAIDs","H. pylori (antral), Zollinger-Ellison, NSAIDs"],["Pain with meals","Worse with eating; food aversion and weight loss","Eases with eating, returns 2 to 3 hours later and at night"],["Malignancy","A gastric adenocarcinoma can look like an ulcer, so gastric ulcers are biopsied","Essentially never malignant"],["Typical bleeding vessel","Left gastric artery (lesser curvature)","Gastroduodenal artery (posterior wall)"]],"caption":"Food buffers acid, which relieves duodenal pain until gastric emptying ends; in the stomach a meal stimulates acid and pepsin onto the ulcer."},{"type":"h","text":"Complications follow anatomy"},{"type":"p","text":"Hemorrhage is the most common complication and presents as hematemesis, coffee-ground emesis, or melena. The gastroduodenal artery runs behind the first part of the duodenum, so a posterior ulcer that erodes it bleeds briskly; a posterior ulcer may also penetrate into the pancreas, causing back pain and raised amylase. An anterior duodenal ulcer, or a gastric ulcer, can instead perforate into the free peritoneal cavity. Air escapes and rises to the highest point, so an upright chest film shows free air under the diaphragm, usually under the right hemidiaphragm. Gastric acid causes chemical peritonitis with sudden severe pain, a rigid abdomen, and sometimes shoulder pain referred through the phrenic nerve (C3 to C5). Scarring at the pylorus can cause gastric outlet obstruction with vomiting of undigested food."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-07-C06","obj":"GI-13-G.6","title":"Acute erosive gastritis and stress ulcers","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Acute gastritis is a transient mucosal inflammation that develops when something abruptly weakens mucosal defense or perfusion. In its severe form, acute erosive hemorrhagic gastritis, the surface shows multiple erosions and petechial hemorrhages. Because erosions stop above the muscularis mucosae, they bleed from capillaries rather than named arteries and heal without scarring if the cause is removed. Patients may have epigastric pain, nausea, and vomiting, or present only with hematemesis, coffee-ground emesis, or melena."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of acute erosive gastritis by mechanism","cols":["Cause","Main mechanism"],"rows":[["NSAIDs, including low-dose aspirin","COX-1 inhibition removes prostaglandin support of mucus, bicarbonate, and blood flow; weak acids are trapped and injure epithelial cells directly"],["Alcohol (heavy or binge)","Direct toxicity to surface cells, disruption of the mucus barrier, and increased back-diffusion of acid"],["Shock, sepsis, severe trauma","Splanchnic vasoconstriction causes mucosal ischemia, so bicarbonate delivery and acid removal fail"],["Uremia","Ammonia generated from urea inhibits bicarbonate transport into the mucus layer, and uremic toxins injure the mucosa"],["Chemotherapy, radiation, bile reflux","Direct epithelial injury and impaired renewal"]],"caption":"Every cause either removes a defense or adds an aggressive factor."},{"type":"h","text":"Stress ulcers"},{"type":"p","text":"Stress-related mucosal disease occurs in critically ill patients, particularly those with shock, sepsis, respiratory failure, coagulopathy, burns, or head injury. The central mechanism is mucosal ischemia: low splanchnic perfusion reduces delivery of bicarbonate and removal of back-diffusing hydrogen ions, so epithelial cells become acidotic and die. Stress lesions are usually multiple, shallow, and most common in the acid-secreting body and fundus, and they progress from erosions to ulcers when ischemia persists."},{"type":"p","text":"Two named stress ulcers differ in mechanism. A Curling ulcer follows severe burns. Plasma leaks from burned capillaries, causing hypovolemia, and the resulting splanchnic vasoconstriction makes the gastric and proximal duodenal mucosa ischemic; acid output is not the driver. A Cushing ulcer follows intracranial injury, tumor, or surgery. Raised intracranial pressure or direct injury stimulates the vagal nuclei, and vagal acetylcholine drives parietal cells to secrete excess acid. Cushing ulcers occur in the stomach, duodenum, or esophagus and tend to be deeper, so they carry a higher risk of perforation."},{"type":"table","title":"Curling versus Cushing ulcer","cols":["Feature","Curling ulcer","Cushing ulcer"],"rows":[["Setting","Severe burns","Head injury, brain tumor, neurosurgery"],["Key mechanism","Hypovolemia and mucosal ischemia","Vagal stimulation and acid hypersecretion"],["Acid output","Not raised; ischemia is the driver","High"],["Typical site","Stomach and proximal duodenum","Stomach, duodenum, or esophagus; often deeper"]],"caption":"Curling is a defense failure from low perfusion; Cushing is an aggressive-factor excess from vagal drive."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-07-C07","obj":"GI-13-G.7","title":"Reflux, eosinophilic, and pill esophagitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Normal esophageal squamous epithelium has a basal zone of no more than about 15% of its thickness, and connective tissue papillae that do not reach the upper third of the epithelium. Injury speeds cell turnover, so the basal zone expands and the papillae lengthen. The cause of the injury, however, determines where the lesion is, which cells infiltrate, and what the patient notices."},{"type":"h","text":"Reflux esophagitis"},{"type":"p","text":"Acid and pepsin repeatedly kill surface cells of the distal esophagus. The basal zone expands to more than about 20% of the epithelial thickness (basal cell hyperplasia), papillae extend into the upper third, intercellular spaces dilate, and scattered eosinophils, neutrophils, and lymphocytes enter the epithelium. Changes are greatest just above the gastroesophageal junction, and the typical patient has heartburn and regurgitation."},{"type":"h","text":"Eosinophilic esophagitis"},{"type":"p","text":"Eosinophilic esophagitis is a chronic Th2-mediated allergic disease triggered mainly by food antigens. IL-5 and IL-13 recruit and activate eosinophils, and IL-13 induces epithelial eotaxin-3, which draws in more. Eosinophil granule proteins and TGF-beta cause subepithelial fibrosis and smooth muscle remodeling, so the wall stiffens. Patients are often young men with asthma, eczema, or food allergy, who report solid food dysphagia and food impaction. The diagnosis needs 15 or more intraepithelial eosinophils per high-power field, often with eosinophil microabscesses, and involvement of the proximal as well as distal esophagus. Endoscopy shows concentric rings, longitudinal furrows, and white exudates. Because acid is not the primary cause, reflux control alone often does not normalize the biopsy."},{"type":"h","text":"Pill esophagitis"},{"type":"p","text":"A tablet that lodges in the esophagus dissolves against one patch of mucosa and burns it. Doxycycline and tetracycline are acidic, potassium chloride is hyperosmolar and caustic, NSAIDs act topically and by prostaglandin loss, and bisphosphonates and iron are also common culprits. Pills stick where the esophagus is narrowed by the aortic arch or an enlarged left atrium, especially when taken with little water just before lying down. The patient has sudden odynophagia and retrosternal pain, and endoscopy shows a discrete ulcer with normal mucosa around it."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing the three","cols":["Feature","Reflux esophagitis","Eosinophilic esophagitis","Pill esophagitis"],"rows":[["Cause","Acid and pepsin","Th2 allergy to food antigens","Local chemical burn"],["Location","Distal","Proximal and distal","Mid esophagus at narrowing"],["Histology","Basal cell hyperplasia, elongated papillae, few eosinophils","15 or more eosinophils per high-power field, microabscesses","Focal ulcer with nonspecific inflammation"],["Endoscopy","Erythema, erosions above the junction","Rings, furrows, white exudates","Discrete ulcer, normal surrounding mucosa"],["Main symptom","Heartburn, regurgitation","Dysphagia, food impaction","Sudden odynophagia"]],"caption":"Eosinophils alone do not separate reflux from eosinophilic esophagitis; density and proximal involvement do."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A biopsy from the distal esophagus shows 6 eosinophils per high-power field with basal cell hyperplasia, and the proximal biopsy is normal. Which diagnosis fits better?","explain":"Reflux esophagitis. Reflux brings a few eosinophils to the distal epithelium, but eosinophilic esophagitis requires 15 or more per high-power field and usually involves the proximal esophagus as well.","check":"The comparison table in C07."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-07-C08","obj":"GI-13-G.8","title":"Chronic gastritis: H. pylori versus autoimmune","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The stomach has two functional zones. The body and fundus contain oxyntic glands with parietal cells, which secrete acid and intrinsic factor, and chief cells, which secrete pepsinogen. The antrum contains G cells that release gastrin and D cells that release somatostatin. The two main chronic gastritides attack opposite zones, which explains why their effects on acid, gastrin, and B12 are opposite."},{"type":"h","text":"H. pylori gastritis"},{"type":"p","text":"H. pylori gastritis is the most common chronic gastritis worldwide. It begins in the antrum, where fewer parietal cells mean a less acidic niche. Histology shows lymphocytes and plasma cells in the lamina propria, neutrophils in the gland necks when the disease is active, lymphoid follicles, and curved rods in the surface mucus. Over decades, inflammation can spread to the body, destroy glands (atrophy), and cause intestinal metaplasia. This multifocal atrophic gastritis is the field in which intestinal-type gastric adenocarcinoma arises through the sequence inflammation, atrophy, metaplasia, dysplasia, carcinoma. Chronic antigen stimulation also drives B-cell proliferation in the induced lymphoid follicles, which can become extranodal marginal zone (MALT) lymphoma; early lesions may regress when the antigen is removed, while those with t(11;18) usually do not."},{"type":"h","text":"Autoimmune gastritis"},{"type":"p","text":"Autoimmune gastritis is caused by CD4 T cells directed against the parietal cell H+/K+ ATPase, accompanied by antibodies to that pump and to intrinsic factor. Parietal cells in the body and fundus are destroyed, while the antrum is spared. Loss of acid removes the negative feedback on antral G cells, so gastrin rises very high; gastrin is trophic to enterochromaffin-like cells, which become hyperplastic and can form gastric neuroendocrine (carcinoid) tumors. Loss of intrinsic factor prevents ileal absorption of B12, causing pernicious anemia: megaloblastic anemia, glossitis, and subacute combined degeneration of the dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts. Because acid helps absorb dietary iron, iron deficiency may appear first. The disease is linked to other autoimmune disorders such as Hashimoto thyroiditis and type 1 diabetes, and also raises adenocarcinoma risk."},{"type":"table","title":"H. pylori versus autoimmune gastritis","cols":["Feature","H. pylori gastritis","Autoimmune gastritis"],"rows":[["Region","Antrum first, may spread to body","Body and fundus; antrum spared"],["Cause","Infection","T cells and antibodies against H+/K+ ATPase and intrinsic factor"],["Acid","Normal or raised in antral disease; low if atrophy spreads","Low to absent (achlorhydria)"],["Serum gastrin","Normal or mildly raised","Markedly raised"],["Serum pepsinogen I","Normal or raised early","Low (chief cells lost)"],["Hallmark","Neutrophils in gland necks, lymphoid follicles","Oxyntic gland atrophy, ECL cell hyperplasia"],["Main risks","Peptic ulcer, intestinal-type adenocarcinoma, MALT lymphoma","Pernicious anemia, neuroendocrine tumor, adenocarcinoma"]],"caption":"Which zone is destroyed decides acid, gastrin, and B12 status."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading a high gastrin","text":"Two patients have fasting gastrin about 10 times normal. Patient A has gastric pH 7 and a low B12. Patient B has gastric pH 1 and three duodenal ulcers. In A, gastrin is high because acid is absent: parietal cells are gone, so acid no longer triggers antral somatostatin to restrain G cells. This is appropriate secondary hypergastrinemia from autoimmune gastritis, and low B12 reflects lost intrinsic factor. In B, gastrin is high despite strong acid, so the feedback loop is being ignored. That inappropriate, autonomous gastrin points to a gastrinoma (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome). Gastric pH, not the gastrin value alone, separates the two."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-07-C09","obj":"GI-13-G.9","title":"Gastroesophageal reflux disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The antireflux barrier has three parts. The lower esophageal sphincter (LES) is a 3 to 4 cm zone of tonically contracted smooth muscle with a resting pressure of about 10 to 30 mm Hg above gastric pressure. The right crus of the diaphragm wraps around it and squeezes during inspiration. The intra-abdominal position of the LES and the acute angle of His add a flap valve effect. Once acid does reflux, peristalsis clears the volume and swallowed salivary bicarbonate neutralizes what is left."},{"type":"h","text":"Three ways the barrier fails"},{"type":"p","text":"Transient LES relaxations are the most common mechanism. They are vagovagal reflexes triggered by distension of the gastric fundus, are not preceded by a swallow, and last longer than the brief relaxation that accompanies swallowing; this is why reflux follows large and fatty meals. Low resting LES tone allows free reflux at any time; it occurs with scleroderma, in which smooth muscle fibrosis also weakens peristalsis, and with smoking, alcohol, chocolate, and drugs that relax smooth muscle such as anticholinergics, calcium channel blockers, and nitrates. A sliding hiatal hernia pulls the LES up into the chest, separates it from the crural pinch, and creates a supradiaphragmatic pouch where acid pools and refluxes."},{"type":"p","text":"Obesity raises intra-abdominal pressure, which increases the gastroesophageal pressure gradient and promotes hiatal hernia. Pregnancy combines progesterone, which relaxes LES smooth muscle, with upward pressure from the uterus."},{"type":"flow","title":"From barrier failure to complications","steps":["Transient relaxations, low LES tone, or hiatal hernia","Acid, pepsin, and bile reach the esophagus","Clearance fails (supine, weak peristalsis, little saliva)","Squamous injury: reflux esophagitis","Deep ulceration heals by fibrosis: peptic stricture","Chronic injury: Barrett metaplasia"],"caption":"Reflux injures only when exposure outlasts clearance; complications depend on depth and duration."},{"type":"table","title":"Manifestations of GERD and their mechanism","cols":["Manifestation","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Heartburn, worse after meals, lying down, or bending","Acid stimulates chemoreceptors in the distal esophageal mucosa; posture and gastric distension promote reflux"],["Regurgitation, sour taste","Gastric contents reach the pharynx"],["Chronic cough, asthma flares","Microaspiration and an esophagobronchial vagal reflex"],["Hoarseness, laryngitis","Acid and pepsin injure the larynx (laryngopharyngeal reflux)"],["Dental erosion","Acid dissolves enamel"],["Noncardiac chest pain","Esophageal and cardiac afferents converge on the same spinal segments"],["Progressive solid food dysphagia","Peptic stricture: smooth, short, distal fibrotic narrowing"]],"caption":"Extraesophageal symptoms can occur without heartburn."},{"type":"p","text":"A peptic stricture forms when ulceration reaches the submucosa and heals by collagen deposition, narrowing the distal lumen and producing slowly progressive dysphagia for solids. Barrett metaplasia (C02) links chronic GERD to esophageal adenocarcinoma."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-07-C10","obj":"GI-13-G.10","title":"Crohn disease versus ulcerative colitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) is chronic, relapsing inflammation that results from an inappropriate mucosal immune response to commensal gut bacteria in a genetically susceptible host, with a defective epithelial barrier. In Crohn disease, loss-of-function variants of NOD2, an intracellular sensor of bacterial peptidoglycan, and of autophagy genes such as ATG16L1 impair the handling of bacteria that cross the epithelium. Macrophages then drive Th1 (interferon gamma) and Th17 (IL-17) responses that organize into granulomas and extend through the wall. Ulcerative colitis (UC) shows a mucosal response with more IL-13 and a defective mucus and epithelial barrier. Smoking raises the risk of Crohn disease but is associated with a lower risk of UC."},{"type":"h","text":"Distribution and depth decide everything"},{"type":"p","text":"Crohn disease can affect any segment from mouth to anus, most often the terminal ileum and proximal colon, with skip lesions of normal bowel between diseased segments, and the rectum is often spared. Inflammation is transmural. Early lesions are aphthous ulcers over lymphoid aggregates that merge into long, knife-like linear fissures; fissures between edematous mucosa create a cobblestone appearance. Mesenteric fat wraps around the serosa (creeping fat), and the thick fibrotic wall narrows the lumen, giving the string sign on barium study. Noncaseating granulomas are characteristic but are found in only a minority of biopsies, so their absence does not exclude Crohn disease. Patients typically have crampy right lower quadrant pain, diarrhea usually without gross blood, weight loss, and perianal disease."},{"type":"p","text":"UC always involves the rectum and extends proximally in a continuous pattern, limited to the colon apart from mild backwash ileitis. Inflammation is confined to the mucosa and superficial submucosa. Neutrophils enter crypts (cryptitis) and fill them (crypt abscesses), and chronic injury distorts and branches crypts. Broad ulcers leave islands of regenerating mucosa that stand up as pseudopolyps, and long-standing disease shortens the colon and erases haustra (lead pipe colon). Because the friable mucosa bleeds and the rectum is inflamed, patients have bloody diarrhea with mucus, urgency, and tenesmus."},{"type":"table","title":"Crohn disease versus ulcerative colitis","cols":["Feature","Crohn disease","Ulcerative colitis"],"rows":[["Location","Mouth to anus; terminal ileum most often; rectum often spared","Rectum always, extending proximally in the colon"],["Distribution","Skip lesions","Continuous"],["Depth","Transmural","Mucosa and superficial submucosa"],["Gross","Cobblestone mucosa, linear fissures, creeping fat, thick wall, strictures","Friable bleeding mucosa, broad ulcers, pseudopolyps, loss of haustra"],["Microscopy","Noncaseating granulomas, transmural lymphoid aggregates","Crypt abscesses, crypt distortion, no granulomas"],["Immune profile","Th1 and Th17","Atypical Th2 (IL-13)"],["Typical stool","Diarrhea, usually nonbloody","Bloody diarrhea with mucus, tenesmus"],["Serology","Anti-Saccharomyces cerevisiae antibodies (ASCA) more often","Perinuclear ANCA (p-ANCA) more often"],["Smoking","Raises risk","Associated with lower risk"]],"caption":"Transmural versus mucosal depth explains most downstream differences, including the complications in C11."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-07-C11","obj":"GI-13-G.11","title":"Complications of inflammatory bowel disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Complications are predictable from two facts in C10: Crohn disease is transmural and favors the terminal ileum, while ulcerative colitis is mucosal and confined to the colon."},{"type":"h","text":"Crohn disease: depth and the ileum"},{"type":"p","text":"Transmural fissures can tunnel through the serosa into an adherent structure, forming fistulas to other loops of bowel (enteroenteric), the bladder (pneumaturia and recurrent infection), the vagina, or the skin, and perianal fistulas and abscesses are common. Repeated transmural inflammation heals by fibrosis, so strictures cause colicky pain and small bowel obstruction. The terminal ileum is the only site that absorbs vitamin B12 bound to intrinsic factor and that actively reabsorbs conjugated bile acids. Ileal disease or resection therefore causes B12 deficiency with megaloblastic anemia. When less bile acid is lost, the excess entering the colon stimulates chloride and water secretion and causes bile acid diarrhea; when the loss exceeds hepatic synthesis, the bile acid pool shrinks, micelles fail, and steatorrhea follows. A depleted bile acid pool also makes bile supersaturated with cholesterol, causing cholesterol gallstones."},{"type":"flow","title":"Ileal Crohn disease to calcium oxalate stones","steps":["Diseased or resected terminal ileum","Bile acids lost; fat malabsorbed","Fatty acids bind luminal calcium (soaps)","Oxalate left free in the colon","Colonic oxalate absorption rises","Hyperoxaluria: calcium oxalate kidney stones"],"caption":"Enteric hyperoxaluria needs an intact colon; bile acids and fatty acids also make colonic mucosa more permeable to oxalate."},{"type":"h","text":"Ulcerative colitis: the whole colon and the bile ducts"},{"type":"p","text":"In a severe flare, inflammation can extend into the muscularis propria, and inflammatory mediators such as nitric oxide paralyze colonic smooth muscle. The colon, usually the transverse segment, dilates beyond about 6 cm with fever, tachycardia, and leukocytosis; this toxic megacolon can perforate. Hypokalemia and anticholinergic or opioid drugs worsen the paralysis. Primary sclerosing cholangitis, an immune-mediated concentric onion-skin fibrosis of intrahepatic and extrahepatic bile ducts that gives a beaded cholangiogram and cholestatic enzymes, is strongly linked to UC; most patients with it have IBD, while only a few percent of UC patients develop it. It raises the risk of cholangiocarcinoma and of colorectal cancer."},{"type":"h","text":"Complications shared by both"},{"type":"p","text":"Colorectal cancer risk rises with longer duration (from about 8 to 10 years after onset), greater extent of colonic involvement, and coexisting primary sclerosing cholangitis, so it applies to Crohn colitis as well as UC. Chronic inflammation and regeneration drive carcinogenesis from flat dysplasia, with TP53 mutation occurring early and APC loss late, the reverse of the sporadic adenoma to carcinoma sequence."},{"type":"table","title":"Extraintestinal manifestations","cols":["Manifestation","Description","Relation to bowel activity"],"rows":[["Erythema nodosum","Tender red nodules on the shins; septal panniculitis","Parallels bowel activity"],["Pyoderma gangrenosum","Painful ulcer with undermined violaceous border","Often independent"],["Uveitis and episcleritis","Eye pain, redness, photophobia (uveitis)","Episcleritis parallels activity; uveitis often independent"],["Peripheral arthritis","Large-joint, nonerosive","Parallels bowel activity"],["Ankylosing spondylitis, sacroiliitis","Axial arthritis, linked to HLA-B27","Independent"]],"caption":"All occur in both diseases; primary sclerosing cholangitis is the main extraintestinal link specific to UC."},{"type":"example","title":"Why a Crohn patient forms oxalate stones","text":"A man had 120 cm of terminal ileum removed for Crohn disease, keeps an intact colon, and now has steatorrhea and a calcium oxalate kidney stone. Normally, dietary calcium binds oxalate in the gut lumen, forming an insoluble salt that leaves in stool. With the ileum gone, bile acids are lost and the pool shrinks, so fat is not absorbed. Unabsorbed fatty acids bind calcium as soaps, leaving oxalate soluble. Free oxalate, and colonic mucosa made more permeable by bile acids and fatty acids, together raise oxalate absorption and urinary oxalate. Stones form despite normal blood calcium. A patient with an ileostomy and no colon would not develop this mechanism."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-07-C12","obj":"GI-13-G.12","title":"Mallory-Weiss syndrome versus Boerhaave syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Both conditions begin with the same physical event. During forceful retching or vomiting, the abdominal muscles and diaphragm contract forcefully while the lower esophageal sphincter and distal esophagus fail to relax in time. Intragastric pressure surges into an esophagus that has not relaxed, and the wall at and just above the gastroesophageal junction is suddenly stretched. The two syndromes differ only in how deep the wall gives way, and depth predicts every clinical difference."},{"type":"p","text":"Mallory-Weiss syndrome is a longitudinal laceration of the mucosa, sometimes reaching the submucosa, at the gastroesophageal junction, usually on the gastric cardia side. Typical settings are binge alcohol use, bulimia, and hyperemesis, and a hiatal hernia predisposes. The tear opens submucosal arteries and veins, so the patient vomits bright red blood after a bout of nonbloody retching. Pain is minimal or absent because the wall is intact, and most tears stop bleeding on their own."},{"type":"p","text":"Boerhaave syndrome is a full-thickness, transmural rupture, most often in the left posterolateral wall of the distal esophagus a few centimeters above the junction. This region is weak because its muscle layer is thinner, the esophagus lacks a serosa, and surrounding mediastinal tissue gives little support. Air, gastric acid, food, and bacteria enter the mediastinum. Air dissects along fascial planes into the neck (subcutaneous emphysema, felt as crepitus) and around the heart, where a crunching sound synchronous with the heartbeat (Hamman sign) may be heard. Mediastinal contamination causes chemical then bacterial mediastinitis with severe retrosternal pain, fever, tachycardia, and septic shock. Rupture into the left pleural cavity causes a left effusion containing salivary amylase, low pH, and food particles."},{"type":"table","title":"Tear versus rupture","cols":["Feature","Mallory-Weiss syndrome","Boerhaave syndrome"],"rows":[["Depth","Mucosa, sometimes submucosa","Transmural"],["Site","Gastroesophageal junction, often gastric side","Distal esophagus, left posterolateral wall"],["Main sign","Hematemesis after retching","Severe chest or upper abdominal pain after vomiting"],["Air outside the lumen","None","Pneumomediastinum, subcutaneous emphysema, Hamman sign"],["Systemic course","Usually self-limited bleeding","Mediastinitis, sepsis, shock; often fatal if unrecognized"],["Imaging","Normal chest film","Mediastinal air, left effusion, contrast leak on water-soluble esophagram"]],"caption":"Blood without air means a mucosal tear; air without much blood means rupture."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-07-C13","obj":"GI-13-G.13","title":"Anal fissure, abscess and fistula, proctitis, and rectal prolapse","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The dentate (pectinate) line organizes anorectal disease. Above it, the mucosa is columnar and has visceral innervation, so lesions there are relatively painless. Below it, the anoderm is squamous and supplied by the somatic inferior rectal branch of the pudendal nerve, so lesions there are very painful. The anal crypts and the ducts of the anal glands open at the dentate line, and the internal anal sphincter, a tonically contracted smooth muscle, lies deep to the anoderm."},{"type":"h","text":"Anal fissure"},{"type":"p","text":"An anal fissure is a linear tear of the anoderm below the dentate line, usually caused by passage of a hard stool. About 90% lie in the posterior midline, because the terminal branches of the inferior rectal arteries supply the posterior commissure poorly. Pain makes the internal sphincter go into spasm, the higher pressure compresses those small vessels further, and the ischemic tear fails to heal. The patient has sharp, tearing pain during and after defecation and bright red blood on the paper or stool surface. A chronic fissure shows a sentinel skin tag, a hypertrophied anal papilla, and exposed internal sphincter fibers. A fissure that is lateral, multiple, painless, or unusually large suggests Crohn disease, HIV, syphilis, tuberculosis, or carcinoma."},{"type":"h","text":"Abscess and fistula"},{"type":"p","text":"Most anorectal abscesses are cryptoglandular. An anal gland duct becomes blocked at the dentate line, the gland becomes infected, and pus spreads through the intersphincteric plane to a perianal, ischiorectal, intersphincteric, or supralevator space. The patient has constant throbbing pain, a tender fluctuant swelling, and often fever. When the abscess drains, a persistent epithelialized tract may remain between the internal opening at the crypt and an external opening on the perianal skin; this anal fistula causes chronic intermittent discharge of pus or stool. Crohn disease is the classic cause of complex, multiple, or recurrent fistulas because its transmural inflammation produces them independently of anal glands."},{"type":"table","title":"Anorectal disorders compared","cols":["Disorder","Mechanism","Key findings","Associations"],"rows":[["Anal fissure","Tear of anoderm with ischemia and sphincter spasm","Posterior midline; painful defecation; bright red blood","Constipation; atypical sites suggest Crohn, HIV, syphilis"],["Anorectal abscess","Infected obstructed anal gland","Throbbing pain, tender fluctuant mass, fever","Crohn disease, diabetes, immunosuppression"],["Anal fistula","Persistent tract from crypt to perianal skin","Chronic drainage; external opening near the anus","Prior abscess; Crohn disease (complex fistulas)"],["Proctitis","Inflammation of rectal mucosa","Tenesmus, urgency, mucus and blood","Ulcerative colitis, gonorrhea, chlamydia, HSV, syphilis, radiation"],["Rectal prolapse","Full-thickness rectum protrudes through the anus","Concentric mucosal rings; mucus, incontinence","Older women, chronic straining, pelvic floor weakness; children with cystic fibrosis"]],"caption":"Fissure and abscess are painful because they involve somatically innervated tissue below the dentate line; an open, draining fistula usually causes discharge more than pain."},{"type":"h","text":"Proctitis and rectal prolapse"},{"type":"p","text":"Proctitis is inflammation limited to the rectal mucosa. An inflamed rectum signals fullness constantly, so patients have tenesmus, urgency, and frequent small passages of mucus and blood. Causes include ulcerative proctitis (UC limited to the rectum), sexually transmitted infections acquired by receptive anal intercourse, Crohn disease, and radiation, as described in C04. Rectal prolapse is protrusion of the full thickness of the rectal wall through the anus. It shows concentric folds, unlike the radial folds of prolapsed hemorrhoids or mucosa. It results from a weak pelvic floor and lax fixation of the rectum, often with pudendal neuropathy from childbirth or chronic straining, and in children it is associated with cystic fibrosis because bulky stools and straining push the rectum out."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Across the tube, the same balance decides whether mucosa survives. Aggressive factors include acid and pepsin, bile, drugs, intraluminal pressure, bacteria, and immune effector cells. Defenses include mucus and bicarbonate secreted under prostaglandin control, tight junctions, rapid renewal from stem cells, and above all blood flow. NSAIDs show this at every level, causing gastritis, ulcer, enteropathy, colopathy, and microscopic colitis, and ischemia unites stress ulcers, appendiceal gangrene, radiation stricture, and nonhealing fissures. Anatomy then decides the presentation: depth of injury separates erosion from ulcer, tear from rupture, and UC from Crohn disease; artery position predicts bleeding; and visceral versus somatic innervation predicts pain."},{"type":"p","text":"Chronic inflammation is also the bridge to neoplasia. Repeated injury and regeneration select for mutations, which is why Barrett esophagus, atrophic gastritis with intestinal metaplasia, and long-standing colitis precede adenocarcinoma, and why persistent antigen drives MALT lymphoma. The limits of these models matter. Many people with H. pylori, Barrett esophagus, or diverticula never develop disease, symptoms correlate poorly with severity, and histologic thresholds such as eosinophil counts are diagnostic conventions rather than biologic cutoffs."}],"summary":["Appendicitis is obstruction, then rising pressure, ischemia, and bacterial invasion; pain moves from the T10 periumbilical area to McBurney point when the parietal peritoneum is inflamed.","Barrett esophagus is intestinal metaplasia with goblet cells in the distal esophagus from chronic reflux and progresses through low-grade and high-grade dysplasia to adenocarcinoma.","Colonic diverticula are false diverticula where vasa recta pierce the muscle; bleeding is painless and arterial, while diverticulitis causes left lower quadrant pain, fever, abscess, or fistula.","Microscopic colitis causes chronic watery diarrhea with normal-looking mucosa; collagenous colitis has a thick subepithelial collagen band and lymphocytic colitis excess intraepithelial lymphocytes.","Duodenal ulcers come from excess acid (antral H. pylori, gastrinoma) and ease with meals; gastric ulcers come from weak defense, worsen with meals, and are biopsied to exclude cancer.","Posterior duodenal ulcers bleed from the gastroduodenal artery; anterior ulcers perforate and cause free air under the diaphragm.","Curling ulcers follow burns through hypovolemic ischemia; Cushing ulcers follow brain injury through vagal acid hypersecretion.","Autoimmune gastritis destroys body and fundus parietal cells, causing achlorhydria, high gastrin, and pernicious anemia; H. pylori gastritis starts in the antrum and leads to ulcer, adenocarcinoma, and MALT lymphoma.","Crohn disease is transmural, skipping, and granulomatous, causing fistulas, strictures, B12 and bile acid loss, and oxalate stones; UC is continuous and mucosal from the rectum, causing toxic megacolon and linked to primary sclerosing cholangitis.","Mallory-Weiss is a bleeding mucosal tear after retching; Boerhaave is a transmural rupture with pneumomediastinum and subcutaneous emphysema."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Gastrointestinal Tract: esophagus, stomach, small intestine and colon, appendix, and anal canal"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Physiology of Gastrointestinal Disorders; secretory functions of the alimentary tract, gastric secretion"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease (McPhee and Hammer)","where":"Gastrointestinal Disease: acid peptic disease, inflammatory bowel disease, diverticular disease"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen: stomach, duodenum, appendix, colon, and visceral innervation; Pelvis and Perineum: anal canal"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Helicobacter pylori"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Digestive Tract: esophagus, stomach, small and large intestine"}]},"GI-13-H":{"unit":"GI-13-H","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-08","title":"Congenital Gastrointestinal Disease","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the congenital and early-life gastrointestinal disorders that boards test most: esophageal atresia with tracheoesophageal fistula, esophageal webs and rings, Meckel diverticulum, hypertrophic pyloric stenosis, biliary atresia, abdominal wall defects with malrotation, and cleft lip and palate. Each one traces back to a developmental step that failed: division of the foregut, regression of the vitelline duct, return and rotation of the midgut, or fusion of facial processes. Questions usually give an age, the color of the vomitus or stool, an acid-base pattern, or an associated anomaly. If you can link each clue to the anatomy that produces it, you can name the lesion and predict its complications.","prerequisites":["Foregut, midgut, and hindgut derivatives and their arterial supply (celiac trunk, superior and inferior mesenteric arteries)","Bilirubin metabolism: conjugation in the hepatocyte, biliary excretion, and enterohepatic handling","Gastric acid secretion by parietal cells and the alkaline tide","Renal handling of Na+, K+, Cl-, and HCO3-, and the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system","Pharyngeal arches and the facial prominences"],"connections":[{"to":"Embryology","how":"Every lesion in this chapter is a specific failure of foregut septation, vitelline duct regression, midgut rotation, body wall closure, or facial fusion."},{"to":"Renal and acid-base physiology","how":"Pyloric stenosis is the classic model of chloride-responsive metabolic alkalosis maintained by volume depletion and aldosterone."},{"to":"Liver and biliary disease","how":"Biliary atresia is the main surgical cause of neonatal conjugated hyperbilirubinemia and a route to biliary cirrhosis in infancy."},{"to":"Hematology","how":"Plummer-Vinson syndrome ties iron deficiency anemia to esophageal webs, and bleeding from a Meckel diverticulum can produce anemia in a young child."},{"to":"Genetics and pediatrics","how":"VACTERL, the trisomies, Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome, and 22q11.2 deletion explain why one congenital defect should prompt a search for others."}],"terms":[{"term":"Atresia","def":"Congenital absence or complete closure of a normal lumen, so the tube ends blindly or is replaced by fibrous tissue."},{"term":"Fistula","def":"An abnormal epithelium-lined channel connecting two hollow organs or an organ and the skin."},{"term":"Polyhydramnios","def":"An excess of amniotic fluid, often caused by a fetus that cannot swallow fluid or absorb it in the upper gut."},{"term":"VACTERL association","def":"A nonrandom cluster of vertebral, anal, cardiac, tracheoesophageal, renal, and limb anomalies without one known cause."},{"term":"True diverticulum","def":"An outpouching that contains all layers of the gut wall, including the muscularis propria, as opposed to a false diverticulum of mucosa and submucosa only."},{"term":"Vitelline duct","def":"The embryonic connection between the midgut and the yolk sac, also called the omphalomesenteric duct, which normally obliterates."},{"term":"Paradoxical aciduria","def":"Excretion of acidic urine despite systemic alkalemia, caused by renal Na+ and volume conservation in severe volume and chloride depletion."},{"term":"Acholic stool","def":"Pale, clay-colored stool that lacks bile pigment because conjugated bilirubin is not reaching the intestine."},{"term":"Physiologic umbilical herniation","def":"The normal temporary movement of the growing midgut loop into the umbilical cord from about week 6 to week 10."},{"term":"Ladd bands","def":"Peritoneal bands that run from a malpositioned cecum across the duodenum to the right abdominal wall and can obstruct the duodenum."},{"term":"Intermaxillary segment","def":"The merged medial nasal prominences, which form the philtrum, the premaxilla with the four upper incisors, and the primary palate."},{"term":"Bilious vomiting","def":"Green vomitus containing bile, which means the obstruction lies distal to the major duodenal papilla."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-08-C01","obj":"GI-13-H.1","title":"Esophageal atresia with distal tracheoesophageal fistula","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Esophageal atresia (EA) is a blind ending of the esophagus, and a tracheoesophageal fistula (TEF) is an abnormal channel between the trachea and the esophagus. They usually occur together, in about 1 in 3,000 to 4,500 live births. Both arise in week 4, when the respiratory diverticulum buds from the ventral wall of the foregut. Tracheoesophageal ridges grow in from each side and fuse into the tracheoesophageal septum, which separates the ventral laryngotracheal tube from the dorsal esophagus. When the septum deviates posteriorly or the separation is incomplete, the esophagus ends blindly and part of it stays connected to the trachea."},{"type":"table","title":"Anatomic types of esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula","cols":["Type","Anatomy","Approximate share","Key clue"],"rows":[["EA with distal TEF","Blind upper pouch; lower esophagus opens into the trachea","About 85%","Tube coils in the pouch; gas in stomach and bowel"],["Isolated EA","Both esophageal segments blind; no fistula","About 8%","Tube coils; gasless abdomen; marked polyhydramnios"],["H-type TEF","Esophagus continuous; fistula to the trachea","About 4%","Coughing with feeds and recurrent pneumonia, often found after the newborn period"],["Proximal fistula, with or without distal fistula","Upper pouch opens into the trachea","About 3%","Feeds enter the airway directly"]],"caption":"The common type explains most exam findings; the rarer types are recognized by whether gas reaches the stomach and whether the esophagus is continuous."},{"type":"h","text":"Why each finding appears"},{"type":"p","text":"The fetus normally swallows amniotic fluid, absorbs it in the intestine, and returns it to the mother across the placenta. A blind upper pouch blocks that route, so fluid accumulates as polyhydramnios. In the common type some fluid can still enter the gut through the fistula, so polyhydramnios is often milder than in isolated atresia. After birth the small pouch fills with saliva, which overflows as drooling and spills into the larynx. The first feed does the same on a larger scale, so the infant coughs, chokes, and becomes cyanotic. A nasogastric tube meets resistance and, on radiograph, is seen coiled in the pouch in the upper chest."},{"type":"p","text":"The distal fistula adds two problems. Each breath, and especially each cry, drives air from the trachea into the lower esophagus, so the stomach and bowel fill with gas and the abdomen distends; that gas proves a distal fistula exists. In the opposite direction, acidic gastric juice refluxes up the lower esophagus into the trachea and causes chemical pneumonitis. Tracheomalacia is common because the tracheal wall at the fistula has deficient cartilage and is structurally weak."},{"type":"flow","title":"From faulty septation to the newborn presentation","steps":["Tracheoesophageal septum deviates posteriorly in week 4","Blind upper pouch; lower esophagus joined to the trachea","Fetus cannot move swallowed fluid into the gut: polyhydramnios","Saliva and feeds overflow the pouch: drooling, choking, cyanosis","Air passes through the fistula: gastric gas and distention","Gastric acid refluxes into the trachea: chemical pneumonitis"],"caption":"Each clinical sign follows from the blind pouch or from the open fistula below it."},{"type":"p","text":"About half of infants with EA have another anomaly, most often cardiac. EA belongs to the VACTERL association: vertebral defects, anal atresia, cardiac defects, tracheoesophageal fistula with atresia, renal anomalies, and limb defects, especially of the radial ray. An association is a group of defects that occur together more often than chance would predict without one shared cause, so finding one should prompt a search for the others. EA also occurs with trisomies 18 and 21."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A newborn has a nasogastric tube coiled in the upper chest and no gas anywhere below the diaphragm. Predict the anatomic type and the amniotic fluid history.","explain":"Without a distal fistula, air cannot reach the stomach, so the abdomen is gasless; this is isolated atresia (or a proximal fistula only), and because no fluid enters the gut, polyhydramnios is usually marked.","check":"Compare with the table of anatomic types and the paragraph on the distal fistula."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-08-C02","obj":"GI-13-H.2","title":"Esophageal webs versus Schatzki rings","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Webs and rings are thin, fixed narrowings of the esophageal lumen formed by mucosa and submucosa. Although they are grouped with congenital disease, most are acquired in adults. Because the narrowing is thin and does not move, it catches a solid bolus wider than the remaining lumen while liquids pass freely. Dysphagia to solids without dysphagia to liquids therefore points to a mechanical lesion, whereas a motility disorder such as achalasia impairs both from the start. A short, fixed lesion causes intermittent symptoms that depend on bolus size, while a peptic stricture or carcinoma causes steadily progressive dysphagia, and carcinoma adds weight loss."},{"type":"h","text":"Esophageal webs and Plummer-Vinson syndrome"},{"type":"p","text":"A web is a thin, often eccentric membrane, usually on the anterior wall of the upper (cervical) esophagus just below the cricopharyngeus. Plummer-Vinson syndrome is the combination of upper esophageal webs, iron deficiency anemia, and dysphagia, seen mainly in middle-aged women and now uncommon. Other signs of iron deficiency, such as atrophic glossitis, angular cheilitis, and koilonychia, often accompany it. How iron deficiency produces webs is not fully understood; loss of iron-dependent enzymes in rapidly renewing squamous epithelium is thought to cause mucosal atrophy and injury. That chronically damaged mucosa explains the increased risk of squamous cell carcinoma of the postcricoid hypopharynx and upper esophagus."},{"type":"h","text":"Schatzki rings"},{"type":"p","text":"A Schatzki ring is a thin circumferential ring of mucosa and submucosa at the squamocolumnar junction (the Z line) in the lower esophagus, with squamous epithelium above and columnar epithelium below. It usually marks the upper edge of a sliding hiatal hernia, and gastroesophageal reflux is suspected to contribute. Symptoms are intermittent: a large bite of meat or bread lodges at the ring, sometimes as an acute food impaction, while ordinary meals pass. Dysphagia is likely when the lumen at the ring is less than about 13 mm and uncommon when it is wider than about 20 mm. A Schatzki ring has no recognized malignant potential."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing webs from Schatzki rings","cols":["Feature","Esophageal web","Schatzki ring"],"rows":[["Location","Upper (cervical) esophagus, usually anterior wall","Lower esophagus at the squamocolumnar junction"],["Shape","Thin, often eccentric membrane","Thin, circumferential ring"],["Associations","Iron deficiency anemia (Plummer-Vinson syndrome)","Sliding hiatal hernia; reflux suspected"],["Dysphagia","Solids, near the throat","Intermittent solids, food bolus impaction"],["Cancer risk","Squamous cell carcinoma of postcricoid region and upper esophagus","None recognized"]],"caption":"Level in the esophagus and the presence of iron deficiency separate the two lesions."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-08-C03","obj":"GI-13-H.3","title":"Meckel diverticulum","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"In the early embryo the midgut communicates with the yolk sac through the vitelline (omphalomesenteric) duct. The duct normally narrows, obliterates, and disappears. When its intestinal end persists, it forms a Meckel diverticulum: a blind pouch on the antimesenteric border of the distal ileum. Because it is part of the original gut tube, it contains all layers of the intestinal wall, including the muscularis propria, which makes it a true diverticulum. Acquired outpouchings such as sigmoid diverticula and Zenker diverticulum are false diverticula of mucosa and submucosa only. Other vitelline remnants include a fibrous cord from the ileum to the umbilicus, a vitelline cyst, and a patent vitelline fistula that discharges intestinal contents at the umbilicus."},{"type":"table","title":"The rule of twos (approximate values)","cols":["Feature","Approximate value"],"rows":[["Prevalence","About 2% of the population"],["Location","About 2 feet (60 cm) proximal to the ileocecal valve"],["Length","About 2 inches (5 cm)"],["Ectopic tissue types","Two: gastric (most common) and pancreatic"],["Age at symptoms","Usually before about age 2"],["Sex ratio among symptomatic cases","About 2 males to 1 female"]],"caption":"A memory aid, not a set of exact numbers; individual values vary between series."},{"type":"p","text":"Most Meckel diverticula never cause symptoms. When they do, the mechanism depends on the tissue inside and on how the diverticulum is anchored."},{"type":"flow","title":"How ectopic gastric mucosa causes painless bleeding","steps":["Ectopic gastric mucosa lines the diverticulum","Parietal cells secrete HCl into the pouch","Adjacent ileal mucosa lacks gastric-type acid defenses","Peptic ulcer forms in the ileum at the diverticulum's base","Ulcer erodes a submucosal vessel","Painless bright red or maroon rectal bleeding, possibly anemia"],"caption":"The ulcer usually sits on the ileal side of the junction, not in the gastric mucosa itself."},{"type":"p","text":"Bleeding is the most common presentation in young children. It is painless because the ulcer does not obstruct or stretch the bowel, and the blood is often bright red or maroon because brisk bleeding from the distal ileum passes quickly, before it can turn into black melena. Gastric mucosa in the pouch takes up technetium-99m pertechnetate, which is why a nuclear scan can locate it."},{"type":"p","text":"Obstruction has several mechanisms. The diverticulum can act as a lead point for intussusception, in which a proximal segment telescopes into the distal bowel, compressing its own mesenteric veins and producing colicky pain and currant jelly stool. A fibrous band to the umbilicus can twist the bowel into a volvulus or trap a loop beneath it. Inflammation of the diverticulum (diverticulitis) mimics appendicitis. The distinction to carry into questions is that Meckel bleeding is painless, whereas intussusception is painful."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-08-C04","obj":"GI-13-H.4","title":"Infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis is hypertrophy and hyperplasia of the circular smooth muscle of the pylorus that progressively narrows the gastric outlet. It affects about 2 to 3 per 1,000 infants, with a male to female ratio of about 4 to 1; first-born boys and infants with a family history are at higher risk. Exposure to a macrolide (erythromycin or azithromycin) in the first weeks of life raises the risk. Erythromycin is a motilin receptor agonist that provokes strong antral and pyloric contractions, which is the proposed link to muscle hypertrophy."},{"type":"p","text":"The pylorus is usually normal at birth and thickens over the following weeks, which is why vomiting begins at about 2 to 6 weeks. The obstruction lies above the major duodenal papilla, so the vomitus is gastric contents without bile. Vomiting becomes forceful (projectile) as the stomach contracts harder against the outlet, and gastric peristaltic waves may be seen moving left to right across the epigastrium. The infant stays hungry and feeds eagerly after vomiting, then loses weight and becomes dehydrated. The thickened pylorus may be felt as a firm, mobile, olive-shaped mass in the epigastrium or right upper quadrant, best felt after vomiting."},{"type":"h","text":"Hypochloremic hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis"},{"type":"p","text":"For every H+ a parietal cell secretes, it returns one HCO3- to the blood (the alkaline tide). Normally, pancreatic HCO3- secretion to neutralize that acid returns an equal amount of H+ to the blood. Vomiting removes the HCl before it reaches the duodenum, so the body keeps the HCO3- and loses Cl-: this generates the alkalosis. The kidney normally excretes excess HCO3-, but volume depletion lowers the filtered load and activates angiotensin II, which increases proximal Na+-H+ exchange and HCO3- reabsorption. Aldosterone increases distal Na+ reabsorption in exchange for K+ and H+ secretion, and low Cl- delivery limits the HCO3- secretion that intercalated cells use to correct alkalosis. These forces maintain the alkalosis, waste K+ in the urine, and, when depletion is severe, produce paradoxical aciduria. Urine Cl- is low, marking the alkalosis as chloride responsive. Hypokalemia comes mainly from renal loss, with a contribution from the shift of K+ into cells during alkalosis; gastric juice itself contains relatively little K+."},{"type":"flow","title":"Generation and maintenance of the metabolic alkalosis","steps":["Vomiting removes gastric HCl","Parietal cell HCO3- stays in blood: alkalosis generated","Volume loss activates renin, angiotensin II, and aldosterone","Proximal and distal tubules reabsorb Na+ with HCO3-","Aldosterone drives renal K+ and H+ secretion","Hypokalemia, low urine Cl-, paradoxical aciduria"],"caption":"Loss of HCl generates the alkalosis; volume and chloride depletion keep the kidney from correcting it."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading the electrolytes of a vomiting infant","text":"A 4-week-old first-born boy has 1 week of nonbilious projectile vomiting. Values: Na+ 133 mEq/L (about 135 to 145), K+ 2.9 mEq/L (about 3.5 to 5.0), Cl- 86 mEq/L (about 98 to 106), HCO3- 35 mEq/L (about 22 to 28), pH 7.50, PaCO2 46 mm Hg, urine Cl- 5 mEq/L, urine pH 5.5. Step 1: pH above 7.45 with high HCO3- is a metabolic alkalosis. Step 2: compensation; PaCO2 rises about 0.7 mm Hg per 1 mEq/L rise in HCO3-, so expected PaCO2 is about 40 + 0.7 x 11, or about 48 mm Hg, and 46 is appropriate hypoventilation. Step 3: the anion gap is 133 minus (86 + 35) = 12, which is normal. Step 4: low urine Cl- means chloride and volume depletion, and acidic urine in an alkalemic infant is paradoxical aciduria."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two infants vomit repeatedly: one has pyloric stenosis, the other duodenal obstruction distal to the papilla. Which one is more likely to develop a marked metabolic alkalosis, and why?","explain":"Pyloric stenosis loses pure gastric HCl; with distal obstruction the vomitus also contains alkaline bile and pancreatic juice, so HCO3- is lost along with acid and the alkalosis is smaller.","check":"Compare with the alkaline tide paragraph and the generation and maintenance flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-08-C05","obj":"GI-13-H.5","title":"Biliary atresia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Biliary atresia is a progressive, inflammatory, fibrosing destruction of the extrahepatic bile ducts that obliterates their lumen in early infancy. It affects roughly 1 in 10,000 to 20,000 live births in Western countries and is more common in East Asia. In most infants (the perinatal form) the ducts are thought to be present at birth and then destroyed by an immune-mediated attack on the biliary epithelium, possibly triggered by a virus; the cause is not established. A smaller syndromic form, present from early development, is associated with laterality defects such as polysplenia, situs inversus, and a preduodenal portal vein."},{"type":"p","text":"Hepatocytes continue to take up and conjugate bilirubin, but conjugated bilirubin cannot leave the liver through the obliterated ducts. It regurgitates into the blood, so the hyperbilirubinemia is conjugated (direct). Conjugated bilirubin is water soluble and loosely bound to albumin, so it is filtered by the glomerulus and darkens the urine. No bilirubin reaches the intestine, so no stercobilin is formed and the stools become pale or acholic. Unconjugated bilirubin, by contrast, is tightly bound to albumin, never appears in urine, and leaves stool color normal. A conjugated bilirubin above about 1 mg/dL (about 17 micromol/L) is abnormal at any age and requires evaluation."},{"type":"table","title":"Physiologic jaundice versus the jaundice of biliary atresia","cols":["Feature","Physiologic jaundice of the newborn","Biliary atresia"],"rows":[["Type of bilirubin","Unconjugated","Conjugated"],["Timing","Appears after the first 24 hours, fades by about 2 weeks","Persists or appears after about 2 weeks"],["Urine","Normal color","Dark"],["Stool","Normal pigment","Pale, acholic"],["Liver","Normal","Enlarged and firm, later cirrhotic"],["Mechanism","Immature UDP-glucuronosyltransferase and high red cell turnover","Obstruction of bile flow out of the liver"]],"caption":"Jaundice that persists beyond about 2 weeks needs a conjugated bilirubin level, because only the conjugated form signals cholestasis."},{"type":"p","text":"The infant typically looks well and gains weight at first, which delays recognition. Retained bile acids and bilirubin injure the liver, and the histology shows bile ductular proliferation, bile plugs, and portal edema and fibrosis. Liver enzymes reflecting cholestasis, especially gamma-glutamyl transferase and alkaline phosphatase, are elevated. Because bile acids do not reach the intestinal lumen, micelles do not form, so fat and fat-soluble vitamins are poorly absorbed; vitamin K deficiency prolongs the prothrombin time and can cause bleeding. The fibrosis advances to biliary cirrhosis with portal hypertension within months, and without restored bile flow most children die by about age 2."},{"type":"flow","title":"From duct obliteration to cirrhosis","steps":["Inflammatory fibrosis obliterates extrahepatic bile ducts","Conjugated bilirubin cannot reach the intestine","Conjugated bilirubin enters blood: jaundice, dark urine","No stercobilin formed: acholic stools","No bile acids in lumen: fat and vitamin K malabsorption","Retained bile injures liver: ductular proliferation, portal fibrosis","Biliary cirrhosis and portal hypertension"],"caption":"The time course matters because fibrosis keeps advancing while bile flow is blocked."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-08-C06","obj":"GI-13-H.6","title":"Gastroschisis, omphalocele, and malrotation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"In week 6 the midgut loop grows faster than the abdominal cavity can hold it, so it herniates into the umbilical cord (physiologic umbilical herniation). Viewed from the front, it rotates about 90 degrees counterclockwise around the axis of the superior mesenteric artery while in the cord, then about 180 degrees more as it returns to the abdomen around week 10, for a total of about 270 degrees. The cecum returns last and descends to the right lower quadrant, leaving the mesentery fixed along a long, broad base. Omphalocele and malrotation are failures of this sequence; gastroschisis is a failure of the body wall."},{"type":"h","text":"Omphalocele versus gastroschisis"},{"type":"p","text":"An omphalocele forms when the herniated bowel fails to return. It therefore stays within the umbilical cord and is covered by the cord's sac of peritoneum and amnion, with the cord inserting on the sac. Because it reflects a broad disturbance of early development, about half of affected infants have other major anomalies, including cardiac defects, trisomies 13 and 18, and Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome. Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome is an overgrowth disorder from imprinting defects at chromosome 11p15, with macroglossia, macrosomia, hemihypertrophy, neonatal hypoglycemia from hyperinsulinism, and a higher risk of Wilms tumor and hepatoblastoma."},{"type":"p","text":"Gastroschisis is a full-thickness defect of the abdominal wall beside a normally inserted umbilical cord, usually to its right. Bowel protrudes through it with no covering sac and floats in amniotic fluid, which inflames the serosa, mats the loops, and causes dysmotility; compression at the narrow defect can cause intestinal atresia. Chromosomal anomalies are uncommon, and young maternal age and maternal smoking are risk factors. Maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein is raised in both defects, usually more in gastroschisis because the bowel is uncovered."},{"type":"table","title":"Omphalocele versus gastroschisis","cols":["Feature","Omphalocele","Gastroschisis"],"rows":[["Basic error","Midgut fails to return from the cord","Body wall defect beside the cord"],["Position","Midline, at the umbilicus","Usually right of the umbilicus"],["Covering","Peritoneum and amnion","None; bowel exposed"],["Cord insertion","Onto the sac","Normal, beside the defect"],["Associated anomalies","Common: cardiac, trisomies 13 and 18, Beckwith-Wiedemann","Uncommon; intestinal atresia from bowel injury"]],"caption":"The covering sac follows from the cause: bowel that never left the cord keeps the cord's coverings."},{"type":"h","text":"Malrotation and Ladd bands"},{"type":"p","text":"In malrotation the midgut completes less than the normal rotation, so the cecum lies high, in the right upper quadrant or epigastrium, and the duodenojejunal junction lies to the right of its usual position left of the midline. Peritoneal bands (Ladd bands) run from the high cecum across the second part of the duodenum to the right abdominal wall and can compress it. The more dangerous consequence is a narrow mesenteric base: the whole midgut hangs from a short stalk containing the superior mesenteric vessels and can twist around it (midgut volvulus). Malrotation also accompanies omphalocele, gastroschisis, and congenital diaphragmatic hernia, because the bowel was outside the abdomen when it should have rotated."},{"type":"flow","title":"Malrotation to midgut volvulus","steps":["Incomplete midgut rotation; cecum stays high","Ladd bands cross and compress the duodenum","Narrow mesenteric base around superior mesenteric vessels","Midgut twists: volvulus","Obstruction below the papilla: bilious vomiting","Venous then arterial occlusion: ischemia of the entire midgut"],"caption":"Ischemia spans the superior mesenteric artery territory."},{"type":"example","title":"Using vomitus color to place the obstruction","text":"A 3-week-old who had been feeding well suddenly vomits green fluid and becomes irritable. Step 1: green vomitus contains bile, so the obstruction is distal to the major duodenal papilla in the second part of the duodenum. Pyloric stenosis, with nonbilious vomitus, is excluded. Step 2: abrupt onset in a previously well infant suggests a twist rather than a fixed narrowing that has been present since birth. Step 3: malrotation with midgut volvulus fits both, and it is the diagnosis that cannot wait, because the superior mesenteric vessels are being occluded. Step 4: bloody stool, distention, or shock would signal advancing ischemia."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-08-C07","obj":"GI-13-H.7","title":"Cleft lip and cleft palate","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The face forms between about weeks 4 and 10 from five prominences around the primitive mouth: the frontonasal prominence, which gives rise to the medial and lateral nasal prominences, and the paired maxillary and mandibular prominences of the first pharyngeal arch. The two medial nasal prominences merge to form the intermaxillary segment. The maxillary prominences grow medially and fuse with the medial nasal prominences to complete the upper lip. A cleft lip results when a maxillary prominence fails to fuse with the medial nasal prominence on one or both sides, so the cleft lies lateral to the philtrum, not in the midline. A true median cleft lip is rare and reflects deficient medial nasal tissue, as in holoprosencephaly."},{"type":"p","text":"The palate has two parts. The primary palate comes from the intermaxillary segment and lies in front of the incisive foramen. The secondary palate comes from two palatine shelves that grow from the maxillary prominences, hang vertically beside the tongue, then rise to a horizontal position above it around week 7 and fuse with each other, with the primary palate, and with the nasal septum. A cleft palate results when the shelves fail to grow, rise, or fuse. Anything that keeps the tongue high blocks shelf elevation, as in the Pierre Robin sequence, where a small mandible holds the tongue up and back and leaves a U-shaped cleft palate. The incisive foramen divides anterior clefts (lip, alveolus, and primary palate) from posterior clefts (secondary palate)."},{"type":"table","title":"Facial structures and the clefts that follow failed fusion","cols":["Structure","Embryonic source","Defect when fusion fails"],"rows":[["Philtrum, premaxilla with four upper incisors, primary palate","Merged medial nasal prominences (intermaxillary segment)","Median cleft lip (rare)"],["Lateral upper lip","Maxillary prominences","Cleft lip, lateral to the philtrum"],["Alveolus and primary palate","Intermaxillary segment meeting maxillary tissue","Anterior cleft through the alveolus"],["Secondary palate (hard palate behind the incisive foramen, soft palate, uvula)","Palatine shelves of the maxillary prominences","Cleft palate; mildest form is a bifid uvula"]],"caption":"Lip and palate form at different times from different processes, which is why each can occur without the other."},{"type":"p","text":"Cleft lip with or without cleft palate occurs in about 1 in 1,000 births, is more common in males, and varies by ancestry; isolated cleft palate is less common and more frequent in females, whose shelves rise later. Most cases are multifactorial. Maternal smoking and anticonvulsants such as phenytoin and valproate raise risk, and syndromic clefts occur in trisomy 13 and 22q11.2 deletion syndrome."},{"type":"p","text":"Feeding difficulty follows from mechanics. Effective sucking requires a sealed mouth: the lips close around the nipple and the soft palate closes off the nasopharynx so the tongue and jaw can create negative pressure. A cleft palate connects mouth and nose, so the infant cannot generate suction, swallows air, regurgitates milk through the nose, tires during long feeds, gains weight poorly, and may aspirate. An isolated cleft lip usually allows near-normal feeding because soft tissue can still seal. The palatal muscles that open the auditory tube, levator and tensor veli palatini, insert abnormally, so middle ear effusions and conductive hearing loss are common, and failure of velopharyngeal closure later causes hypernasal speech."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology predicts the lesion and anatomy predicts the signs. Foregut septation in week 4 explains atresia with fistula, vitelline duct regression explains Meckel diverticulum, midgut return and rotation between weeks 6 and 10 explain omphalocele and malrotation, and fusion of facial prominences explains clefts. The level of a blockage relative to the major duodenal papilla decides whether vomitus contains bile, and whether bile can leave the liver decides stool and urine color. Physiology then explains the laboratory picture: loss of gastric HCl produces a chloride-responsive alkalosis that the kidney maintains because volume comes first, and cholestasis raises conjugated bilirubin and impairs fat absorption."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology and genetics add the associations. Atresia with fistula, omphalocele, and clefts often travel with other defects (VACTERL, trisomies, Beckwith-Wiedemann, 22q11.2 deletion), whereas pyloric stenosis and gastroschisis are usually isolated. These associations raise or lower probability but do not decide a diagnosis on their own. Several lesions in this chapter, such as webs, Schatzki rings, and probably most biliary atresia, are acquired after birth even though they are grouped with congenital disease, so the age of onset and the mechanism matter more than the label."}],"summary":["In the common form of esophageal atresia a blind upper pouch causes polyhydramnios, drooling, and choking with feeds, while a distal tracheoesophageal fistula puts air in the stomach; a gasless abdomen means no distal fistula.","Esophageal atresia belongs to the VACTERL association, so one defect should prompt a search for vertebral, anal, cardiac, renal, and limb anomalies.","Webs lie in the upper esophagus and, with iron deficiency anemia, form Plummer-Vinson syndrome with a raised risk of squamous cell carcinoma; Schatzki rings lie at the lower squamocolumnar junction and cause intermittent solid food dysphagia.","Meckel diverticulum is a persistent vitelline duct forming a true diverticulum on the antimesenteric ileum; acid from ectopic gastric mucosa ulcerates adjacent ileum and causes painless rectal bleeding.","Pyloric muscle hypertrophy causes nonbilious projectile vomiting at about 2 to 6 weeks and an olive-shaped mass, with first-born males and early macrolide exposure as risks.","Vomiting gastric HCl generates a hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis; volume depletion and aldosterone maintain it and cause hypokalemia, low urine Cl-, and paradoxical aciduria.","Biliary atresia obliterates the extrahepatic ducts, giving persistent conjugated jaundice, dark urine, and acholic stools after about 2 weeks of age, then biliary cirrhosis.","Omphalocele is midgut that never returned from the cord, so it is midline, sac-covered, and often syndromic; gastroschisis is an uncovered paraumbilical body wall defect, usually on the right.","Malrotation leaves a high cecum, Ladd bands across the duodenum, and a narrow mesentery that can twist into midgut volvulus with bilious vomiting and ischemia.","Cleft lip is failed fusion of the maxillary and medial nasal prominences, and cleft palate is failed fusion of the palatine shelves; an open palate prevents suction and causes feeding difficulty."],"sources":[{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Digestive System (foregut, midgut rotation, vitelline duct, body wall defects); Head and Neck (face and palate); Respiratory System (tracheoesophageal septum)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Gastrointestinal Tract, congenital abnormalities and esophageal obstruction; Liver and Gallbladder, biliary atresia"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen, small intestine and peritoneal relations; Neck, esophagus"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Acid-Base Regulation, metabolic alkalosis; Secretory Functions of the Alimentary Tract, gastric secretion"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Acid-base balance, renal handling of bicarbonate in metabolic alkalosis"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Heme metabolism, bilirubin conjugation and excretion"}]},"GI-13-I":{"unit":"GI-13-I","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-09","title":"Conditions of the Abdominal Cavity: Ascites, Peritonitis, and Adhesions","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers three disorders of the peritoneal cavity: fluid accumulation (ascites), infection (peritonitis), and scarring (adhesions). Ascites in cirrhosis is explained through Starling forces in the hepatic sinusoids and renal sodium retention driven by splanchnic vasodilation, and the serum-ascites albumin gradient (SAAG) is read as a measure of portal pressure. Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis is contrasted with secondary peritonitis by source, organisms, fluid findings, and examination. Adhesions are explained as failed peritoneal fibrinolysis, leading to small bowel obstruction. Board questions usually give paracentesis numbers or an abdominal examination and ask for the mechanism or the cause.","prerequisites":["Starling forces across capillaries and the role of lymph flow (Guyton and Hall)","Hepatic sinusoid structure, the space of Disse, and portal venous anatomy (GI-2, GI-5)","Renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, ADH, and baroreceptor control of sodium balance","Peritoneum, peritoneal spaces, and visceral versus parietal innervation (GI-4)","Coagulation and fibrinolysis: fibrin, plasminogen, tPA, and PAI-1"],"connections":[{"to":"Hepatic diseases and disorders (GI-13-C)","how":"Cirrhosis supplies the sinusoidal portal hypertension and splanchnic vasodilation that cause ascites, and the same circulation leads to hepatorenal syndrome."},{"to":"The gut in the body cavities (GI-4)","how":"Peritoneal recesses determine where ascites, pus, and blood collect, and parietal somatic innervation explains localized pain and guarding."},{"to":"Obstructive gastrointestinal diseases (GI-13-F)","how":"Adhesions are the leading cause of small bowel obstruction in adults who have had abdominal surgery."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal pathogens (GI-12)","how":"Enterobacteriaceae cause spontaneous bacterial peritonitis, while Bacteroides fragilis and other anaerobes mark secondary peritonitis and abscess."},{"to":"Cardiovascular and renal physiology","how":"Right heart failure also raises sinusoidal pressure and gives high-SAAG ascites, and effective arterial underfilling activates the same neurohormonal response as in heart failure."}],"terms":[{"term":"Ascites","def":"Pathologic accumulation of free fluid in the peritoneal cavity."},{"term":"Portal hypertension","def":"Elevated pressure in the portal venous system, measured as a hepatic venous pressure gradient above 5 mmHg."},{"term":"Hepatic venous pressure gradient (HVPG)","def":"Wedged minus free hepatic venous pressure, an estimate of sinusoidal pressure relative to the systemic veins."},{"term":"Effective arterial blood volume","def":"The portion of blood volume that fills the arterial tree and is sensed by baroreceptors, which can be low even when total body fluid is high."},{"term":"Serum-ascites albumin gradient (SAAG)","def":"Serum albumin minus ascitic albumin measured on the same day; 1.1 g/dL or more indicates portal hypertension."},{"term":"Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP)","def":"Infection of preexisting ascites with no intra-abdominal surgical source, defined by ascitic neutrophils of 250/mm3 or more."},{"term":"Secondary peritonitis","def":"Peritoneal infection from a breach or inflamed focus in an abdominal organ, such as a perforated ulcer or ruptured appendix."},{"term":"Bacterial translocation","def":"Passage of gut bacteria or their products across the intestinal wall to mesenteric lymph nodes and beyond."},{"term":"Peritoneal adhesion","def":"An abnormal fibrous band joining peritoneal surfaces that are normally separate."},{"term":"Plasminogen activator inhibitor 1 (PAI-1)","def":"The main inhibitor of tissue plasminogen activator; its rise after peritoneal injury allows fibrin to persist."},{"term":"Closed-loop obstruction","def":"Obstruction of a bowel segment at two points, so the trapped loop cannot decompress and strangulates quickly."},{"term":"Strangulation","def":"Loss of blood supply to an obstructed bowel segment, leading to ischemia, necrosis, and perforation."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-09-C01","obj":"GI-13-I.1","title":"Ascites in cirrhosis and the serum-ascites albumin gradient","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Ascites forms when filtration into the peritoneal space outpaces lymphatic return. In cirrhosis, local Starling forces push fluid out of the liver and gut, and the kidneys keep retaining sodium and water that refill the leak."},{"type":"h","text":"Sinusoidal hypertension and the leak"},{"type":"p","text":"Fibrous septa, regenerative nodules, and contracting activated stellate cells raise intrahepatic resistance. Portal hypertension is an HVPG above 5 mmHg and becomes clinically significant at about 10 mmHg or more. Sinusoids are fenestrated and leaky, so raised sinusoidal pressure drives fluid into the space of Disse and greatly increases hepatic lymph formation. When hepatic lymph flow exceeds what the thoracic duct can return, fluid weeps from the liver capsule and splanchnic capillaries into the peritoneum. The pressure must be sinusoidal: prehepatic portal vein thrombosis rarely causes ascites, whereas cirrhosis, Budd-Chiari syndrome, and right heart failure all do. Hypoalbuminemia from reduced hepatocyte synthesis lowers plasma oncotic pressure and adds to the leak but is not the primary cause."},{"type":"h","text":"Splanchnic vasodilation and renal sodium retention"},{"type":"p","text":"Portal hypertension and translocated bacterial products increase splanchnic nitric oxide and other vasodilators, so splanchnic arterioles dilate. Systemic vascular resistance and arterial pressure fall, and baroreceptors sense a low effective arterial blood volume even though total body water and sodium are increased. As after hemorrhage, renin, angiotensin II, aldosterone, and sympathetic tone rise, and ADH is released without an osmotic stimulus. Urine sodium becomes very low, ADH causes dilutional hyponatremia, and the retained fluid leaks out as ascites. Extreme renal vasoconstriction in the same setting produces hepatorenal syndrome (GI-13-C)."},{"type":"flow","title":"How cirrhosis produces ascites","steps":["Fibrosis and nodules raise sinusoidal resistance","Sinusoidal and portal pressure rise","Splanchnic nitric oxide dilates arterioles","Low effective arterial volume sensed by baroreceptors","RAAS, sympathetic tone, and nonosmotic ADH rise","Kidneys retain sodium and water","Sinusoidal pressure and low albumin push fluid out; lymph overwhelmed"],"caption":"Starling forces create the leak; renal sodium retention refills it."},{"type":"h","text":"Reading the fluid"},{"type":"p","text":"The SAAG is serum albumin minus ascitic albumin, measured the same day. At equilibrium the oncotic difference across the barrier balances the hydrostatic difference, so a large gradient means high portal pressure. A SAAG of 1.1 g/dL or more indicates portal hypertension with about 97% accuracy. Ascitic total protein then separates the causes. In cirrhosis, collagen deposition in the space of Disse and loss of fenestrae (capillarization) make sinusoids less permeable, so fluid is protein poor (under 2.5 g/dL). In heart failure and early Budd-Chiari syndrome the sinusoids are normal, so fluid is protein rich (2.5 g/dL or more). Shifting dullness usually needs about 1,500 mL of fluid to detect."},{"type":"table","title":"SAAG and ascitic total protein","cols":["Pattern","SAAG","Total protein","Typical causes"],"rows":[["Cirrhotic sinusoids","1.1 g/dL or more","Under 2.5 g/dL","Cirrhosis, alcoholic hepatitis"],["Hepatic venous congestion","1.1 g/dL or more","2.5 g/dL or more","Right heart failure, constrictive pericarditis, early Budd-Chiari syndrome"],["Peritoneal disease","Under 1.1 g/dL","Usually 2.5 g/dL or more","Peritoneal carcinomatosis, tuberculous peritonitis, pancreatic ascites"],["Low oncotic pressure","Under 1.1 g/dL","Under 2.5 g/dL","Nephrotic syndrome"]],"caption":"SAAG shows whether portal pressure is high; total protein shows how much protein the leaking surface lets through."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: two high-SAAG fluids","text":"Patient A: serum albumin 2.6 g/dL, ascitic albumin 0.8 g/dL, ascitic total protein 1.2 g/dL. Patient B: serum albumin 3.6 g/dL, ascitic albumin 1.9 g/dL, total protein 3.4 g/dL. Step 1: SAAG is 1.8 g/dL for A and 1.7 g/dL for B, so both have portal hypertension. Step 2: A has protein-poor fluid from capillarized sinusoids, which fits cirrhosis; protein under 1.5 g/dL also raises SBP risk (C02). Step 3: B has protein-rich fluid from intact sinusoids, which fits right heart failure or Budd-Chiari syndrome."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Portal vein thrombosis with a normal liver causes varices. Will marked ascites develop?","explain":"Usually not. The block is presinusoidal, so sinusoidal pressure and hepatic lymph formation stay near normal.","check":"The sinusoidal hypertension paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-09-C02","obj":"GI-13-I.2","title":"Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis versus secondary peritonitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Peritonitis is inflammation of the peritoneum, usually from infection, and the key distinction is the route. Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP), a form of primary peritonitis, is infection of preexisting ascites with no hole or abscess in the gut. Secondary peritonitis follows spillage from, or spread of inflammation through, the wall of an abdominal organ."},{"type":"h","text":"How SBP develops"},{"type":"p","text":"Cirrhosis weakens every barrier between gut bacteria and ascites. Slow transit allows bacterial overgrowth, portal hypertension makes the mucosa leaky, and enteric bacteria translocate to mesenteric lymph nodes and then into the blood. Portosystemic shunting lets portal blood bypass Kupffer cells, the liver's main bacterial filter, so bacteremia persists and seeds the ascites. Ascitic fluid contains little complement and opsonin, especially when total protein is under about 1.5 g/dL, so bacteria are poorly phagocytosed. Translocation favors aerobic gram-negative rods, so SBP is usually monomicrobial: Escherichia coli most often, then Klebsiella pneumoniae, and less often streptococci or enterococci. Anaerobes point to a perforation."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of spontaneous bacterial peritonitis","steps":["Bacterial overgrowth and leaky mucosa in portal hypertension","Gram-negative rods reach mesenteric lymph nodes","Shunted portal blood bypasses Kupffer cells","Bacteremia seeds low-protein ascites","Low complement and opsonins impair killing","Ascitic neutrophils reach 250/mm3 or more"],"caption":"Barrier failure, hepatic bypass, and weak opsonization combine."},{"type":"p","text":"Ascites separates the visceral and parietal peritoneum and dilutes the inflammatory exudate, so SBP often produces fever, mild diffuse tenderness, worsening encephalopathy, or rising creatinine instead of a rigid abdomen, and some patients have no abdominal signs. Neutrophils arrive before cultures turn positive, so an ascitic neutrophil count of 250/mm3 or more establishes the diagnosis. In bloody fluid, about 1 neutrophil is subtracted for every 250 red cells. SBP cytokines worsen splanchnic vasodilation and can precipitate hepatorenal syndrome. In children, primary peritonitis classically complicates nephrotic ascites, often with Streptococcus pneumoniae."},{"type":"h","text":"Secondary peritonitis"},{"type":"p","text":"A perforated peptic ulcer, ruptured appendix, perforated diverticulum, infarcted bowel, or leaking anastomosis releases mixed flora: enteric gram-negative rods together with anaerobes such as Bacteroides fragilis, plus enterococci. The B. fragilis capsule promotes abscess formation. The parietal peritoneum is supplied by somatic nerves (T7 to L1 anteriorly, the phrenic nerve under the central diaphragm), so its inflammation causes sharp, localized pain, reflex guarding, and a rigid, board-like abdomen with rebound tenderness. Paralytic ileus, fluid shifts into the peritoneum and bowel wall, hypovolemia, and sepsis follow. A perforated viscus releases free air under the diaphragm on an upright radiograph."},{"type":"table","title":"Spontaneous versus secondary peritonitis","cols":["Feature","Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis","Secondary peritonitis"],"rows":[["Setting","Cirrhotic ascites, protein under 1.5 g/dL","Perforated or ischemic organ"],["Route","Translocation and bacteremia","Spillage from an organ"],["Organisms","One, usually E. coli or Klebsiella","Several, including anaerobes (B. fragilis)"],["Abdomen","Soft or mildly tender","Rigid with guarding and rebound"],["Ascitic neutrophils","250/mm3 or more","250/mm3 or more, often thousands"],["Fluid chemistry","Low protein; usually fails the criteria at right","Two or more: protein above 1 g/dL, glucose under 50 mg/dL, LDH above serum upper limit"],["Imaging","No free air","Free air if a viscus perforated"]],"caption":"The chemistry criteria flag secondary peritonitis arising within cirrhotic ascites."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: infected ascites, but which kind","text":"A man with cirrhosis has fever of 38.4 °C (101.1 °F). Ascitic fluid: neutrophils 3,900/mm3, total protein 2.4 g/dL, glucose 30 mg/dL, LDH above the serum upper limit; Gram stain shows several organism types. Step 1: neutrophils of 250/mm3 or more confirm infected ascites. Step 2: protein exceeds 1 g/dL, bacteria and neutrophils consumed glucose, and cell breakdown raised LDH, so all three criteria are met. Step 3: with mixed organisms, this is secondary peritonitis from a perforation, not SBP."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why can SBP lack guarding when a perforated ulcer causes rigidity?","explain":"In SBP, ascites dilutes the exudate and separates the layers, so parietal irritation is mild. Spilled acid and bowel flora intensely inflame the somatically innervated parietal peritoneum, causing reflex spasm.","check":"The SBP signs and secondary peritonitis paragraphs."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-09-C03","obj":"GI-13-I.3","title":"Peritoneal adhesions and small bowel obstruction","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The peritoneum is a single layer of mesothelial cells on a basement membrane over loose connective tissue. Its surfaces glide because they are smooth and lubricated, and it heals unusually well: after injury, mesothelium regenerates across the whole raw area at once from surrounding and underlying cells, so a large defect heals as fast as a small one, within about a week."},{"type":"h","text":"Why fibrin becomes scar"},{"type":"p","text":"Any injury (surgical handling, drying, cautery, sutures that leave tissue ischemic, foreign material, blood, infection, radiation, or endometriosis) triggers inflammation within hours. Vascular permeability rises and a fibrin-rich exudate coats the damaged surfaces and glues neighboring ones together. Whether this glue dissolves depends on fibrinolysis. Healthy mesothelium releases tissue plasminogen activator (tPA), which converts plasminogen to plasmin, and plasmin normally clears the fibrin within about 3 days. Injury and especially ischemia reduce tPA, while inflammatory cytokines (TNF-alpha, IL-1, TGF-beta) raise PAI-1. Fibrin then persists, and fibroblasts, macrophages, and new capillaries invade it. TGF-beta drives collagen deposition, and the fibrin bridge matures into a vascular fibrous band, sometimes containing nerve fibers. Most people develop adhesions after laparotomy, and peritonitis causes dense ones because infection and ischemia suppress fibrinolysis most strongly."},{"type":"flow","title":"From peritoneal injury to adhesion","steps":["Surgery, infection, or blood injures mesothelium","Fibrin-rich exudate bridges adjacent surfaces","Ischemia lowers tPA; cytokines raise PAI-1","Fibrin persists beyond about 3 days","Fibroblasts and capillaries invade the fibrin","TGF-beta drives collagen; a fibrous band forms"],"caption":"The balance between fibrin deposition and fibrinolysis decides whether the surfaces heal free or scar together."},{"type":"h","text":"Small bowel obstruction"},{"type":"p","text":"Adhesions are usually silent until a band kinks mobile small bowel or forms a ring that traps a loop. They cause most small bowel obstructions in adults who have had abdominal surgery, often years later; hernias lead in people who have not. Fixed colon is rarely obstructed by adhesions. Proximal bowel fills with swallowed air and secretions, peristalsis strains against the block (colicky pain, high-pitched bowel sounds), and vomiting, distension, and failure to pass flatus follow. Fluid lost by vomiting and sequestered in the lumen and wall causes hypovolemia, and in proximal obstruction, vomited gastric acid gives hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis. Radiographs show dilated small bowel loops with air-fluid levels and little colonic gas; adhesions themselves are not visible."},{"type":"flow","title":"Obstruction to strangulation","steps":["Adhesive band kinks or traps a loop","Gas and fluid distend the proximal bowel","Wall pressure rises; venous outflow fails","Wall edema; then arterial inflow fails","Ischemia, necrosis, bacterial translocation","Perforation and peritonitis"],"caption":"A closed loop cannot decompress in either direction, so it strangulates fastest."},{"type":"p","text":"Strangulation changes the picture: pain becomes constant, and fever, tachycardia, leukocytosis, raised lactate, and peritoneal signs appear. In the pelvis, adhesions around the fallopian tubes and ovaries cause infertility and ectopic pregnancy, and some adhesions cause chronic pain."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A woman had an appendectomy 12 years ago. She now has colicky pain, bilious vomiting, distension, and no flatus. What lesion is most likely, and what finding would mean the bowel is dying?","explain":"An adhesive band from the old surgery obstructing the small bowel. Constant pain with fever, tachycardia, leukocytosis, raised lactate, or peritoneal signs indicates strangulation.","check":"The small bowel obstruction subsection and the strangulation flow."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Physiology and anatomy explain where fluid goes. Starling forces and lymph flow decide whether fluid leaves the sinusoids, and sinusoid histology decides how much protein it carries, which is why SAAG measures pressure while total protein measures permeability. The kidney is normal in cirrhosis; it retains sodium because baroreceptors read a dilated splanchnic bed as underfilling, the same reflex that defends against hemorrhage. Peritoneal anatomy then shapes the clinical signs: somatic parietal nerves explain guarding in secondary peritonitis, and dependent recesses such as the hepatorenal pouch collect fluid and pus."},{"type":"p","text":"Microbiology, immunology, and pathology link the three conditions. Translocated aerobic gram-negative rods and weak ascitic opsonization explain monomicrobial SBP, while spilled colonic flora explains polymicrobial secondary peritonitis with anaerobic abscesses. Peritonitis in turn suppresses fibrinolysis and leaves dense adhesions, which later obstruct the bowel. These patterns have limits: secondary peritonitis can arise in cirrhotic ascites, heart failure gives high-SAAG fluid without cirrhosis, and bowel obstruction in a patient without prior surgery points to hernia or tumor rather than adhesions."}],"summary":["Ascites in cirrhosis needs sinusoidal portal hypertension; prehepatic portal vein thrombosis rarely causes it.","Splanchnic nitric oxide vasodilation lowers effective arterial volume and activates RAAS, sympathetic nerves, and nonosmotic ADH, so the kidneys retain sodium and water.","Hypoalbuminemia contributes to ascites, but sinusoidal pressure and renal sodium retention are the main drivers.","A SAAG of 1.1 g/dL or more means portal hypertension; ascitic protein under 2.5 g/dL suggests cirrhosis, and 2.5 g/dL or more suggests heart failure or Budd-Chiari syndrome.","SBP results from bacterial translocation, shunting past Kupffer cells, and poor opsonization in low-protein ascites; it is usually monomicrobial E. coli or Klebsiella.","Ascitic neutrophils of 250/mm3 or more diagnose infected ascites; SBP may cause only fever, encephalopathy, or kidney injury.","Secondary peritonitis is polymicrobial with anaerobes, inflames the somatically innervated parietal peritoneum, and causes a rigid abdomen and free air if a viscus perforated.","Adhesions form when injury and ischemia lower tPA and raise PAI-1, so fibrin persists and is organized into collagen bands.","Adhesions are the most common cause of small bowel obstruction after abdominal surgery; a closed loop strangulates fastest."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Liver and Gallbladder, sections on portal hypertension and ascites; Oral Cavity and Gastrointestinal Tract, section on the peritoneal cavity"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"The Microcirculation and Lymphatic System: Capillary Fluid Exchange, Interstitial Fluid, and Lymph Flow"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Liver Disease, sections on cirrhosis, ascites, and spontaneous bacterial peritonitis"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Enteric gram-negative rods (Enterobacteriaceae); anaerobic infections and Bacteroides"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen, section on the peritoneum and peritoneal cavity"}]},"GI-13-J":{"unit":"GI-13-J","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-10","title":"Gastrointestinal Vascular Diseases","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the vascular diseases of the gut: veins that dilate under pressure (esophageal varices and hemorrhoids), small mucosal vessels that degenerate with age (angiodysplasia), and bowel that loses its arterial supply (mesenteric ischemia, ischemic colitis, and necrotizing enterocolitis). Each disease follows directly from vascular anatomy and simple physics: where portal and systemic veins meet, which artery supplies which segment, where collateral flow is weakest, and how wall tension rises with vessel radius. Board questions usually give the setting (cirrhosis, atrial fibrillation, aortic stenosis, a premature infant) and the character of the bleeding or pain, and ask you to name the vessel, the lesion, and the mechanism.","prerequisites":["Portal venous system and portosystemic anastomoses (GI-5.4)","Celiac, superior mesenteric, and inferior mesenteric artery territories and the marginal artery (GI-5.2)","Anal canal above and below the pectinate line (GI-5.5)","Pressure, flow, and resistance relationships and the law of Laplace (CV-6.4)","Primary hemostasis and von Willebrand factor (HE-4.2)"],"connections":[{"to":"Cirrhosis and portal hypertension (GI-13-C)","how":"Raised intrahepatic resistance in cirrhosis is the usual cause of varices, and liver failure adds the coagulopathy that makes variceal bleeding lethal."},{"to":"Arrhythmias and ischemic heart disease (CV-7, CV-18-C)","how":"Atrial fibrillation and mural thrombus after myocardial infarction are the main sources of emboli to the superior mesenteric artery."},{"to":"Valvular heart disease (CV-18-D)","how":"High shear stress across a stenotic aortic valve destroys large von Willebrand factor multimers and links aortic stenosis to bleeding angiodysplasia."},{"to":"Disorders of hemostasis (HE-9-C)","how":"Thrombocytopenia, clotting factor deficiency, uremic platelet dysfunction, and acquired von Willebrand defects explain why these lesions bleed more in particular patients."},{"to":"Neonatal immunology and the gut microbiota","how":"Necrotizing enterocolitis results from an immature mucosal barrier and innate immune response meeting abnormal bacterial colonization."}],"terms":[{"term":"Portal hypertension","def":"Raised pressure in the portal venous system, defined by a hepatic venous pressure gradient above about 5 mmHg."},{"term":"Hepatic venous pressure gradient (HVPG)","def":"Wedged minus free hepatic venous pressure, an estimate of the pressure difference between the portal vein and the inferior vena cava."},{"term":"Portosystemic anastomosis","def":"A site where tributaries of the portal vein meet systemic veins, which can carry blood around the liver when portal pressure rises."},{"term":"Varix","def":"An abnormally dilated, tortuous vein; esophageal varices are dilated submucosal veins of the distal esophagus."},{"term":"Law of Laplace","def":"For a cylindrical vessel, wall tension equals transmural pressure times radius, so wide vessels carry more tension at the same pressure."},{"term":"Pectinate (dentate) line","def":"The line in the anal canal that marks the junction of endoderm-derived hindgut and ectoderm-derived proctodeum."},{"term":"Anal cushions","def":"Normal submucosal vascular cushions of the upper anal canal that help seal it; when enlarged and displaced they are called hemorrhoids."},{"term":"Watershed zone","def":"A region at the border between two arterial territories, perfused by the ends of both, that is first to suffer when flow falls."},{"term":"Non-occlusive mesenteric ischemia","def":"Intestinal ischemia caused by low flow and splanchnic vasoconstriction without a blocked artery."},{"term":"Angiodysplasia (vascular ectasia)","def":"Acquired dilated, thin-walled vessels in the mucosa and submucosa, most often in the cecum and ascending colon of older adults."},{"term":"Acquired von Willebrand syndrome","def":"Loss of von Willebrand factor function from a nonhereditary cause, such as shear-induced cleavage of large multimers in aortic stenosis."},{"term":"Pneumatosis intestinalis","def":"Gas within the bowel wall seen on radiograph, the hallmark radiographic sign of necrotizing enterocolitis."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-10-C01","obj":"GI-13-J.1","title":"Esophageal varices in portal hypertension","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The portal vein, formed by the superior mesenteric and splenic veins, carries about three quarters of hepatic blood flow at about 5 to 10 mmHg, and the hepatic venous pressure gradient (HVPG) is normally about 1 to 5 mmHg. Portal veins have no valves, so blood follows the pressure gradient in either direction."},{"type":"p","text":"Pressure equals flow times resistance, and cirrhosis raises both. Fibrosis, regenerative nodules, and loss of sinusoidal fenestrations fix resistance high, and activated stellate cells contract because the liver has too much endothelin and too little nitric oxide. Meanwhile excess nitric oxide in splanchnic arterioles increases portal inflow. Blood that cannot pass the liver turns back into collaterals where portal and systemic veins meet."},{"type":"p","text":"The dangerous collateral lies at the gastroesophageal junction, where the left gastric (coronary) vein, a portal tributary, connects with esophageal veins that drain to the azygos system and superior vena cava. In the distal 2 to 5 cm of the esophagus these veins lie superficially in the lamina propria and submucosa with little support, so they dilate into varices, generally once the HVPG reaches about 10 mmHg; bleeding is rare below about 12 mmHg."},{"type":"table","title":"Portosystemic anastomoses and what they produce","cols":["Site","Portal side","Systemic side","Result in portal hypertension"],"rows":[["Distal esophagus","Left gastric vein","Esophageal veins to the azygos system","Esophageal varices"],["Gastric fundus","Short gastric veins (from the splenic vein)","Esophageal and left inferior phrenic veins","Gastric fundal varices"],["Umbilicus","Paraumbilical veins","Superficial and inferior epigastric veins","Caput medusae"],["Rectum","Superior rectal vein","Middle and inferior rectal veins","Anorectal varices (not hemorrhoids)"]],"caption":"The esophageal site bleeds catastrophically because its veins are thin, superficial, and exposed to the lumen."},{"type":"flow","title":"From cirrhosis to variceal hemorrhage","steps":["Fibrosis and stellate cell contraction raise intrahepatic resistance","Splanchnic vasodilation increases portal inflow","Portal pressure rises (HVPG above about 10 mmHg)","Left gastric vein flow reverses into esophageal veins","Thin-walled distal submucosal veins dilate","Wall tension exceeds wall strength and the varix ruptures","Painless massive hematemesis and hypovolemic shock"],"caption":"Resistance and inflow both raise portal pressure; rupture depends on pressure, size, and wall thinness."},{"type":"p","text":"Large, thin-walled varices (red wale marks at endoscopy) rupture most, as the worked example shows. Rupture is painless because it is a tear of a distended vein, without inflammation, ulceration, or muscle spasm to stimulate pain fibers. It causes sudden massive hematemesis, often with melena, and hypovolemic shock."},{"type":"p","text":"Liver disease makes the bleeding worse and its aftermath dangerous. Splenic sequestration lowers the platelet count, and the failing liver makes too little of most clotting factors. Colonic bacteria convert the protein in swallowed blood to ammonia, so a bleed often precipitates hepatic encephalopathy. Variceal hemorrhage is a leading cause of death in cirrhosis, with a mortality of roughly 15 to 20% per episode."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: why large varices bleed","text":"Wall tension (T) in a cylinder equals pressure (P) times radius (r), and wall stress is T divided by wall thickness. At 20 mmHg and a radius of 2 mm, T is proportional to 40. If the varix widens to 4 mm at the same pressure, T doubles to 80, and if its wall thins by half as it stretches, stress doubles again to four times the start. Dilation therefore feeds on itself, and tension falls whenever portal pressure falls, even if size is unchanged."},{"type":"p","text":"By contrast, a Mallory-Weiss tear is a mucosal laceration at the gastroesophageal junction after forceful retching, usually self-limited, and Boerhaave syndrome is a transmural rupture with severe chest pain and mediastinitis."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Splenic vein thrombosis from pancreatitis occurs in a patient with a normal liver. Where do varices form, and is the HVPG raised?","explain":"Splenic blood diverts through the short gastric veins, so isolated gastric fundal varices form; the liver is normal, so the HVPG is normal.","check":"Compare with the gastric fundus row of the anastomosis table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-10-C02","obj":"GI-13-J.2","title":"Internal and external hemorrhoids","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hemorrhoids begin as normal anatomy. The upper anal canal contains three main submucosal vascular cushions (classically left lateral, right anterior, and right posterior) made of venous sinusoids with direct arteriovenous communications from branches of the superior rectal artery. Smooth muscle and connective tissue anchor them, and they seal the canal and contribute roughly 15 to 20% of resting anal pressure. The term hemorrhoid means a cushion that has enlarged and slid downward."},{"type":"p","text":"The pectinate (dentate) line explains why the two types behave differently. It marks the junction of the endoderm-derived hindgut and the ectoderm-derived proctodeum, so epithelium, blood supply, lymph drainage, and nerves all change there."},{"type":"table","title":"The anal canal above and below the pectinate line","cols":["Feature","Above (internal hemorrhoids)","Below (external hemorrhoids)"],"rows":[["Embryologic origin","Endoderm (hindgut)","Ectoderm (proctodeum)"],["Lining","Simple columnar to transitional epithelium","Nonkeratinized stratified squamous anoderm, then skin"],["Arterial supply","Superior rectal artery (from the inferior mesenteric artery)","Inferior rectal artery (from the internal pudendal artery)"],["Venous drainage","Superior rectal vein to the inferior mesenteric vein and portal system","Inferior rectal vein to the internal pudendal and internal iliac veins (systemic)"],["Lymph drainage","Internal iliac and inferior mesenteric nodes","Superficial inguinal nodes"],["Innervation","Visceral (autonomic) afferents, stretch only","Somatic inferior rectal nerve (from the pudendal), sharp pain, touch, temperature"],["Typical presentation","Painless bright red bleeding, prolapse, mucus, itching","Acutely painful bluish perianal lump when thrombosed; skin tag afterward"]],"caption":"The nerve row explains pain; the vessel rows explain bleeding and the portal versus systemic distinction."},{"type":"p","text":"Anything that forces the cushions downward or engorges them promotes hemorrhoids. Straining at stool shears the cushions distally, and raised abdominal pressure slows venous outflow from them. A low-fiber diet produces small hard stools that need more straining, and constipation prolongs it. In pregnancy, the gravid uterus compresses pelvic veins, progesterone relaxes smooth muscle and slows colonic transit, and blood volume rises. With age, the supporting connective tissue and smooth muscle weaken, so the cushions slide more easily."},{"type":"p","text":"Internal hemorrhoids bleed bright red blood that streaks the stool, drips into the bowl, or appears on the paper after defecation. The blood is bright because the cushions are fed through arteriovenous communications, so it is well oxygenated. They are painless because their covering has only visceral innervation. As they enlarge they prolapse: grade I bleeds without prolapse, grade II prolapses and reduces on its own, grade III needs manual reduction, and grade IV cannot be reduced. An important exception is a prolapsed internal hemorrhoid that becomes trapped and strangulated, which hurts because it swells, becomes ischemic, and drags on sensitive anoderm."},{"type":"p","text":"External hemorrhoids lie under somatically innervated anoderm. They cause little trouble until a clot forms in them, producing an acutely painful, tense, bluish perianal lump that resolves over days and often leaves a skin tag."},{"type":"p","text":"Two distinctions are tested. First, the dilated rectal veins of portal hypertension are anorectal varices, a lesion separate from hemorrhoids, and hemorrhoid prevalence is not clearly raised in portal hypertension. Second, rectal bleeding in an older adult cannot be assumed to be hemorrhoidal, because colorectal carcinoma and angiodysplasia also bleed painlessly. An anal fissure, by contrast, is a tear in sensitive anoderm and causes sharp pain with defecation."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-10-C03","obj":"GI-13-J.3","title":"Acute and chronic mesenteric ischemia and ischemic colitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The celiac trunk supplies the foregut, the superior mesenteric artery (SMA) the midgut from the distal duodenum to the distal transverse colon, and the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA) the hindgut to the upper rectum. Pancreaticoduodenal arcades, the marginal artery, and the rectal arteries link these territories, and the splanchnic circulation receives about a quarter of resting cardiac output, more after meals."},{"type":"p","text":"The mucosa is injured first. Villus tips have high metabolic demand, and because arteriole and venule run side by side, oxygen shunts across near the villus base (countercurrent exchange). Injury progresses from mucosal to mural to transmural infarction, and restored flow adds reperfusion injury from reactive oxygen species."},{"type":"h","text":"Acute mesenteric ischemia"},{"type":"p","text":"Arterial embolism is the most common cause, from a left atrial thrombus in atrial fibrillation or a mural thrombus after myocardial infarction. The SMA is wide and leaves the aorta at an acute angle nearly parallel to aortic flow, so emboli enter it readily and often lodge beyond the middle colic origin, sparing the proximal jejunum. Thrombosis on ostial atherosclerosis blocks the origin and infarcts the whole midgut. Non-occlusive ischemia follows shock, heart failure, or vasoconstrictors, and mesenteric venous thrombosis (hypercoagulable states, portal hypertension) gives a slower, hemorrhagic infarct."},{"type":"flow","title":"Acute embolic occlusion of the superior mesenteric artery","steps":["Atrial fibrillation forms a left atrial thrombus","Embolus lodges in the SMA","Midgut mucosa becomes hypoxic; lactate rises","Severe diffuse visceral pain; abdomen still soft","Mucosal sloughing causes bloody stool","Transmural necrosis inflames parietal peritoneum: guarding, rebound","Perforation, bacterial translocation, sepsis"],"caption":"Peritoneal signs appear only after the full wall dies, so early pain is out of proportion to the exam."},{"type":"p","text":"Pain is out of proportion to the examination because ischemic bowel first activates visceral afferents, which cause intense diffuse pain, while guarding and rebound need parietal peritoneal irritation that comes only with transmural necrosis. Anaerobic glycolysis raises lactate and causes an anion gap acidosis, but lactate rises late, so a normal value does not exclude early ischemia."},{"type":"h","text":"Chronic mesenteric ischemia and ischemic colitis"},{"type":"p","text":"Chronic mesenteric ischemia is demand ischemia, the gut equivalent of stable angina. Because collaterals are rich, symptoms usually need severe atherosclerotic narrowing of at least two of the three arteries. Flow cannot rise after a meal, so pain starts about 15 to 60 minutes after eating, and patients fear food and lose weight."},{"type":"p","text":"Ischemic colitis, the most common form of intestinal ischemia, usually reflects low flow in older adults with vascular disease, hypotension, or recent aortic surgery. It strikes the watershed zones at the splenic flexure (SMA and IMA) and rectosigmoid junction (IMA and internal iliac), while the rectum is spared by its dual supply. Milder, crampy, left-sided pain is followed within a day by bloody diarrhea, and submucosal edema and hemorrhage give thumbprinting on imaging."},{"type":"table","title":"Comparing the main forms of intestinal ischemia","cols":["Feature","Acute mesenteric ischemia","Chronic mesenteric ischemia","Ischemic colitis"],"rows":[["Usual mechanism","SMA embolus or thrombosis, low flow, venous thrombosis","Atherosclerosis of two or more mesenteric arteries","Low flow to colonic watershed zones"],["Bowel affected","Small bowel and right colon","Mainly small bowel","Splenic flexure and rectosigmoid"],["Pain","Sudden, severe, out of proportion to exam","Postprandial, recurrent","Crampy, milder, usually left-sided"],["Other features","Raised lactate, acidosis, later peritonitis","Food fear, weight loss","Bloody diarrhea, thumbprinting"]],"caption":"Pain severity tracks how much bowel is at risk and how deep the injury goes."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: reading an ischemia vignette","text":"A 74-year-old with an irregularly irregular pulse has sudden severe periumbilical pain, a soft, mildly tender abdomen, lactate 6 mmol/L (reference about 0.5 to 2.0), and low bicarbonate. Atrial fibrillation suggests a cardiac embolus. Periumbilical pain localizes to midgut visceral afferents, and the soft abdomen means the parietal peritoneum is not yet involved. The lactate and low bicarbonate show anaerobic metabolism with an anion gap acidosis. Gradual left-sided cramps and bloody diarrhea after hypotension would instead fit ischemic colitis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-10-C04","obj":"GI-13-J.4","title":"Angiodysplasia, Heyde syndrome, and kidney failure","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Angiodysplasia (vascular ectasia) is an acquired, degenerative lesion of older adults, usually over 60. It consists of clusters of dilated, tortuous, thin-walled veins, venules, and capillaries in the mucosa and submucosa, lined by endothelium with little or no smooth muscle. Lesions are usually under 5 mm and multiple, and most lie in the cecum and ascending colon, though they also occur in the small bowel, where they are a leading cause of obscure bleeding in older adults."},{"type":"p","text":"The leading explanation is mechanical and depends on the law of Laplace. The cecum has the widest diameter in the colon, so at a given intraluminal pressure its wall tension is highest. Each contraction compresses the submucosal veins where they pass through the muscularis propria. Over years, this repeated partial obstruction dilates the submucosal veins, then the venules and capillaries that feed them, until the precapillary sphincters become incompetent and small arteriovenous communications form."},{"type":"flow","title":"Formation of angiodysplasia in the right colon","steps":["Wide cecal diameter gives the highest wall tension","Muscle contraction compresses veins crossing the muscularis","Repeated partial venous obstruction over years","Submucosal veins, venules, and capillaries dilate","Precapillary sphincters fail; small arteriovenous communications form","Fragile ectatic vessels bleed intermittently and painlessly"],"caption":"Age-related, low-grade venous obstruction explains why lesions are acquired, multiple, and right-sided."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the lesion is a cluster of fragile low-pressure vessels without inflammation or a mass, bleeding is painless, intermittent, and usually slow. It may appear as occult blood with iron deficiency anemia, or as episodes of maroon or bright red blood that often stop on their own. Colonoscopy shows flat, bright red, fern-like lesions; angiography shows a vascular tuft with an early-filling, slowly emptying vein."},{"type":"p","text":"Heyde syndrome is the association of calcific aortic stenosis with bleeding angiodysplasia. Large von Willebrand factor (vWF) multimers are the most effective at binding platelet glycoprotein Ib under high shear. Turbulent high-velocity flow through a stenotic valve unfolds these multimers, exposing a cleavage site to the plasma protease ADAMTS13, which cuts them into smaller, less active forms. The result is an acquired von Willebrand syndrome that resembles type 2A, with loss of high molecular weight multimers. The stenosis mainly makes existing lesions bleed more, and the multimer pattern returns toward normal when the stenosis is relieved, which supports the mechanism. The clue is a crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur at the right upper sternal border in an older adult with iron deficiency."},{"type":"p","text":"End-stage kidney disease is also associated with angiodysplasia. Uremic toxins impair platelet adhesion and aggregation (including vWF binding and glycoprotein IIb/IIIa function), anemia reduces the red cells that normally push platelets toward the vessel wall, and heparin is used during hemodialysis. These factors make existing lesions bleed more readily; why lesions may be more common in kidney failure is less certain."},{"type":"table","title":"Painless lower GI bleeding in adults","cols":["Cause","Vessel or lesion","Typical site","Clues"],"rows":[["Angiodysplasia","Ectatic mucosal and submucosal veins and capillaries","Cecum and ascending colon, small bowel","Older adult, intermittent, iron deficiency, aortic stenosis or kidney failure"],["Diverticular bleeding","Rupture of a vasa recta stretched over a diverticulum","Diverticula mainly left-sided; bleeding often right-sided","Sudden, large-volume, often self-limited"],["Hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia","Inherited telangiectasias and arteriovenous malformations","Whole GI tract, nose, lips, lungs, liver, brain","Autosomal dominant, recurrent nosebleeds, family history"],["Right-sided colon cancer","Friable tumor surface","Cecum and ascending colon","Occult bleeding, iron deficiency, weight loss"]],"caption":"Angiodysplasia is acquired and right-sided; hereditary telangiectasia is inherited and widespread."},{"type":"stop","predict":"An 80-year-old with a harsh systolic murmur has recurrent iron deficiency anemia. Why would the valve lesion make a colonic lesion bleed?","explain":"Shear across the stenotic valve lets ADAMTS13 cleave large vWF multimers, causing an acquired von Willebrand defect that impairs platelet adhesion at fragile ectatic vessels.","check":"Compare with the Heyde syndrome paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-10-C05","obj":"GI-13-J.5","title":"Necrotizing enterocolitis in premature infants","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC) is ischemic and inflammatory necrosis of the intestinal wall, the most common gastrointestinal emergency in neonates. Most cases occur in preterm infants, especially those of very low birth weight (under 1500 g), and onset is usually after enteral feeding begins, often in the second or third week of life. The less mature the infant, the later the onset tends to be. The terminal ileum and proximal colon are most often involved, but any segment can be."},{"type":"p","text":"NEC arises where an immature gut meets bacteria and feeds. The preterm intestine has weak tight junctions, a thin mucus layer, few Paneth cell defensins, slow motility that lets contents stagnate, and an abnormal, low-diversity microbiota. Preterm enterocytes express high levels of toll-like receptor 4 (TLR4), so bacterial lipopolysaccharide triggers enterocyte apoptosis, poor mucosal repair, and release of inflammatory mediators such as TNF, IL-6, and platelet-activating factor. Regulation of mesenteric vascular tone is also immature, and the balance tips toward vasoconstriction (more endothelin, less nitric oxide), so inflammation and low flow reinforce each other. Bacteria then invade the damaged wall."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pathogenesis of necrotizing enterocolitis","steps":["Preterm gut: weak barrier, slow motility, abnormal flora","Enteral feeds and bacteria activate enterocyte TLR4","Enterocyte death and inflammatory cytokine release","Mesenteric vasoconstriction causes mucosal ischemia","Bacteria invade the necrotic wall and produce gas","Pneumatosis, transmural necrosis, perforation, sepsis"],"caption":"Barrier failure, an exaggerated innate response, and ischemia drive each other forward."},{"type":"p","text":"Human milk lowers the risk compared with formula. It supplies secretory IgA, lactoferrin, growth factors such as epidermal growth factor, and oligosaccharides that favor protective bifidobacteria and dampen TLR4 signaling. In term infants, NEC is uncommon and usually follows a clear low-flow insult, such as congenital heart disease or perinatal asphyxia."},{"type":"p","text":"Each finding follows from the mechanism. Stasis and ileus cause feeding intolerance, increased gastric residuals, bilious vomiting, and abdominal distension. Mucosal sloughing produces bloody stools. Bacterial invasion and cytokine release cause systemic signs that are nonspecific in a neonate: temperature instability, apnea, bradycardia, and lethargy, with thrombocytopenia, metabolic acidosis, and sometimes neutropenia or disseminated intravascular coagulation. Tenderness, abdominal wall redness, and a palpable mass suggest transmural necrosis or perforation."},{"type":"table","title":"Radiographic signs in necrotizing enterocolitis","cols":["Sign","Appearance","What it means"],"rows":[["Dilated loops","Distended gas-filled bowel","Ileus; nonspecific early finding"],["Pneumatosis intestinalis","Bubbly or linear lucencies in the bowel wall","Gas from bacterial fermentation within the wall; the hallmark of NEC"],["Portal venous gas","Branching lucencies over the liver","Intramural gas has entered mesenteric and portal veins; more extensive disease"],["Pneumoperitoneum","Free air, outlining both sides of the bowel wall or the falciform ligament","Perforation"],["Fixed dilated loop","Same loop unchanged on serial films","Possible full-thickness necrosis"]],"caption":"Severity is often staged from suspected disease, to definite NEC with pneumatosis, to advanced disease with shock or perforation (modified Bell staging)."},{"type":"p","text":"Complications include perforation, sepsis, and later strictures (often in the colon), and extensive necrosis that requires resection can leave short bowel syndrome. NEC must be distinguished from spontaneous intestinal perforation, which affects extremely preterm infants in the first week or so, often before feeds, as an isolated perforation of the terminal ileum without pneumatosis or widespread necrosis. Midgut volvulus from malrotation, in which the bowel twists around the SMA and obstructs, causing bilious vomiting, and Hirschsprung enterocolitis are other causes of an acute abdomen in a newborn."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy predicts every disease in this chapter. Valveless portal veins and the left gastric to azygos connection explain varices; the pectinate line, where hindgut endoderm meets proctodeal ectoderm, sorts hemorrhoids into painless and painful types; arterial territories and their weakest collaterals explain where emboli and low flow injure bowel. Physiology supplies the physics: pressure equals flow times resistance in portal hypertension, and the law of Laplace explains why wide varices rupture and why angiodysplasia favors the cecum. Countercurrent exchange in the villus explains why ischemic injury starts in the mucosa, and anaerobic glycolysis explains the lactate."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology and immunology explain why these lesions become dangerous. Cirrhosis adds thrombocytopenia and factor deficiency, aortic stenosis removes the large vWF multimers that platelets need under shear, uremia impairs platelet function, and in the preterm gut an exaggerated TLR4 response to bacteria turns ischemia into transmural necrosis. The limits of this reasoning are worth remembering: lactate is a late and nonspecific marker, and the reason angiodysplasia is more common in kidney failure is not settled."}],"summary":["Portal hypertension reverses flow through the valveless left gastric vein into esophageal veins draining to the azygos system, dilating thin distal submucosal veins into varices.","Variceal rupture is painless and massive; thrombocytopenia, low clotting factors, and ammonia from digested blood make it a leading cause of death in cirrhosis.","Internal hemorrhoids lie above the pectinate line with visceral innervation, so they bleed bright red blood painlessly and prolapse; external hemorrhoids lie below it with somatic innervation and hurt when thrombosed.","Straining, constipation, low-fiber diet, and pregnancy push the anal cushions down and engorge them; anorectal varices of portal hypertension are a separate lesion.","Acute mesenteric ischemia, most often an SMA embolus from atrial fibrillation, causes pain out of proportion to the exam because visceral pain precedes parietal peritoneal irritation; lactate rises late.","Chronic mesenteric ischemia is postprandial demand ischemia with food fear and weight loss; ischemic colitis is low-flow injury to the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid watershed zones.","Angiodysplasia forms in the wide cecum through years of intermittent venous obstruction and causes painless intermittent bleeding and iron deficiency in older adults.","In Heyde syndrome, shear across a stenotic aortic valve lets ADAMTS13 cleave large vWF multimers; uremic platelet dysfunction similarly worsens bleeding in kidney failure.","Necrotizing enterocolitis in preterm infants combines barrier immaturity, TLR4-driven inflammation, and mucosal ischemia with bacterial invasion, giving distension, bloody stools, and pneumatosis intestinalis."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Gastrointestinal Tract, sections on esophageal varices, hemorrhoids, ischemic bowel disease, angiodysplasia, and necrotizing enterocolitis"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Liver and Gallbladder, section on portal hypertension"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen, hepatic portal vein and portosystemic anastomoses; Pelvis and Perineum, anal canal"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Digestive System, hindgut, cloaca, and anal canal"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Gastrointestinal physiology, splanchnic circulation and countercurrent blood flow in the villi"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Liver Disease, cirrhosis and portal hypertension"}]},"GI-13-K":{"unit":"GI-13-K","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-11","title":"Gastrointestinal Neoplasms","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers tumors of the digestive tract from the mouth and salivary glands to the anus, along with the liver, pancreas, and bile ducts. For each tumor it explains the precursor lesion and the molecular driver, why the tumor arises where it does, and how its growth and secretions produce the findings that appear in board questions: dysphagia, painless jaundice, refractory ulcers, flushing, anemia, or a palpable node. Most questions give a risk factor, a site, a marker, or a sign of spread and ask you to name the tumor or predict its behavior. If you can trace each finding back to anatomy, tumor biology, or a secreted product, you can reason through unfamiliar presentations.","prerequisites":["Hallmarks of cancer: oncogenes, tumor suppressor genes, and DNA repair genes","Metaplasia, dysplasia, and carcinoma in situ","Histology of the GI tract layers and of squamous versus columnar epithelium","Portal venous and lymphatic drainage of the abdomen, including the thoracic duct","Regulation of gastric acid secretion by gastrin, somatostatin, and secretin"],"connections":[{"to":"Gastritis and peptic ulcer disease","how":"H. pylori gastritis is the starting point for intestinal-type gastric cancer and MALT lymphoma, and gastrinoma is a cause of ulcers that resist the usual pattern."},{"to":"Cirrhosis and viral hepatitis","how":"Chronic hepatocyte injury and regeneration in cirrhosis, and HBV DNA integration, are the main routes to hepatocellular carcinoma."},{"to":"Inflammatory bowel disease and sclerosing cholangitis","how":"Long-standing colitis and primary sclerosing cholangitis cause dysplasia that progresses to colorectal carcinoma and cholangiocarcinoma."},{"to":"Endocrine system","how":"Gastrinoma is part of MEN1, and carcinoid tumors show how first-pass hepatic metabolism controls whether a hormone reaches the circulation."},{"to":"Microbiology: oncogenic infections","how":"H. pylori, HBV, HCV, EBV, HPV-16, and liver flukes each cause GI cancer by a different mechanism."}],"terms":[{"term":"Barrett esophagus","def":"Replacement of distal esophageal squamous epithelium by intestinal-type columnar epithelium with goblet cells, caused by chronic reflux."},{"term":"Signet ring cell","def":"A single tumor cell distended by intracellular mucin that pushes the nucleus to the periphery, typical of diffuse gastric cancer."},{"term":"Linitis plastica","def":"Diffuse thickening and rigidity of the stomach wall from infiltrating diffuse-type carcinoma and desmoplasia."},{"term":"Interstitial cells of Cajal","def":"KIT-expressing pacemaker cells in the muscularis propria that generate slow waves and give rise to GIST."},{"term":"Gastrinoma","def":"A neuroendocrine tumor of the duodenum or pancreas that secretes gastrin autonomously, causing Zollinger-Ellison syndrome."},{"term":"5-HIAA","def":"5-Hydroxyindoleacetic acid, the urinary metabolite of serotonin formed by monoamine oxidase and raised in carcinoid syndrome."},{"term":"Alpha-fetoprotein","def":"A fetal plasma protein re-expressed by many hepatocellular carcinomas and yolk sac tumors."},{"term":"Leukoplakia","def":"A white oral patch that cannot be scraped off or attributed to another disease and may contain dysplasia."},{"term":"Courvoisier sign","def":"A palpable, nontender gallbladder in a jaundiced patient, pointing to malignant obstruction of the distal common bile duct."},{"term":"Adenoma","def":"A benign neoplasm of glandular epithelium; in the colon its epithelium is dysplastic and it is a precursor of carcinoma."},{"term":"Microsatellite instability","def":"Variation in the length of short repeated DNA sequences caused by defective mismatch repair."},{"term":"Pectinate line","def":"The line in the anal canal separating endoderm-derived columnar lining above from ectoderm-derived squamous lining below."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-11-C01","obj":"GI-13-K.1","title":"Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The esophagus is lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium until the gastroesophageal junction, and its two carcinomas follow from that lining. Squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) arises where carcinogens bathe squamous mucosa for years, so it favors the upper and middle thirds (the middle third most often). Adenocarcinoma needs glandular epithelium first, which reflux supplies by converting the distal squamous lining to intestinal-type columnar epithelium (Barrett esophagus); it therefore sits in the distal third and at the gastroesophageal junction."},{"type":"p","text":"For SCC, alcohol and tobacco act together, and very hot beverages, nitrosamines, and poor diet add risk. Anything that keeps irritants in contact with the mucosa or causes chronic injury raises risk: retained, fermenting food above an achalasic sphincter, a lye (caustic) stricture, and the iron deficiency, glossitis, and upper esophageal webs of Plummer-Vinson syndrome. TP53 mutation is common. SCC is the most common type worldwide, while adenocarcinoma predominates in the United States and other Western countries, driven by chronic gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD), obesity (raised intra-abdominal pressure promotes reflux), and smoking."},{"type":"flow","title":"From reflux to esophageal adenocarcinoma","steps":["Chronic acid and bile reflux injures distal squamous mucosa","Columnar intestinal metaplasia with goblet cells (Barrett esophagus)","Acquired mutations (TP53 among the earliest) produce low-grade dysplasia","High-grade dysplasia","Invasive adenocarcinoma of the distal esophagus"],"caption":"Metaplasia is an adaptive change that becomes a precursor because the new epithelium keeps proliferating under injury."},{"type":"p","text":"Both types grow into the lumen and around the wall, so they cause mechanical dysphagia. A solid bolus needs a wide lumen, so dysphagia starts with solids (meat, bread) and progresses to liquids as the lumen narrows further. This contrasts with motility disorders such as achalasia, where solids and liquids are difficult from the start. Weight loss reflects both reduced intake and cancer cachexia driven by cytokines such as TNF and IL-6. Odynophagia, hoarseness from recurrent laryngeal nerve invasion, and cough from a tracheoesophageal fistula indicate local extension."},{"type":"p","text":"Most of the esophagus has adventitia instead of a serosa, and its submucosa has a rich longitudinal lymphatic network, so tumors spread early and often present late. Lymph node spread follows the level: cervical nodes for upper tumors, mediastinal and tracheobronchial nodes for middle tumors, and gastric and celiac nodes for distal tumors."},{"type":"table","title":"Esophageal carcinoma types","cols":["Feature","Squamous cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma"],"rows":[["Site","Upper and middle third","Distal third and gastroesophageal junction"],["Precursor","Squamous dysplasia","Barrett esophagus with dysplasia"],["Main risks","Alcohol, tobacco, hot liquids, achalasia, caustic injury, Plummer-Vinson","GERD, Barrett esophagus, obesity, tobacco"],["Where most common","Worldwide","United States and Western countries"],["Nodal spread","Cervical and mediastinal","Gastric and celiac"]],"caption":"Both cause progressive dysphagia, solids then liquids, with weight loss."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-11-C02","obj":"GI-13-K.2","title":"Gastric adenocarcinoma: intestinal and diffuse types","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Gastric adenocarcinoma is the most common gastric malignancy. The Lauren classification divides it by how tumor cells hold together, and the two types differ in cause, appearance, and spread. Most cases present late with weight loss, early satiety, dyspepsia, and iron deficiency anemia from slow bleeding."},{"type":"h","text":"Intestinal type"},{"type":"p","text":"Intestinal-type carcinoma forms glands that resemble colonic glands. It develops through a stepwise sequence of chronic gastritis, atrophy, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia, and carcinoma. Helicobacter pylori is the main driver: chronic inflammation generates reactive oxygen species and cell turnover, and atrophy lowers acid so bacteria colonize the stomach and convert dietary nitrates to N-nitroso compounds. Nitrosamines in smoked, salted, and pickled foods, low intake of fruit and vegetables, and autoimmune atrophic gastritis add risk. It typically forms an ulcerated or exophytic mass, often on the lesser curvature of the antrum, and is more common in older men."},{"type":"h","text":"Diffuse type"},{"type":"p","text":"Diffuse-type carcinoma begins with loss of E-cadherin, the adhesion protein encoded by CDH1 (germline in hereditary diffuse gastric cancer, or acquired). Without it, cells detach and infiltrate the wall singly. Each cell fills with mucin that pushes the nucleus to the edge, giving the signet ring cell. The infiltrating cells provoke a desmoplastic reaction that thickens and stiffens the whole wall (linitis plastica, the leather-bottle stomach), so the stomach cannot distend and early satiety is prominent. It is not linked to intestinal metaplasia, affects younger patients, and has an equal sex distribution."},{"type":"table","title":"Intestinal versus diffuse gastric adenocarcinoma","cols":["Feature","Intestinal type","Diffuse type"],"rows":[["Histology","Gland-forming","Signet ring cells infiltrating singly"],["Key molecular event","Stepwise mutations after metaplasia","Loss of E-cadherin (CDH1)"],["Precursor","Atrophic gastritis with intestinal metaplasia","None recognized"],["Gross form","Ulcerated or polypoid mass","Diffuse thickening (linitis plastica)"],["Patient","Older, male predominance","Younger, equal sex distribution"]],"caption":"Classify by cell cohesion: glands mean intestinal type; single signet ring cells mean diffuse type."},{"type":"h","text":"Signs of spread"},{"type":"p","text":"Abdominal lymph drains through the cisterna chyli and thoracic duct into the junction of the left internal jugular and subclavian veins, so a hard left supraclavicular node (Virchow node) signals abdominal cancer. A periumbilical nodule (Sister Mary Joseph nodule) reflects peritoneal and lymphatic spread to the umbilicus. Bilateral ovarian metastases filled with mucin-rich signet ring cells form a Krukenberg tumor, classically from diffuse gastric cancer. Hematogenous spread goes first to the liver through the portal vein."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-11-C03","obj":"GI-13-K.3","title":"Gastric MALT lymphoma and gastrointestinal stromal tumor","blocks":[{"type":"h","text":"MALT lymphoma"},{"type":"p","text":"The normal gastric mucosa has no organized lymphoid tissue. Chronic H. pylori infection recruits B and T cells and builds acquired mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT) with lymphoid follicles. Within it, a B cell clone can expand into an extranodal marginal zone lymphoma, an indolent, low-grade B cell neoplasm. Its neoplastic cells are small to medium B cells (CD20 positive, CD5 negative, CD10 negative) that invade and destroy gastric glands, forming lymphoepithelial lesions."},{"type":"p","text":"Early tumor growth is antigen driven. The malignant B cells depend on help from H. pylori-specific T cells (CD40 ligand and cytokines that activate NF-kB), so early lesions regress when the bacterial antigen is removed. Tumors that acquire the translocation t(11;18)(q21;q21) produce an API2-MALT1 fusion protein that activates NF-kB on its own; they no longer need T cell help and do not regress. With further mutations, a MALT lymphoma can transform to diffuse large B cell lymphoma."},{"type":"h","text":"Gastrointestinal stromal tumor"},{"type":"p","text":"A gastrointestinal stromal tumor (GIST) is the most common mesenchymal tumor of the GI tract. It arises from the interstitial cells of Cajal, the pacemaker cells within the muscularis propria that generate slow waves and express the receptor tyrosine kinase KIT (CD117). Most GISTs carry a gain-of-function KIT mutation, and a smaller group carries a PDGFRA mutation. The mutant receptor dimerizes and signals without its ligand (stem cell factor), driving continuous proliferation through the RAS-MAPK and PI3K-AKT pathways."},{"type":"p","text":"About 60% arise in the stomach and about 30% in the small intestine. Because the tumor grows within the muscle wall, it forms a well-circumscribed submucosal mass under intact or ulcerated mucosa; ulceration causes bleeding, and large masses cause fullness or obstruction. Histology shows spindle or epithelioid cells, and immunostaining for KIT (CD117) and DOG1 confirms the diagnosis. Malignant behavior is predicted by size, mitotic rate, and site, with gastric tumors behaving better than small bowel tumors of the same size."},{"type":"table","title":"MALT lymphoma versus GIST","cols":["Feature","Gastric MALT lymphoma","GIST"],"rows":[["Cell of origin","Marginal zone B cell","Interstitial cell of Cajal"],["Driver","Chronic H. pylori antigen; t(11;18) in autonomous tumors","Activating KIT (CD117) or PDGFRA mutation"],["Layer","Mucosa (lymphoepithelial lesions)","Muscularis propria (submucosal mass)"],["Markers","CD20 positive, CD5 and CD10 negative","CD117 and DOG1 positive"],["Most common site","Stomach","Stomach, then small intestine"]],"caption":"One is an immune cell tumor driven by antigen; the other is a pacemaker cell tumor driven by a mutant receptor."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-11-C04","obj":"GI-13-K.4","title":"Zollinger-Ellison syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Zollinger-Ellison syndrome is the result of a gastrinoma, a neuroendocrine tumor that secretes gastrin without feedback control. Most arise in the duodenum, and fewer in the pancreas, within the region bounded by the junction of the cystic and common bile ducts, the second and third parts of the duodenum, and the junction of the neck and body of the pancreas. Many are malignant, and the liver is the main site of metastasis. About a quarter occur in multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 (MEN1, loss of the menin tumor suppressor), where they are often multiple and duodenal and accompany parathyroid hyperplasia and pituitary adenomas; hypercalcemia from the parathyroid component further stimulates gastrin release."},{"type":"p","text":"Normally, gastric acid in the antrum stimulates D cells to release somatostatin, which inhibits G cells, so gastrin falls as acid rises. Tumor cells lack this control. Gastrin also acts as a trophic hormone, causing parietal and enterochromaffin-like cell hyperplasia and thick gastric folds, so both the number of acid-producing cells and the signal to them rise."},{"type":"flow","title":"Gastrinoma to ulcers and steatorrhea","steps":["Gastrinoma secretes gastrin without acid feedback","Parietal cell hyperplasia; maximal acid secretion","Acid exceeds duodenal and pancreatic bicarbonate buffering","Multiple, refractory ulcers, including distal duodenum and jejunum","Low intestinal pH inactivates lipase and precipitates bile acids","Fat malabsorption: diarrhea and steatorrhea"],"caption":"Each manifestation follows from acid delivered where it is not normally neutralized."},{"type":"p","text":"The manifestations follow directly. Ulcers are multiple, recur, resist usual healing, and appear beyond the duodenal bulb (postbulbar duodenum or jejunum), a location ordinary peptic ulcers rarely reach. Diarrhea is common, may be the presenting symptom, and has two causes: the large volume of secreted acid overwhelms intestinal absorption, and acid irreversibly inactivates pancreatic lipase and precipitates conjugated bile acids, so micelles fail and fat is malabsorbed (steatorrhea)."},{"type":"p","text":"Diagnosis rests on an inappropriately high gastrin in the presence of acid. A raised gastrin alone is not specific, because achlorhydria from atrophic gastritis and acid suppression with proton pump inhibitors also raise gastrin through loss of acid feedback; the gastric pH separates them. Secretin normally inhibits gastrin release from G cells, but gastrinoma cells express secretin receptors that stimulate release, so intravenous secretin produces a paradoxical rise in serum gastrin."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: two patients with high gastrin","text":"Patient A has fasting gastrin 900 pg/mL (reference under about 100 pg/mL) and gastric pH 1.5; after secretin, gastrin rises to 1,150 pg/mL. Patient B has fasting gastrin 700 pg/mL, gastric pH 6.5, and vitamin B12 deficiency. Step 1: in B, high pH means no acid, so gastrin is high because feedback is absent; this fits autoimmune atrophic gastritis. Step 2: in A, gastrin is high despite strong acid, so secretion is autonomous. Step 3: the rise of about 250 pg/mL after secretin, which would normally lower gastrin, confirms a gastrinoma."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-11-C05","obj":"GI-13-K.5","title":"Carcinoid tumors and carcinoid syndrome","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Carcinoid tumors are well-differentiated neuroendocrine tumors arising from enterochromaffin cells of the gut epithelium. They stain for chromogranin A and synaptophysin and grow slowly. The small intestine, especially the ileum, is the most common site of carcinoids that metastasize and cause symptoms, while appendiceal carcinoids are often small, found incidentally, and rarely spread. Small bowel carcinoids may cause obstruction or intussusception, and their secretions provoke dense fibrosis of the mesentery that kinks the bowel."},{"type":"p","text":"Midgut carcinoids make serotonin (5-hydroxytryptamine) from tryptophan by tryptophan hydroxylase and aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase, along with kallikrein (which generates bradykinin), tachykinins, and prostaglandins; histamine comes mainly from foregut (gastric and bronchial) carcinoids. Serotonin is inactivated by monoamine oxidase (MAO) to 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid (5-HIAA), which is excreted in urine. A raised 24-hour urinary 5-HIAA is the classic marker. Heavy diversion of tryptophan to serotonin can leave too little for niacin synthesis, producing pellagra."},{"type":"p","text":"Only a minority of patients with carcinoid tumors develop carcinoid syndrome, and the reason is anatomical. Venous blood from a small bowel tumor enters the portal vein, and hepatic MAO inactivates serotonin before it reaches the systemic circulation. Once tumor deposits grow in the liver, their products enter the hepatic veins directly and reach the right heart. Carcinoids that drain outside the portal system, such as bronchial or ovarian tumors, can cause the syndrome without liver metastasis."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why carcinoid syndrome needs the liver","steps":["Ileal carcinoid secretes serotonin into portal venous blood","Hepatic MAO converts serotonin to 5-HIAA","No systemic symptoms while the liver clears it","Liver metastases release products into hepatic veins","Flushing, secretory diarrhea, and bronchospasm","Right heart valves exposed; lung MAO protects the left heart"],"caption":"First-pass hepatic metabolism hides the tumor's products until metastases bypass it."},{"type":"table","title":"Carcinoid syndrome: findings and mechanisms","cols":["Finding","Main mediators and mechanism"],"rows":[["Episodic flushing","Kallikrein and bradykinin, tachykinins, histamine cause vasodilation"],["Watery diarrhea","Serotonin increases motility and intestinal secretion"],["Wheezing","Bronchoconstriction from serotonin, bradykinin, and histamine"],["Right-sided valve disease","Serotonin stimulates fibroblasts; plaques cause tricuspid regurgitation and pulmonary stenosis"],["Pellagra","Tryptophan diverted from niacin synthesis"]],"caption":"Urinary 5-HIAA is raised because the metastatic tumor mass produces large amounts of serotonin."},{"type":"p","text":"Other small intestinal tumors are uncommon relative to the size of the organ. Besides carcinoid, they include adenocarcinoma (most often duodenal and periampullary), lymphoma, and GIST."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with liver metastases from an ileal carcinoid has tricuspid regurgitation. Why are the mitral and aortic valves spared?","explain":"Serotonin reaches the right heart through the hepatic veins and inferior vena cava, but pulmonary MAO inactivates it before blood reaches the left heart. A right-to-left shunt or a bronchial carcinoid can involve the left side.","check":"The flow figure and the paragraph on hepatic first-pass inactivation."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-11-C06","obj":"GI-13-K.6","title":"Hepatocellular carcinoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) is the most common primary malignancy of the liver. Most cases arise in a cirrhotic liver, where repeated cycles of hepatocyte necrosis and regeneration, chronic inflammation with reactive oxygen species, and telomere shortening accumulate mutations in a field of proliferating cells. The risk factors are therefore mainly causes of chronic liver injury, with two important exceptions in which a carcinogen acts directly on hepatocyte DNA."},{"type":"table","title":"Risk factors for hepatocellular carcinoma","cols":["Risk factor","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Chronic hepatitis B","DNA virus integrates into host DNA; HBx protein disrupts growth control and p53; can cause HCC without cirrhosis"],["Chronic hepatitis C","RNA virus that does not integrate; acts through chronic inflammation and cirrhosis"],["Alcohol","Cirrhosis; acetaldehyde and oxidative injury"],["Hereditary hemochromatosis","Iron catalyzes free radical formation; fibrosis and cirrhosis"],["Aflatoxin B1","Hepatic activation to an epoxide that mutates TP53 codon 249"],["Metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease","Steatohepatitis progressing to fibrosis"]],"caption":"HBV and aflatoxin act directly on DNA; most other risks work through cirrhosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Two exceptions deserve detail. Hepatitis B virus is a DNA virus whose genome integrates into hepatocyte chromosomes, causing insertional mutations and genomic instability, and its HBx protein transactivates host growth genes and binds p53. HBV can therefore produce HCC even without cirrhosis. Aflatoxin B1 comes from Aspergillus flavus growing on improperly stored grains and peanuts. Hepatic cytochrome P450 enzymes convert it to aflatoxin B1-8,9-epoxide, which binds guanine and causes a G to T transversion at codon 249 of TP53 (arginine to serine). HBV and aflatoxin together multiply risk, which is why HCC is common in parts of sub-Saharan Africa and East Asia."},{"type":"p","text":"HCC may present as hepatomegaly, right upper quadrant pain, weight loss, and a liver mass, or as sudden decompensation of known cirrhosis with new ascites, encephalopathy, or variceal bleeding. Serum alpha-fetoprotein (AFP), a fetal plasma protein that the tumor re-expresses, is raised in many patients (normal adult level under about 10 ng/mL); it is not specific, because it also rises in pregnancy, yolk sac tumors, and regenerating liver, and it is normal in a substantial minority of HCC."},{"type":"p","text":"HCC has a strong tendency to invade blood vessels. Growth into the portal vein causes portal vein thrombosis and worsens portal hypertension, invasion of the hepatic veins can produce Budd-Chiari syndrome, and hematogenous metastasis goes most often to the lungs. Paraneoplastic effects include erythrocytosis from erythropoietin and hypoglycemia. The fibrolamellar variant is an important exception: it affects adolescents and young adults without cirrhosis or viral hepatitis, shows tumor cells in lamellar bands of collagen, and usually has a normal AFP."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-11-C07","obj":"GI-13-K.7","title":"Liver metastases, peritoneal tumors, and benign liver lesions","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Metastases are far more common than primary tumors in both the liver and the peritoneum. The liver receives the whole portal venous drainage of the GI tract plus systemic arterial blood, and its sinusoids are lined by fenestrated endothelium without a continuous basement membrane, so circulating tumor cells lodge and reach hepatocytes easily. The usual primaries are colon, pancreas, stomach, breast, and lung, plus melanoma. Metastases typically form multiple nodules of different sizes; subcapsular nodules may show central necrosis and umbilication. Because a large reserve of normal liver remains, synthetic function is preserved until late, and the earliest laboratory change is often a raised alkaline phosphatase and gamma-glutamyl transferase from local bile duct obstruction."},{"type":"p","text":"The peritoneum is seeded by transcoelomic spread, most often from ovarian, gastric, colorectal, and pancreatic carcinomas. Tumor implants on serosal surfaces and the omentum (an omental cake) leak protein and block lymphatic drainage, producing malignant ascites. Primary peritoneal tumors are rare and include malignant mesothelioma, associated with asbestos exposure, and primary peritoneal serous carcinoma. Mucinous neoplasms of the appendix can fill the peritoneal cavity with gelatinous mucin (pseudomyxoma peritonei)."},{"type":"h","text":"Benign liver lesions"},{"type":"p","text":"Cavernous hemangioma is the most common benign tumor of the liver. It consists of large, blood-filled vascular channels lined by a single layer of endothelium, is usually small, subcapsular, and asymptomatic, and is found incidentally on imaging. Its vascular nature means percutaneous needle biopsy risks serious bleeding."},{"type":"p","text":"Hepatocellular adenoma is a benign proliferation of hepatocytes that lacks portal tracts and bile ducts. It occurs mainly in young women using oral contraceptives and in anabolic steroid users, because estrogens and androgens stimulate hepatocyte growth, and in glycogen storage disease. The tumor is supplied by thin-walled arteries without a portal system, often lies beneath the capsule, and can rupture into the peritoneal cavity, causing sudden pain and hemorrhagic shock; risk is greatest in pregnancy, when estrogen and blood volume are high. Adenomas with activating beta-catenin mutations can transform into hepatocellular carcinoma."},{"type":"table","title":"Liver masses","cols":["Lesion","Typical patient","Distinguishing features"],"rows":[["Metastases","Known colon, pancreas, stomach, breast, or lung cancer","Most common liver malignancy; multiple nodules"],["Hepatocellular carcinoma","Cirrhosis, chronic HBV","Solitary, multifocal, or diffuse; raised AFP; vein invasion"],["Cavernous hemangioma","Any adult","Most common benign tumor; vascular channels; biopsy causes bleeding"],["Hepatocellular adenoma","Young woman on oral contraceptives; anabolic steroid use","No portal tracts; rupture and hemorrhage, especially in pregnancy"],["Focal nodular hyperplasia","Young women","Hyperplastic nodule with central stellate scar; not premalignant"]],"caption":"Begin with metastasis for any liver mass in a patient with a known primary cancer."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-11-C08","obj":"GI-13-K.8","title":"White oral lesions and oral and oropharyngeal carcinoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Oral mucosa is stratified squamous epithelium that is moist and usually nonkeratinized. When it thickens, keratinizes, or is covered by a membrane, it looks white, so several unrelated processes share that appearance. The first bedside test is whether the white material scrapes off, because that separates a surface coating from a change within the epithelium itself."},{"type":"table","title":"White lesions of the mouth","cols":["Lesion","Cause and tissue change","Scrapes off?","Setting and significance"],"rows":[["Leukoplakia","Hyperkeratosis or acanthosis, sometimes with dysplasia","No","Tobacco and alcohol; potentially premalignant, so biopsy"],["Pseudomembranous candidiasis","Candida albicans hyphae, fibrin, and debris on the surface","Yes, leaving a red, raw base","Infants, antibiotics, inhaled corticosteroids, diabetes, dentures, HIV"],["Hairy leukoplakia","Epstein-Barr virus replicating in squamous cells","No","Corrugated plaques on lateral tongue; HIV or other immunosuppression; not premalignant"]],"caption":"Leukoplakia is a clinical description, not a diagnosis; only histology shows whether dysplasia is present."},{"type":"p","text":"Leukoplakia is defined clinically as a white patch or plaque that cannot be scraped off and cannot be attributed to another disease. Histology ranges from simple hyperkeratosis to dysplasia or carcinoma in situ, and a proportion progresses to invasive cancer, so the lesion must be sampled. A red velvety patch (erythroplakia) is less common but much more often dysplastic. Hairy leukoplakia is a marker of immune suppression rather than a precursor of cancer."},{"type":"h","text":"Oral and oropharyngeal squamous cell carcinoma"},{"type":"p","text":"Most malignancies of the oral cavity are squamous cell carcinomas. Tobacco (smoked or chewed, including betel quid) and alcohol act synergistically: alcohol dissolves carcinogens and increases their penetration of the mucosa, and its metabolite acetaldehyde is itself mutagenic. Because the whole lining is exposed, many separate areas accumulate mutations (field cancerization), which explains multiple primary tumors and second primaries in the larynx, esophagus, and lung. TP53 mutation is common. Typical sites are the lateral tongue and floor of the mouth, and the lower lip is affected by ultraviolet exposure. Spread is to cervical lymph nodes."},{"type":"p","text":"Oropharyngeal carcinoma of the tonsils and base of tongue follows a different path. High-risk human papillomavirus, mainly HPV-16, infects the reticulated epithelium of the tonsillar crypts. Its E6 protein targets p53 for degradation and its E7 protein inactivates Rb, so TP53 is typically not mutated. Loss of Rb function leads to overexpression of p16, which serves as a surrogate marker. These tumors occur in younger patients, often nonsmokers, frequently present as a cystic neck node, and have a better prognosis than tobacco-related tumors."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-11-C09","obj":"GI-13-K.9","title":"Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Ductal adenocarcinoma accounts for about 85% of pancreatic cancers and is one of the most lethal cancers, because it is usually unresectable or metastatic at diagnosis. It arises from ductal epithelium through microscopic precursors called pancreatic intraepithelial neoplasia (PanIN). An activating KRAS mutation is found in over 90% of tumors and appears early; it locks the RAS GTPase in its active GTP-bound state and drives proliferation. Inactivation of CDKN2A (p16), then TP53 and SMAD4, follows as the lesion progresses. Smoking is the strongest environmental risk; chronic pancreatitis, long-standing diabetes, obesity, age, and inherited syndromes (BRCA2, hereditary pancreatitis, Peutz-Jeghers, Lynch) add risk. The tumor is gray-white and very hard because of intense desmoplasia, and it typically invades nerves, lymphatics, and the retroperitoneum early."},{"type":"p","text":"Location determines presentation. About 60% arise in the head, where the intrapancreatic common bile duct passes through the gland."},{"type":"flow","title":"Pancreatic head tumor to Courvoisier sign","steps":["Tumor in pancreatic head encases the distal common bile duct","Slow, progressive obstruction without inflammation","Conjugated hyperbilirubinemia: jaundice, dark urine, pale stool, pruritus","Bile backs up into a previously healthy gallbladder","Thin, compliant wall distends: palpable, nontender gallbladder"],"caption":"Gradual obstruction of a normal duct system causes painless jaundice with a distended gallbladder."},{"type":"p","text":"Courvoisier sign, a palpable nontender gallbladder with jaundice, points to malignant obstruction of the distal common bile duct, classically a pancreatic head carcinoma. A gallstone in the common bile duct usually does not produce it, because repeated cholecystitis has made the gallbladder fibrotic and unable to distend. Tumors of the body and tail obstruct nothing early, so they present late with weight loss and deep epigastric pain radiating to the back, from invasion of the celiac nerve plexus."},{"type":"p","text":"Weight loss reflects cachexia plus maldigestion when the pancreatic duct is blocked. Migratory thrombophlebitis (Trousseau syndrome), recurrent venous thromboses at changing sites, arises because tumor cells release mucins and tissue factor that activate coagulation. New-onset diabetes in an older adult can be an early sign. CA 19-9, a sialylated Lewis blood group antigen, is raised in most patients, but it is not specific (it rises in any biliary obstruction and in other cancers) and is absent in people who cannot synthesize Lewis antigens, so it is used to follow known disease rather than to screen."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A jaundiced patient has a palpable, nontender gallbladder. Why does this argue against a common bile duct stone?","explain":"Stone disease usually follows years of cholecystitis that leaves the gallbladder scarred and noncompliant, so it cannot distend. Slow malignant obstruction of a healthy system lets the gallbladder fill and stretch.","check":"The flow figure and the paragraph on Courvoisier sign."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-11-C10","obj":"GI-13-K.10","title":"Colorectal carcinoma","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Colorectal adenocarcinoma is one of the most common causes of cancer death, with incidence rising steeply after about age 50. Most cases follow one of three molecular routes from a benign precursor to invasive cancer. The chromosomal instability (adenoma-carcinoma) pathway is the most common. The mismatch repair pathway underlies Lynch syndrome and some sporadic tumors: loss of MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, or PMS2 lets replication errors accumulate in short repeated sequences (microsatellite instability). The serrated pathway, described with polyps, starts with BRAF mutation and promoter methylation."},{"type":"flow","title":"Adenoma-carcinoma sequence","steps":["Loss of APC in normal colonic mucosa","Beta-catenin accumulates and drives proliferation","Small adenoma forms","KRAS mutation enlarges the adenoma","Loss of TP53 (with other changes, often chromosome 18q)","Invasive adenocarcinoma through the muscularis mucosae"],"caption":"APC normally targets beta-catenin for destruction; its loss is the first step in familial adenomatous polyposis and most sporadic tumors."},{"type":"p","text":"Hereditary syndromes show each pathway in its purest form. Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP), from a germline APC mutation, produces hundreds to thousands of adenomas, and carcinoma develops in nearly all affected people by middle age. Lynch syndrome, from a germline mismatch repair gene mutation, produces few polyps but rapid progression, mainly in the right colon, with added endometrial, ovarian, and other cancers; both are autosomal dominant. Chronic inflammatory bowel disease (especially extensive, long-standing ulcerative colitis) raises risk through flat dysplasia in inflamed mucosa, with TP53 changes occurring early. Other risks include age, family history, diets high in red and processed meat and low in fiber, obesity, and smoking."},{"type":"p","text":"Symptoms depend on the side of the colon. The cecum and ascending colon have a wide lumen and liquid contents, so tumors grow as bulky exophytic masses and bleed slowly before they obstruct, producing iron deficiency anemia, fatigue, and weight loss. The descending and sigmoid colon is narrower and carries formed stool, so an annular tumor (napkin-ring, apple-core narrowing on imaging) causes a change in bowel habit, thin stools, visible blood, and obstruction. Rectal tumors add tenesmus and bright red bleeding. Streptococcus gallolyticus (formerly S. bovis) bacteremia or endocarditis is associated with colonic neoplasia."},{"type":"table","title":"Right-sided versus left-sided colon cancer","cols":["Feature","Right-sided","Left-sided"],"rows":[["Lumen and contents","Wide; liquid stool","Narrow; formed stool"],["Growth pattern","Exophytic, polypoid mass","Annular, napkin-ring constriction"],["Main presentation","Occult bleeding, iron deficiency anemia, fatigue","Change in bowel habit, obstruction, visible blood"],["Associated pathway","Mismatch repair and serrated tumors more common","Chromosomal instability more common"]],"caption":"Right-sided tumors bleed silently; left-sided tumors obstruct."},{"type":"p","text":"Carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) is a glycoprotein expressed by many adenocarcinomas. It is not used to screen, because it is often normal in early cancer and is raised in smokers, inflammatory bowel disease, and other cancers. A raised preoperative CEA that falls after complete resection and then rises again indicates recurrence. Colorectal cancer spreads to regional nodes and through the portal vein to the liver, while low rectal tumors can drain into systemic veins and reach the lungs."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-11-C11","obj":"GI-13-K.11","title":"Colonic polyps","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"A polyp is any mass projecting into the lumen. It may be sessile (broad-based) or pedunculated (on a stalk), and its importance depends on whether its epithelium is neoplastic. Classification follows the cell biology."},{"type":"p","text":"Hyperplastic polyps are the most common non-neoplastic polyps. They are small (usually under 5 mm), found mainly in the rectosigmoid, and form because surface epithelial cells are shed more slowly and pile up, giving a serrated, saw-tooth outline confined to the upper crypts. They have negligible malignant potential."},{"type":"p","text":"Adenomas are neoplastic by definition, because their epithelium is dysplastic, with enlarged, elongated, hyperchromatic nuclei. They are the precursors of most colorectal cancer. They are classified by architecture: tubular adenomas (mostly tubular glands, the most common type), tubulovillous adenomas (mixed), and villous adenomas (long finger-like projections, usually sessile). Malignant risk rises with size (especially over about 1 cm), with the villous component, and with high-grade dysplasia, because each reflects more accumulated mutations. Large villous adenomas can secrete mucus rich in protein and potassium, causing hypokalemia."},{"type":"p","text":"Sessile serrated lesions look like hyperplastic polyps but are premalignant. They are flat, often in the right colon, and serration extends to the base of the crypts, which become dilated and spread sideways along the muscularis mucosae. They begin with a BRAF mutation and widespread CpG island methylation; methylation that silences MLH1 produces microsatellite instability. Because they are flat and pale, they are easily missed."},{"type":"p","text":"Hamartomatous polyps are disorganized overgrowths of tissue normally present at the site. Juvenile polyps occur in children, usually in the rectum, and contain cystically dilated glands in an inflamed lamina propria; they bleed or prolapse. A solitary juvenile polyp carries little risk, but juvenile polyposis syndrome (SMAD4 or BMPR1A) raises cancer risk. Peutz-Jeghers syndrome (autosomal dominant, STK11) causes hamartomatous polyps with branching smooth muscle, mainly in the small intestine, where they can lead to intussusception, plus dark freckles on the lips and oral mucosa and a raised risk of GI, breast, pancreatic, and gonadal cancers."},{"type":"table","title":"Colonic polyps","cols":["Polyp","Key features","Malignant potential"],"rows":[["Hyperplastic","Small, rectosigmoid; serration in upper crypts","Negligible"],["Tubular adenoma","Most common adenoma; dysplastic tubular glands","Present; lowest among adenomas"],["Tubulovillous adenoma","Mixed tubular and villous architecture","Intermediate"],["Villous adenoma","Finger-like villi; often sessile; may cause hypokalemia","Highest among adenomas"],["Sessile serrated lesion","Flat, right colon; serration and dilation at crypt base; BRAF","Yes, through the serrated pathway"],["Juvenile polyp","Child, rectum; cystic glands","Very low if solitary; raised in juvenile polyposis"],["Peutz-Jeghers polyp","Small bowel; branching smooth muscle; lip pigmentation","Raised risk of GI and other cancers"]],"caption":"For adenomas, size, villous architecture, and grade of dysplasia predict cancer risk."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two 6 mm serrated polyps are removed, one from the sigmoid with serration in the upper crypts and one from the cecum with dilated, sideways crypt bases. Which is a precursor of cancer?","explain":"The cecal lesion is a sessile serrated lesion; its BRAF mutation and methylation, including MLH1 silencing, lead to carcinoma. The sigmoid lesion is a hyperplastic polyp from slowed cell shedding.","check":"The hyperplastic polyp and sessile serrated lesion paragraphs."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-11-C12","obj":"GI-13-K.12","title":"Salivary gland tumors","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Most salivary gland tumors arise in the parotid gland, and most parotid tumors are benign. The proportion that is malignant rises as gland size falls, so a mass in the submandibular, sublingual, or a minor salivary gland is more likely to be malignant. A benign parotid tumor typically presents as a slow-growing, painless, mobile mass in front of or below the ear. Pain, rapid growth, fixation, or facial nerve weakness suggests malignancy, because the facial nerve runs through the parotid and infiltrating tumors invade it."},{"type":"p","text":"Pleomorphic adenoma is the most common salivary gland tumor and is usually parotid. It is called a mixed tumor because it contains epithelial and myoepithelial cells in a chondromyxoid stroma that can resemble cartilage; myoepithelial cells are thought to produce the mesenchymal-appearing matrix. The tumor is only partly encapsulated, and small fingerlike projections extend through the capsule, so a lesion shelled out along its surface leaves tumor behind and recurs. Long-standing or recurrent tumors carry a small risk of malignant transformation (carcinoma ex pleomorphic adenoma)."},{"type":"p","text":"Warthin tumor (papillary cystadenoma lymphomatosum) is the second most common benign salivary tumor and occurs almost only in the parotid. It affects older adults and is strongly associated with smoking, and it is bilateral or multifocal in about 10% of cases. Histology is distinctive: cystic spaces lined by a double layer of oncocytic epithelial cells rest on a dense lymphoid stroma with germinal centers."},{"type":"p","text":"Mucoepidermoid carcinoma is the most common primary malignant salivary tumor and most often arises in the parotid. It is composed of mucus-secreting, squamous (epidermoid), and intermediate cells, and its behavior varies with grade. Many carry a CRTC1-MAML2 fusion gene from t(11;19). Adenoid cystic carcinoma, which often arises in minor salivary glands, is notable for perineural invasion that causes pain and allows recurrence along nerves."},{"type":"table","title":"Salivary gland tumors","cols":["Tumor","Behavior","Key features"],"rows":[["Pleomorphic adenoma","Benign; most common salivary tumor","Parotid; epithelial and myoepithelial cells in chondromyxoid stroma; recurs if incompletely excised"],["Warthin tumor","Benign","Parotid; older smokers; may be bilateral; oncocytic cysts with lymphoid stroma"],["Mucoepidermoid carcinoma","Most common salivary malignancy","Parotid; mucous, squamous, and intermediate cells"],["Adenoid cystic carcinoma","Malignant","Minor salivary glands; perineural invasion"]],"caption":"In the parotid, a painless mobile mass is usually benign; nerve involvement suggests carcinoma."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-11-C13","obj":"GI-13-K.13","title":"Biliary tract carcinomas and anal carcinoma","blocks":[{"type":"h","text":"Cholangiocarcinoma"},{"type":"p","text":"Cholangiocarcinoma is an adenocarcinoma of bile duct epithelium. It is classified by site as intrahepatic, perihilar (at the confluence of the right and left hepatic ducts, the most common site), or distal. Its risk factors share chronic inflammation and bile stasis, which keep cholangiocytes proliferating under injury. Primary sclerosing cholangitis, a fibrosing disease of the bile ducts usually accompanying ulcerative colitis, is the main Western risk. In East and Southeast Asia, the liver flukes Clonorchis sinensis and Opisthorchis viverrini, acquired from raw freshwater fish, live in the ducts and cause chronic inflammation; choledochal cysts and hepatolithiasis add risk."},{"type":"p","text":"Most patients present with painless obstructive jaundice, pruritus, pale stools, dark urine, raised alkaline phosphatase, and weight loss. The gallbladder finding helps localize the block: a perihilar tumor lies above the cystic duct, so the gallbladder stays empty, while a distal tumor allows it to distend like a pancreatic head tumor. Adenocarcinoma of the ampulla of Vater obstructs early, so it presents with jaundice while still small; necrosis of the tumor can make jaundice fluctuate, and bleeding into the duodenum can cause occult blood loss and anemia."},{"type":"h","text":"Gallbladder adenocarcinoma"},{"type":"p","text":"Gallbladder carcinoma is the most common malignancy of the extrahepatic biliary tract. Gallstones are present in most cases and are thought to act through chronic irritation and inflammation; a calcified (porcelain) gallbladder also signals risk. It affects older women more than men. Early disease causes no specific symptoms, and the thin wall lies against the liver, so it usually invades the liver or bile ducts before diagnosis; jaundice then signals advanced disease."},{"type":"h","text":"Anal carcinoma"},{"type":"p","text":"The pectinate (dentate) line divides the anal canal by embryology. Above it, the lining is columnar (endoderm), drains to internal iliac nodes, and has visceral innervation, so tumors there are usually adenocarcinomas. Below it, the lining is stratified squamous (ectoderm), drains to superficial inguinal nodes, and has somatic pudendal innervation, so lesions there are painful. Anal squamous cell carcinoma arises at the transformation zone and below, and most cases are caused by high-risk HPV, mainly HPV-16, through the same E6 and E7 mechanism as in cervical cancer. Risk is highest with receptive anal intercourse and HIV or other immunosuppression; the precursor is anal intraepithelial neoplasia."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: locating painless jaundice","text":"Three patients have painless conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with raised alkaline phosphatase. Patient A has a distended, nontender gallbladder; B has dilated intrahepatic ducts, a collapsed gallbladder, and long-standing ulcerative colitis; C has jaundice that waxes and wanes with occult blood in the stool. Step 1: a distended gallbladder means the block lies below the cystic duct, so A fits a pancreatic head or distal bile duct tumor. Step 2: in B the block lies above the cystic duct; with ulcerative colitis, suspect primary sclerosing cholangitis with perihilar cholangiocarcinoma. Step 3: in C, a small tumor that necroses and bleeds into the duodenum fits ampullary adenocarcinoma."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"The same few mechanisms recur along the whole tract. Chronic injury forces epithelium to keep proliferating, and either metaplasia (Barrett esophagus, intestinal metaplasia of the stomach) or regeneration (cirrhosis, inflamed colitis, inflamed bile ducts) gives mutations a dividing population in which to accumulate. Infection works through several routes: H. pylori through inflammation and antigen-driven B cell growth, HBV through integration, HPV-16 through E6 and E7 inactivation of p53 and Rb, and liver flukes through chronic ductal injury. Single-gene drivers explain other tumors: APC and mismatch repair genes in the colon, KRAS in the pancreas, CDH1 in diffuse gastric cancer, and KIT in GIST."},{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and physiology then decide how a tumor shows itself. Lumen size and stool consistency separate right and left colon cancer; the course of the common bile duct through the pancreatic head explains painless jaundice; the thoracic duct explains the Virchow node; and portal drainage explains both why the liver is the commonest site of metastasis and why carcinoid syndrome waits for liver deposits. Markers such as AFP, CEA, CA 19-9, and urinary 5-HIAA reflect the biology of the tumor cell, but each has false positives and false negatives, so they support a diagnosis or track known disease rather than establish one alone."}],"summary":["Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma arises in the upper and middle third from alcohol, tobacco, and chronic mucosal injury; adenocarcinoma arises distally from reflux through Barrett esophagus.","Intestinal-type gastric cancer forms glands after H. pylori gastritis and intestinal metaplasia; diffuse type has signet ring cells, CDH1 loss, and linitis plastica.","Gastric MALT lymphoma depends on H. pylori-driven T cell help until t(11;18) makes it autonomous; GIST arises from KIT-mutant interstitial cells of Cajal.","Gastrinoma causes multiple and distal ulcers and steatorrhea from acid-inactivated lipase, with a paradoxical gastrin rise after secretin and a link to MEN1.","Carcinoid syndrome appears after liver metastases bypass hepatic MAO; lung MAO protects the left heart, so right-sided valves scar; urinary 5-HIAA is raised.","HBV and aflatoxin B1 (TP53 codon 249) can cause hepatocellular carcinoma directly; most other risks act through cirrhosis; AFP is raised and the tumor invades veins.","Metastases are the most common malignancy in the liver and peritoneum; hemangioma is the most common benign liver tumor, and hepatocellular adenoma can rupture.","Pancreatic head adenocarcinoma (KRAS) causes painless jaundice with Courvoisier sign, plus migratory thrombophlebitis, weight loss, and raised CA 19-9.","Right colon cancer bleeds silently and causes iron deficiency anemia; left colon cancer constricts and obstructs; CEA tracks recurrence, not screening.","Adenoma risk rises with size, villous architecture, and dysplasia; sessile serrated lesions are premalignant through BRAF and methylation, unlike hyperplastic polyps."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Head and Neck; The Gastrointestinal Tract"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Liver and Gallbladder; Pancreas; Neoplasia"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Human Cancer Viruses; Hepatitis Viruses"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Secretory Functions of the Alimentary Tract, regulation of gastric secretion"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Conversion of amino acids to specialized products, serotonin synthesis and degradation"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen, anal canal and pectinate line; lymphatic drainage and the thoracic duct"}]},"GI-13-L":{"unit":"GI-13-L","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-12","title":"Infectious Gastrointestinal Diseases","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers infection along the whole digestive tract: the mouth and teeth, the esophagus, the small and large bowel, and the liver. The organisms differ, but the reasoning is the same each time. Ask what normally keeps the organism in check, which cell or layer it attacks, and whether it damages tissue by invasion, by toxin, or through the host immune response. That mechanism predicts the stool (watery or bloody), the lesion (plaque, vesicle, ulcer, or abscess), the timing, and the complication. Board questions give a setting, such as recent antibiotics, a cruise ship, an AIDS patient with painful swallowing, or a serology panel, and ask you to name the organism and predict what comes next.","prerequisites":["Histology of the oral mucosa, periodontium, esophagus, small intestinal villi, colon, and liver lobule","Intestinal fluid secretion through CFTR and absorption through sodium-coupled transport","G protein signaling through cAMP and cGMP","Bacterial structure, spores, exotoxins, and endotoxin; viral genome types","Innate and adaptive immunity, including cytotoxic T cells, antibody classes, and type III hypersensitivity"],"connections":[{"to":"Renal system","how":"Shiga toxin injures glomerular endothelium, so EHEC infection can end in acute kidney injury through hemolytic uremic syndrome."},{"to":"Nervous system","how":"Antibodies raised against Campylobacter cross-react with peripheral nerve gangliosides and cause Guillain-Barre syndrome."},{"to":"Musculoskeletal system","how":"Reactive arthritis after Shigella, Salmonella, Campylobacter, or Yersinia enteritis is strongly linked to HLA-B27."},{"to":"Immunology and HIV infection","how":"Thrush and esophagitis from Candida, HSV, and CMV track the fall in CD4 T cells and mark advancing immunosuppression."},{"to":"Hepatic pathology and neoplasia","how":"Chronic HBV and HCV infection drive cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma by different molecular routes."}],"terms":[{"term":"Pseudomembrane","def":"An adherent plaque of fibrin, mucus, neutrophils, and necrotic epithelium that forms on injured mucosa and resembles a membrane."},{"term":"Enterotoxin","def":"A bacterial toxin that acts on intestinal epithelium to cause fluid secretion, often without killing the cell."},{"term":"Dysentery","def":"Frequent small-volume stools containing blood and mucus with cramps and tenesmus, caused by mucosal invasion or cytotoxic injury of the colon."},{"term":"Hemolytic uremic syndrome","def":"A thrombotic microangiopathy with microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney injury."},{"term":"Molecular mimicry","def":"Cross-reaction of an immune response against a microbial antigen with a structurally similar host molecule."},{"term":"Odynophagia","def":"Pain on swallowing, which points to ulceration or inflammation of the esophageal mucosa."},{"term":"Biofilm","def":"A structured community of bacteria embedded in a self-made extracellular matrix and attached to a surface."},{"term":"Pseudohyphae","def":"Chains of elongated budding yeast cells that stay attached, with constrictions at each septum, typical of Candida in tissue."},{"term":"Window period","def":"The interval in hepatitis B after HBsAg has cleared and before anti-HBs is detectable, when IgM anti-HBc is the only marker of acute infection."},{"term":"Quasispecies","def":"A population of closely related but genetically different viral variants within one host, produced by an error-prone polymerase."},{"term":"Cryoglobulin","def":"An immunoglobulin or immune complex that precipitates in the cold and redissolves on warming."},{"term":"Anchovy paste fluid","def":"The thick, odorless, reddish-brown aspirate of an amebic liver abscess, made of liquefied liver tissue and blood with few neutrophils."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-12-C01","obj":"GI-13-L.1","title":"Pseudomembranous colitis from Clostridioides difficile","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The healthy colon holds a dense anaerobic flora that resists colonization by newcomers. Part of that resistance is metabolic: resident bacteria convert primary bile acids into secondary bile acids, and they use up the nutrients a newcomer would need. Primary bile acids such as taurocholate trigger germination of C. difficile spores, while secondary bile acids inhibit growth of the vegetative cells."},{"type":"p","text":"Clostridioides difficile is a gram-positive, spore-forming, obligately anaerobic bacillus. Its spores survive drying, alcohol hand rubs, and many surface disinfectants, so they persist on hospital surfaces and hands and are swallowed. Almost any antibiotic can disturb the flora, but clindamycin, fluoroquinolones, and cephalosporins carry the highest risk. Older age, hospital or nursing home stay, and acid suppression add risk, and disease can follow even a single dose or begin weeks after the drug is stopped."},{"type":"flow","title":"From antibiotic exposure to pseudomembranes","steps":["Antibiotic depletes resident colonic flora","Secondary bile acids fall; primary bile acids rise","Ingested spores germinate into toxin-producing cells","Toxins A and B glucosylate Rho family GTPases","Actin collapses, tight junctions open, colonocytes die","Neutrophils, fibrin, and mucus form pseudomembranes"],"caption":"Loss of colonization resistance lets spores germinate; toxin injury and the neutrophil response build the pseudomembranes."},{"type":"p","text":"The organism does not invade. Disease is caused by two large exotoxins. Toxin A (TcdA) is mainly an enterotoxin that draws fluid and neutrophils, and toxin B (TcdB) is a more potent cytotoxin. Both enter colonocytes and glucosylate Rho, Rac, and Cdc42, the GTPases that maintain the actin cytoskeleton. The cells round up, tight junctions open, fluid leaks into the lumen, and dying cells release IL-8 and other signals that recruit neutrophils. Some epidemic strains also make a binary toxin and produce more toxin A and B."},{"type":"p","text":"Grossly the colon shows raised yellow-white plaques scattered over a reddened mucosa. Microscopically the exudate bursts out of damaged crypts in a mushroom or volcano pattern. Patients have watery, usually nonbloody diarrhea, cramping, fever, and often a marked neutrophil leukocytosis. In severe colitis the inflamed wall loses tone and the colon dilates (toxic megacolon, usually a transverse colon diameter over about 6 cm with systemic toxicity), which can perforate."},{"type":"p","text":"Diagnosis rests on finding the toxin or its genes in unformed stool, by enzyme immunoassay for toxin or by nucleic acid amplification for the toxin B gene. Because people can carry the organism without disease, a positive test is meaningful only in a patient with three or more unformed stools in a day. Endoscopic pseudomembranes are characteristic but not required."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a patient develop C. difficile colitis after a course of antibiotics even though the antibiotic did not bring the organism?","explain":"The antibiotic removed the resident flora that made secondary bile acids and competed for nutrients, so swallowed spores could germinate and the toxin-producing cells could multiply.","check":"Compare with the first paragraph and the flow figure on colonization resistance."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-12-C02","obj":"GI-13-L.2","title":"Bloody enterocolitis and its complications","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Diarrhea becomes bloody when the organism breaks the mucosa, either by invading epithelial cells or by killing them with a cytotoxin. The result is dysentery: frequent small stools with blood, mucus, and pus, cramping, and tenesmus. Invasive bacteria usually cause fever and many fecal leukocytes. Two useful exceptions are EHEC, which is not invasive and usually causes little or no fever, and Entamoeba histolytica, whose trophozoites lyse neutrophils so fecal leukocytes may be scant."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of bloody enterocolitis","cols":["Organism","Biology and source","How it injures the mucosa","Classic complication"],"rows":[["Shigella","Gram-negative, nonmotile, non-lactose fermenting rod; person to person; infectious dose of a few hundred organisms or fewer","Enters M cells, escapes into cytoplasm, spreads cell to cell by actin polymerization; ulcers of the distal colon","Reactive arthritis; HUS with Shiga toxin of S. dysenteriae type 1"],["Campylobacter jejuni","Curved or S-shaped gram-negative rod, oxidase positive, grows best at 42 C (107.6 F); undercooked poultry, raw milk","Invades jejunal, ileal, and colonic mucosa","Guillain-Barre syndrome; reactive arthritis"],["Nontyphoidal Salmonella","Motile, flagellated, H2S-producing gram-negative rod; poultry, eggs, reptiles; acid-sensitive, so large inoculum needed","Invades through M cells with a type III secretion system, survives in macrophages; ileum and colon","Bacteremia; osteomyelitis in sickle cell disease; reactive arthritis"],["EHEC O157:H7","Does not ferment sorbitol; undercooked ground beef, unpasteurized juice, produce","Attaches to colonic cells and releases Shiga toxin; not invasive","Hemolytic uremic syndrome"],["Entamoeba histolytica","Protozoan; cysts in fecally contaminated water or food","Trophozoites lyse cells and form flask-shaped ulcers, often in the cecum and ascending colon; trophozoites contain ingested red cells","Amebic liver abscess through the portal vein"]],"caption":"Invasion or cytotoxic injury puts blood and inflammatory cells in the stool; each organism has a complication it is known for."},{"type":"h","text":"Hemolytic uremic syndrome after EHEC"},{"type":"p","text":"Shiga toxin has one A subunit and five B subunits. The B subunits bind the glycolipid Gb3, which is abundant on glomerular endothelium. The A subunit removes an adenine from the 28S ribosomal RNA of the 60S subunit, halting protein synthesis and injuring the endothelial cell. Hemolytic uremic syndrome follows in roughly 5 to 15% of infected children, about 5 to 10 days after diarrhea begins."},{"type":"flow","title":"Shiga toxin to hemolytic uremic syndrome","steps":["EHEC in colon releases Shiga toxin","Toxin absorbed and binds Gb3 on renal endothelium","A subunit inactivates 60S ribosome; endothelium injured","Platelet microthrombi form in glomerular capillaries","Red cells sheared into schistocytes; platelets consumed","Anemia, thrombocytopenia, acute kidney injury"],"caption":"Endothelial injury, not bacterial invasion, explains each part of the HUS triad."},{"type":"h","text":"Postinfectious immune complications"},{"type":"p","text":"Guillain-Barre syndrome follows Campylobacter infection in a small fraction of cases, usually 1 to 3 weeks later, and Campylobacter is the most commonly identified antecedent infection. The lipooligosaccharide of some strains mimics peripheral nerve gangliosides such as GM1, so antibodies raised against the bacterium bind the axolemma or myelin and cause an ascending, areflexic, flaccid weakness."},{"type":"p","text":"Reactive arthritis is a sterile, asymmetric oligoarthritis of the lower limbs that appears 1 to 4 weeks after infection with Shigella, Salmonella, Campylobacter, or Yersinia (or after Chlamydia urethritis). It may come with conjunctivitis and urethritis and is strongly associated with HLA-B27, which is thought to shape how bacterial antigens are presented and how long they persist in the joint."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A child has bloody diarrhea without fever after a barbecue. A week later the urine output falls and the platelet count is low. What links the two events?","explain":"EHEC releases Shiga toxin that binds Gb3 on glomerular endothelium; endothelial injury causes microthrombi, red cell fragmentation, platelet consumption, and renal failure.","check":"Compare with the Shiga toxin flow figure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-12-C03","obj":"GI-13-L.3","title":"Infectious esophagitis in the immunocompromised patient","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The esophagus is lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium over a lamina propria that holds capillaries and fibroblasts. Intact epithelium, saliva, and peristalsis normally keep organisms from establishing infection. When T cell immunity fails, as in HIV infection with low CD4 counts, transplantation, chemotherapy, or high-dose corticosteroids, three agents dominate: Candida, herpes simplex virus (mostly HSV-1), and cytomegalovirus. All three cause odynophagia, which signals mucosal ulceration, and dysphagia."},{"type":"p","text":"Each organism produces a different lesion because it infects a different cell. Candida grows on and into the squamous surface. HSV infects squamous epithelial cells, so its damage is in the epithelium and its inclusions are found in the squamous cells at the edge of an ulcer. CMV does not infect squamous cells well; it infects endothelial cells, fibroblasts, and macrophages in the lamina propria, so its ulcers form by injury to the tissue beneath the epithelium and its inclusions lie in the ulcer base. This is why a biopsy for HSV is taken from the ulcer edge and a biopsy for CMV from the ulcer base."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing infectious esophagitis","cols":["Feature","Candida","HSV-1","CMV"],"rows":[["Endoscopic appearance","White or yellow adherent plaques (pseudomembranes)","Small, shallow, punched-out ulcers, sometimes with vesicles","Large, shallow, linear or longitudinal ulcers"],["Cell infected","Squamous surface epithelium","Squamous epithelial cells","Endothelial cells, fibroblasts, macrophages in the lamina propria"],["Microscopy","Budding yeast and pseudohyphae invading epithelium (PAS or silver stain)","Multinucleated cells with molded nuclei, ground-glass chromatin, Cowdry A intranuclear inclusions","Enlarged cells with an owl's eye intranuclear inclusion and granular cytoplasmic inclusions"],["Where to biopsy","Plaque","Ulcer edge","Ulcer base"],["Usual immune setting","Most common type; in HIV usually CD4 below about 100 per microliter","Can occur with milder immunosuppression, rarely in healthy hosts","Advanced HIV, usually CD4 below about 50 per microliter, and transplant recipients"]],"caption":"The cell each organism infects predicts the lesion, the inclusion, and where the diagnostic tissue lies."},{"type":"p","text":"Candida is the most common cause and often coexists with oral thrush, but thrush can be absent. Esophageal candidiasis, and CMV disease outside the liver, spleen, and nodes, are AIDS-defining conditions. HSV and CMV ulcers may coexist with Candida, so finding plaques does not exclude a viral cause in a patient with severe odynophagia. Pill injury and reflux esophagitis are the main noninfectious alternatives."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-12-C04","obj":"GI-13-L.4","title":"Acute watery gastroenteritis by setting","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The watery diarrhea of these infections arises in the small intestine, which handles most of the fluid entering the gut each day. It occurs when absorption by the villus enterocytes is lost or when crypt secretion is switched on. Neither process requires killing many cells or invading the mucosa, so the stool is large in volume and free of blood and neutrophils. The main danger is loss of water, sodium, potassium, and bicarbonate, causing dehydration, hypokalemia, and a non-anion gap metabolic acidosis."},{"type":"table","title":"Watery gastroenteritis by setting","cols":["Organism","Typical setting","Mechanism","Clues"],"rows":[["Rotavirus","Unvaccinated infants and young children, mostly 6 months to 2 years; cooler months","Nonenveloped, segmented double-stranded RNA virus (Reoviridae); kills mature villus tip enterocytes; NSP4 enterotoxin drives Ca2+-dependent secretion","Vomiting then watery diarrhea; often severe dehydration"],["Norovirus","Cruise ships, nursing homes, schools, catered food","Nonenveloped positive-sense RNA calicivirus; blunts small intestinal villi","Explosive vomiting; incubation 12 to 48 hours; tiny infectious dose; survives on surfaces"],["ETEC","Traveler's diarrhea; contaminated food and water in low-resource regions","Fimbriae attach to small bowel; heat-labile toxin raises cAMP, heat-stable toxin raises cGMP; no invasion","Watery diarrhea, usually within the first week of travel"],["Vibrio cholerae","Fecally contaminated water, often after disasters; undercooked shellfish","Comma-shaped, oxidase-positive gram-negative rod; cholera toxin locks Gs on","Painless rice-water stool, up to about 1 L per hour; shock"],["Bacillus cereus","Cooked rice held warm and reheated","Spores survive cooking; emetic toxin (cereulide) is preformed and heat stable; diarrheal enterotoxins form in the gut","Vomiting 1 to 6 hours after eating; diarrheal form 8 to 16 hours"]],"caption":"The setting names the organism; the mechanism explains the timing and the stool."},{"type":"p","text":"Cholera is the model of secretory diarrhea. The toxin's B subunits bind GM1 ganglioside on enterocytes, and the A1 fragment ADP-ribosylates the alpha subunit of Gs. This blocks its GTPase activity, so adenylyl cyclase stays on. ETEC heat-labile toxin works the same way; ETEC heat-stable toxin reaches the same result through guanylate cyclase C and cGMP."},{"type":"flow","title":"How cholera toxin produces rice-water stool","steps":["B subunits bind GM1 on enterocytes","A1 fragment ADP-ribosylates Gs alpha","Gs cannot hydrolyze GTP; adenylyl cyclase stays active","cAMP rises; protein kinase A opens CFTR","Crypt cells secrete Cl-; Na+ and water follow","Villus NaCl absorption is also inhibited"],"caption":"Secretion is switched on without cell death, so the stool is watery, clear, and free of blood and pus."},{"type":"p","text":"Sodium-glucose cotransport through SGLT1 remains intact in cholera, which is the physiologic basis for glucose-sodium oral rehydration. Incubation time follows mechanism. A preformed toxin in food, as with emetic B. cereus or Staphylococcus aureus, causes vomiting within hours because nothing needs to grow. Organisms that must colonize and then make toxin, such as ETEC and V. cholerae, take a day or more."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: reading the timing","text":"Twelve people at a buffet develop vomiting about 3 hours after eating fried rice that was cooked the day before and kept warm. Step 1: onset in hours means a preformed toxin; an infection that must multiply could not act this fast. Step 2: vomiting dominates, which fits an emetic toxin acting on the gut and vagal afferents. Step 3: rice that sat warm lets B. cereus spores, which survive boiling, germinate and make cereulide, which reheating does not destroy. Compare a passenger who develops diarrhea and vomiting 36 hours after boarding a ship along with many others: a delay of 1 to 2 days and person-to-person spread fit norovirus."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-12-C05","obj":"GI-13-L.5","title":"Plaque, periodontal disease, caries, and tooth abscess","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The periodontium anchors each tooth. The gingiva seals around the neck of the tooth through junctional epithelium, leaving a shallow sulcus about 1 to 3 mm deep. Below it, collagen fibers of the periodontal ligament run from cementum on the root to the alveolar bone of the jaw. Within minutes of cleaning, salivary glycoproteins coat the enamel as a pellicle, and bacteria attach to it and build dental plaque, a biofilm whose matrix protects them from saliva and host defenses."},{"type":"h","text":"Gingivitis and periodontitis"},{"type":"p","text":"Plaque left at the gum line for days to weeks provokes gingivitis: red, swollen gums that bleed on brushing. The inflammation is confined to the gingiva and the attachment is intact, so removing plaque returns the tissue to normal. As plaque matures below the gum line, the flora shifts toward gram-negative anaerobes, including Porphyromonas gingivalis. P. gingivalis makes proteases called gingipains that degrade host proteins and disrupt complement, and it alters the local immune response so that inflammation increases while the bacteria survive."},{"type":"p","text":"Most of the tissue loss in periodontitis is done by the host. Neutrophils, macrophages, and lymphocytes release IL-1, TNF, prostaglandin E2, and matrix metalloproteinases that digest ligament collagen, and RANKL from activated T cells and stromal cells drives osteoclasts to resorb alveolar bone. The junctional epithelium migrates down the root and the sulcus deepens into a periodontal pocket. Ligament and bone do not regrow on their own, so the attachment loss is permanent and teeth become mobile and are lost. Smoking and poorly controlled diabetes are the main systemic risk factors."},{"type":"h","text":"Dental caries and tooth abscess"},{"type":"p","text":"Streptococcus mutans uses glucosyltransferases to turn dietary sucrose into sticky glucans that anchor it within plaque, and it ferments sugars to lactic acid while tolerating the acid it makes. When plaque pH falls below about 5.5, enamel hydroxyapatite dissolves. Saliva buffers acid with bicarbonate and supplies calcium and phosphate for remineralization, and fluoride is built into enamel as fluorapatite, which resists acid. Caries results when acid attacks outpace repair, so frequent sugar intake and low saliva flow raise risk."},{"type":"flow","title":"From sugar to periapical abscess","steps":["S. mutans ferments sucrose to lactic acid","Plaque pH below about 5.5 dissolves enamel","Lesion advances through dentin toward the pulp","Bacteria reach the pulp; pulpitis develops","Pulp swells in a rigid chamber and necroses","Infection exits the root apex: periapical abscess"],"caption":"Caries becomes an abscess when infection passes through the dead pulp and out of the root tip into bone."},{"type":"p","text":"The pulp is enclosed in a rigid chamber, so inflammatory swelling compresses its vessels and the pulp dies. Infection then leaves through the root apex into the surrounding bone. When no sinus tract drains the pus, pressure builds, and the tooth throbs and is very tender to biting or tapping. A periapical abscess differs from a periodontal abscess, which forms in a periodontal pocket beside a living tooth. Untreated dental infection can spread into the fascial spaces of the face and neck; infection from lower molars can reach the submandibular space; bilateral cellulitis of the submandibular and sublingual spaces is Ludwig angina."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-12-C06","obj":"GI-13-L.6","title":"Oral thrush","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Candida albicans lives harmlessly in the mouth of many healthy people. Several defenses keep it in that state: resident bacteria compete for space and nutrients, saliva flushes the surface and carries antimicrobial proteins, the epithelium sheds and renews, and Th17 cells release IL-17 and IL-22 that recruit neutrophils and stimulate antifungal peptides from epithelial cells. CD4 T cell immunity therefore guards the mucosa, while neutrophils guard against spread into the blood and deep organs."},{"type":"p","text":"Thrush, or pseudomembranous candidiasis, is overgrowth on the oral mucosa when one of these defenses fails. It appears as creamy white, curd-like plaques on the buccal mucosa, tongue, palate, or pharynx. The plaques are made of yeast, pseudohyphae, desquamated epithelium, and debris, so they scrape off and leave a red, sometimes bleeding base. A potassium hydroxide preparation or Gram stain shows budding yeast with pseudohyphae. In tissue, Candida forms pseudohyphae and true hyphae, which help it adhere and penetrate."},{"type":"table","title":"Who gets thrush and why","cols":["Setting","Defense that is lost"],"rows":[["Newborns and young infants","Immature T cell immunity and a resident flora not yet established; Candida may be acquired during vaginal birth"],["Inhaled corticosteroids","Drug deposited in the oropharynx suppresses local immune responses"],["Broad-spectrum antibiotics","Bacterial competitors are removed"],["Diabetes mellitus","Glucose in saliva feeds the yeast; hyperglycemia impairs neutrophil function"],["HIV infection","Loss of CD4 T cells and Th17 responses; thrush is common once CD4 falls below about 200 to 300 per microliter"],["Other","Dentures, dry mouth, chemotherapy, and systemic corticosteroids"]],"caption":"Each risk factor removes competition, mucosal immunity, or neutrophil function."},{"type":"p","text":"Thrush in a healthy adult with none of these risks suggests undiagnosed immunodeficiency, especially HIV. Thrush that extends into the esophagus with pain on swallowing means more severe immunosuppression. Two white lesions must be told apart. Oral hairy leukoplakia, caused by Epstein-Barr virus, forms white corrugated patches on the lateral tongue that do not scrape off. Leukoplakia, a white patch from epithelial thickening that can be premalignant, also does not scrape off."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-12-C07","obj":"GI-13-L.7","title":"Stomatitis: vesicles and ulcers of the mouth","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Stomatitis means inflammation of the oral mucosa. Most painful oral lesions are either vesicles that rupture into ulcers, which suggests a virus, or ulcers that begin as ulcers, which suggests aphthae. The site in the mouth, the age of the patient, fever, and lesions elsewhere on the body sort the causes."},{"type":"p","text":"Primary herpes simplex virus type 1 infection in young children often causes herpetic gingivostomatitis. HSV-1 is an enveloped double-stranded DNA virus that replicates in mucosal epithelial cells, causing them to swell, fuse into multinucleated giant cells, and separate into vesicles. Fever, irritability, and tender cervical nodes accompany swollen, red, bleeding gums and clusters of vesicles on the gingiva, tongue, buccal mucosa, and lips that break into painful ulcers. The virus then travels up sensory axons and becomes latent in the trigeminal ganglion. Reactivation produces herpes labialis at the lip border, not a repeat of the widespread primary illness."},{"type":"p","text":"Coxsackie A viruses are nonenveloped positive-sense RNA enteroviruses that spread by the fecal-oral and respiratory routes, mostly in summer and fall. In herpangina, fever and sore throat come with small vesicles and ulcers on the posterior mouth: the soft palate, uvula, and tonsillar pillars, while the gums are spared. In hand-foot-and-mouth disease, usually from coxsackie A16 (enterovirus 71 and coxsackie A6 also cause it), oral ulcers on the tongue and buccal mucosa come with vesicles on the palms, soles, and often the buttocks."},{"type":"p","text":"Aphthous ulcers (canker sores) are not infections. They are shallow, round, painful ulcers with a gray-yellow fibrin base and a red rim, appear on nonkeratinized mucosa such as the inner lips, cheeks, and floor of the mouth, have no vesicle stage, and recur. They are thought to reflect T cell-mediated injury to the epithelium. Recurrent aphthae can signal systemic disease: Crohn disease, which can involve any part of the gut from mouth to anus; Behcet disease, a vasculitis with recurrent oral and genital ulcers and uveitis that is linked to HLA-B51; celiac disease; and deficiency of iron, folate, or vitamin B12."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing causes of stomatitis","cols":["Condition","Cause","Who and when","Oral lesions","Clues outside the mouth"],"rows":[["Herpetic gingivostomatitis","Primary HSV-1","Young children; any season","Vesicles and ulcers on gingiva, anterior mouth, and lips; swollen bleeding gums","High fever, cervical lymphadenopathy; Tzanck smear shows multinucleated giant cells"],["Herpangina","Coxsackie A","Children; summer and fall","Vesicles and ulcers on soft palate, uvula, tonsillar pillars; gums spared","Fever, sore throat; no rash"],["Hand-foot-and-mouth disease","Coxsackie A16 mainly","Young children; summer and fall","Ulcers on tongue and buccal mucosa","Vesicles on palms, soles, and buttocks"],["Aphthous ulcers","Noninfectious, immune mediated","Any age; recurrent","Round ulcers with fibrin base and red rim on nonkeratinized mucosa; no vesicles","Crohn disease, Behcet disease (genital ulcers, uveitis), celiac disease, nutrient deficiency"]],"caption":"Location within the mouth, a vesicle stage, and lesions on the hands, feet, or genitals separate the four."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 3-year-old has fever and painful ulcers. Where in the mouth would you expect them if the cause is coxsackie A herpangina rather than primary HSV-1?","explain":"Herpangina affects the posterior mouth (soft palate, uvula, tonsillar pillars) and spares the gums; primary HSV-1 involves the gingiva and the front of the mouth and lips.","check":"Compare with the stomatitis table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-12-C08","obj":"GI-13-L.8","title":"Interpreting hepatitis B serology","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hepatitis B virus is an enveloped, partially double-stranded DNA virus (Hepadnaviridae) that copies its genome through an RNA intermediate using its own reverse transcriptase. Each marker in a serology panel comes from a specific part of the virus or of the immune response, so knowing the source of each marker makes the panel readable without memorizing patterns."},{"type":"table","title":"What each hepatitis B marker means","cols":["Marker","Source","Meaning when present"],"rows":[["HBsAg","Surface (envelope) protein, made in great excess","Current infection, acute or chronic"],["Anti-HBs","Neutralizing antibody to surface antigen","Immunity, from recovery or vaccination"],["Anti-HBc, IgM","Early antibody to core protein","Acute or recent infection; the only marker in the window period"],["Anti-HBc, IgG","Long-lived antibody to core protein","Past or ongoing infection; never produced by vaccination"],["HBeAg","Secreted protein from the precore and core gene","Active replication and high infectivity"],["Anti-HBe","Antibody to HBeAg","Lower replication and infectivity"],["HBV DNA","Viral genome in serum","Directly measures replication"]],"caption":"Core antigen itself stays inside the virion and infected cells, so serum tests detect antibody to core, not core antigen."},{"type":"flow","title":"Markers over time in acute hepatitis B that resolves","steps":["Exposure; HBsAg appears in about 1 to 10 weeks","HBeAg and HBV DNA rise; patient highly infectious","Symptoms and ALT rise; IgM anti-HBc appears","HBeAg falls and anti-HBe appears","HBsAg clears, usually by 4 to 6 months","Window: IgM anti-HBc alone until anti-HBs appears","Recovery: anti-HBs plus lifelong IgG anti-HBc"],"caption":"Surface antigen and its antibody rarely coexist, which creates the window period between them."},{"type":"p","text":"Liver injury in hepatitis B is caused mainly by cytotoxic T cells killing infected hepatocytes, which is why ALT and symptoms rise with the immune response rather than with the first appearance of virus. The window period exists because anti-HBs forms immune complexes with the large amount of HBsAg in serum, so free antibody becomes detectable only after the antigen has cleared. A patient tested in that gap is HBsAg negative and anti-HBs negative and could be wrongly called uninfected unless anti-HBc is measured."},{"type":"table","title":"Reading a hepatitis B panel","cols":["Interpretation","HBsAg","Anti-HBs","Anti-HBc"],"rows":[["Acute infection","Positive","Negative","IgM"],["Window period","Negative","Negative","IgM"],["Chronic infection","Positive for more than 6 months","Negative","IgG"],["Resolved infection, immune","Negative","Positive","IgG"],["Vaccinated, immune","Negative","Positive","Negative"],["Never infected, not immune","Negative","Negative","Negative"]],"caption":"Anti-HBc separates infection from vaccination, IgM versus IgG separates recent from old, and HBsAg beyond 6 months defines chronic infection."},{"type":"p","text":"Chronic carriers may be HBeAg positive, with high viral DNA and high infectivity, or HBeAg negative with anti-HBe. HBeAg-negative carriers can still have active disease when precore mutant virus replicates without making HBeAg, so HBV DNA is the better guide to replication. An isolated IgG anti-HBc with no HBsAg or anti-HBs usually means a remote infection in which anti-HBs has waned, but it can also reflect low-level occult infection or a false positive result."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: a hepatitis B panel","text":"A health care worker has HBsAg negative, anti-HBs negative, IgM anti-HBc positive, and ALT about 600 U/L (reference about 7 to 56 U/L). Step 1: anti-HBc is positive, so this person has been infected; vaccination alone never produces anti-HBc. Step 2: the antibody is IgM, so the infection is recent. Step 3: HBsAg has cleared but anti-HBs is not yet detectable. This is the window period of an acute infection that is resolving. If the same person instead had HBsAg positive and IgG anti-HBc a year later, the infection would be chronic. If the only positive marker were anti-HBs, the person would be immune from vaccination."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-12-C09","obj":"GI-13-L.9","title":"Outcomes of viral hepatitis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Whether a hepatitis virus is cleared depends on the vigor of the T cell response and on the virus's ability to evade it. A strong response clears virus but causes acute hepatitis; a weak or tolerant response causes little early injury but lets the infection persist. Persistence matters because years of hepatocyte death and regeneration lead to fibrosis, cirrhosis, and cancer."},{"type":"table","title":"Hepatitis viruses and their outcomes","cols":["Virus","Genome and spread","Chronic infection","Distinctive outcomes"],"rows":[["HAV","Positive-sense RNA picornavirus; fecal-oral","Never","Usually self-limited; fulminant failure rare"],["HBV","Partially double-stranded DNA with reverse transcriptase; blood, sex, birth","About 90% of infected neonates, about 25 to 50% of young children, under 5% of adults","Cirrhosis; hepatocellular carcinoma, sometimes without cirrhosis; polyarteritis nodosa; membranous nephropathy"],["HCV","Positive-sense RNA flavivirus; mainly blood","About 75 to 85% of adults","Cirrhosis in about 10 to 20% over 20 to 30 years; hepatocellular carcinoma on cirrhosis; mixed cryoglobulinemia"],["HDV","Defective RNA virus that needs HBsAg for its envelope; blood","Most superinfections of HBV carriers; few coinfections","Superinfection speeds progression to cirrhosis and raises risk of fulminant failure"],["HEV","Positive-sense RNA hepevirus; fecal-oral, water, undercooked pork","Not in healthy hosts; can persist in transplant recipients","High mortality in pregnancy from fulminant hepatic failure"]],"caption":"Fecal-oral viruses cause acute disease only; the blood-borne viruses cause chronic disease and its consequences."},{"type":"p","text":"Age at HBV infection is the strongest predictor of chronicity. A neonate infected at birth, especially from an HBeAg-positive mother, has an immature T cell response, and maternal HBeAg crossing the placenta is thought to induce tolerance. The virus therefore replicates with little liver injury for years, and about 90% of such infants become chronic carriers. Most adults mount a vigorous cytotoxic T cell response, have a clinically obvious acute hepatitis, and clear the virus."},{"type":"p","text":"HCV takes the opposite course. Acute infection is usually mild or silent, yet most adults never clear it. Its RNA-dependent RNA polymerase lacks proofreading, so the virus exists as a quasispecies, and variants in the envelope protein E2, especially its hypervariable region, keep escaping neutralizing antibody and T cell recognition."},{"type":"p","text":"The two viruses cause cancer by different routes. HBV DNA integrates into the host genome, which can disrupt or activate cellular genes, and its HBx protein interferes with p53 and growth control, so hepatocellular carcinoma can arise in a noncirrhotic liver. HCV is an RNA virus that does not integrate; its cancers arise mainly from chronic inflammation, regeneration, and cirrhosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Chronic HCV stimulates B cells continuously, and clonal B cells make an IgM rheumatoid factor that binds polyclonal IgG and viral antigen. These complexes precipitate in the cold (type II mixed cryoglobulinemia) and deposit in small vessels and glomeruli, where they fix complement and cause a type III hypersensitivity vasculitis. The result is palpable purpura of the legs, arthralgia, weakness, peripheral neuropathy, and membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis, with low C4. HCV is also associated with B cell lymphoma, porphyria cutanea tarda, and lichen planus."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-13-12-C10","obj":"GI-13-L.10","title":"Pyogenic versus amebic liver abscess","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The liver receives blood from the portal vein, which drains the bowel, and from the hepatic artery, and it drains bile through the biliary tree. Each of these channels is also a route for infection. Kupffer cells normally clear the small numbers of gut bacteria that enter portal blood, but a large load, an obstructed bile duct, or impaired host defense allows an abscess to form."},{"type":"p","text":"A pyogenic abscess is a cavity of pus made of neutrophils and necrotic liver. The most common route is biliary: stones, strictures, or cancer obstruct the ducts, bile stagnates, and bacteria ascend (ascending cholangitis), often producing several abscesses. Portal spread comes from intra-abdominal infection such as appendicitis or diverticulitis, sometimes with septic thrombophlebitis of the portal vein (pylephlebitis). Arterial spread occurs during bacteremia, as with Staphylococcus aureus endocarditis. Escherichia coli and Klebsiella pneumoniae are the leading organisms, often with streptococci of the anginosus group and anaerobes. Hypervirulent Klebsiella strains, common in East Asia and in people with diabetes, can cause a single abscess and seed the eye or brain. Patients are usually older adults with fever, right upper quadrant pain, leukocytosis, and a raised alkaline phosphatase."},{"type":"p","text":"An amebic abscess follows intestinal infection with Entamoeba histolytica. Trophozoites that have invaded the colon wall enter portal venules and are carried to the liver, most often the right lobe, which receives most of the blood from the cecum and ascending colon. They kill hepatocytes and neutrophils on contact, so the cavity fills with liquefied liver and blood rather than true pus. The aspirate is thick, odorless, and reddish brown (anchovy paste), and trophozoites are found mainly in the abscess wall rather than in the fluid. Patients are typically young men who have lived in or traveled to an endemic area, often months earlier, and many have no current diarrhea. Serum antibody to E. histolytica is usually positive, while stool microscopy is often negative."},{"type":"table","title":"Pyogenic versus amebic liver abscess","cols":["Feature","Pyogenic abscess","Amebic abscess"],"rows":[["Organisms","E. coli, Klebsiella, anginosus streptococci, anaerobes; often polymicrobial","Entamoeba histolytica"],["Route","Biliary (most common), portal, arterial, direct spread, cryptogenic","Portal vein from colonic ulcers"],["Patient","Older adult; biliary disease, diabetes, abdominal infection","Young adult man; travel to or residence in an endemic area"],["Number","Single or multiple; multiple when biliary","Usually single, right lobe"],["Aspirate","Purulent and often foul smelling; Gram stain and culture show bacteria","Anchovy paste, odorless, few neutrophils; culture negative"],["Key test","Blood and aspirate cultures","Serum antibody to E. histolytica"],["Complication","Sepsis; rupture","Rupture through the diaphragm into the pleura or lung, or into the pericardium or peritoneum"]],"caption":"Route of spread, patient background, and the character of the aspirate separate the two abscesses."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Microbiology and pathology meet in the mechanism of injury. Organisms that switch on secretion through cAMP or cGMP (V. cholerae, ETEC) cause watery small-bowel diarrhea without inflammation, organisms that invade or kill colonocytes (Shigella, Campylobacter, Salmonella, Entamoeba) cause dysentery, and the host response itself causes much of the damage in periodontitis and hepatitis B. Immunology explains the postinfectious diseases: molecular mimicry in Guillain-Barre syndrome, HLA-B27 in reactive arthritis, and immune complexes in HCV cryoglobulinemia."},{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy explains the spread. Portal venous drainage carries amebae and gut bacteria to the liver, rigid tooth and fascial compartments turn dental infection into abscess and deep neck infection, and Gb3 on glomerular endothelium makes the kidney the target of Shiga toxin. The limits of the patterns are real: stool tests detect carriage as well as disease, a negative HBsAg does not exclude acute hepatitis B, and organisms overlap in their presentations, so the setting, timing, and mechanism must be read together."}],"summary":["Antibiotics remove the colonic flora that blocks C. difficile; toxins A and B inactivate Rho GTPases, producing pseudomembranes, watery diarrhea, and risk of toxic megacolon, diagnosed by stool toxin or toxin gene tests.","Bloody diarrhea means invasion or cytotoxic injury: Shigella, Campylobacter, Salmonella, EHEC, and Entamoeba histolytica.","Shiga toxin binds Gb3 on renal endothelium and stops protein synthesis, causing HUS; Campylobacter mimicry of gangliosides causes Guillain-Barre syndrome; HLA-B27 predisposes to reactive arthritis.","In immunocompromised patients, Candida causes white esophageal plaques, HSV punched-out ulcers with squamous inclusions at the edge, and CMV linear ulcers with endothelial and stromal inclusions in the base.","Watery diarrhea follows lost absorption or toxin-driven secretion: rotavirus in infants, norovirus in outbreaks, ETEC in travelers, cholera from water, and preformed B. cereus toxin from reheated rice.","Plaque causes reversible gingivitis; periodontitis is host-mediated destruction of ligament and bone driven by organisms such as P. gingivalis; S. mutans acid causes caries that can end in periapical abscess.","Thrush is Candida overgrowth when flora, saliva, Th17 or CD4 immunity, or neutrophil function fails; its plaques scrape off, unlike hairy leukoplakia.","Primary HSV-1 affects the gums and front of the mouth with fever, herpangina affects the posterior pharynx, hand-foot-and-mouth adds palm and sole vesicles, and aphthae have no vesicle stage and suggest Crohn or Behcet disease.","In HBV serology, HBsAg means infection, anti-HBs means immunity, IgM anti-HBc means recent infection, IgG anti-HBc means infection at any time, and anti-HBs alone means vaccination.","HBV becomes chronic in most neonates but few adults, HCV in most adults; both cause cirrhosis and cancer, and HCV causes mixed cryoglobulinemia; pyogenic abscesses come from biliary or portal spread, amebic abscesses from colonic amebae."],"sources":[{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Head and Neck, sections on the oral cavity; The Gastrointestinal Tract, sections on infectious esophagitis, infectious enterocolitis, and pseudomembranous colitis; Liver and Gallbladder, sections on viral hepatitis and liver abscess"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick, and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Enteric gram-negative rods, vibrios and campylobacters, spore-forming gram-positive bacilli, herpesviruses, picornaviruses, and hepatitis viruses"},{"book":"Abbas, Cellular and Molecular Immunology","where":"Immunity to microbes and hypersensitivity disorders"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Secretory functions of the alimentary tract and physiology of gastrointestinal disorders, section on diarrhea"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Infectious diseases and liver disease chapters, sections on infectious diarrhea and viral hepatitis"}]},"GI-2":{"unit":"GI-2","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-02","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-02-01","title":"Gastrointestinal Histology","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter teaches the microscopic structure of the digestive tube and its glands: the four-layer wall and its nerve plexuses, the epithelium of each region, the cells of the gastric gland, the features that identify each intestinal segment, the liver lobule and acinus, and the exocrine pancreas and salivary glands. Board questions usually show a section or describe a cell and ask what it makes, where it sits, or what fails when it is lost. Each structure here is explained by its job, so that a finding such as high gastrin with low acid, or necrosis around the central vein, can be reasoned out rather than memorized.","prerequisites":["Basic tissue types: epithelium, connective tissue, smooth and skeletal muscle","Cell organelles and the secretory pathway (rough endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi, secretory granules)","Gross anatomy of the digestive tract, peritoneum, and retroperitoneum (GI-1)","Germ layers and derivatives of the foregut, midgut, and hindgut","Hematoxylin and eosin staining: what basophilia and eosinophilia indicate"],"connections":[{"to":"Gastrointestinal physiology (secretion and motility)","how":"The cells and plexuses identified here are the effectors of gastric acid secretion, enzyme release, and peristalsis."},{"to":"Biochemistry (vitamin B12 and drug metabolism)","how":"Intrinsic factor from parietal cells and cytochrome P450 in zone 3 hepatocytes link gastric and liver histology to B12 deficiency and toxic metabolites."},{"to":"Immunology (mucosal immunity)","how":"Lamina propria lymphocytes, Peyer patches, and M cells form the gut-associated lymphoid tissue that samples antigen and drives IgA responses."},{"to":"Pathology of the esophagus, stomach, and liver","how":"Barrett metaplasia, autoimmune gastritis, and zonal hepatic necrosis are explained directly by the histology of each organ."},{"to":"Embryology (neural crest and the cloaca)","how":"Neural crest migration forms the enteric plexuses, and the endoderm and ectoderm junction at the pectinate line sets the anal canal's epithelium and drainage."}],"terms":[{"term":"Muscularis mucosae","def":"The thin layer of smooth muscle that forms the deepest part of the mucosa and separates it from the submucosa."},{"term":"Enteric nervous system","def":"The intrinsic neurons of the gut wall, organized as the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses, that can run secretion and motility without input from the brain."},{"term":"Interstitial cells of Cajal","def":"Pacemaker cells associated with the myenteric plexus that generate the slow waves of gut smooth muscle."},{"term":"Metaplasia","def":"Reversible replacement of one differentiated cell type by another that better withstands a chronic stress."},{"term":"Pectinate (dentate) line","def":"The line in the anal canal where hindgut endoderm meets the ectoderm of the proctodeum, marking a change in epithelium, blood supply, lymph drainage, and nerves."},{"term":"Oxyntic gland","def":"The acid-secreting gland of the gastric fundus and body, containing parietal, chief, mucous neck, enterochromaffin-like, and D cells."},{"term":"Intrinsic factor","def":"A glycoprotein from parietal cells that binds vitamin B12 and is required for its uptake in the terminal ileum."},{"term":"Brunner glands","def":"Submucosal glands of the duodenum that secrete alkaline mucus to protect the mucosa from gastric acid."},{"term":"M cell","def":"A specialized epithelial cell over Peyer patches that transports luminal antigens and microbes to underlying immune cells."},{"term":"Space of Disse","def":"The narrow space between the sinusoidal endothelium and hepatocytes, into which hepatocyte microvilli project and where stellate cells live."},{"term":"Hepatic acinus","def":"The functional unit of the liver, centered on the terminal branches of a portal triad and divided into zones 1 to 3 by distance from that blood supply."},{"term":"Centroacinar cell","def":"A pale duct cell, unique to the pancreas, that lies inside the acinus where the intercalated duct begins."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-02-01-C01","obj":"GI-2.1","title":"The four layers of the gut wall and the enteric plexuses","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"From the esophagus to the anal canal, the digestive tube is built on one plan of four concentric layers. Learning that plan once lets you read any section: identify the layers first, then look for the local changes in epithelium, glands, folds, and lymphoid tissue that identify the segment (C02 and C04)."},{"type":"p","text":"The mucosa has three parts. The epithelium protects, secretes, and absorbs. The lamina propria beneath it is loose connective tissue with capillaries, lymphatics (the lacteals of the small intestine), and many lymphocytes and plasma cells, which makes the mucosa a major immune organ. The muscularis mucosae, a thin sheet of smooth muscle, moves the mucosa independently of the wall and marks the mucosa's lower border. That border matters in the colon, where the lamina propria has few lymphatics: a carcinoma confined above the muscularis mucosae rarely spreads, while one that crosses it into the submucosa can reach lymphatics and metastasize."},{"type":"p","text":"The submucosa is dense connective tissue carrying larger vessels and lymphatics, glands in the esophagus and duodenum, and the submucosal (Meissner) plexus. The muscularis externa has an inner circular layer that narrows the lumen and an outer longitudinal layer that shortens the segment; the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus lies between them and coordinates the two into peristalsis, with interstitial cells of Cajal setting the slow-wave rhythm. Each plexus sits beside what it controls: the submucosal plexus regulates glandular secretion, mucosal blood flow, and the muscularis mucosae, and the myenteric plexus regulates motility."},{"type":"table","title":"Layers of the gut wall","cols":["Layer","Contents","Regional variations"],"rows":[["Mucosa","Epithelium, lamina propria, muscularis mucosae","Epithelium changes by region; villi only in the small intestine"],["Submucosa","Dense connective tissue, vessels, lymphatics, Meissner plexus","Glands in the esophagus and duodenum (Brunner); Peyer patches extend into it in the ileum"],["Muscularis externa","Inner circular and outer longitudinal smooth muscle, Auerbach plexus between them","Stomach adds an inner oblique layer; upper esophagus is skeletal muscle; colon gathers longitudinal muscle into taeniae coli"],["Serosa or adventitia","Serosa: mesothelium on connective tissue. Adventitia: connective tissue only","Serosa over intraperitoneal organs; adventitia over the thoracic esophagus and the nonperitoneal surfaces of retroperitoneal segments"]],"caption":"The circular layer thickens locally to form sphincters such as the pylorus and the internal anal sphincter."},{"type":"p","text":"The outer coat depends on the peritoneum. Organs suspended in the peritoneal cavity are covered by serosa, a mesothelium that lets them slide. Where peritoneum is absent, as around the thoracic esophagus and behind retroperitoneal segments such as most of the duodenum and the ascending and descending colon, adventitia anchors the organ instead. With no serosal barrier, esophageal cancer spreads readily into the mediastinum."},{"type":"p","text":"Enteric neurons derive from neural crest cells that migrate down the gut from cranial to caudal. Inhibitory motor neurons, which release nitric oxide and vasoactive intestinal peptide, are needed for smooth muscle to relax, so losing them causes failure of relaxation rather than paralysis. In achalasia, loss of inhibitory myenteric neurons keeps the lower esophageal sphincter closed; Trypanosoma cruzi (Chagas disease) destroys the plexus with similar effects."},{"type":"flow","title":"Hirschsprung disease from missing ganglion cells","steps":["Neural crest cells fail to complete migration","Distal colon lacks ganglion cells in both plexuses","No inhibitory neurons, so the segment cannot relax","Tonically contracted segment causes functional obstruction","Bowel above dilates (megacolon); meconium passage is delayed"],"caption":"The aganglionic segment always begins at the rectum and extends proximally for a variable length."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A rectal suction biopsy samples only mucosa and submucosa. How can it diagnose Hirschsprung disease when the myenteric plexus lies deeper?","explain":"The submucosal (Meissner) plexus lies in the submucosa, and in Hirschsprung disease both plexuses lack ganglion cells. Absent submucosal ganglion cells, usually with hypertrophied nerve trunks, are enough.","check":"The location of each plexus in the layer table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-02-01-C02","obj":"GI-2.2","title":"Epithelium along the tube and Barrett metaplasia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The epithelium in each region matches its main stress. Where food or stool rubs the surface, many cell layers protect it and are shed and replaced from below. Where the job is secretion or absorption, a single layer of columnar cells keeps the distance to blood short."},{"type":"table","title":"Epithelium by region","cols":["Region","Epithelium","Functional reason"],"rows":[["Esophagus","Nonkeratinized stratified squamous","Resists abrasion from swallowed food; submucosal glands lubricate"],["Stomach","Simple columnar; every surface cell secretes mucus (no goblet cells)","Mucus and bicarbonate gel protect against acid"],["Small intestine","Simple columnar enterocytes with brush border, plus goblet cells","Absorption across a large surface"],["Colon and rectum","Simple columnar in straight crypts, many goblet cells","Water and salt absorption; mucus lubricates drying stool"],["Anal canal above pectinate line","Simple columnar (colorectal type), then a narrow transitional zone","Continuous with rectal mucosa"],["Anal canal below pectinate line","Nonkeratinized stratified squamous, becoming keratinized skin at the anal verge","Resists friction during defecation"]],"caption":"Abrupt junctions occur at the gastroesophageal junction and the pectinate line."},{"type":"p","text":"At the gastroesophageal junction the pale squamous mucosa meets the red columnar gastric mucosa at an irregular squamocolumnar junction (Z line). Reflux of acid, pepsin, and bile bathes the squamous side, which lacks the surface mucus and bicarbonate layer that protects the stomach. With chronic injury the distal esophagus can undergo metaplasia: the squamous lining is replaced by columnar epithelium of intestinal type, which resists acid better. Endoscopically, salmon-colored tongues of mucosa extend upward from the junction."},{"type":"p","text":"Barrett esophagus is diagnosed on biopsy by intestinal metaplasia: columnar epithelium containing goblet cells. Goblet cells are the key, because normal esophageal and gastric surface epithelium contains none. Metaplasia protects in the short term, but the cells continue to divide under inflammatory stress and accumulate mutations (TP53 loss is common), so the lining can progress through dysplasia to adenocarcinoma of the distal esophagus. Squamous cell carcinoma, by contrast, arises from squamous epithelium, usually in the middle third, and is linked to tobacco and alcohol."},{"type":"flow","title":"From reflux to esophageal adenocarcinoma","steps":["Chronic reflux of acid, pepsin, and bile","Repeated injury to distal squamous epithelium","Intestinal metaplasia: columnar cells with goblet cells","Accumulating mutations produce dysplasia","Invasive adenocarcinoma of the distal esophagus"],"caption":"Each step is a histologic stage that a biopsy can identify."},{"type":"p","text":"The pectinate line marks where the endoderm of the hindgut meets the ectoderm of the proctodeum. Because two embryologic territories meet there, the epithelium, vessels, lymphatics, and nerves all change at the same line, and the table below lets you predict the behavior of hemorrhoids and cancers on either side."},{"type":"table","title":"Above versus below the pectinate line","cols":["Feature","Above","Below"],"rows":[["Origin","Hindgut endoderm","Ectoderm of the proctodeum"],["Epithelium","Simple columnar","Stratified squamous"],["Artery","Superior rectal (inferior mesenteric)","Inferior rectal (internal pudendal)"],["Vein","Superior rectal to the portal system","Inferior rectal to the systemic (caval) system"],["Lymph","Internal iliac and inferior mesenteric nodes","Superficial inguinal nodes"],["Nerves and pain","Visceral; insensitive to cutting","Somatic (inferior rectal branch of pudendal); painful"],["Hemorrhoids","Internal: painless bleeding","External: painful, especially if thrombosed"],["Typical carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma, often HPV related"]],"caption":"The portal and systemic venous connection here is a site of portosystemic anastomosis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-02-01-C03","obj":"GI-2.3","title":"Gastric gland cells and autoimmune gastritis","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The stomach has three glandular regions. The cardia and the antrum (pyloric region) have mainly mucous glands, and the antral glands also contain G cells. The fundus and body have oxyntic glands, which make acid and enzymes. An oxyntic gland opens into a gastric pit lined by surface mucous cells; below it lie the isthmus, which holds the stem cells, then the neck with mucous neck cells and many parietal cells, then the base with chief cells."},{"type":"table","title":"Cells of the gastric glands","cols":["Cell","Location","Appearance on H&E","Product"],"rows":[["Surface mucous","Surface and pits, whole stomach","Columnar, pale apical mucin","Mucus and bicarbonate"],["Mucous neck","Neck of oxyntic glands","Small, pale mucin granules","Mucus"],["Parietal","Fundus and body, mostly upper half of gland","Large, round, central nucleus, pink cytoplasm (fried egg)","HCl and intrinsic factor"],["Chief","Fundus and body, gland base","Basophilic base, apical granules","Pepsinogen and gastric lipase"],["ECL","Fundus and body","Not distinct on H&E","Histamine"],["D","Body and antrum","Not distinct on H&E","Somatostatin"],["G","Antrum (also duodenum)","Not distinct on H&E","Gastrin into blood"]],"caption":"Parietal and chief cells are confined to the fundus and body; G cells are in the antrum."},{"type":"p","text":"Appearance follows function. Parietal cells are pink because they are packed with mitochondria that supply ATP to the H+/K+-ATPase. At rest the pump is stored in tubulovesicles; on stimulation by acetylcholine, gastrin, and histamine these fuse with deep intracellular canaliculi, multiplying the secretory surface. Carbonic anhydrase supplies H+, and the matching bicarbonate exits into blood in exchange for Cl-, producing the postprandial alkaline tide. Chief cells are basophilic because of rough endoplasmic reticulum making pepsinogen, which acid converts to pepsin."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Acid feedback on gastrin","steps":["Meal buffers acid; antral pH rises","G cells release gastrin into blood","Gastrin stimulates ECL histamine and parietal acid","Antral pH falls","D cells release somatostatin","Somatostatin suppresses G cells; gastrin falls"],"caption":"Acid in the antrum is the main brake on gastrin. Removing acid removes the brake."},{"type":"p","text":"Autoimmune gastritis is directed at the oxyntic mucosa. CD4 T cells attack the parietal cell H+/K+-ATPase, and antibodies to parietal cells and intrinsic factor appear. Fundic and body glands atrophy and are partly replaced by intestinal metaplasia, while the antrum, which has no parietal cells, is spared. This distribution distinguishes it from Helicobacter pylori gastritis, which usually begins in the antrum."},{"type":"table","title":"Predicted secretions in autoimmune gastritis","cols":["Secretion","Change","Reason"],"rows":[["HCl","Falls to achlorhydria","Parietal cells destroyed"],["Intrinsic factor","Falls","Parietal cells destroyed; blocking antibodies"],["Pepsinogen I","Falls","Made by chief and mucous neck cells of oxyntic glands"],["Gastrin","Rises","Antral G cells intact; no acid to trigger somatostatin"],["ECL cells (histamine source)","Rises","Gastrin is trophic to ECL cells; hyperplasia can form neuroendocrine tumors"]],"caption":"Every loss is a body and fundus product; every rise comes from the spared antrum or from gastrin acting downstream."},{"type":"p","text":"Loss of intrinsic factor prevents B12 uptake in the terminal ileum. Because the liver stores several years of B12, pernicious anemia appears late, with megaloblastic anemia and subacute combined degeneration of the dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts. Achlorhydria also reduces nonheme iron absorption and raises the risk of gastric adenocarcinoma."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading the laboratory pattern","text":"A woman with hypothyroidism has fatigue, macrocytic anemia, and reduced vibration sense. Serum gastrin is very high and pepsinogen I is low. First, high gastrin has two possible sources: a gastrinoma, or loss of acid feedback. Low pepsinogen I says chief cells of the oxyntic mucosa are gone, which favors atrophy rather than a tumor; a gastrinoma would bring high acid and ulcers, not atrophy. Atrophy of the body and fundus also removes parietal cells, explaining achlorhydria and lost intrinsic factor, which explains B12 deficiency, macrocytosis, and dorsal column signs. Coexisting autoimmune thyroid disease fits autoimmune gastritis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-02-01-C04","obj":"GI-2.4","title":"Identifying the duodenum, jejunum, ileum, and colon","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The small intestine enlarges its absorptive surface at three scales. Plicae circulares are permanent folds of mucosa with a submucosal core; villi are finger-like projections of mucosa, each with a capillary network and a central lacteal; and microvilli form the brush border of each enterocyte. Together they expand the surface several hundredfold compared with a flat tube. Between the villi, tubular crypts (of Lieberkuhn) extend down to the muscularis mucosae. Stem cells near the crypt base renew the epithelium every few days, and Paneth cells at the very base, with bright eosinophilic granules, release lysozyme and defensins that shape the crypt's microbial environment."},{"type":"p","text":"The segments share this plan, so they are told apart by a few added features, each explained by the segment's job. The duodenum receives acidic chyme from the stomach, so Brunner glands in its submucosa secrete alkaline mucus to protect the mucosa until pancreatic bicarbonate arrives. The jejunum does most nutrient absorption, so it has the tallest and most closely spaced plicae and long villi. The ileum faces a larger bacterial load and carries Peyer patches: aggregated lymphoid follicles in the lamina propria that bulge into the submucosa, on the side opposite the mesentery. The epithelium over them contains M cells, which pass antigens to dendritic cells and lymphocytes and start IgA responses. The terminal ileum also absorbs the B12 and intrinsic factor complex and reabsorbs bile acids."},{"type":"p","text":"The colon absorbs water and salt and lubricates stool, so it has no villi and no plicae circulares, only straight crypts crowded with goblet cells. Its outer longitudinal muscle is gathered into three taeniae coli, which pucker the wall into haustra."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing features of each segment","cols":["Feature","Duodenum","Jejunum","Ileum","Colon"],"rows":[["Villi","Broad, leaf-like","Long, finger-like","Shorter","None"],["Plicae circulares","Begin beyond the first part","Tallest, most numerous","Fewer, absent distally","None"],["Submucosal glands","Brunner glands","None","None","None"],["Lymphoid tissue","Scattered","Scattered","Peyer patches","Solitary follicles"],["Goblet cells","Fewer","More","More still","Most abundant"],["Paneth cells","Present","Present","Most numerous","Normally absent beyond the proximal colon"]],"caption":"Villi separate small intestine from colon; Brunner glands mark the duodenum; Peyer patches mark the ileum."},{"type":"example","title":"Naming a segment from a section","text":"A slide shows simple columnar epithelium with tall villi and crypts. Large lymphoid follicles with germinal centers fill the lamina propria and push into the submucosa, and the submucosa contains no glands. Work in order. Villi are present, so this is small intestine, not colon or appendix. No submucosal glands rules out the duodenum. Organized lymphoid follicles extending into the submucosa are Peyer patches, so the segment is ileum. If the same section had tall closely packed plicae without large follicles, it would be jejunum. A small lymphoid aggregate alone does not prove ileum, because solitary follicles occur throughout the gut."},{"type":"p","text":"These features predict where disease appears. Celiac disease injures the proximal small bowel, where gluten arrives first, flattening villi and lengthening crypts; iron and folate, absorbed proximally, become deficient. Salmonella Typhi and Yersinia enter through M cells, so typhoid ulcers form over Peyer patches and can perforate. Crohn disease favors the terminal ileum, causing B12 deficiency and bile acid loss. Paneth cells appearing in the distal colon signal chronic mucosal injury, as in ulcerative colitis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-02-01-C05","obj":"GI-2.5","title":"The hepatic lobule, acinus, and zonal injury","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hepatocytes form plates one to two cells thick separated by sinusoids. The liver receives about 75% of its blood from the portal vein, carrying absorbed nutrients and toxins, and about 25% from the hepatic artery, which is richer in oxygen. Branches of both, with a bile duct and lymphatics, run together in each portal triad (portal tract). Blood from both mixes in the sinusoids, flows toward the central vein, and leaves through the hepatic veins. Bile moves in the opposite direction, from canaliculi between hepatocytes toward the bile duct in the triad."},{"type":"p","text":"The same tissue can be divided in two useful ways. The classic lobule is a hexagon centered on a central vein with portal triads at its corners; it is easy to see on a slide. The acinus is centered instead on the terminal vessels running between two triads and extends to the two nearest central veins. Because it follows the direction of blood flow, the acinus explains injury patterns."},{"type":"p","text":"The sinusoidal endothelium has open fenestrations and almost no basement membrane, so plasma, including large proteins and lipoproteins, passes freely into the space of Disse, where hepatocyte microvilli exchange material with it. Two other cell types live at this interface."},{"type":"table","title":"Cells and spaces of the sinusoid","cols":["Structure","Location","Normal role","Role in disease"],"rows":[["Sinusoidal endothelium","Lines sinusoids","Fenestrated, open filter","Loses fenestrations and gains a basement membrane in cirrhosis"],["Space of Disse","Between endothelium and hepatocytes","Plasma contacts hepatocyte microvilli","Filled with collagen in fibrosis, impairing exchange"],["Kupffer cells","Within the sinusoid lumen","Macrophages clearing gut bacteria, debris, old red cells","Release cytokines that activate stellate cells"],["Stellate (Ito) cells","In the space of Disse","Store vitamin A in lipid droplets","Become myofibroblasts that lay down collagen"]],"caption":"In chronic injury, TGF-beta and other signals turn quiescent stellate cells into collagen-producing myofibroblasts, the central event in fibrosis and cirrhosis."},{"type":"p","text":"Zone 1 hepatocytes lie next to the triad and receive blood first, so they get the most oxygen and nutrients; they are rich in enzymes of gluconeogenesis, fatty acid oxidation, and urea synthesis. Zone 3 hepatocytes surround the central vein and receive blood last, after upstream cells have extracted much of the oxygen, so their oxygen tension is lowest. Zone 3 also contains the most cytochrome P450, including CYP2E1. Both facts make zone 3 the most vulnerable to hypoperfusion and to drugs that become toxic when metabolized. Shock causes centrilobular necrosis, right heart failure causes centrilobular congestion (nutmeg liver), and alcohol injury is also concentrated there. Zone 1 meets portal blood first, and periportal necrosis is typical of eclampsia; zone 2 necrosis is classic for yellow fever."},{"type":"flow","title":"Acetaminophen injury in zone 3","steps":["Overdose saturates glucuronidation and sulfation","CYP2E1 oxidizes excess drug to reactive NAPQI","Zone 3 glutathione is depleted","NAPQI binds proteins, damaging mitochondria","Centrilobular (zone 3) hepatocyte necrosis"],"caption":"Injury is greatest where the oxidizing enzyme is most concentrated and glutathione is first exhausted."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Why does a chronic heavy drinker develop liver injury after a smaller acetaminophen overdose than a nondrinker?","explain":"Chronic alcohol use induces CYP2E1, so more NAPQI forms, and poor nutrition lowers glutathione stores, so less is neutralized. Both changes act in zone 3.","check":"The zone 3 enzyme content and the acetaminophen flow."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-02-01-C06","obj":"GI-2.6","title":"Exocrine pancreas, islets, and salivary glands","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The pancreas and the salivary glands are both branched glands whose secretory acini drain through a tree of ducts. An acinus makes the protein product; the ducts modify the water and electrolytes."},{"type":"p","text":"The pancreatic acinar cell is a classic protein-exporting cell. Its base is deeply basophilic from rough endoplasmic reticulum, and its apex is filled with eosinophilic zymogen granules. These store inactive proenzymes (trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, procarboxypeptidase) with amylase and lipase, plus a trypsin inhibitor that protects the cell. Pale centroacinar cells sit inside the acinus where the intercalated duct begins; with the duct cells they secrete bicarbonate-rich fluid through CFTR in response to secretin, flushing the enzymes to the duodenum. Premature activation of trypsin inside acinar cells causes autodigestion in acute pancreatitis."},{"type":"p","text":"Islets of Langerhans are pale, rounded clusters scattered among the acini, making up only about 1% to 2% of pancreatic mass. They look pale because endocrine cells lack the dense rough endoplasmic reticulum and large zymogen granules of acinar cells. They have no ducts and secrete into a rich network of fenestrated capillaries: beta cells (insulin) are the majority, with alpha (glucagon), delta (somatostatin), and PP cells. Because islets and acini are separate, disease can remove one and spare the other. In cystic fibrosis, defective CFTR leaves secretions thick, ducts plug, and acini atrophy and fibrose, while islets persist until late, when diabetes may develop."},{"type":"p","text":"Salivary acini come in two types. Serous cells have round basal nuclei, basophilic cytoplasm, and granules of watery, protein-rich secretion containing amylase, so serous acini stain dark. Mucous cells look pale and foamy, with nuclei flattened against the base, because the mucin they store is lost during tissue processing. In mixed glands, serous cells can cap mucous tubules as serous demilunes. Myoepithelial cells around the acini contract to expel saliva. Striated ducts, lined by cells with basal infoldings and many mitochondria, reabsorb Na+ and Cl- and secrete K+ and bicarbonate, which makes saliva hypotonic."},{"type":"table","title":"Telling the salivary glands and pancreas apart","cols":["Gland","Acini","Distinguishing features"],"rows":[["Parotid","Almost entirely serous","Striated ducts; adipocytes increase with age; no islets"],["Submandibular","Mixed, mostly serous","Mucous tubules with serous demilunes"],["Sublingual","Mixed, mostly mucous","Pale mucous acini dominate"],["Exocrine pancreas","Serous","Islets and centroacinar cells; no striated ducts"]],"caption":"Parotid and pancreas both look purely serous, so islets and centroacinar cells identify the pancreas."},{"type":"p","text":"Shared histology explains shared disease. Mumps virus targets serous glands, causing parotitis and sometimes pancreatitis, and either can raise serum amylase. In Sjogren syndrome, lymphocytes destroy salivary and lacrimal acini, producing dry mouth and eyes."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology and anatomy set the structure: neural crest migration builds the enteric plexuses, and the meeting of endoderm and ectoderm sets the anal canal's pectinate line. The peritoneal relationship of each segment decides whether it carries serosa or adventitia and therefore how a tumor spreads."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology, biochemistry, immunology, and pathology then follow from the cells. Parietal cells link the stomach to B12 metabolism and autoimmunity; the gastrin and somatostatin loop explains why atrophy raises gastrin; M cells and Peyer patches link the ileum to mucosal immunity and to enteric infection; and the oxygen and enzyme gradient across the acinus explains zonal necrosis. Section features overlap, so a single lymphoid aggregate or a few goblet cells can mislead; identify a section from several features together."}],"summary":["The gut wall has mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, and serosa or adventitia; the Meissner plexus in the submucosa controls secretion and blood flow, and the Auerbach plexus between the muscle layers controls motility.","Hirschsprung disease is absence of ganglion cells in both plexuses from failed neural crest migration, so the distal segment cannot relax.","Stratified squamous epithelium lines the esophagus and the anal canal below the pectinate line; simple columnar epithelium lines the stomach through the upper anal canal.","Barrett esophagus is intestinal metaplasia with goblet cells caused by chronic reflux and is the precursor of distal esophageal adenocarcinoma.","Parietal cells (HCl, intrinsic factor) and chief cells (pepsinogen) are in the fundus and body; G cells (gastrin) are in the antrum.","Autoimmune gastritis destroys body and fundus glands: acid, intrinsic factor, and pepsinogen I fall, gastrin rises from the spared antrum, and B12 deficiency follows.","Villi mean small intestine; Brunner glands mean duodenum, tall plicae mean jejunum, Peyer patches mean ileum, and goblet-rich crypts without villi mean colon.","Blood flows from portal triad to central vein, so zone 3 has the least oxygen and the most CYP2E1 and is injured first by ischemia and acetaminophen.","Kupffer cells are sinusoidal macrophages; stellate cells in the space of Disse store vitamin A and become collagen-producing myofibroblasts in fibrosis.","Pancreatic acini have basophilic bases and zymogen granules; pale islets and centroacinar cells distinguish the pancreas from the purely serous parotid."],"sources":[{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Digestive Tract"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Organs Associated with the Digestive Tract"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Gastrointestinal Tract (esophagus, stomach, and small and large intestine)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Liver and Gallbladder, patterns of hepatic injury"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Secretory Functions of the Alimentary Tract"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Digestive System, hindgut and the anal canal"}]},"GI-3":{"unit":"GI-3","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-03","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-03-01","title":"Gastrointestinal Organs and Glands","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers the gross anatomy of the stomach and duodenum, the peritoneal relationships of the abdominal gut, the liver and biliary tree, the features that set the colon apart from the small intestine, and the three major salivary glands. Board questions rarely ask for a structure in isolation. They give a finding, such as bilious or nonbilious vomiting, jaundice with or without pancreatitis, flank bruising, right lower quadrant pain, or facial weakness with a parotid mass, and expect you to place the lesion. If you know where each organ sits, what lies next to it, and whether peritoneum covers it, you can explain those findings by location.","prerequisites":["Development of the foregut, midgut, and hindgut and gut rotation (GI-1)","Layers of the gut wall: mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, serosa or adventitia (GI-2)","Branches of the celiac trunk and superior mesenteric artery (GI-5)","Visceral versus somatic pain pathways","Cranial nerves VII and IX and the parasympathetic ganglia of the head"],"connections":[{"to":"The gut in the body cavities (GI-4)","how":"The peritoneal sacs, omenta, and ligaments described there are the same folds that make organs in this chapter intraperitoneal or retroperitoneal."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal blood supply (GI-5)","how":"The major duodenal papilla marks the change from celiac to superior mesenteric supply, and the gastroduodenal artery behind the duodenal bulb explains bleeding from posterior ulcers."},{"to":"Gallbladder diseases (GI-13-D)","how":"The level at which a stone lodges in the biliary tree decides whether the patient has colic, cholecystitis, jaundice, cholangitis, or pancreatitis."},{"to":"Salivary gland disease (GI-13-A)","how":"Duct anatomy and the tight parotid capsule explain the pain of parotitis and why stones favor the submandibular gland."},{"to":"Pancreatic disease and neoplasms (GI-13-B, GI-13-K)","how":"The bile duct runs in or behind the pancreatic head, so a mass there causes obstructive jaundice."}],"terms":[{"term":"Intraperitoneal organ","def":"An organ almost completely covered by visceral peritoneum and suspended by a mesentery or ligament, which leaves it mobile."},{"term":"Secondarily retroperitoneal organ","def":"An organ that once had a mesentery but was pressed against the posterior body wall during development, so that its peritoneum fused with the parietal peritoneum and it became fixed."},{"term":"Duodenal bulb","def":"The dilated, mobile first 2 cm or so of the superior part of the duodenum, just beyond the pylorus."},{"term":"Major duodenal papilla","def":"The elevation on the posteromedial wall of the descending duodenum where the hepatopancreatic ampulla opens."},{"term":"Hepatopancreatic ampulla (of Vater)","def":"The short dilated channel formed where the common bile duct and the main pancreatic duct unite before opening into the duodenum."},{"term":"Sphincter of Oddi","def":"The smooth muscle sphincter around the terminal bile duct, the terminal pancreatic duct, and the ampulla that controls flow into the duodenum."},{"term":"Porta hepatis","def":"The transverse fissure on the visceral surface of the liver where the portal vein and hepatic artery enter and the hepatic ducts and lymphatics leave."},{"term":"Bare area of the liver","def":"The part of the diaphragmatic surface of the liver that lies directly against the diaphragm without a peritoneal covering, bounded by the layers of the coronary ligament."},{"term":"Taeniae coli","def":"Three flat bands into which the outer longitudinal muscle of the cecum and colon is gathered."},{"term":"Haustra","def":"The sacculations of the colonic wall produced because the taeniae are shorter than the bowel they run along."},{"term":"McBurney point","def":"The surface point one third of the way along a line from the right anterior superior iliac spine to the umbilicus, marking the usual position of the base of the appendix."},{"term":"Parotid duct","def":"The duct of the parotid gland (Stensen duct), which crosses the masseter, pierces the buccinator, and opens opposite the upper second molar."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-03-01-C01","obj":"GI-3.1","title":"Parts of the stomach and the four parts of the duodenum","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The stomach is the dilated part of the foregut between the esophagus and the duodenum. Its regions are defined by position. The cardia surrounds the cardial orifice where the esophagus enters. The fundus is the dome that rises above the level of that orifice under the left hemidiaphragm, which is why swallowed air collects there in an upright patient. The body is the large central part, and the pyloric part follows it: the wide pyloric antrum, the narrower pyloric canal, and the pylorus, a thickened ring of circular smooth muscle that meters chyme into the duodenum. The short lesser curvature faces right and the long greater curvature faces left; the angular incisure, a notch on the lesser curvature, marks where body becomes antrum."},{"type":"p","text":"The regions differ in function as well as shape. Parietal cells, which secrete acid and intrinsic factor, and chief cells, which secrete pepsinogen, are concentrated in the fundus and body. Gastrin-secreting G cells lie in the antrum. This is why resection or atrophy of the body and fundus impairs acid and intrinsic factor secretion, while antral disease affects gastrin."},{"type":"p","text":"The duodenum is about 25 cm long and curves in a C around the head of the pancreas. Its parts are best learned by what each one touches, because questions turn on those relationships. The major duodenal papilla lies on the posteromedial wall of the descending (second) part. Just distal to it, the duodenum changes from foregut to midgut, and its arterial supply changes from celiac branches (superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries) to superior mesenteric branches (inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries). A minor papilla about 2 cm above the major one drains the accessory pancreatic duct."},{"type":"table","title":"The four parts of the duodenum","cols":["Part","Approximate length and level","Key relations","Clinical relevance"],"rows":[["First (superior)","About 5 cm, at L1","Bulb is mobile; gastroduodenal artery and common bile duct pass behind it","Most duodenal ulcers; posterior ulcer can erode the gastroduodenal artery"],["Second (descending)","About 7 to 10 cm, right of L1 to L3","Major and minor papillae; head of pancreas medially; right kidney behind","Site where bile and pancreatic juice enter; foregut to midgut junction"],["Third (horizontal)","About 6 to 8 cm, crosses L3","Superior mesenteric artery (SMA) and vein in front; aorta and inferior vena cava behind","Compression between SMA and aorta (SMA syndrome)"],["Fourth (ascending)","About 5 cm, rises to L2","Ends at the duodenojejunal flexure, held by the suspensory muscle (ligament of Treitz)","Landmark dividing upper from lower GI bleeding"]],"caption":"Lengths and vertebral levels are approximate and vary with body habitus and posture."},{"type":"p","text":"Location also explains the color of vomitus. Bile enters at the second part, so an obstruction proximal to the papilla, such as infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis, produces nonbilious vomiting, while most duodenal atresias lie distal to the papilla and produce bilious vomiting. Ulcers favor the bulb because it receives acidic chyme from the pylorus before pancreatic bicarbonate arrives at the papilla. An anterior bulb ulcer tends to perforate into the peritoneal cavity, whereas a posterior ulcer tends to bleed from the gastroduodenal artery."},{"type":"stop","predict":"An infant vomits forcefully after every feed, and the vomitus contains no bile. Where along the stomach and duodenum must the obstruction lie?","explain":"Proximal to the major duodenal papilla in the second part of the duodenum, because bile only enters the lumen there; the pylorus is the classic site.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on bilious and nonbilious vomiting and the duodenum table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-03-01-C02","obj":"GI-3.2","title":"Intraperitoneal and retroperitoneal organs","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The peritoneum is a serous membrane: parietal peritoneum lines the abdominal wall, and visceral peritoneum covers organs. An intraperitoneal organ is almost completely wrapped in visceral peritoneum and hangs from a mesentery or peritoneal ligament, so it moves with posture and peristalsis. A retroperitoneal organ lies behind the parietal peritoneum, covered by it only on its anterior surface, and is fixed to the posterior body wall."},{"type":"p","text":"Retroperitoneal organs come in two kinds. Primarily retroperitoneal organs, such as the kidneys, ureters, suprarenal glands, aorta, and inferior vena cava, developed behind the peritoneum and never had a mesentery. Secondarily retroperitoneal organs began on a mesentery and were later pressed against the posterior wall as the gut rotated and grew. Their mesentery fused with the parietal peritoneum to form a fusion fascia."},{"type":"flow","title":"How a gut segment becomes secondarily retroperitoneal","steps":["Gut segment hangs on dorsal mesentery, fully intraperitoneal","Rotation and growth press it against the posterior wall","Posterior visceral peritoneum and mesentery fuse with parietal peritoneum","Fusion fascia forms behind the segment","Segment is fixed, covered by peritoneum only in front"],"caption":"Sequence that fixes duodenum parts 2 to 4, most of the pancreas, and the ascending and descending colon."},{"type":"table","title":"Peritoneal status of the abdominal gut and its glands","cols":["Organ or segment","Peritoneal status","Detail"],"rows":[["Stomach, jejunum, ileum","Intraperitoneal","Suspended by omenta and the mesentery proper"],["Duodenum, part 1","Partly intraperitoneal","Bulb (first 2 cm) is intraperitoneal and mobile; the distal 3 cm is retroperitoneal and fixed"],["Duodenum, parts 2 to 4","Secondarily retroperitoneal","Fixed around the pancreatic head"],["Pancreas","Secondarily retroperitoneal except the tail","Tail runs in the splenorenal ligament to the splenic hilum"],["Cecum and appendix","Intraperitoneal","Cecum often lacks a true mesentery but is largely covered; appendix has a mesoappendix"],["Ascending and descending colon","Secondarily retroperitoneal","Mesocolon fused to the posterior wall"],["Transverse and sigmoid colon","Intraperitoneal","Keep the transverse and sigmoid mesocolons"],["Rectum","Changes along its length","Upper third covered in front and on the sides, middle third in front only, lower third below the peritoneum"],["Liver and gallbladder","Intraperitoneal","Except the bare area of the liver and the gallbladder surface attached to the liver"],["Spleen","Intraperitoneal","Held by the gastrosplenic and splenorenal ligaments"]],"caption":"Duodenum, pancreas, and rectum change status along their length."},{"type":"p","text":"The distinction has clinical consequences because the retroperitoneum is outside the peritoneal cavity. Blood or enzyme-rich fluid from a retroperitoneal organ tracks through retroperitoneal fat instead of spreading over the peritoneal surfaces, so peritoneal signs may be slight or delayed. Hemorrhagic pancreatitis can track to the flanks and produce flank bruising (Grey Turner sign). A perforation of a retroperitoneal duodenal segment, or a crush injury of the duodenum against the vertebral column in blunt trauma, can leak into the retroperitoneum without free air under the diaphragm, while an anterior perforation of the intraperitoneal bulb releases air into the peritoneal cavity. Mobility follows the same rule: intraperitoneal segments on long mesenteries, such as the sigmoid colon, can twist (volvulus), whereas fixed retroperitoneal segments cannot."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: where did the air go?","text":"Two patients have perforated duodenal ulcers. In the first, an upright chest film shows air under the right hemidiaphragm and the abdomen is rigid. In the second, there is no free air, but CT shows gas around the right kidney. Step 1: free subdiaphragmatic air means the leak entered the peritoneal cavity, so the ulcer was on the anterior wall of the intraperitoneal duodenal bulb. Step 2: gas around the kidney means the leak entered the retroperitoneum, so the perforation was in a retroperitoneal segment, the fixed distal first part or the second part. Step 3: the second patient has fewer peritoneal signs because the retroperitoneum lacks the parietal peritoneal surface that, when irritated, produces guarding and rigidity."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-03-01-C03","obj":"GI-3.3","title":"The liver and the path of bile to the duodenum","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The liver, the largest gland in the body (about 1.5 kg), has four anatomic lobes. The falciform ligament divides the right lobe from the left lobe on the diaphragmatic surface. On the visceral surface, the caudate lobe lies above the porta hepatis between the inferior vena cava and the fissure for the ligamentum venosum, and the quadrate lobe lies below the porta between the gallbladder fossa and the fissure for the ligamentum teres. Surgeons use a functional division instead: a plane from the gallbladder fossa to the inferior vena cava (Cantlie line) separates functional right and left livers, each with its own portal vein, hepatic artery, and bile duct branches. The quadrate lobe then belongs to the left liver, and the caudate lobe drains directly into the inferior vena cava."},{"type":"p","text":"The liver develops within the ventral mesentery, so its peritoneal folds (falciform, coronary, and triangular ligaments and the lesser omentum) are remnants of that mesentery. The two layers of the coronary ligament separate widely on the posterosuperior surface and leave the bare area, where liver tissue touches the diaphragm directly. The porta hepatis is the hilum of the liver. The portal vein and proper hepatic artery enter there and the right and left hepatic ducts leave, all carried in the free edge of the lesser omentum, the hepatoduodenal ligament. In that ligament the bile duct lies anterior and to the right, the hepatic artery anterior and to the left, and the portal vein posterior. The hepatic veins do not use the porta; they leave the posterior surface directly into the inferior vena cava."},{"type":"flow","title":"Path of bile from hepatocyte to duodenum","steps":["Hepatocytes secrete bile into bile canaliculi","Canals of Hering drain into interlobular bile ducts in portal tracts","Right and left hepatic ducts leave at the porta hepatis","They unite as the common hepatic duct","Cystic duct joins to form the common bile duct","Bile duct passes behind duodenal part 1 and the pancreatic head","Joins the main pancreatic duct at the hepatopancreatic ampulla","Enters duodenal part 2 at the major papilla through the sphincter of Oddi"],"caption":"Between meals the sphincter is closed, so bile backs up the cystic duct into the gallbladder for storage and concentration."},{"type":"p","text":"The sphincter of Oddi includes separate muscle around the end of the bile duct, the end of the pancreatic duct, and the ampulla. Cholecystokinin (CCK), released by the duodenum when fat and protein arrive, contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter, so bile reaches the lumen when it is needed for fat digestion."},{"type":"p","text":"Because all bile funnels into one duct that shares an outlet with the pancreas, the level of an obstruction predicts its effects (table). A common bile duct block raises conjugated bilirubin and alkaline phosphatase (ALP), darkens the urine (conjugated bilirubin is water soluble and filtered), and makes stools pale because no bilirubin reaches the gut to form stercobilin. A tumor of the pancreatic head compresses the bile duct slowly, so the gallbladder distends without inflammation and may be palpable and painless (Courvoisier sign)."},{"type":"table","title":"Level of biliary obstruction and its consequences","cols":["Site of block","Bilirubin and ALP","Gallbladder","Pancreas"],"rows":[["Cystic duct","Normal","Distends; colic or cholecystitis","Unaffected"],["Common hepatic duct","Conjugated bilirubin and ALP rise","Not filled with new bile","Unaffected"],["Common bile duct above the ampulla","Conjugated bilirubin and ALP rise","May distend","Unaffected"],["Ampulla of Vater","Conjugated bilirubin and ALP rise","May distend","Pancreatic duct can be blocked: pancreatitis"]],"caption":"Each step downstream adds territory: gallbladder, liver, then pancreas."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A stone lodges in the cystic duct. Why does the patient have pain but no jaundice?","explain":"Bile from the liver still flows down the common hepatic and common bile ducts to the duodenum; only the gallbladder is cut off, and it contracts painfully against the stone.","check":"Compare with the obstruction table and the bile flow figure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-03-01-C04","obj":"GI-3.4","title":"Features of the colon and the position of the appendix","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Both the small and large intestine have an inner circular and an outer longitudinal layer of smooth muscle, but in the cecum and colon the longitudinal layer is gathered into three flat bands about 1 cm wide, the taeniae coli (named taenia mesocolica, taenia omentalis, and taenia libera). Because the taeniae are shorter than the wall they run along, they pull it into a series of pouches, the haustra. Small fat-filled tags of peritoneum, the omental appendices (appendices epiploicae), hang from the colonic surface. None of these three features is found on the jejunum or ileum, so any one of them identifies colon at surgery or in a specimen."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing the colon from the small intestine","cols":["Feature","Small intestine (jejunum and ileum)","Colon"],"rows":[["Outer longitudinal muscle","Continuous layer","Three taeniae coli"],["Wall contour","Smooth tube","Haustra (sacculations)"],["Serosal fat tags","Absent","Omental appendices"],["Mucosal folds","Plicae circulares with villi","No plicae circulares or villi; straight crypts rich in goblet cells"],["Folds on contrast imaging","Plicae cross the full width of the lumen","Haustral folds cross only part of the lumen"],["Position and caliber","Central, mobile, narrower","Frames the abdomen, wider, partly fixed"]],"caption":"Taeniae, haustra, and omental appendices are the gross features; the rest support the distinction."},{"type":"p","text":"The taeniae begin at the base of the appendix and run along the colon to the rectosigmoid junction, where they spread out into a continuous longitudinal layer again. This is why the rectum and the appendix lack taeniae and haustra. The wall between the taeniae has a thinner muscle coat, and where the vasa recta penetrate it the wall is weakest, so colonic diverticula (herniations of mucosa and submucosa without muscle) form at these points, most often in the sigmoid colon."},{"type":"p","text":"The vermiform appendix is a narrow blind tube, about 6 to 10 cm long, arising from the posteromedial wall of the cecum below the ileocecal junction. Its tip is variable: most often retrocecal, but it may lie in the pelvis, below the cecum, or beside the terminal ileum. Its base, however, is constant, because all three taeniae converge on it, and a surgeon who follows the taeniae down the cecum will reach it. The surface marking of the base is McBurney point, one third of the way along a line from the right anterior superior iliac spine to the umbilicus. The appendix is supplied by the appendicular artery, a branch of the ileocolic artery running in the mesoappendix. It is an end artery, so when obstruction and swelling compress it the wall becomes ischemic and can necrose and perforate."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why appendicitis pain moves","steps":["Lumen obstructed by fecalith or lymphoid hyperplasia","Distension stimulates visceral afferents entering the cord at about T10","Poorly localized periumbilical pain","Inflammation spreads through the wall to the serosa","Parietal peritoneum, with somatic innervation, is irritated","Sharp pain localizes to the right lower quadrant near McBurney point"],"caption":"Visceral pain is referred to the dermatome of the gut segment; somatic pain is felt at the site."},{"type":"p","text":"The variable tip changes the findings. A retrocecal appendix lies on the iliopsoas, so extending the right hip stretches the inflamed tissue and causes pain (psoas sign), while anterior tenderness may be mild. A pelvic appendix can lie on the obturator internus, so internal rotation of the flexed right hip causes pain (obturator sign), and tenderness may be felt mainly on rectal examination."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-03-01-C05","obj":"GI-3.5","title":"The major salivary glands, their ducts, and the facial nerve in the parotid","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Three paired major glands produce most of the roughly 1 to 1.5 L of saliva secreted daily; minor glands in the lips, cheeks, and palate add the rest. The glands differ in cell type: serous acini secrete a watery fluid rich in salivary amylase, and mucous acini secrete viscous mucins. The parotid is purely serous, the submandibular gland is mixed but mostly serous, and the sublingual gland is mixed but mostly mucous."},{"type":"table","title":"The major salivary glands","cols":["Gland","Location","Duct opening","Secretion","Secretomotor nerve"],"rows":[["Parotid","Over the masseter, in front of and below the ear","Parotid (Stensen) duct, opposite the upper second molar","Serous","CN IX via the otic ganglion"],["Submandibular","Submandibular triangle, around the posterior border of the mylohyoid","Submandibular (Wharton) duct, at the sublingual caruncle beside the lingual frenulum","Mixed, mostly serous","CN VII via chorda tympani and the submandibular ganglion"],["Sublingual","Floor of the mouth under the sublingual fold","Many small ducts along the sublingual fold","Mixed, mostly mucous","CN VII via chorda tympani and the submandibular ganglion"]],"caption":"The parotid is the largest gland and the sublingual the smallest."},{"type":"p","text":"The parotid duct crosses the masseter about a fingerbreadth below the zygomatic arch, then pierces the buccinator; pus appears at its opening in bacterial parotitis. The submandibular duct is long and runs upward from the gland to the floor of the mouth, and its saliva is more viscous and more alkaline, with more calcium; these features slow flow and favor precipitation, which is why about 80 to 90% of salivary stones form in this gland. The lingual nerve loops under the submandibular duct, so it is at risk when the duct is opened."},{"type":"p","text":"The facial nerve (CN VII) leaves the skull through the stylomastoid foramen, enters the posterior surface of the parotid, and divides inside the gland into a plexus that gives five terminal branches: temporal, zygomatic, buccal, marginal mandibular, and cervical. These branches emerge from the anterior border of the gland and fan out to the muscles of facial expression. The nerve only passes through; it does not supply the parotid, whose secretomotor fibers come from CN IX. From superficial to deep, the facial nerve plexus, the retromandibular vein, and the external carotid artery all lie within the gland."},{"type":"flow","title":"Secretomotor pathway to the parotid gland","steps":["Inferior salivatory nucleus in the medulla","Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) and its tympanic branch","Lesser petrosal nerve","Synapse in the otic ganglion","Postganglionic fibers ride the auriculotemporal nerve (V3)","Parotid acini secrete serous saliva"],"caption":"Submandibular and sublingual glands use CN VII: chorda tympani, lingual nerve, submandibular ganglion."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the nerve runs through the gland, parotid disease can reach it. Most salivary tumors arise in the parotid, and most are benign; the commonest, pleomorphic adenoma, pushes nerve branches aside. A malignant tumor such as mucoepidermoid carcinoma can infiltrate the nerve, so facial weakness with a parotid mass suggests malignancy. Damage within the gland, from tumor or surgery, gives a lower motor neuron palsy that includes the forehead. A late complication of parotidectomy is gustatory sweating (Frey syndrome): cut parasympathetic fibers of the auriculotemporal nerve regrow into the sweat glands of the overlying skin, so eating causes local sweating and flushing. The gland is also enclosed in a tight fascial sheath from the investing layer of deep cervical fascia, so inflammation such as mumps raises pressure and causes pain that worsens with chewing."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with a firm parotid mass cannot wrinkle the forehead, close the eye tightly, or raise the mouth corner on that side. Upper or lower motor neuron pattern, and why is the mass concerning?","explain":"Lower motor neuron: the nerve is damaged in the gland, distal to the brainstem, so every branch including the temporal is affected. Benign tumors usually displace the nerve, so paralysis suggests an invading malignancy.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on parotid tumors."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology explains most of this chapter's anatomy. The stomach and the liver develop in the dorsal and ventral mesenteries, and gut rotation presses the duodenum, pancreas, and ascending and descending colon against the posterior wall, where their mesenteries fuse. That single event decides which organs are mobile, which bleed into the retroperitoneum, and which can twist. The major papilla sits at the foregut to midgut junction, so it also marks the change in arterial supply (celiac to superior mesenteric) and the boundary between nonbilious and bilious vomiting. The taeniae and haustra reflect how the colonic muscle is arranged, and the points where vessels pierce that muscle are where diverticula form."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology and physiology then work through location. A gallstone causes colic, jaundice, or pancreatitis according to the duct it blocks, and the effects follow bile and bilirubin physiology: conjugated bilirubin in the urine, no stercobilin in the stool. Pain from the appendix or gallbladder changes character when inflammation reaches somatically innervated parietal peritoneum. In the head, the facial nerve's course through the parotid and the separate CN IX and CN VII secretomotor pathways explain why a parotid tumor can cause facial palsy even though the facial nerve does not supply the gland, and why mumps causes a painful, tense gland."}],"summary":["The stomach has cardia, fundus, body, and pyloric part (antrum, canal, pylorus); acid and intrinsic factor come from the fundus and body, gastrin from the antrum.","The duodenum has four parts: the ulcer-prone bulb, the descending part with the major papilla, the horizontal part between the superior mesenteric artery and aorta, and the ascending part ending at the suspensory ligament.","Obstruction proximal to the major papilla gives nonbilious vomiting; obstruction distal to it gives bilious vomiting.","Secondarily retroperitoneal organs (duodenum parts 2 to 4, pancreas except the tail, ascending and descending colon) lost their mesenteries by fusion; the rectum is covered progressively less toward its lower third.","Retroperitoneal bleeding or perforation tracks behind the peritoneum, giving flank signs and fewer peritoneal signs, without free air under the diaphragm.","The liver has right, left, caudate, and quadrate anatomic lobes; the porta hepatis transmits the portal vein, hepatic artery, and hepatic ducts, while the hepatic veins drain directly to the inferior vena cava.","Bile flows from the hepatic ducts to the common hepatic duct, joins the cystic duct as the common bile duct, and meets the pancreatic duct at the ampulla of Vater under the sphincter of Oddi.","Taeniae coli, haustra, and omental appendices identify the colon; the taeniae converge at the constant base of the appendix, marked at McBurney point.","The parotid duct opens opposite the upper second molar; the submandibular duct opens beside the lingual frenulum and is the usual site of stones.","The facial nerve divides into its five branches inside the parotid without supplying it, so parotid malignancy or surgery can cause lower motor neuron facial palsy."],"sources":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen: peritoneum and peritoneal cavity, stomach, small intestine, large intestine, liver, biliary ducts and gallbladder"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head: parotid region and oral region (salivary glands)"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Digestive System: mesenteries, duodenum, liver and gallbladder"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Digestive Tract; Organs Associated with the Digestive Tract"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Secretory Functions of the Alimentary Tract (salivary and biliary secretion)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Head and Neck (salivary gland tumors); Liver and Gallbladder"}]},"GI-4":{"unit":"GI-4","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-04","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-04-01","title":"The Gut in the Body Cavities","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter places the gut within the body cavities: the course of the esophagus through the neck and mediastinum, the nerve supply of the tongue, the peritoneal sacs and the ligaments that carry vessels to the upper abdominal organs, the inguinal region where hernias form, and the dependent spaces where fluid collects. Board questions here usually give a finding, such as a lodged pill, a deviated tongue, a groin bulge, or fluid on ultrasound, and ask which structure or space is involved. Most of this anatomy follows from development: rotation of the stomach, the pharyngeal arch origins of the tongue, and descent of the gonads explain the relations you need to remember.","prerequisites":["Foregut, midgut, and hindgut and the rotation of the stomach (GI-1)","Pharyngeal arches and the cranial nerve of each arch","Layers of the anterior abdominal wall, from skin to parietal peritoneum","Gross anatomy of the stomach, duodenum, liver, spleen, and pancreas (GI-3)","Branches of the celiac trunk and the tributaries of the portal vein"],"connections":[{"to":"Gut development (GI-1)","how":"Gastric rotation creates the lesser sac and turns the vagal trunks, and the liver and spleen take their ligaments from the ventral and dorsal mesenteries."},{"to":"Gut blood supply and portal circulation (GI-5)","how":"The lower esophagus, the falciform ligament, and the short gastric veins are sites where portal and systemic veins connect and enlarge in portal hypertension."},{"to":"Motility disorders (GI-13-F)","how":"Zenker diverticulum and dysphagia from extrinsic compression arise at the narrowings and relations described for the esophagus."},{"to":"Peritoneal disease (GI-13-I)","how":"Ascites, peritonitis, and abscesses spread along the gutters and pool in the dependent recesses described here."},{"to":"Neuroanatomy of the cranial nerves","how":"Lesions of CN V3, VII, IX, X, and XII each leave a distinct pattern of tongue sensation, taste, and movement."}],"terms":[{"term":"Posterior mediastinum","def":"The part of the thoracic midline space behind the pericardium, containing the esophagus, descending aorta, azygos veins, and thoracic duct."},{"term":"Upper esophageal sphincter","def":"The cricopharyngeal part of the inferior pharyngeal constrictor, a band of skeletal muscle that keeps the top of the esophagus closed between swallows."},{"term":"Chorda tympani","def":"A branch of the facial nerve that carries taste from the anterior two thirds of the tongue and parasympathetic fibers to the submandibular ganglion."},{"term":"Peritoneal ligament","def":"A double fold of peritoneum connecting an organ to another organ or to the abdominal wall, carrying vessels and nerves between its layers."},{"term":"Lesser sac (omental bursa)","def":"The part of the peritoneal cavity behind the stomach and lesser omentum, opening into the greater sac only through the omental foramen."},{"term":"Omental (epiploic) foramen","def":"The opening between the lesser and greater sacs, bounded in front by the hepatoduodenal ligament and behind by the inferior vena cava."},{"term":"Portal triad","def":"The hepatic portal vein, proper hepatic artery, and common bile duct, carried together in the hepatoduodenal ligament."},{"term":"Processus vaginalis","def":"A fetal pouch of peritoneum that precedes the testis through the abdominal wall and normally closes after descent."},{"term":"Inguinal (Hesselbach) triangle","def":"The area of the lower anterior abdominal wall bounded by the inferior epigastric vessels, the lateral edge of rectus abdominis, and the inguinal ligament."},{"term":"Paracolic gutter","def":"A peritoneal channel lateral to the ascending or descending colon that connects the upper abdomen with the pelvis."},{"term":"Dependent recess","def":"The lowest part of a body cavity for a given posture, where free fluid collects under gravity."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-04-01-C01","obj":"GI-4.1","title":"Course and narrowings of the esophagus","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The esophagus is a muscular tube about 25 cm long that carries a bolus from the pharynx to the stomach. It begins at the lower border of the cricoid cartilage, level with C6, where the cricopharyngeal part of the inferior pharyngeal constrictor forms the upper esophageal sphincter. Its muscle changes from skeletal in roughly the upper third to smooth in the lower third, so diseases of skeletal muscle disturb the start of swallowing while loss of myenteric neurons disturbs the lower esophagus."},{"type":"p","text":"In the neck the esophagus lies behind the trachea and in front of the vertebral column, slightly to the left. It enters the superior mediastinum through the thoracic inlet, still between the trachea and the vertebrae, and then descends in the posterior mediastinum behind the left main bronchus, the pericardium, and the left atrium. The descending aorta lies at first to its left and then behind it, as the esophagus moves forward and to the left to reach the esophageal hiatus, formed by fibers of the right crus of the diaphragm at T10. A short abdominal segment joins the cardia of the stomach at about T11."},{"type":"table","title":"The three esophageal narrowings","cols":["Narrowing","Level","Distance from the incisor teeth","What narrows the lumen","Clinical relevance"],"rows":[["Cervical (pharyngoesophageal junction)","C6","About 15 cm","Tone of the cricopharyngeus","Coins and food lodge here; Zenker diverticulum forms just above"],["Thoracic (bronchoaortic)","About T4 to T5","About 22 to 23 cm (arch) and 27 to 28 cm (bronchus)","Aortic arch, then left main bronchus, pressing from outside","Tablets stall: pill esophagitis"],["Diaphragmatic","T10","About 40 cm","Hiatus in the right crus and lower sphincter tone","Food impaction; hiatal hernia passes here"]],"caption":"Endoscopists report lesion position by distance from the incisors."},{"type":"p","text":"At each narrowing a muscle ring or a firm neighboring structure keeps the lumen from widening, so a slowly moving bolus, coin, or tablet tends to stop there. A tablet held against the mucosa dissolves in place and releases a concentrated irritant solution that ulcerates the wall (pill esophagitis), classically at the aortic arch when a pill is taken with little water at bedtime. The same relations explain extrinsic compression: an enlarged left atrium, as in mitral stenosis, indents the anterior wall of the middle esophagus on a barium swallow and can cause dysphagia, as can an aortic aneurysm or enlarged subcarinal lymph nodes."},{"type":"p","text":"Just above the cricopharyngeus, the posterior pharyngeal wall has a weak area between the thyropharyngeal and cricopharyngeal parts of the inferior constrictor (Killian triangle). When the cricopharyngeus does not relax fully, swallowing pressure pushes mucosa and submucosa out through this gap as a Zenker diverticulum, a false diverticulum that traps food and causes regurgitation and halitosis. At the lower end, a sudden pressure rise against a closed sphincter during forceful vomiting can tear the full wall (Boerhaave syndrome), usually on the left posterolateral side just above the diaphragm."},{"type":"p","text":"The two vagus nerves form the esophageal plexus on the lower esophagus and leave it as two trunks through the hiatus. Because the stomach rotates during development, the left vagus becomes the anterior trunk and the right vagus the posterior trunk. The lower esophagus is also a portosystemic junction: esophageal tributaries of the left gastric vein (portal) connect with veins draining to the azygos system (systemic), so portal hypertension dilates these submucosal veins into varices (GI-5)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A 70-year-old with mitral stenosis has difficulty swallowing solid food. Where would a barium swallow show the esophagus indented, and why there?","explain":"On the anterior wall of the middle thoracic esophagus, below the aortic and bronchial impressions, because the left atrium (in the middle mediastinum) lies directly in front of the esophagus, which descends in the posterior mediastinum.","check":"The course paragraph and the narrowings table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-04-01-C02","obj":"GI-4.2","title":"Innervation of the tongue and lesion patterns","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The nerve supply of the tongue follows its embryology. The mucosa of the anterior two thirds develops from the first pharyngeal arch, so its general sensation (touch, pain, and temperature) is carried by the nerve of that arch, the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (CN V3), through its lingual branch. The posterior third develops mainly from the third arch, so the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) carries both general sensation and taste there, including the vallate papillae just in front of the V-shaped sulcus terminalis. A small area at the root near the epiglottis, from the fourth arch, is supplied by the vagus (CN X) through the internal laryngeal nerve."},{"type":"p","text":"Taste from the anterior two thirds does not travel centrally with V3. It is carried by the chorda tympani, a branch of the facial nerve (CN VII) that leaves the facial canal, crosses the middle ear, and joins the lingual nerve in the infratemporal fossa. Its taste fibers have their cell bodies in the geniculate ganglion and end, like the taste fibers of CN IX and CN X, in the nucleus of the solitary tract. The chorda tympani also carries preganglionic parasympathetic fibers, which leave the lingual nerve to synapse in the submandibular ganglion that supplies the submandibular and sublingual glands."},{"type":"p","text":"The tongue muscles develop from occipital somites and bring their own nerve, the hypoglossal nerve (CN XII), which supplies all intrinsic muscles and the extrinsic genioglossus, hyoglossus, and styloglossus. The exception is palatoglossus, which develops with the muscles of the soft palate and is supplied by the vagus through the pharyngeal plexus. Each genioglossus pulls the tongue forward and toward the opposite side, so when both contract equally the tongue protrudes in the midline."},{"type":"table","title":"Nerves of the tongue and the deficit from a lesion","cols":["Nerve","Supplies","Deficit from a lesion"],"rows":[["Lingual nerve (CN V3)","Touch, pain, temperature, anterior two thirds","Numbness of the anterior two thirds; taste also lost if injured beyond the chorda tympani junction"],["Chorda tympani (CN VII)","Taste, anterior two thirds; secretomotor to submandibular and sublingual glands","Loss of taste on the anterior two thirds; less saliva from those glands"],["Glossopharyngeal (CN IX)","Sensation and taste, posterior third","Loss of both on the posterior third; no gag when that side is touched (afferent limb)"],["Vagus (CN X)","Palatoglossus; sensation and taste at the root","Palate droops on that side, uvula deviates to the other side"],["Hypoglossal (CN XII)","All other tongue muscles","Tongue deviates toward the lesion, with atrophy and fasciculations"]],"caption":"Deficits are on the side of the lesion. Sensation and taste of the anterior two thirds travel in different nerves; the posterior third uses CN IX for both."},{"type":"p","text":"Two motor patterns are tested most. In a lower motor neuron lesion of CN XII, the genioglossus on the injured side is paralyzed, so the healthy muscle pushes the protruded tongue toward the weak side, and denervation produces atrophy and fasciculations on that side. In an upper motor neuron lesion, such as a cortical stroke, corticobulbar input to genioglossus is mainly crossed, so the tongue deviates away from the side of the brain lesion, without atrophy."},{"type":"example","title":"Localizing a lesion from the tongue examination","text":"After removal of a lower right third molar, a patient has lost touch and taste on the right anterior two thirds of the tongue, with normal posterior third sensation, gag reflex, and protrusion. Touch loss localizes to the lingual nerve; taste loss as well places the injury beyond the point where the chorda tympani joins it, which fits the lingual nerve lying against the inner mandible beside the third molar. A facial nerve lesion in the facial canal above the chorda tympani instead removes taste but keeps touch, with facial weakness. A lesion at the stylomastoid foramen spares taste, because the chorda tympani has already left."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-04-01-C03","obj":"GI-4.3","title":"Greater and lesser sacs, omenta, and the omental foramen","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Parietal peritoneum lines the abdominal wall and visceral peritoneum covers the organs, with the peritoneal cavity, a potential space holding a thin film of fluid, between them. Double folds connecting an organ to the wall or to another organ are called mesenteries, ligaments, or omenta, and they carry the organ's vessels, nerves, and lymphatics. The cavity has a large greater sac and a small lesser sac (omental bursa)."},{"type":"p","text":"The lesser sac is a product of gastric rotation. The stomach originally hangs between a dorsal mesogastrium and a ventral mesogastrium. As it rotates about 90 degrees clockwise around its long axis (viewed from above), its left side comes to face forward and the dorsal mesogastrium is swept to the left, opening a recess behind the stomach. The liver grows into the ventral mesogastrium, which becomes the falciform ligament in front of the liver and the lesser omentum between the liver and the stomach. The dorsal mesogastrium elongates into the greater omentum."},{"type":"flow","title":"How the lesser sac forms","steps":["Stomach hangs between dorsal and ventral mesogastria","Stomach rotates 90 degrees clockwise about its long axis","Dorsal mesogastrium swings left, opening a space behind","Space persists as the lesser sac, open only at the omental foramen"],"caption":"The same rotation brings the left vagus to the front of the stomach."},{"type":"p","text":"The lesser omentum runs from the lesser curvature and the first 2 cm of the duodenum to the liver. Its larger part, the hepatogastric ligament, attaches to the fissure for the ligamentum venosum, and its thick right free edge, the hepatoduodenal ligament, runs to the porta hepatis. The greater omentum hangs like an apron from the greater curvature, folds back on itself, and attaches to the transverse colon; its layers usually fuse, so the lesser sac extends only a short way into it. The omentum is mobile and adheres to inflamed organs, which helps wall off local infection."},{"type":"table","title":"Boundaries of the lesser sac and the omental foramen","cols":["Boundary","Lesser sac","Omental (epiploic) foramen"],"rows":[["Anterior","Lesser omentum, posterior stomach, gastrocolic ligament","Hepatoduodenal ligament (portal triad)"],["Posterior","Pancreas, left kidney and adrenal, transverse mesocolon","Inferior vena cava, right crus"],["Superior","Caudate lobe of the liver, diaphragm","Caudate lobe of the liver"],["Inferior","Recess between greater omental layers, often fused","First part of the duodenum"],["Left and right","Left: spleen and its ligaments; right: omental foramen","Opens between the two sacs"]],"caption":"The omental foramen is the only natural communication between the sacs."},{"type":"p","text":"The hepatoduodenal ligament carries the portal triad: the common bile duct in front on the right, the proper hepatic artery in front on the left, and the hepatic portal vein behind both. Because the ligament forms the front wall of the omental foramen and the inferior vena cava its back wall, compressing the free edge (Pringle maneuver) stops hepatic arterial and portal inflow to the liver, while bleeding from the hepatic veins or vena cava, which lie outside the ligament, continues."},{"type":"p","text":"These relations decide where fluid goes when the stomach is breached. A perforated ulcer on the posterior wall of the stomach releases its contents into the lesser sac, where they may stay contained or escape through the omental foramen into the right subhepatic space, whereas an anterior perforation spills directly into the greater sac and releases free air under the diaphragm. Fluid from acute pancreatitis also gathers in the lesser sac, because the pancreas forms its posterior wall, and a pseudocyst there can push the stomach forward."},{"type":"stop","predict":"An ulcer perforates through the posterior wall of the stomach. Why may early signs of spreading peritonitis be limited, and where can the fluid leave the space it first enters?","explain":"Gastric contents enter the lesser sac, which is closed except at the omental foramen, so fluid can only escape to the right through the foramen into the right subhepatic space and hepatorenal recess.","check":"The boundaries table and the paragraph on perforation."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-04-01-C04","obj":"GI-4.4","title":"Peritoneal ligaments and the vessels they carry","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Each peritoneal ligament carries vessels between its two layers, so knowing its contents predicts which artery an ulcer erodes, which veins enlarge in portal hypertension, and which structure is at risk when an organ is freed. The ventral mesentery gives rise to the falciform ligament and the lesser omentum, which reach the liver. The dorsal mesogastrium gives rise to the gastrosplenic, splenorenal, and gastrocolic ligaments, which reach the greater curvature, the spleen, and the transverse colon."},{"type":"table","title":"Peritoneal ligaments of the upper abdomen","cols":["Ligament","Origin","Connects","Contents","Why it matters"],"rows":[["Falciform","Ventral mesentery","Liver to anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm","Ligamentum teres hepatis in its free edge; paraumbilical veins","Paraumbilical veins enlarge in portal hypertension; separates right and left subphrenic spaces"],["Hepatoduodenal","Ventral mesentery (lesser omentum)","Liver to first part of the duodenum","Portal vein, proper hepatic artery, common bile duct","Front wall of the omental foramen"],["Gastrohepatic (hepatogastric)","Ventral mesentery (lesser omentum)","Liver to lesser curvature","Left and right gastric arteries and veins","Lesser curvature ulcers can erode the left gastric artery; left gastric vein feeds esophageal varices"],["Gastrocolic","Dorsal mesogastrium (greater omentum)","Greater curvature to transverse colon","Right and left gastroepiploic (gastro-omental) vessels","Front wall of the lower lesser sac"],["Gastrosplenic","Dorsal mesogastrium","Greater curvature to splenic hilum","Short gastric and left gastroepiploic vessels","Route for fundal varices when the splenic vein is blocked"],["Splenorenal","Dorsal mesogastrium","Splenic hilum to posterior wall over the left kidney","Splenic artery and vein, tail of the pancreas","Pancreatic tail reaches the splenic hilum and is at risk when the spleen is freed"]],"caption":"Ligaments from the ventral mesentery reach the liver; those from the dorsal mesogastrium reach the greater curvature, spleen, and colon."},{"type":"p","text":"The falciform ligament attaches the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and diaphragm and marks the surface boundary between the anatomical right and left lobes. Its free lower edge contains the ligamentum teres hepatis, the fibrous remnant of the left umbilical vein, which carried oxygenated placental blood to the fetal liver and closes after birth. Small paraumbilical veins run beside it, linking the left branch of the portal vein with veins of the abdominal wall around the umbilicus. When portal pressure rises, these portosystemic channels enlarge and radiate from the umbilicus as caput medusae (GI-5)."},{"type":"p","text":"The gastrohepatic ligament carries the left gastric artery, a branch of the celiac trunk, and the right gastric artery, usually from the proper hepatic artery, which join along the lesser curvature, with companion veins that drain into the portal system. The left gastric vein also receives esophageal tributaries, so it is the main portal source of esophageal varices. A penetrating ulcer on the lesser curvature can erode the left gastric artery, whereas an ulcer in the posterior wall of the duodenal bulb erodes the gastroduodenal artery, which runs behind the duodenum outside these ligaments."},{"type":"p","text":"The spleen develops within the dorsal mesogastrium, so it stays tied to the stomach by the gastrosplenic ligament and to the posterior wall by the splenorenal ligament. The gastrosplenic ligament carries the short gastric vessels to the fundus and the left gastroepiploic vessels to the greater curvature. The splenorenal ligament carries the splenic artery and vein to the hilum together with the tail of the pancreas, the one part of the pancreas that is not retroperitoneal. Because the splenic vein runs directly behind the body and tail of the pancreas, pancreatitis or pancreatic cancer can cause it to thrombose, and splenic blood must then return through the short gastric veins."},{"type":"flow","title":"Splenic vein thrombosis and fundal varices","steps":["Pancreatitis or tumor involves the pancreatic body and tail","The splenic vein behind the pancreas thromboses","Splenic blood diverts into the short gastric veins","Veins in the gastric fundus dilate","Isolated fundal varices with normal portal vein pressure"],"caption":"This left-sided (sinistral) portal hypertension is local: the liver and portal vein are normal, so esophageal varices are usually absent."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-04-01-C05","obj":"GI-4.5","title":"Inguinal canal, Hesselbach triangle, and groin hernias","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The inguinal canal is an oblique passage about 4 cm long running inferomedially just above the medial half of the inguinal ligament. It carries the spermatic cord in males or the round ligament of the uterus in females, plus the ilioinguinal nerve, which enters partway along rather than through the deep ring. The canal exists because the testis, preceded by the processus vaginalis, descended through the wall and took a covering from each layer: external spermatic fascia (external oblique), cremaster (internal oblique), and internal spermatic fascia (transversalis fascia)."},{"type":"p","text":"The deep (internal) ring is an opening in the transversalis fascia just above the midpoint of the inguinal ligament, lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels. The superficial (external) ring is a split in the external oblique aponeurosis just above and lateral to the pubic tubercle. Because the rings are offset, each weak point is backed by a strong wall, and straining contracts the arching roof muscles to flatten the canal like a valve."},{"type":"table","title":"Walls of the inguinal canal","cols":["Wall","Formed by","Reinforcement"],"rows":[["Anterior","External oblique aponeurosis","Internal oblique laterally, in front of the deep ring"],["Posterior","Transversalis fascia","Conjoint tendon (falx inguinalis) medially, behind the superficial ring"],["Roof","Arching fibers of internal oblique and transversus abdominis","Contract and lower the roof during straining"],["Floor","Inguinal ligament, the rolled lower edge of the external oblique aponeurosis","Lacunar ligament medially"]],"caption":"Medial to the inferior epigastric vessels the posterior wall is only transversalis fascia and a variable conjoint tendon."},{"type":"p","text":"Viewed from inside, the inferior epigastric vessels raise the lateral umbilical fold. The inguinal (Hesselbach) triangle lies medial to them, bounded laterally by these vessels, medially by the lateral edge of rectus abdominis, and inferiorly by the inguinal ligament. The fossa lateral to the fold leads to the deep ring."},{"type":"p","text":"Each hernia type follows a weak point. An indirect hernia follows the congenital route of testicular descent: it enters the deep ring lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels through a processus vaginalis that did not close, travels inside all three cord coverings, and can reach the scrotum or labium majus. A direct hernia is acquired: transversalis fascia in the Hesselbach triangle weakens with age and repeated straining, and viscera bulge straight through medial to the vessels, outside the inner cord coverings. A femoral hernia passes below the inguinal ligament through the femoral ring, medial to the femoral vein. That ring is small and bounded by unyielding structures (inguinal ligament in front, pectineal ligament behind, lacunar ligament medially, femoral vein laterally), so femoral hernias are the most likely to incarcerate and strangulate."},{"type":"table","title":"Groin hernias compared","cols":["Feature","Indirect inguinal","Direct inguinal","Femoral"],"rows":[["Entry","Deep inguinal ring","Hesselbach triangle in the posterior wall","Femoral ring"],["Relation to inferior epigastric vessels","Lateral","Medial","Below the inguinal ligament, medial to the femoral vein"],["Relation to pubic tubercle","Above and medial","Above and medial","Below and lateral"],["Basis","Patent processus vaginalis","Weak transversalis fascia","Small rigid femoral ring"],["Typical patient","Infants and young males; most common overall","Older men","Women more than men, though indirect is still the most common hernia in women"],["Strangulation risk","Moderate","Low (wide neck)","Highest"]],"caption":"The inferior epigastric vessels separate the inguinal types; the inguinal ligament separates inguinal from femoral."},{"type":"example","title":"Naming a hernia from its landmarks","text":"A 68-year-old man with a chronic cough has a groin bulge above the inguinal ligament and medial to the pubic tubercle that does not enter the scrotum; from inside the abdomen, the defect lies medial to the lateral umbilical fold. Above the ligament and medial to the tubercle means inguinal, not femoral. The lateral umbilical fold is raised by the inferior epigastric vessels, so a defect medial to it lies in the Hesselbach triangle: a direct hernia, fitting chronic coughing against fascia weakened by age. A defect lateral to the fold would be at the deep ring, an indirect hernia."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-04-01-C06","obj":"GI-4.6","title":"Dependent peritoneal spaces and abscess formation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Free fluid in the peritoneal cavity, whether ascites, blood, pus, or bile, follows the peritoneal attachments and gravity and collects in the lowest recess for the patient's position. The transverse mesocolon divides the cavity into supracolic and infracolic compartments, and the root of the small bowel mesentery divides the infracolic compartment into right and left spaces. The paracolic gutters, lateral to the ascending and descending colon, connect the upper abdomen with the pelvis."},{"type":"p","text":"In the supracolic compartment, the falciform ligament separates right and left subphrenic spaces between the diaphragm and the liver. Below the right lobe lies the right subhepatic space, whose posterior extension between the liver and right kidney is the hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch). When the patient is supine it is the most dependent part of the upper abdomen, and it opens into the right subphrenic space above, the right paracolic gutter below, and the lesser sac through the omental foramen. On the left, the phrenicocolic ligament, from the splenic flexure to the diaphragm, partly closes the top of the left gutter, so fluid moves less freely between the left upper quadrant and the pelvis."},{"type":"p","text":"When the patient is upright, the lowest part of the cavity is in the pelvis: the rectouterine pouch (of Douglas) between rectum and uterus, and the rectovesical pouch between rectum and bladder in men. Blood from a ruptured ectopic pregnancy and pus from appendicitis, diverticulitis, or pelvic inflammatory disease gather here. The rectouterine pouch lies just behind the posterior vaginal fornix and the rectovesical pouch in front of the rectum, so a pelvic collection can be felt on vaginal or rectal examination."},{"type":"table","title":"Where peritoneal fluid collects","cols":["Space","Location","When it fills","Typical collection"],"rows":[["Hepatorenal recess (Morison pouch)","Between right lobe of liver and right kidney","Supine","Blood after trauma; fluid from the lesser sac; abscess"],["Right subphrenic","Between diaphragm and right lobe","Fluid drawn up the right gutter","Abscess after perforation, appendicitis, or surgery"],["Left subphrenic","Between diaphragm and left lobe, stomach, spleen","Less often; phrenicocolic ligament limits inflow","Abscess after gastric, splenic, or colonic perforation or surgery"],["Rectouterine pouch","Between rectum and uterus","Upright","Ectopic pregnancy blood; pelvic abscess"],["Rectovesical pouch","Between rectum and bladder","Upright","Pelvic abscess"]],"caption":"Supine collections favor the hepatorenal recess; upright ones favor the pelvic pouches."},{"type":"p","text":"Fluid does not only fall. Each breath lowers pressure beneath the diaphragm, and lymphatic openings in the diaphragmatic peritoneum absorb fluid, so peritoneal fluid is drawn upward toward the subphrenic spaces, mainly along the open right paracolic gutter. This is why subphrenic abscesses follow perforated ulcers, appendicitis, and abdominal surgery, why they are more common on the right, and why pelvic infection can reach the liver capsule (perihepatitis in Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome). The central diaphragmatic peritoneum is supplied by the phrenic nerve (C3 to C5), so its irritation is referred to the shoulder tip, whose skin is supplied by the supraclavicular nerves (C3, C4); the peripheral diaphragm is supplied by lower intercostal nerves, so irritation there is felt over the lower chest and upper abdominal wall."},{"type":"flow","title":"From a ruptured appendix to a right subphrenic abscess","steps":["Appendix perforates in the right iliac fossa","Pus enters the right paracolic gutter","Breathing draws fluid up the open right gutter","Pus reaches the hepatorenal recess and right subphrenic space","Abscess irritates phrenic-supplied diaphragmatic peritoneum: shoulder-tip pain"],"caption":"In an upright patient, gravity carries pus down the same gutter into the pelvis instead."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A woman with pelvic inflammatory disease develops right upper quadrant pain that worsens on deep breathing. How did the infection reach the liver, and why the right side?","explain":"Infected fluid travels up the right paracolic gutter, which opens freely into the hepatorenal recess and right subphrenic space, drawn upward by respiratory pressure changes; the phrenicocolic ligament limits this spread on the left.","check":"The paragraph on upward spread and the table of spaces."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology explains nearly every relation in this chapter. Rotation of the stomach creates the lesser sac and turns the vagal trunks, the liver growing into the ventral mesentery gives the falciform ligament and the route of the portal triad, the spleen forming in the dorsal mesogastrium gives its ligaments and its tie to the pancreatic tail, the arch origins of the tongue explain its split sensory supply, and descent of the testis explains the inguinal canal and the indirect hernia. When an anatomical rule seems arbitrary, its developmental origin usually makes sense of it."},{"type":"p","text":"The same anatomy routes disease. Portal hypertension opens collaterals in the lower esophagus, the falciform ligament, and the short gastric veins; perforation and pancreatitis fill the lesser sac; and infections from the appendix, colon, or fallopian tubes, whether mixed gut flora or Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Chlamydia trachomatis, collect in the dependent spaces. The limits are real: vessel origins, the extent of the lesser sac, and fusion of the omental layers vary between people, and fluid distribution also depends on volume, adhesions, and how long the patient has been in one position, so imaging findings are read together with the clinical picture."}],"summary":["The esophagus runs from C6 behind the trachea and left atrium to the hiatus at T10, with narrowings at the cricopharyngeus, the aortic arch and left main bronchus, and the diaphragm.","Swallowed objects and tablets stall at the narrowings; an enlarged left atrium indents the middle esophagus from the front.","Anterior two thirds of the tongue: touch by the lingual nerve (V3), taste by the chorda tympani (VII); posterior third: touch and taste by CN IX.","CN XII moves the tongue except palatoglossus (CN X); a lower motor neuron CN XII lesion makes the tongue deviate toward the lesion.","Gastric rotation forms the lesser sac behind the stomach, which opens to the greater sac only through the omental foramen.","The hepatoduodenal ligament forms the front of the omental foramen and carries the bile duct (front right), hepatic artery (front left), and portal vein (behind); the IVC lies behind the foramen.","Falciform carries the ligamentum teres, gastrohepatic the gastric arteries, gastrosplenic the short gastric vessels, and splenorenal the splenic vessels and pancreatic tail.","Indirect hernias enter the deep ring lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels; direct hernias cross the Hesselbach triangle medial to them; femoral hernias pass below the inguinal ligament and strangulate most often.","Fluid pools in the hepatorenal recess when supine and in the rectouterine or rectovesical pouch when upright, and breathing draws it up the right gutter to the subphrenic spaces.","Subphrenic irritation of the central diaphragm is referred to the shoulder tip through the phrenic nerve (C3 to C5)."],"sources":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Thorax, posterior mediastinum and the esophagus"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen, the inguinal region and the peritoneum and peritoneal cavity"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head, oral region and the tongue"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Digestive system (mesenteries and rotation of the stomach) and head and neck (development of the tongue)"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Gastrointestinal Tract"}]},"GI-5":{"unit":"GI-5","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-05","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-05-01","title":"Gastrointestinal Blood Supply","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows blood into, through, and out of the gut. It covers the three unpaired aortic branches and their territories, the watershed zones of the colon, the portal venous system and the escape routes portal blood takes when the liver obstructs it, the two territories of the anal canal, and the dual inflow of the liver. Board questions usually give a bleeding ulcer, a hypotensive episode, a sign on the abdominal wall, or an enlarged groin node and ask for the vessel, segment, or drainage path responsible. If you know which embryonic gut segment each organ comes from and that portal blood must cross a second capillary bed in the liver, most of these questions can be reasoned out.","prerequisites":["Foregut, midgut, and hindgut divisions and their derivatives (GI-1)","Peritoneal relations: hepatoduodenal ligament, lesser sac, and retroperitoneal organs (GI-4)","Liver lobule, acinus, and sinusoid histology (GI-2)","Pressure, flow, and resistance relationships and Starling forces in capillaries","Visceral versus somatic afferent innervation"],"connections":[{"to":"Hepatic diseases (GI-13-C)","how":"Cirrhosis is the usual cause of portal hypertension, and its complications (varices, splenomegaly, ascites, encephalopathy) follow from the venous anatomy in this chapter."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal vascular diseases (GI-13-J)","how":"Acute and chronic mesenteric ischemia, ischemic colitis, and variceal bleeding are applications of the arterial territories and anastomoses taught here."},{"to":"Inflammatory gastrointestinal diseases (GI-13-G)","how":"The site of a peptic ulcer predicts whether it bleeds from a named artery or perforates into the peritoneal cavity."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal neoplasms (GI-13-K)","how":"Venous drainage explains why gut cancers metastasize first to the liver, and lymph drainage across the pectinate line predicts nodal spread of anal cancers."},{"to":"Cardiovascular physiology","how":"Shock, heart failure, and atrial fibrillation emboli injure the gut and liver through the same flow, pressure, and watershed principles that apply in other organs."}],"terms":[{"term":"Watershed zone","def":"A region at the border between two arterial territories, supplied by the most distal branches of each, where perfusion pressure is lowest during a global fall in flow."},{"term":"Marginal artery","def":"A continuous arterial channel along the mesenteric border of the colon formed by anastomoses between the colic branches of the SMA and IMA."},{"term":"Portal system","def":"A venous arrangement in which blood passes through two capillary beds in series; the hepatic portal vein carries blood from the gut capillaries to the liver sinusoids."},{"term":"Sinusoid","def":"A wide, fenestrated hepatic capillary without a continuous basement membrane, where portal and arterial blood mix and exchange with hepatocytes across the space of Disse."},{"term":"Portal hypertension","def":"Abnormally high pressure in the portal venous system, caused by increased resistance to flow before, within, or after the liver."},{"term":"Hepatic venous pressure gradient (HVPG)","def":"The difference between wedged (sinusoidal) and free hepatic venous pressure; normally about 1 to 5 mmHg."},{"term":"Portosystemic anastomosis","def":"A site where a tributary of the portal vein communicates with a systemic vein, providing a bypass around the liver when portal pressure rises."},{"term":"Varix","def":"A dilated, tortuous vein; in portal hypertension, thin-walled submucosal veins at portosystemic anastomoses enlarge into varices."},{"term":"Caput medusae","def":"Dilated superficial abdominal wall veins radiating from the umbilicus, fed by paraumbilical veins in portal hypertension."},{"term":"Pectinate line","def":"The serrated line formed by the bases of the anal valves, marking the junction of hindgut endoderm above and proctodeal ectoderm below."},{"term":"Hepatic arterial buffer response","def":"Dilation of hepatic arterioles when portal flow falls, mediated by accumulation of adenosine that portal flow normally washes away."},{"term":"Peribiliary vascular plexus","def":"The capillary network around the intrahepatic bile ducts, supplied by branches of the hepatic artery."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-05-01-C01","obj":"GI-5.1","title":"The celiac trunk and its branches","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Three unpaired arteries leave the front of the abdominal aorta to supply the gut, and each serves one embryonic segment. The celiac trunk arises at about T12, just below the aortic hiatus, and supplies the foregut derivatives: the abdominal esophagus, stomach, duodenum to the major papilla, liver, gallbladder, and much of the pancreas. The spleen, which develops in the dorsal mesogastrium, is supplied by the same trunk. After a short course the trunk divides into three branches."},{"type":"table","title":"Branches of the celiac trunk","cols":["Branch","Course","Main branches","Supplies"],"rows":[["Left gastric","Up to the esophagogastric junction, then along the lesser curvature","Esophageal branches","Lower esophagus and lesser curvature"],["Splenic","Tortuous, along the upper border of the pancreas, behind the stomach","Pancreatic branches, short gastrics, left gastro-omental (gastroepiploic)","Body and tail of pancreas, fundus, upper greater curvature, spleen"],["Common hepatic","To the right, then divides near the duodenum","Proper hepatic (right and left hepatic, usually right gastric) and gastroduodenal","Liver, gallbladder (cystic artery, usually from right hepatic), pylorus, lesser curvature"],["Gastroduodenal (from common hepatic)","Descends behind the first part of the duodenum","Right gastro-omental, superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries","First part of duodenum, pancreatic head, lower greater curvature"]],"caption":"Each curvature of the stomach carries a pair of arteries that anastomose: left and right gastric along the lesser curvature, left and right gastro-omental along the greater."},{"type":"p","text":"Because the stomach receives arteries from every celiac branch and these join along both curvatures, gastric infarction is rare. The splenic artery, by contrast, divides into segmental branches within the spleen that behave as end arteries, so emboli produce wedge-shaped splenic infarcts. The superior pancreaticoduodenal arteries from the gastroduodenal artery anastomose around the pancreatic head with the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery from the SMA. This arcade marks the foregut to midgut boundary and is the main collateral between the celiac and SMA territories, so slow narrowing of one origin can be bypassed through the other."},{"type":"h","text":"Ulcer location predicts the bleeding vessel"},{"type":"p","text":"A peptic ulcer erodes whatever lies beneath it. The gastroduodenal artery runs directly behind the first part of the duodenum, so a posterior duodenal bulb ulcer can open it and cause brisk hematemesis or melena with hemodynamic instability. A lesser curvature gastric ulcer can erode the left gastric artery. An anterior duodenal ulcer has no large artery behind it, and its base faces the free peritoneal cavity, so it is more likely to perforate, releasing gas that collects under the diaphragm on an upright radiograph and causing chemical then bacterial peritonitis."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with a posterior duodenal bulb ulcer and another with an anterior duodenal ulcer both deteriorate suddenly. Which is more likely to show free air under the diaphragm, and which a falling hemoglobin?","explain":"The anterior ulcer perforates into the peritoneal cavity, so gas escapes and rises under the diaphragm. The posterior ulcer sits on the gastroduodenal artery and erodes it, so it bleeds into the lumen and the hemoglobin falls.","check":"The paragraph on ulcer location and the course of the gastroduodenal artery in the table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-05-01-C02","obj":"GI-5.2","title":"Mesenteric arteries, the marginal artery, and watershed ischemia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The superior mesenteric artery (SMA) arises at about L1, passes behind the neck of the pancreas and in front of the third part of the duodenum, and supplies the midgut: the duodenum below the major papilla, jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and proximal two thirds of the transverse colon. The inferior mesenteric artery (IMA) arises at about L3 and supplies the hindgut: the distal third of the transverse colon, descending and sigmoid colon, and upper rectum."},{"type":"table","title":"Mesenteric artery territories","cols":["Artery","Aortic level","Branches","Territory"],"rows":[["SMA","About L1","Inferior pancreaticoduodenal, jejunal and ileal, ileocolic, right colic, middle colic","Distal duodenum to proximal two thirds of transverse colon"],["IMA","About L3","Left colic, sigmoid, superior rectal","Distal third of transverse colon to upper rectum"],["Internal iliac branches","Pelvis","Middle rectal; inferior rectal via internal pudendal","Lower rectum and anal canal"]],"caption":"The rectum has three arterial sources, which is why it is usually spared in low-flow ischemia."},{"type":"p","text":"Branches of the colic arteries join near the mesenteric border of the colon to form the marginal artery, a continuous channel from the ileocolic region to the rectosigmoid. It links the SMA and IMA, so a slowly occluding IMA (as in atherosclerosis) can be bypassed. The link is weakest at two points. At the splenic flexure, the terminal branches of the middle colic (SMA) and left colic (IMA) meet, and the marginal artery there is thin or sometimes discontinuous. At the rectosigmoid junction, the last sigmoid artery meets the superior rectal artery with few connecting vessels."},{"type":"p","text":"These watershed zones sit at the far end of two territories, so pressure has already fallen along each supply before reaching them. When systemic pressure or cardiac output falls, as in shock, heart failure, dehydration, or after major surgery, they are the first to lose adequate flow. The mucosa is injured first because it has the highest metabolic demand and lies at the end of the penetrating vessels. Ischemic colitis typically presents in an older patient with sudden cramping left-sided abdominal pain followed within about a day by bloody diarrhea, because sloughing mucosa bleeds. Imaging may show thumbprinting, the rounded indentations produced by submucosal edema and hemorrhage. Most cases are nonocclusive and confined to the mucosa; transmural infarction leads to perforation and peritonitis."},{"type":"flow","title":"From hypotension to ischemic colitis","steps":["Cardiac output or systemic pressure falls","Mesenteric vasoconstriction diverts flow to heart and brain","Watershed zones receive the least perfusion pressure","Mucosa, with the highest demand, becomes ischemic first","Mucosal necrosis and sloughing","Cramping pain, then bloody diarrhea"],"caption":"Splenic flexure and rectosigmoid are affected first; the rectum is spared by its dual supply."},{"type":"p","text":"Ischemic colitis differs from acute mesenteric ischemia of the small bowel. An embolus from the left atrium in atrial fibrillation enters the SMA because it leaves the aorta at an acute angle, and it often lodges near the origin of the middle colic artery. Visceral pain fibers sense ischemia while the parietal peritoneum is not yet involved, so pain is severe but the abdomen is soft: pain out of proportion to the examination. Lactate rises as the bowel shifts to anaerobic metabolism. Chronic narrowing of two or more mesenteric origins produces postprandial pain (intestinal angina), because digestion demands a rise in flow the narrowed vessels cannot deliver, so patients fear food and lose weight."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-05-01-C03","obj":"GI-5.3","title":"The portal venous pathway through the liver","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Venous blood from the abdominal gut, spleen, pancreas, and gallbladder does not go straight to the inferior vena cava (IVC). It collects in the hepatic portal vein and passes through a second capillary bed, the liver sinusoids, before returning to the heart. Absorbed nutrients, drugs, hormones such as insulin and glucagon, and bacterial products therefore reach hepatocytes first, which allows first-pass metabolism, nutrient storage, and clearance of gut-derived toxins such as ammonia and endotoxin."},{"type":"p","text":"The superior mesenteric vein (SMV) and splenic vein join behind the neck of the pancreas to form the portal vein. The inferior mesenteric vein usually drains into the splenic vein, though it may enter the SMV or the confluence. The portal vein passes behind the first part of the duodenum and ascends in the free edge of the hepatoduodenal ligament, where it lies posterior to the common bile duct (on the right) and the proper hepatic artery (on the left). Along the way it receives the left and right gastric veins, and the cystic and paraumbilical veins drain into it or its branches. At the porta hepatis it divides into right and left branches that follow the arteries and bile ducts into the portal tracts."},{"type":"flow","title":"Path of portal blood","steps":["SMV and splenic vein join behind the pancreatic neck","Portal vein ascends in the hepatoduodenal ligament","Right and left branches to portal tract venules","Mixes with hepatic arterial blood in sinusoids","Central veins collect sinusoidal blood","Sublobular veins to right, middle, and left hepatic veins","Hepatic veins enter the IVC below the diaphragm"],"caption":"Blood flows from the portal tract (zone 1) toward the central vein (zone 3), while bile flows the opposite way toward the portal tract."},{"type":"p","text":"The sinusoids are fenestrated and lack a continuous basement membrane, so plasma reaches hepatocyte microvilli in the space of Disse. Because blood enters at the portal tract and leaves at the central vein, hepatocytes in zone 3 (around the central vein) receive the least oxygen. They are the first to die in shock and the first to congest when hepatic venous pressure rises in right heart failure, producing the nutmeg liver."},{"type":"p","text":"Normal portal pressure is low, about 5 to 10 mmHg, and hepatic venous pressure is near 0 mmHg, so the liver offers little resistance. The portal system has essentially no valves, so any rise in resistance between the gut and the right atrium raises pressure throughout it. Classifying the obstruction by its site explains the findings."},{"type":"table","title":"Causes of portal hypertension by site","cols":["Site","Examples","Distinguishing feature"],"rows":[["Prehepatic","Portal or splenic vein thrombosis","Liver function often normal"],["Intrahepatic, presinusoidal","Schistosomiasis (periportal fibrosis)","Wedged hepatic pressure may be normal"],["Intrahepatic, sinusoidal","Cirrhosis","Most common cause; HVPG raised"],["Posthepatic","Budd-Chiari syndrome (hepatic vein thrombosis), right heart failure, constrictive pericarditis","Tender hepatomegaly with centrilobular congestion"]],"caption":"HVPG above about 5 mmHg defines sinusoidal portal hypertension; above about 10 mmHg it is clinically significant."},{"type":"p","text":"As portal pressure rises, splenic venous outflow is impeded, so the spleen congests and enlarges, which sequesters platelets (hypersplenism). Raised sinusoidal pressure drives fluid into the space of Disse faster than hepatic lymphatics can remove it, which contributes to ascites."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-05-01-C04","obj":"GI-5.4","title":"Portosystemic anastomoses and their signs","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"At a few sites, small tributaries of the portal vein and of systemic veins drain the same tissue and connect with each other. In health these connections carry little blood, because portal pressure is only slightly higher than systemic venous pressure. When portal pressure rises, blood flows down the new gradient from the portal side into the systemic side, and the thin connecting veins dilate. The same shunting lets gut blood bypass hepatocytes, so ammonia and other gut-derived compounds reach the brain and contribute to hepatic encephalopathy."},{"type":"table","title":"Portosystemic anastomoses","cols":["Site","Portal side","Systemic side","Result in portal hypertension"],"rows":[["Distal esophagus","Left gastric vein","Esophageal veins draining to the azygos and hemiazygos veins","Esophageal varices; hematemesis"],["Umbilicus","Paraumbilical veins in the falciform ligament","Superficial and deep epigastric and thoracoepigastric veins","Caput medusae"],["Rectum and anal canal","Superior rectal vein","Middle and inferior rectal veins draining to the internal iliac veins","Anorectal varices"],["Retroperitoneum","Colic, splenic, and pancreatic veins","Lumbar, renal, and other retroperitoneal veins","Retroperitoneal collaterals seen on imaging"]],"caption":"The first three sites are the classic examination triad: top, middle, and bottom of the abdominal gut."},{"type":"p","text":"Esophageal varices are the most dangerous. They lie in the submucosa of the lower esophagus with little surrounding tissue support. By the law of Laplace, wall stress equals pressure times radius divided by wall thickness, so as a varix widens and its wall thins, stress rises until the wall ruptures; the risk is substantial once HVPG exceeds about 12 mmHg. Bleeding is brisk because the vein carries portal pressure."},{"type":"flow","title":"From cirrhosis to variceal hemorrhage","steps":["Fibrosis and sinusoidal narrowing raise intrahepatic resistance","Portal pressure rises; HVPG above about 10 mmHg","Left gastric vein flow reverses toward the esophagus","Submucosal esophageal veins dilate into varices","Wall stress rises as radius grows and wall thins","Rupture causes massive hematemesis"],"caption":"Coagulopathy and thrombocytopenia from liver disease and hypersplenism worsen the bleed."},{"type":"p","text":"The paraumbilical veins accompany the ligamentum teres in the falciform ligament from the left portal vein to the umbilicus. Portal blood reaching them flows into abdominal wall veins and radiates outward from the navel as caput medusae: upward above the umbilicus and downward below it. In IVC obstruction, by contrast, leg blood returns to the heart through abdominal wall veins, so flow below the umbilicus runs upward. Anorectal varices form where the superior rectal vein meets the middle and inferior rectal veins. They are not the same as hemorrhoids, which are enlarged anal vascular cushions caused mainly by straining and raised abdominal pressure rather than by portal hypertension."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Dilated abdominal wall veins are seen in one patient with cirrhosis and in another with IVC obstruction. How would emptying a vein below the umbilicus and watching it refill tell them apart?","explain":"In portal hypertension blood radiates away from the umbilicus, so veins below it fill from above and flow downward. In IVC obstruction, blood from the legs must return to the heart through the abdominal wall, so veins below the umbilicus flow upward, often along the flanks.","check":"The caput medusae paragraph and the umbilical row of the table."},{"type":"example","title":"Gastric varices with a normal liver","text":"A patient with chronic pancreatitis vomits blood. Endoscopy shows large varices in the gastric fundus but none in the esophagus, and liver tests are normal. Inflammation in the pancreatic bed has thrombosed the splenic vein, which runs along the back of the pancreas. Splenic blood reroutes through the short gastric veins into the submucosal veins of the fundus, which dilate, and then reaches the portal vein through the gastric veins. Portal pressure itself is normal, so the esophageal anastomosis is not stressed. This is sinistral (left-sided) portal hypertension, a prehepatic cause, and the spleen is congested and enlarged."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-05-01-C05","obj":"GI-5.5","title":"The anal canal above and below the pectinate line","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The pectinate (dentate) line is formed by the bases of the anal valves about midway along the anal canal. It marks the site of the embryonic anal membrane, where the endoderm of the hindgut (cloaca) met the ectoderm of the proctodeum. Tissue above the line therefore behaves like gut and tissue below it like skin, and every feature follows from that origin."},{"type":"table","title":"Anal canal by the pectinate line","cols":["Feature","Above the line (endoderm)","Below the line (ectoderm)"],"rows":[["Epithelium","Simple columnar, with a narrow transitional zone near the line","Stratified squamous nonkeratinized, becoming keratinized skin at the anal verge"],["Arterial supply","Superior rectal artery (from IMA)","Inferior rectal artery (from internal pudendal, internal iliac)"],["Venous drainage","Superior rectal vein to IMV to portal vein","Inferior rectal vein to internal pudendal and internal iliac veins to IVC (systemic)"],["Lymph drainage","Internal iliac nodes (upper canal also along superior rectal vessels to inferior mesenteric nodes)","Superficial inguinal nodes"],["Innervation","Visceral afferents with the inferior hypogastric plexus","Somatic inferior rectal nerves (branches of the pudendal nerve, S2 to S4)"],["Pain","Senses stretch only; painless to cutting or pinching","Sensitive to pain, touch, and temperature"],["Typical lesions","Internal hemorrhoids (painless bleeding); adenocarcinoma","External hemorrhoids (painful when thrombosed); fissures; squamous cell carcinoma"]],"caption":"The middle rectal arteries and veins (internal iliac) also supply and drain the region around the anorectal junction."},{"type":"p","text":"Pain is the clearest clinical consequence. Visceral afferents above the line respond to distension but not to cutting, so internal hemorrhoids, polyps, and early tumors there are painless, and bright red blood on the stool may be the only sign. Below the line, the inferior rectal nerves carry somatic sensation, so a thrombosed external hemorrhoid or an anal fissure (a tear in the squamous lining, usually in the posterior midline) causes sharp, well-localized pain, especially on defecation."},{"type":"p","text":"Drainage predicts spread. Venous blood above the line enters the portal system, so adenocarcinoma of the rectum and upper canal can metastasize to the liver, while blood below the line enters the IVC and reaches the lungs first. Lymph from below the line goes to the superficial inguinal nodes, which can be felt in the groin, whereas nodes draining the upper canal lie deep in the pelvis and are not palpable. Squamous cell carcinoma, which arises from the squamous and transitional epithelium and is strongly associated with human papillomavirus, therefore often presents with an enlarged inguinal node."},{"type":"example","title":"Localizing an anal lesion","text":"A patient reports painful defecation, a firm mass at the anal verge, and a hard, enlarged node in the groin. Pain means somatic innervation, so the lesion is below the pectinate line. Squamous epithelium lines that region, so the likely cancer is squamous cell carcinoma, and its lymph drains to the superficial inguinal nodes, explaining the palpable node. A second patient has painless bright red bleeding and a mass felt above the line on digital examination. Visceral innervation explains the absent pain, the columnar lining predicts adenocarcinoma, nodal spread goes to pelvic nodes that cannot be felt, and venous spread through the superior rectal vein goes to the liver."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-05-01-C06","obj":"GI-5.6","title":"Dual blood supply of the liver","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The liver receives about 1350 mL of blood per minute, roughly a quarter of resting cardiac output. About 75% arrives through the portal vein at low pressure and about 25% through the hepatic artery at systemic arterial pressure. Portal blood has already passed through the gut and spleen and is partly deoxygenated, but its larger volume means each inflow delivers roughly half of the oxygen the liver uses. Both streams enter the same sinusoids from the portal tracts, so every hepatocyte is perfused by both."},{"type":"p","text":"The liver cannot control portal inflow, which is set by the splanchnic arterioles upstream in the gut and spleen. It does control hepatic arterial inflow, and it uses this to buffer changes in portal flow."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Hepatic arterial buffer response","steps":["Portal flow falls","Less adenosine is washed from around hepatic arterioles","Adenosine accumulates and dilates the arterioles","Hepatic arterial flow rises","Total flow and adenosine washout partly recover"],"caption":"The response works in one direction only: arterial flow compensates for portal changes, but portal flow does not compensate for arterial changes."},{"type":"p","text":"These features explain why hepatic infarction is uncommon. Occluding a portal vein branch usually causes only a sharply outlined red zone of sinusoidal congestion and hepatocyte atrophy without necrosis (the infarct of Zahn, which is not a true infarct). Ligating the common hepatic artery proximal to the gastroduodenal artery is usually tolerated, because blood from the SMA reaches the proper hepatic artery backward through the pancreaticoduodenal arcades and gastroduodenal artery (C01). True infarction needs loss of arterial flow together with poor portal flow or absent collaterals."},{"type":"p","text":"Global hypoperfusion is a different problem. In shock or severe heart failure both inflows fall together, and the zone 3 hepatocytes at the end of the sinusoid die first. This ischemic hepatitis (shock liver) produces a sharp rise in aminotransferases, often above 1000 U/L, that falls quickly once perfusion is restored."},{"type":"p","text":"The bile ducts have no such backup. Their peribiliary vascular plexus is supplied only by branches of the hepatic artery, so hepatocytes may survive arterial occlusion while ducts do not. After liver transplantation, the graft has no arterial collaterals, and hepatic artery thrombosis causes ischemic cholangiopathy: duct necrosis, bile leaks, strictures, and cholangitis. A similar arterial preference explains imaging of hepatocellular carcinoma, which draws its blood mainly from the hepatic artery, so it enhances brightly in the arterial phase and appears to wash out in the portal venous phase."},{"type":"stop","predict":"After liver transplantation, one patient develops portal vein thrombosis and another hepatic artery thrombosis. Which is more likely to develop bile duct strictures, and why?","explain":"Hepatic artery thrombosis. The peribiliary plexus depends on hepatic arterial blood alone, and the graft has no collaterals, so the ducts become ischemic. Portal vein thrombosis reduces hepatocyte inflow, but the hepatic arterial buffer response raises arterial flow and the ducts keep their supply.","check":"The buffer response cycle and the bile duct paragraph."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Embryology organizes the anatomy. The foregut, midgut, and hindgut each take one unpaired aortic branch, their borders are where collateral arcades and watershed zones form, and the junction of endoderm and ectoderm at the pectinate line divides the anal canal into a gut-like and a skin-like territory with separate vessels, nodes, and nerves. Physiology supplies the mechanisms: watershed zones fail first because pressure has fallen by the time blood reaches them, mucosa is injured first because its demand is highest, and the liver protects its hepatocytes through two inflows and the adenosine-driven buffer response while leaving the bile ducts dependent on the artery alone."},{"type":"p","text":"Pathology and microbiology apply the same map. Cirrhosis from alcohol, viral hepatitis, or fatty liver disease raises sinusoidal resistance, schistosome eggs block presinusoidal venules, and pancreatitis thromboses the splenic vein, and each produces a predictable pattern of varices and splenomegaly. Venous drainage explains why colorectal cancer seeds the liver, and lymph drainage explains which nodes an anal cancer reaches. Arterial branching and collateral capacity vary between individuals, so these rules predict the typical case."}],"summary":["The celiac trunk (about T12), SMA (about L1), and IMA (about L3) supply the foregut, midgut, and hindgut, with the spleen supplied by the celiac trunk.","A posterior duodenal ulcer erodes the gastroduodenal artery and bleeds; a lesser curvature gastric ulcer erodes the left gastric artery; an anterior duodenal ulcer tends to perforate.","The marginal artery links SMA and IMA, but it is weakest at the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid junction, which are the first sites of ischemic colitis after hypotension.","The SMV and splenic vein form the portal vein behind the pancreatic neck; portal blood passes through sinusoids to central veins, hepatic veins, and the IVC.","Portal hypertension is classified as prehepatic, intrahepatic (presinusoidal or sinusoidal), or posthepatic, and cirrhosis is the most common cause.","The portosystemic anastomoses at the esophagus, umbilicus, and rectum produce esophageal varices, caput medusae, and anorectal varices.","Above the pectinate line the canal is columnar, portal-drained, internal iliac node-drained, and painless; below it is squamous, systemically drained, inguinal node-drained, and painful.","The liver receives about 75% of its blood from the portal vein and about 25% from the hepatic artery, each providing about half its oxygen, so infarction is uncommon.","The hepatic arterial buffer response raises arterial inflow when portal flow falls, through local adenosine accumulation.","The bile ducts depend on the hepatic artery alone, so hepatic artery thrombosis after transplantation causes ischemic cholangiopathy and strictures."],"sources":[{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Abdomen: vasculature of the abdominal viscera, the hepatic portal system, and the anal canal"},{"book":"Langman's Medical Embryology","where":"Digestive System"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Splanchnic circulation and gastrointestinal blood flow; The Liver"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Gastrointestinal Tract, vascular disorders of the bowel; Liver and Gallbladder, circulatory disorders"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Liver Disease, section on portal hypertension"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Organs associated with the digestive tract: the liver"}]},"GI-6":{"unit":"GI-6","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-06","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-06-01","title":"Motility, Digestion, and Absorption","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows a meal from the mouth to the anus. It explains how chewing and swallowing move food past the airway, how slow waves and enteric reflexes propel it, how the stomach makes acid, how gut hormones match secretion and motility to what reaches the duodenum, how carbohydrate, protein, and fat are digested and absorbed, and how defecation and vomiting are controlled. Board questions usually describe a meal, a receptor blocker, a nerve injury, or a set of electrolytes and ask you to predict the hormone, secretion, motor pattern, or acid-base change that follows. Each response is produced by a specific cell, nerve, or receptor, so knowing which one lets you predict the effect of losing it.","prerequisites":["Layers of the gut wall and the positions of the myenteric and submucosal plexuses","Smooth muscle excitation-contraction coupling and gap junctions","G protein signaling (Gs, Gi, Gq, cAMP, IP3, and intracellular Ca2+)","Cranial nerves V, IX, X, and XII and the sacral spinal cord (S2 to S4)","Renal handling of Na+, K+, H+, and HCO3- and the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system"],"connections":[{"to":"Biochemistry of carbohydrates and lipids","how":"The monosaccharides and chylomicrons produced here are the substrates of glycolysis and of lipoprotein metabolism."},{"to":"Endocrine pancreas","how":"GIP and GLP-1 are incretins, so the gut shapes the insulin response to an oral meal."},{"to":"Neuroanatomy of the brainstem and sacral cord","how":"Swallowing and vomiting are medullary programs and defecation is a sacral reflex, so lesion level predicts the deficit."},{"to":"Renal and acid-base physiology","how":"The kidney decides whether the alkalosis of vomiting persists, through aldosterone, chloride, and potassium."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal pathology","how":"Achalasia, Hirschsprung disease, gastrinoma, and malabsorption are each the loss of one mechanism taught here."}],"terms":[{"term":"Interstitial cells of Cajal","def":"Pacemaker cells in the gut wall that generate slow waves and pass them to smooth muscle through gap junctions."},{"term":"Slow wave","def":"A rhythmic oscillation of smooth muscle membrane potential that sets the maximum frequency of contraction but does not by itself cause most contractions."},{"term":"Spike potential","def":"A Ca2+-carried action potential that fires on a slow wave crest when the membrane passes threshold and triggers contraction."},{"term":"Migrating motor complex (MMC)","def":"A cyclic fasting pattern of intense contractions that sweeps from the stomach to the ileum about every 90 to 120 minutes."},{"term":"Receptive relaxation","def":"Vagally mediated relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter and proximal stomach that lets a swallowed bolus enter with little rise in pressure."},{"term":"Potentiation","def":"A combined response larger than the sum of the separate responses, as when histamine, acetylcholine, and gastrin act together on the parietal cell."},{"term":"Enteroendocrine cell","def":"An epithelial cell that senses luminal contents and releases a hormone or paracrine signal from its basal surface."},{"term":"Brush border enzyme","def":"A hydrolase anchored in the enterocyte microvillus membrane that completes digestion next to the transporters."},{"term":"Mixed micelle","def":"A small aggregate of bile salts, monoglycerides, fatty acids, cholesterol, and phospholipid that carries lipid through the unstirred water layer."},{"term":"Rectoanal inhibitory reflex","def":"Enteric reflex in which rectal distension relaxes the internal anal sphincter."},{"term":"Chemoreceptor trigger zone","def":"Neurons of the area postrema, outside the blood-brain barrier, that detect emetic chemicals in blood and cerebrospinal fluid."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-06-01-C01","obj":"GI-6.1","title":"Mastication, swallowing, and relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Mastication breaks food into small particles, mixes it with saliva, and enlarges the surface that digestive enzymes can reach. The four muscles of mastication (masseter, temporalis, medial pterygoid, and lateral pterygoid) derive from the first pharyngeal arch and are all supplied by the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (CN V3). The masseter, temporalis, and medial pterygoid close the jaw, while the lateral pterygoids protrude the mandible and help open it; alternating action of the two sides produces the grinding movement. Rhythmic chewing is largely a brainstem reflex: a bolus pressing on the palate inhibits the jaw closers, the jaw drops, the stretch then triggers rebound contraction, and the cycle repeats while the cortex decides when it starts and stops."},{"type":"p","text":"Swallowing has three phases. The oral phase is voluntary: the tongue (CN XII) presses the bolus up against the hard palate and back into the oropharynx. Once the bolus stimulates receptors around the pharyngeal opening, afferents mainly in CN IX and CN X reach the swallowing center in the medulla and lower pons (around the nucleus tractus solitarius and nucleus ambiguus). From that point the pharyngeal and esophageal phases run as an involuntary reflex that cannot be stopped. The center drives a fixed motor sequence through CN V, IX, X, and XII and briefly inhibits the respiratory center, so breathing pauses while the airway is sealed."},{"type":"flow","title":"The swallowing sequence","steps":["Tongue pushes bolus into oropharynx (voluntary, CN XII)","Pharyngeal afferents (CN IX, X) activate medullary swallowing center","Soft palate rises and seals the nasopharynx","Larynx rises, epiglottis tilts, vocal folds close, breathing pauses","Upper esophageal sphincter (cricopharyngeus) relaxes","Pharyngeal constrictors drive bolus into the esophagus","Primary peristalsis carries bolus toward the stomach","LES and proximal stomach relax ahead of the bolus"],"caption":"The oral phase is voluntary; every later step is a medullary reflex that protects the airway and clears the esophagus."},{"type":"p","text":"The upper third of the esophagus is skeletal muscle driven directly by vagal motor neurons of the nucleus ambiguus, the lower third is smooth muscle controlled by vagal preganglionic fibers acting through the myenteric plexus, and the middle third is mixed. The primary peristaltic wave continues the pharyngeal wave and reaches the stomach in about 8 to 10 seconds. If food remains, local distension starts secondary peristalsis through enteric and vagal reflexes without a new swallow."},{"type":"p","text":"The lower esophageal sphincter (LES) is tonically contracted circular smooth muscle that keeps gastric contents out of the esophagus. When a swallow begins, vagal preganglionic fibers activate inhibitory myenteric neurons that release nitric oxide and vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP). Nitric oxide raises cGMP and VIP raises cAMP in the muscle; both lower cytosolic Ca2+, so the sphincter relaxes before the wave arrives. The same vagovagal pathway relaxes the fundus and upper body of the stomach (receptive relaxation), so a meal is accommodated with little rise in gastric pressure."},{"type":"table","title":"Failures of swallowing and LES control","cols":["Disorder","Mechanism","Manifestation"],"rows":[["Achalasia (idiopathic, or Chagas disease from Trypanosoma cruzi)","Loss of inhibitory NO and VIP myenteric neurons in the distal esophagus","LES fails to relax and peristalsis is lost; dysphagia to solids and liquids, bird-beak narrowing on barium study"],["Systemic sclerosis","Atrophy and fibrosis of esophageal smooth muscle","Weak LES and absent distal peristalsis; reflux with dysphagia"],["Gastroesophageal reflux","Transient LES relaxations not triggered by swallowing, low tone, or hiatal hernia","Acid in the esophagus, heartburn, and mucosal injury"],["Proximal vagotomy","Loss of vagovagal receptive relaxation","Gastric pressure rises quickly with filling; early satiety"],["Medullary stroke or CN IX and X lesions","Pharyngeal phase and airway protection fail","Oropharyngeal dysphagia, nasal regurgitation, aspiration"]],"caption":"Each disorder removes one element of the sequence, and the symptom follows from that element."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-06-01-C02","obj":"GI-6.2","title":"Slow waves, peristalsis, and the migrating motor complex","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Gastrointestinal smooth muscle cells are linked by gap junctions, so each layer behaves as an electrical syncytium. Their membrane potential, normally about -50 to -60 mV, oscillates in slow waves of roughly 5 to 15 mV. Slow waves are generated by interstitial cells of Cajal (ICC), pacemaker cells concentrated at the myenteric plexus between the circular and longitudinal layers, and spread to the muscle through gap junctions."},{"type":"p","text":"In most regions a slow wave by itself does not cause contraction. Contraction needs spike potentials, which fire when a slow wave crest carries the membrane past threshold (about -40 mV) and which are carried mainly by Ca2+ entering through voltage-gated L-type channels. That Ca2+ triggers contraction. Because spikes occur only near crests, slow wave frequency sets the maximum contraction rate of each region, while nerves and hormones decide whether spikes occur and how many. Acetylcholine, stretch, and parasympathetic activity depolarize the muscle and increase spiking; norepinephrine hyperpolarizes it and reduces spiking."},{"type":"table","title":"Slow wave frequency by region","cols":["Region","Approximate frequency"],"rows":[["Stomach","3 per minute"],["Duodenum","12 per minute"],["Terminal ileum","8 to 9 per minute"]],"caption":"The fall in frequency along the small intestine favors net aboral movement."},{"type":"p","text":"Peristalsis is a local reflex of the enteric nervous system, sometimes called the law of the gut. Distension stimulates mucosal enterochromaffin cells to release serotonin and activates intrinsic sensory neurons, which drive myenteric interneurons that send excitation orally and inhibition aborally. Above the bolus, excitatory motor neurons release acetylcholine and substance P and the circular muscle contracts; below it, inhibitory motor neurons release VIP and nitric oxide and the circular muscle relaxes. Because the circuit is intrinsic, peristalsis continues after vagotomy, but it fails wherever myenteric ganglia are missing."},{"type":"flow","title":"The peristaltic reflex","steps":["Bolus stretches the gut wall","Enterochromaffin cells release serotonin; intrinsic sensory neurons fire","Myenteric interneurons signal up and down the gut","Above bolus: ACh and substance P contract circular muscle","Below bolus: VIP and nitric oxide relax circular muscle","Bolus moves aborally and the reflex repeats"],"caption":"Peristalsis needs only the myenteric plexus; extrinsic nerves adjust its strength."},{"type":"p","text":"Between meals the stomach and small intestine switch to the migrating motor complex (MMC), which recurs about every 90 to 120 minutes. Phase I is quiescence, phase II brings irregular contractions, and phase III is a few minutes of intense contractions at the maximal slow wave rate that moves from the stomach or duodenum to the ileum. The pylorus opens widely during phase III, so indigestible solids, debris, and bacteria are swept onward. Motilin from M cells of the duodenum and jejunum rises cyclically during fasting and initiates gastric phase III. Eating suppresses motilin and replaces the MMC with mixing contractions. Erythromycin binds motilin receptors, which is why it provokes strong gastric contractions."},{"type":"p","text":"Loss of the MMC, as in diabetic autonomic neuropathy or systemic sclerosis, lets bacteria accumulate in the small intestine (small intestinal bacterial overgrowth). In Hirschsprung disease, neural crest cells fail to colonize the rectum and a variable length of distal colon, so the aganglionic segment has no inhibitory neurons, stays contracted, and obstructs the dilated bowel above it."},{"type":"stop","predict":"After a truncal vagotomy, does peristalsis in the small intestine continue?","explain":"Yes. The peristaltic reflex is built from intrinsic sensory neurons, interneurons, and motor neurons within the myenteric plexus. Vagotomy weakens extrinsic modulation and abolishes receptive relaxation of the stomach, but the local circuit still contracts above and relaxes below a bolus. Peristalsis fails only where the plexus itself is absent, as in Hirschsprung disease.","check":"The paragraph on the peristaltic reflex and the Hirschsprung paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-06-01-C03","obj":"GI-6.3","title":"Gastric acid secretion and its regulation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Parietal (oxyntic) cells in the glands of the gastric body and fundus secrete hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor. Inside the cell, carbonic anhydrase converts CO2 and water to H+ and HCO3-. The H+/K+ ATPase pumps H+ into the lumen in exchange for K+, while HCO3- leaves across the basolateral membrane in exchange for Cl-, producing the alkaline tide in gastric venous blood after a meal. Cl- and K+ leave through apical channels, so K+ recycles and HCl accumulates. At rest the pumps sit in cytoplasmic tubulovesicles; stimulation inserts them into the apical canalicular membrane, making the pump the final common step for every stimulus."},{"type":"table","title":"Regulators of the parietal cell","cols":["Signal","Source","Receptor and messenger","Effect"],"rows":[["Acetylcholine","Vagal postganglionic enteric neurons","M3, Gq, IP3 and Ca2+","Stimulates parietal cells and ECL cells"],["Gastrin","G cells of the antrum","CCK-B (CCK2), Gq, Ca2+","Stimulates parietal cells weakly and ECL histamine release strongly"],["Histamine","Enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells","H2, Gs, cAMP","Strong stimulation; potentiates the other two"],["Somatostatin","D cells of the antrum and body","Somatostatin receptor, Gi","Inhibits parietal, G, and ECL cells"]],"caption":"Three stimulants converge on one pump; somatostatin opposes all three."},{"type":"p","text":"Histamine signals through cAMP while acetylcholine and gastrin signal through Ca2+, so together they produce more acid than the sum of their separate effects (potentiation). Much of gastrin's action is indirect, through ECL histamine. Potentiation explains why an H2 receptor antagonist also reduces acid secreted in response to acetylcholine and gastrin, whereas blocking the H+/K+ ATPase stops acid whatever the stimulus."},{"type":"p","text":"Antral D cells sense luminal acid. When antral pH falls below about 3, they release somatostatin, which inhibits gastrin release and acid secretion once the meal's buffering capacity is used up. The vagus pushes the other way: it stimulates G cells through gastrin-releasing peptide (GRP) and inhibits D cells. Because the G cell transmitter is GRP rather than acetylcholine, atropine does not block vagally driven gastrin release."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Somatostatin feedback on acid secretion","steps":["Food buffers acid and antral pH rises","G cells release gastrin","ECL histamine and parietal cells increase HCl","Antral pH falls below about 3","D cells release somatostatin","Gastrin and acid secretion fall"],"caption":"Negative feedback through antral pH limits acid output once buffering is exhausted."},{"type":"table","title":"Phases of gastric acid secretion","cols":["Phase","Trigger","Main mediators","Share of meal response"],"rows":[["Cephalic","Sight, smell, taste, chewing, thought of food","Vagus: ACh to parietal and ECL cells, GRP to G cells","About 30%"],["Gastric","Distension; peptides and amino acids in the stomach","Vagovagal and local reflexes; gastrin","About 60%"],["Intestinal","Protein products in the duodenum stimulate slightly; acid, fat, and hyperosmolar chyme inhibit","Duodenal gastrin; inhibition by secretin, GIP, and the enterogastric reflex","About 10%"]],"caption":"Each phase is named for where the signal starts; the intestinal phase is mainly inhibitory."},{"type":"p","text":"Feedback explains two opposite causes of high gastrin. A gastrinoma (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome) secretes gastrin autonomously, so acid stays high despite low pH; the result is multiple or distal duodenal and jejunal ulcers, and diarrhea with steatorrhea because acid inactivates pancreatic lipase and precipitates bile salts. In autoimmune atrophic gastritis, parietal cells are destroyed, acid is absent, D cells are no longer stimulated, and gastrin rises steeply, which also drives ECL cell hyperplasia."},{"type":"stop","predict":"How does fasting serum gastrin change when parietal cell acid secretion is suppressed for months?","explain":"It rises. Without acid, antral pH stays high, D cells release little somatostatin, and G cells are released from inhibition. The same feedback explains the very high gastrin of atrophic gastritis, whereas in gastrinoma the gastrin is high while acid is also high.","check":"The somatostatin feedback cycle and the final paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-06-01-C04","obj":"GI-6.4","title":"Gut hormones and the response to a meal","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Enteroendocrine cells are scattered among the epithelial cells of the stomach and small intestine. Their apical surfaces sample the lumen, and their basal granules release hormone into the blood when they detect a particular nutrient, acid, stretch, or neural signal. Each hormone therefore reports one feature of the chyme, and its actions correct that feature: secretin answers acid, CCK answers fat and protein, and GIP answers absorbed fuel."},{"type":"table","title":"The five classic gut hormones","cols":["Hormone","Cell and site","Main stimuli","Main actions"],"rows":[["Gastrin","G cells, gastric antrum (also duodenum)","Peptides and amino acids, gastric distension, vagal GRP; inhibited by antral pH below about 3","Gastric acid secretion (directly and through ECL histamine), growth of the gastric mucosa"],["Cholecystokinin (CCK)","I cells, duodenum and jejunum","Fatty acids, monoglycerides, amino acids, and small peptides","Pancreatic enzyme secretion, gallbladder contraction, sphincter of Oddi relaxation, slower gastric emptying, satiety"],["Secretin","S cells, duodenum","Duodenal pH below about 4.5; fatty acids","Pancreatic and biliary HCO3- secretion; inhibits gastrin and gastric acid"],["Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP)","K cells, duodenum and jejunum","Glucose, fatty acids, amino acids","Insulin release when glucose is high (incretin); reduces gastric acid at high levels"],["Motilin","M cells, duodenum and jejunum","Fasting, released in cycles; suppressed by eating","Initiates phase III of the migrating motor complex"]],"caption":"Source cell letters: G for gastrin, I for CCK, S for secretin, K for GIP, M for motilin."},{"type":"p","text":"The hormones fall into structural families. Gastrin and CCK share the same five C-terminal amino acids. The CCK-B receptor binds both about equally, which is how gastrin acts on parietal and ECL cells, whereas the CCK-A receptor, which mediates gallbladder contraction and pancreatic secretion, strongly prefers CCK. Secretin, GIP, VIP, and glucagon form a second family."},{"type":"p","text":"Secretin acts on pancreatic duct cells through cAMP, opening CFTR Cl- channels; luminal Cl- then exchanges for HCO3-, which pours into the duct. The response is proportional to the acid load because secretin release continues until duodenal pH rises. CCK stimulates acinar enzyme secretion, partly through vagovagal reflexes, and CCK and secretin potentiate each other on the pancreas. GIP explains why oral glucose releases more insulin than the same glucose given intravenously: GIP and GLP-1 (from L cells) amplify insulin release only when glucose is high."},{"type":"example","title":"Predicting the response to an acidic, fatty meal","text":"Acidic chyme rich in fat enters the duodenum. First, duodenal pH below about 4.5 stimulates S cells: secretin rises, pancreatic and biliary bicarbonate neutralize the acid, and secretin inhibits gastrin. Second, fatty acids and monoglycerides stimulate I cells: CCK rises, enzymes are secreted, the gallbladder contracts, the sphincter of Oddi relaxes, and gastric emptying slows. Third, fatty acids and any glucose stimulate K cells: GIP rises and enhances insulin release. Fourth, gastrin and acid fall, because secretin inhibits G cells, antral acid releases somatostatin, and the enterogastric reflex adds neural inhibition. Fifth, feeding suppresses motilin, so the MMC stops. Net effect: the duodenum slows delivery from the stomach and neutralizes and digests what has already arrived."},{"type":"p","text":"Measured hormone levels follow from the same logic. Excess gastrin with high acid points to a gastrinoma; in that tumor, intravenous secretin paradoxically raises gastrin, the reverse of its effect on normal G cells. Loss of duodenal mucosa in celiac disease reduces secretin and CCK release, which weakens pancreatic and gallbladder responses and adds to malabsorption."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-06-01-C05","obj":"GI-6.5","title":"Digestion and absorption of carbohydrate, protein, and fat","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Dietary starch is amylose (straight alpha 1,4 glucose chains) and amylopectin (alpha 1,4 chains with alpha 1,6 branches). Salivary alpha-amylase starts digestion but is inactivated by gastric acid once it mixes with chyme, so pancreatic alpha-amylase does most of the work in the duodenum. Amylase cleaves only internal alpha 1,4 bonds, leaving maltose, maltotriose, and alpha-limit dextrins that retain the branch points; it cannot cleave the beta 1,4 bonds of cellulose, so fiber reaches the colon intact. Brush border enzymes finish the job next to the transporters: lactase splits lactose, sucrase-isomaltase splits sucrose and the alpha 1,6 bonds of limit dextrins, and maltase-glucoamylase splits maltose and maltotriose. Glucose and galactose enter by SGLT1, which couples them to two Na+ ions using the gradient made by the basolateral Na+/K+ ATPase; fructose enters by facilitated diffusion through GLUT5; all three leave through GLUT2."},{"type":"p","text":"Protein digestion begins with pepsin, formed from chief cell pepsinogen in acid and active at pH about 2 to 3, but it is not essential. The decisive step is in the duodenum: enteropeptidase (enterokinase), anchored in the brush border, converts trypsinogen to trypsin. Trypsin then activates more trypsinogen and the other zymogens: chymotrypsinogen, proelastase, procarboxypeptidases, prophospholipase A2, and procolipase. Keeping the activator on the intestinal surface, along with storage as zymogens and trypsin inhibitor (SPINK1) in acinar cells, protects the pancreas from digesting itself; premature trypsin activation underlies pancreatitis. Brush border peptidases produce amino acids and di- and tripeptides. Peptides enter by the H+-coupled transporter PepT1 and amino acids by Na+-dependent carriers, and cytoplasmic peptidases release amino acids to the portal blood."},{"type":"p","text":"Fat is mostly triglyceride, and the problem is that lipase works only at an oil-water interface. Gastric churning and bile salts, which are amphipathic, emulsify fat into small droplets. Bile salts on the droplet surface would displace pancreatic lipase, so colipase anchors lipase to the surface. Lipase removes the fatty acids at positions 1 and 3, leaving 2-monoglyceride; cholesterol esterase and phospholipase A2 digest the other lipids. Long-chain fatty acids reach the blood by lymph, while medium-chain fatty acids enter portal blood directly."},{"type":"flow","title":"Digestion and absorption of long-chain fat","steps":["Churning and bile salts emulsify fat into small droplets","Colipase anchors pancreatic lipase to the droplet surface","Lipase releases 2-monoglyceride and two free fatty acids","Products join bile salts in mixed micelles","Micelles cross the unstirred water layer; lipids enter enterocyte","Smooth ER rebuilds triglyceride; MTP loads it onto apoB-48","Chylomicrons enter lacteals and reach blood by thoracic duct","Bile salts are reabsorbed in terminal ileum"],"caption":"Bile salts carry lipid to the brush border but are absorbed separately in the ileum and recycled by the liver."},{"type":"table","title":"Predicting malabsorption from the missing step","cols":["Defect","Mechanism","Result"],"rows":[["Lactase deficiency","Lactose remains in the lumen and is fermented by colonic bacteria","Osmotic diarrhea, bloating, high breath hydrogen, acidic stool; fat absorption normal"],["Pancreatic insufficiency (chronic pancreatitis, cystic fibrosis)","Loss of lipase, colipase, proteases, and amylase","Prominent steatorrhea, deficiency of vitamins A, D, E, and K; D-xylose absorption normal"],["Enteropeptidase deficiency","Trypsinogen is not activated, so no zymogen cascade","Protein malabsorption, hypoproteinemia, edema, failure to thrive"],["Terminal ileal resection or ileal Crohn disease","Bile salts lost to the colon; pool shrinks","Fat malabsorption, bile acid diarrhea, vitamin B12 deficiency"],["Abetalipoproteinemia","MTP deficiency prevents chylomicron assembly","Lipid-laden enterocytes, steatorrhea, fat-soluble vitamin deficiency, acanthocytes"],["Celiac disease","Villous atrophy removes brush border enzymes and surface","Global malabsorption; D-xylose absorption low"]],"caption":"D-xylose needs no digestion, so it separates mucosal disease from pancreatic insufficiency."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-06-01-C06","obj":"GI-6.6","title":"Defecation and the gastrocolic reflex","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The rectum is usually empty until a mass movement, a strong propulsive contraction of a long colonic segment that occurs a few times a day, pushes feces into it. Two sphincters keep the anal canal closed. The internal anal sphincter is a thickening of the circular smooth muscle; it is tonically contracted, provides most of the resting anal pressure, and is not under voluntary control. The external anal sphincter is skeletal muscle supplied by the inferior rectal branches of the pudendal nerve (S2 to S4); together with the puborectalis sling, which holds the anorectal angle, it is under voluntary control."},{"type":"p","text":"Rectal distension starts two reflexes. The intrinsic myenteric reflex sends peristaltic waves through the descending and sigmoid colon and relaxes the internal sphincter (the rectoanal inhibitory reflex), but it is weak alone. A parasympathetic reflex through the sacral cord amplifies it: afferents enter the cord and pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2 to S4) return to strengthen colonic contraction and internal sphincter relaxation. At the same time the sensation of rectal filling reaches the cortex."},{"type":"flow","title":"The defecation reflex","steps":["Mass movement fills and stretches the rectum","Myenteric reflex relaxes internal sphincter; urge is felt","Sacral parasympathetic reflex (pelvic nerves, S2 to S4) amplifies contraction","Cortex chooses: keep external sphincter closed or relax it","If delayed, rectum accommodates and urge fades","If allowed, puborectalis relaxes and Valsalva expels stool"],"caption":"The internal sphincter relaxes by reflex; the decision is made at the external sphincter."},{"type":"p","text":"If the time is not right, voluntary contraction of the external sphincter and puborectalis holds stool back; the rectal wall relaxes to accommodate the volume, the internal sphincter regains tone, and the urge fades until the next mass movement. When defecation is chosen, both skeletal muscles relax, the anorectal angle straightens, and a Valsalva maneuver raises intra-abdominal pressure."},{"type":"p","text":"In the gastrocolic reflex, distension of the stomach after a meal increases colonic motility and mass movements within minutes, through extrinsic autonomic nerves and enteric pathways, with gastrin and CCK possibly contributing. It explains the urge to defecate soon after eating, especially after breakfast."},{"type":"table","title":"Predicting incontinence from the lost element","cols":["Lost element","Mechanism","Result"],"rows":[["Internal sphincter (surgical division, obstetric tear)","Low resting pressure","Passive leakage of stool or mucus at rest, often without awareness"],["External sphincter or pudendal nerve (obstetric injury, stretch neuropathy)","Weak voluntary squeeze","Urge incontinence: stool cannot be deferred once the rectum fills"],["Rectal sensation (diabetic neuropathy)","Filling not perceived, so the external sphincter is not contracted in time","Leakage without an urge"],["Spinal cord above the sacral segments","Sacral reflex intact, cortical control lost","Reflex emptying when the rectum fills, with no voluntary control"],["Sacral cord, cauda equina, or pelvic nerves","Reflex arc lost; external sphincter flaccid","Weak propulsion, retention with overflow leakage"],["Rectal compliance (proctitis, radiation)","Small, stiff reservoir","Urgency and frequency"]],"caption":"The internal sphincter guards against passive leakage, the external sphincter against urgency."},{"type":"stop","predict":"After a pudendal nerve stretch injury in childbirth, will the internal sphincter still relax when the rectum fills, and what symptom follows?","explain":"Yes. The rectoanal inhibitory reflex is enteric and the amplifying reflex uses pelvic splanchnic nerves, not the pudendal nerve. The internal sphincter therefore relaxes normally, but the weakened external sphincter cannot hold stool back, so she has urge incontinence.","check":"The two reflexes and the incontinence table."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-06-01-C07","obj":"GI-6.7","title":"Vomiting and the metabolic alkalosis it causes","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Vomiting is a coordinated motor program that empties the upper gut. It is organized by the vomiting center, a network in the dorsal medulla centered on the nucleus tractus solitarius and adjacent reticular formation rather than a single nucleus. The chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) lies in the area postrema in the caudal floor of the fourth ventricle. Its fenestrated capillaries leave it outside the blood-brain barrier, so it detects drugs, toxins, and uremic solutes that most neurons cannot reach and relays them to the vomiting center."},{"type":"table","title":"Inputs to the vomiting center","cols":["Input","Pathway","Typical triggers","Key receptors"],"rows":[["Chemoreceptor trigger zone","Area postrema to nucleus tractus solitarius","Opioids, chemotherapy, toxins, uremia","D2, 5-HT3, NK1"],["Gastrointestinal tract","Vagal afferents; serotonin from enterochromaffin cells","Mucosal irritation, distension, obstruction, chemotherapy","5-HT3"],["Vestibular system","Vestibular nuclei to the medulla","Motion sickness","H1, muscarinic"],["Cortex and limbic system","Descending pathways","Pain, smells, sights, anticipation","Several"]],"caption":"Blood-borne emetics act at the CTZ, gut irritants mainly through vagal 5-HT3 afferents."},{"type":"flow","title":"The act of vomiting","steps":["Nausea with salivation, sweating, pallor, and tachycardia","Retrograde contraction returns small bowel contents to stomach","Deep inspiration; glottis closes; soft palate seals nasopharynx","LES and gastric body relax","Diaphragm and abdominal muscles contract forcefully","Gastric contents are expelled through the open upper sphincter"],"caption":"Airway protection mirrors swallowing; the force comes from abdominal and diaphragmatic muscle, not the stomach."},{"type":"p","text":"For every H+ the parietal cell secretes, one HCO3- enters the blood. Normally that bicarbonate is matched when acid entering the duodenum triggers secretin and pancreatic HCO3- secretion. When gastric HCl is vomited, the pancreas is not stimulated, the bicarbonate stays in the plasma, and Cl- is lost: this generates a hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis. Gastric fluid contains little K+ (roughly 5 to 10 mEq/L), so the vomitus accounts for only a small part of the K+ deficit."},{"type":"p","text":"A healthy kidney would excrete the extra bicarbonate quickly; vomiting maintains the alkalosis through three changes. Volume depletion activates renin, angiotensin II, and aldosterone. Angiotensin II increases proximal HCO3- reabsorption through the Na+/H+ exchanger, and aldosterone increases Na+ reabsorption by principal cells, making the lumen more negative and increasing K+ secretion by principal cells and H+ secretion by alpha-intercalated cells. Chloride depletion limits bicarbonate secretion by the pendrin Cl-/HCO3- exchanger of beta-intercalated cells. Hypokalemia shifts H+ into cells and increases H+ secretion and ammoniagenesis. The resulting urine Cl- is low, often below about 20 mEq/L, and late in the course the urine can be paradoxically acidic. Respiratory compensation by hypoventilation raises PaCO2 by about 0.7 mmHg for each 1 mEq/L rise in HCO3-. Restoring volume and chloride removes the stimulus, so this alkalosis is called saline-responsive."},{"type":"flow","title":"Generation and maintenance of the alkalosis","steps":["Vomiting removes HCl from the stomach","Retained HCO3- raises pH; plasma Cl- falls","Volume loss activates renin, angiotensin II, and aldosterone","Aldosterone increases urinary K+ and H+ loss","Hypokalemia and low Cl- sustain HCO3- reabsorption","Alkalosis persists until volume and Cl- are restored"],"caption":"Loss of acid starts the alkalosis; the kidney's response to volume and chloride loss keeps it going."},{"type":"example","title":"Reading the electrolytes after prolonged vomiting","text":"After four days of vomiting: Na+ 132, K+ 2.9, Cl- 86, HCO3- 34 mEq/L, arterial pH 7.48, PaCO2 47 mmHg, urine Cl- 8 mEq/L. pH above 7.45 with HCO3- above 26 mEq/L is metabolic alkalosis. Expected PaCO2 is 40 + 0.7 x (34 - 24), or 47 mmHg, so compensation is appropriate. The anion gap is 132 - (86 + 34) = 12 mEq/L, normal. Low plasma and urine Cl- mark chloride and volume loss, a saline-responsive pattern; mineralocorticoid excess would give high urine Cl-. The low K+ reflects renal wasting driven by aldosterone. Diarrhea gives the opposite: bicarbonate loss and a normal anion gap metabolic acidosis."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and embryology set the wiring. Chewing and swallowing depend on first arch muscles and the brainstem nuclei of CN V, IX, X, and XII, so a medullary stroke impairs swallowing while sparing the intrinsic motility of the stomach and intestine. Neural crest cells form the enteric plexuses, so their failure to migrate (Hirschsprung disease) or their destruction (achalasia, Chagas disease) removes inhibitory neurons and leaves smooth muscle unable to relax. Defecation depends on the sacral cord, so the level of a spinal lesion predicts reflex or areflexic bowel function."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology, biochemistry, and pathology then explain the outputs. G protein messengers explain potentiation at the parietal cell, and negative feedback through somatostatin explains why gastrin is high both in gastrinoma and in atrophic gastritis. Enzyme biochemistry explains which nutrient fails first in each malabsorption, and lipoprotein assembly links fat absorption to chylomicron metabolism. Renal physiology decides whether vomiting produces a lasting alkalosis. The limits are that many values here (slow wave rates, phase percentages, pH thresholds) are approximate, and that hormone actions overlap, so board questions test the dominant effect rather than every effect."}],"summary":["All muscles of mastication are supplied by CN V3; the oral phase of swallowing is voluntary, and the pharyngeal and esophageal phases are a medullary reflex that protects the airway.","Vagal inhibitory neurons release nitric oxide and VIP to relax the LES and proximal stomach; their loss causes achalasia.","Interstitial cells of Cajal set slow wave frequency (about 3 per minute in the stomach, 12 in the duodenum); contraction needs Ca2+ spike potentials on the crests.","Peristalsis is a myenteric reflex: ACh and substance P contract above the bolus, VIP and nitric oxide relax below it.","Motilin drives the fasting MMC about every 90 to 120 minutes; feeding stops it, and its loss favors bacterial overgrowth.","ACh (M3, Ca2+), gastrin (CCK-B, Ca2+, mostly via ECL histamine), and histamine (H2, cAMP) potentiate one another at the parietal cell; the H+/K+ ATPase is the final step and somatostatin is the brake.","Acid and fat in the duodenum raise secretin, CCK, and GIP, lower gastrin, acid, and motilin, and slow gastric emptying.","Enteropeptidase activates trypsinogen, trypsin activates the other zymogens, and lipase needs colipase and bile salt micelles; chylomicrons leave through lacteals.","Rectal distension relaxes the internal sphincter by reflex; the pudendal nerve gives voluntary control of the external sphincter, so internal sphincter loss causes passive leakage and external sphincter loss causes urge incontinence.","Prolonged vomiting causes hypochloremic hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis, generated by HCl loss and maintained by volume depletion, aldosterone, hypokalemia, and chloride depletion, with low urine Cl-."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Gastrointestinal physiology: general principles of motility, propulsion and mixing of food, and secretory functions of the alimentary tract"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Digestion and absorption in the gastrointestinal tract; physiology of gastrointestinal disorders"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Gastrointestinal physiology: gastric function, pancreatic secretion, nutrient digestion and absorption"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Digestion of dietary carbohydrates, lipids, and proteins"},{"book":"Moore, Clinically Oriented Anatomy","where":"Head: muscles of mastication; Pelvis and perineum: anal canal and anal sphincters"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Gastrointestinal disease; acid-base disorders"}]},"GI-7":{"unit":"GI-7","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-07","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-07-01","title":"Absorption of the Products of Digestion","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows the end products of digestion across the intestinal wall: monosaccharides, amino acids and small peptides, fats, and the vitamins and minerals that depend on one segment of bowel. It then covers the enterohepatic circulation of bile acids and the three mechanisms of diarrhea. Board questions rarely ask for a transporter name alone. They describe a missing carrier, a diseased or resected segment, or a toxin, and ask you to predict the stool, the deficiency, or the laboratory pattern. If you know which membrane each carrier sits on, what gradient drives it, and where along the gut it is found, you can reason through nearly all of them.","prerequisites":["Membrane transport: primary and secondary active transport, facilitated diffusion, and the Na+/K+-ATPase","Histology of the small intestine: villi, crypts, enterocytes, brush border, and lacteals (GI-2)","Luminal digestion by pancreatic enzymes and the secretion of bile (GI-6)","G protein signaling through Gs, adenylyl cyclase, cAMP, and protein kinase A","Lipoprotein structure and the roles of apoB-48 and apoB-100"],"connections":[{"to":"Digestive biochemistry and dietary requirements (GI-8)","how":"Niacin synthesis from tryptophan, fat-soluble vitamin roles, and B12 and folate metabolism explain the deficiencies that follow failed absorption."},{"to":"Deficiency and malabsorption conditions (GI-13-E)","how":"Celiac disease, ileal Crohn disease, and short bowel syndrome are predicted by where each nutrient is absorbed."},{"to":"Gallbladder diseases (GI-13-D)","how":"Loss of ileal bile acid recycling shrinks the bile acid pool and favors cholesterol supersaturation of bile."},{"to":"Gastrointestinal pathogens (GI-12)","how":"Cholera toxin and enterotoxigenic E. coli toxins hijack the crypt cell secretory pathway, while invasive organisms cause inflammatory diarrhea."},{"to":"Renal physiology","how":"The proximal tubule uses the same sodium-coupled glucose and amino acid carriers, so Hartnup disease and cystinuria affect both gut and kidney."}],"terms":[{"term":"Secondary active transport","def":"Uphill movement of one solute driven by the downhill movement of another, usually Na+ or H+, whose gradient is maintained by an ATP-using pump."},{"term":"SGLT1","def":"The apical sodium-glucose cotransporter of enterocytes that carries glucose or galactose into the cell with two Na+ ions."},{"term":"PepT1","def":"The apical H+-coupled carrier that absorbs dipeptides and tripeptides."},{"term":"Unstirred water layer","def":"A thin, poorly mixed layer of fluid next to the brush border that slows diffusion of hydrophobic molecules to the cell surface."},{"term":"Mixed micelle","def":"A small aggregate of bile acids, phospholipid, monoglycerides, fatty acids, cholesterol, and fat-soluble vitamins that carries lipids across the unstirred layer."},{"term":"Critical micellar concentration","def":"The bile acid concentration above which bile acids aggregate into micelles."},{"term":"Chylomicron","def":"The large triglyceride-rich lipoprotein made by enterocytes around apoB-48 and secreted into lymph."},{"term":"Microsomal triglyceride transfer protein (MTP)","def":"The endoplasmic reticulum protein that loads lipid onto apoB to assemble chylomicrons in the gut and VLDL in the liver."},{"term":"Intrinsic factor","def":"A glycoprotein secreted by gastric parietal cells that binds vitamin B12 and is required for its uptake in the terminal ileum."},{"term":"Enterohepatic circulation","def":"The cycle in which bile acids secreted in bile are reabsorbed from the intestine, returned in portal blood, and secreted again by the liver."},{"term":"CFTR","def":"The cAMP-regulated apical chloride channel through which crypt cells secrete Cl- into the intestinal lumen."},{"term":"Stool osmotic gap","def":"290 mOsm/kg minus twice the sum of stool Na+ and K+, an estimate of stool osmolality not explained by electrolytes."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-07-01-C01","obj":"GI-7.1","title":"Monosaccharide absorption and the basis of oral rehydration","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Salivary and pancreatic amylase reduce starch to maltose, maltotriose, and limit dextrins, and the brush border enzymes (lactase, sucrase-isomaltase, and maltase-glucoamylase) finish the job, because enterocytes absorb only monosaccharides: glucose, galactose, and fructose. Each sugar must cross two membranes in series, the apical (luminal) membrane and the basolateral membrane that faces the blood, and the carriers on the two sides differ."},{"type":"p","text":"The basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase pumps three Na+ out for two K+ in, which keeps cytosolic Na+ near 15 mmol/L while luminal Na+ is near 140 mmol/L, and leaves the cell interior negative. SGLT1 on the apical membrane couples the entry of one glucose or galactose molecule to two Na+ ions moving down this electrochemical gradient. The coupling lets the enterocyte take up glucose against its concentration gradient, so absorption continues even when little glucose is left in the lumen. This is secondary active transport: the pump spends the ATP, and SGLT1 spends the gradient. Fructose enters through GLUT5, a facilitated diffusion carrier that needs no Na+ and moves fructose only down its gradient, so fructose uptake is slower and saturable and a large fructose load can reach the colon. All three sugars leave through GLUT2 on the basolateral membrane by facilitated diffusion into portal blood."},{"type":"table","title":"Monosaccharide carriers of the enterocyte","cols":["Carrier","Membrane","Substrates","Mechanism","Inherited loss"],"rows":[["SGLT1","Apical","Glucose, galactose","Secondary active, 2 Na+ per sugar","Glucose-galactose malabsorption: neonatal osmotic diarrhea, fructose tolerated"],["GLUT5","Apical","Fructose","Facilitated diffusion, Na+ independent","No classic syndrome; limited capacity explains fructose malabsorption"],["GLUT2","Basolateral","Glucose, galactose, fructose","Facilitated diffusion to portal blood","Fanconi-Bickel syndrome (mainly hepatic and renal disease)"],["Na+/K+-ATPase","Basolateral","3 Na+ out, 2 K+ in","Primary active (ATP)","Not applicable; it powers every Na+-coupled carrier"]],"caption":"Sugars enter on the apical side by SGLT1 or GLUT5 and leave on the basolateral side by GLUT2; the pump creates the Na+ gradient SGLT1 uses."},{"type":"p","text":"Water in the small intestine is never pumped; it follows solute. When Na+ and glucose are absorbed together, they raise osmolality in the lateral intercellular spaces below the tight junctions, and water follows across the leaky tight junctions and through the cells. Oral rehydration solution uses this coupling. In cholera, the toxin drives Cl- secretion through CFTR and suppresses the electroneutral NaCl absorption of villus cells (C06), but it does not touch SGLT1. Glucose given with Na+ therefore opens an absorptive route for Na+ that the toxin cannot close, and water absorption follows. Each ingredient is needed: without Na+, SGLT1 cannot move glucose, and without glucose, the main Na+ absorptive pathway left is the one the toxin has inhibited. The proportions also matter, because glucose beyond what SGLT1 can carry stays in the lumen as an osmotic load and adds to the stool volume, so effective solutions keep glucose and Na+ at similar molar concentrations and the total osmolarity at or below that of plasma."},{"type":"p","text":"Glucose-galactose malabsorption, the autosomal recessive loss of SGLT1, confirms the map: newborns have severe watery diarrhea with glucose, galactose, or lactose, yet tolerate fructose because GLUT5 and GLUT2 still work. Lactase deficiency differs in kind, since it is a failure of digestion: the disaccharide is never split, so the carriers have nothing to absorb."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Would a solution of fructose and NaCl rehydrate a patient with cholera as well as a solution of glucose and NaCl?","explain":"No. GLUT5 moves fructose without Na+, so fructose uptake does not carry Na+ or water into the cell. The toxin has suppressed electroneutral NaCl absorption, so only Na+-coupled glucose (or galactose) uptake through SGLT1 restores net Na+ and water absorption.","check":"The carrier table and the paragraph on oral rehydration."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-07-01-C02","obj":"GI-7.2","title":"Absorption of amino acids and small peptides; Hartnup disease","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Pepsin begins protein digestion in the stomach, but pancreatic proteases do most of the work. Enteropeptidase (enterokinase) on the duodenal brush border converts trypsinogen to trypsin, which activates the other pancreatic zymogens, and brush border peptidases then trim the fragments. What reaches the apical membrane is a mixture of free amino acids and peptides of two or three residues. Larger peptides are not absorbed in nutritionally important amounts."},{"type":"p","text":"Free amino acids enter on several apical carriers, each serving a class of side chains rather than a single amino acid. Most are cotransporters driven by the same inward Na+ gradient that powers SGLT1, so they are secondary active. The main neutral amino acid carrier is B0AT1 (gene SLC6A19). An important exception is the carrier for cystine and the dibasic amino acids (lysine, arginine, ornithine), which exchanges them for neutral amino acids without Na+; its inherited loss causes cystinuria in kidney and gut. Amino acids then leave across the basolateral membrane on facilitated diffusion and exchange carriers into portal blood."},{"type":"p","text":"Dipeptides and tripeptides use a separate route. PepT1 is a single broad-specificity carrier coupled to H+ rather than Na+. The apical Na+/H+ exchanger (NHE3) keeps an acidic microclimate at the brush border, and that H+ gradient drives peptide entry, so peptide uptake is still powered indirectly by the Na+ gradient. Cytosolic peptidases split most absorbed peptides, which is why portal blood after a meal carries mainly free amino acids. Because one carrier accepts thousands of different peptides, PepT1 carries a large share of absorbed amino nitrogen and is a backup when a free amino acid carrier fails."},{"type":"table","title":"Main routes of amino acid and peptide entry","cols":["Substrate","Apical carrier","Driving force","Disease when lost"],"rows":[["Neutral amino acids (including tryptophan)","B0AT1","Na+","Hartnup disease"],["Cystine and dibasic amino acids (lysine, arginine, ornithine)","b0,+ exchanger (rBAT with b0,+AT)","Exchange for neutral amino acids, Na+ independent","Cystinuria (cystine kidney stones)"],["Acidic amino acids (glutamate, aspartate)","EAAT3","Na+ and H+, with K+ exit","Dicarboxylic aminoaciduria (rare)"],["Proline, hydroxyproline, glycine","IMINO and PAT1","Na+ with Cl-, or H+","Iminoglycinuria (benign, mainly renal)"],["Dipeptides and tripeptides","PepT1","H+, gradient kept by NHE3","No known human syndrome"]],"caption":"Free amino acids use several class-specific carriers, most Na+-coupled; small peptides share one H+-coupled carrier. The same free amino acid carriers work in the proximal tubule."},{"type":"p","text":"Hartnup disease is the autosomal recessive loss of B0AT1, which is expressed in both the small intestine and the renal proximal tubule. The renal sign is a neutral aminoaciduria: alanine, serine, threonine, valine, leucine, isoleucine, phenylalanine, tyrosine, tryptophan, histidine, glutamine, and asparagine appear in urine, while proline, glycine, and the dibasic amino acids are normal because they use other carriers. That selective pattern separates Hartnup disease from the generalized aminoaciduria of Fanconi syndrome. Unabsorbed tryptophan reaching the colon is converted by bacteria to indole compounds, which are absorbed and excreted as urinary indican."},{"type":"p","text":"The clinical findings come from tryptophan, the precursor of part of the body's niacin (NAD+) by the kynurenine pathway, at roughly 60 mg of tryptophan per 1 mg of niacin. With tryptophan lost in urine and poorly absorbed, a marginal dietary niacin intake becomes inadequate and pellagra-like findings appear: photosensitive dermatitis on sun-exposed skin, diarrhea, intermittent cerebellar ataxia, and psychiatric change. Most people with the genotype stay well, because PepT1 still absorbs tryptophan inside peptides and a niacin-adequate diet covers the shortfall; symptoms tend to appear with poor diet, illness, or growth."},{"type":"flow","title":"From B0AT1 loss to pellagra-like disease","steps":["B0AT1 absent in gut and proximal tubule","Neutral amino acids poorly absorbed and lost in urine","Less tryptophan enters the kynurenine pathway","Less niacin (NAD+) synthesized from tryptophan","Marginal dietary niacin no longer sufficient","Dermatitis, diarrhea, ataxia, psychiatric change"],"caption":"Hartnup disease is a transport defect whose symptoms are those of niacin deficiency; peptide uptake by PepT1 limits severity."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-07-01-C03","obj":"GI-7.3","title":"Fat absorption, chylomicron assembly, and abetalipoproteinemia","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Dietary fat is mostly triglyceride, which does not dissolve in water. Bile acids and phospholipid emulsify it into droplets, and pancreatic lipase, anchored at the droplet surface by colipase, removes the fatty acids at positions 1 and 3, leaving 2-monoglyceride and two free fatty acids. Cholesterol esterase and phospholipase A2 digest cholesterol esters and phospholipids."},{"type":"p","text":"These products are poorly soluble, and the brush border is covered by an unstirred water layer through which hydrophobic molecules diffuse slowly. Above their critical micellar concentration, bile acids form mixed micelles that carry monoglycerides, fatty acids, cholesterol, lysophospholipid, and the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K across this layer. Micelles are not absorbed intact. In the slightly acidic microclimate at the cell surface, fatty acids become protonated and leave the micelle, entering the cell by diffusion and by membrane proteins such as CD36 for long-chain fatty acids and NPC1L1 for cholesterol. The bile acids stay in the lumen to be reused farther down the gut and are reclaimed in the terminal ileum (C05)."},{"type":"p","text":"Inside the enterocyte, smooth endoplasmic reticulum rebuilds triglyceride from 2-monoglyceride and fatty acyl-CoA, and cholesterol is re-esterified. Re-esterification keeps free fatty acid and monoglyceride low in the cytosol, preserving the inward gradient. Microsomal triglyceride transfer protein (MTP) then loads triglyceride onto apolipoprotein B-48 to form the chylomicron. ApoB-48 is made only in the intestine: it comes from the same gene as hepatic apoB-100, but intestinal mRNA editing converts a cytidine to uridine and creates a stop codon, so the protein is 48% of full length and lacks the LDL receptor binding region. Chylomicrons pass through the Golgi and are exocytosed basolaterally. They are too large to enter blood capillaries, so they enter the central lacteal through its loose endothelial junctions and travel through the thoracic duct into the venous system at the junction of the left internal jugular and subclavian veins, bypassing the liver on first pass. Medium-chain fatty acids (about 6 to 12 carbons) are more water-soluble, are largely not re-esterified, and pass directly into portal blood bound to albumin."},{"type":"flow","title":"Dietary triglyceride from lumen to blood","steps":["Bile acids emulsify fat; lipase with colipase forms 2-monoglyceride and fatty acids","Mixed micelles cross the unstirred water layer","Lipids leave micelles and enter the enterocyte","Smooth ER re-esterifies them to triglyceride","MTP loads triglyceride onto apoB-48, forming chylomicrons","Exocytosis into the lacteal, then thoracic duct lymph","Entry into blood at the left venous angle"],"caption":"Long-chain fat needs bile acids, MTP, and apoB-48 to reach the blood; medium-chain fatty acids go straight to portal blood."},{"type":"p","text":"Abetalipoproteinemia is the autosomal recessive loss of MTP (gene MTTP). Without MTP neither the enterocyte nor the hepatocyte can assemble apoB-containing lipoproteins, so chylomicrons, VLDL, and LDL are essentially absent and plasma cholesterol and triglyceride are very low. Each feature follows from that single defect, as the table shows."},{"type":"table","title":"Abetalipoproteinemia: each finding and its mechanism","cols":["Finding","Mechanism"],"rows":[["Steatorrhea, failure to thrive, lipid-laden enterocytes on biopsy","Triglyceride enters the enterocyte but cannot leave without chylomicrons"],["Very low plasma cholesterol and triglyceride; no apoB","No chylomicrons from the gut and no VLDL (hence no LDL) from the liver"],["Acanthocytes (spiculated red cells)","Abnormal plasma lipids alter red cell membrane lipid composition"],["Ataxia, loss of position and vibration sense, areflexia","Vitamin E deficiency (spinocerebellar and dorsal column degeneration); vitamin E needs apoB lipoproteins for absorption and delivery"],["Pigmentary retinopathy","Vitamin E and vitamin A deficiency"],["Prolonged prothrombin time in some patients","Vitamin K malabsorption"]],"caption":"One missing protein, MTP, explains the gut, blood, and neurologic findings."},{"type":"stop","predict":"In a child with abetalipoproteinemia, would fat from medium-chain triglycerides be absorbed into the blood?","explain":"Largely yes. Medium-chain fatty acids enter portal blood bound to albumin without re-esterification or chylomicrons, so they do not need MTP or apoB-48.","check":"The paragraph on chylomicron assembly and the end of the fat flow figure."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-07-01-C04","obj":"GI-7.4","title":"Where iron, folate, vitamin B12, and bile acids are absorbed","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The whole small intestine absorbs, but several nutrients depend on carriers concentrated in one segment. The duodenum takes up most iron and most actively transported calcium, the duodenum and proximal jejunum take up folate, the jejunum (with the tallest villi and largest surface) absorbs most sugar, amino acid, and fat, and the terminal ileum is the only site of vitamin B12 uptake with intrinsic factor and of active bile acid reabsorption. The ileum can take over bulk absorption after jejunal loss, but no segment can replace ileal B12 and bile acid uptake, so the site of disease predicts the deficiency."},{"type":"p","text":"Iron absorption is tied to the duodenum by its chemistry. Nonheme iron is mostly ferric (Fe3+) and must be reduced to ferrous (Fe2+) by duodenal cytochrome b and dietary ascorbate before DMT1 (divalent metal transporter 1) carries it in with H+. Gastric acid keeps iron soluble, and the acidic microclimate of the proximal duodenum supplies the H+ for DMT1. Iron leaves through ferroportin, is oxidized by hephaestin, and binds transferrin; hepcidin from the liver degrades ferroportin, so body iron is controlled at this exit step. Active, calcitriol-induced calcium uptake is likewise concentrated in the duodenum, while passive paracellular calcium absorption continues along the jejunum and ileum."},{"type":"p","text":"Food folate is a polyglutamate. Brush border folate conjugase (glutamate carboxypeptidase II) trims it to the monoglutamate, which the proton-coupled folate transporter carries in best at the slightly acidic pH of the duodenum and proximal jejunum. Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) follows the longest route, shown below, and its final receptor, cubilin with amnionless, is found only on terminal ileal enterocytes. Body stores of about 2 to 5 mg, mostly in the liver, cover daily losses for several years, so B12 deficiency appears long after the ileum is lost."},{"type":"flow","title":"Vitamin B12 from food to tissues","steps":["Acid and pepsin free B12 from food protein","B12 binds haptocorrin (R binder) in the stomach","Pancreatic proteases digest haptocorrin in the duodenum","Freed B12 binds intrinsic factor from parietal cells","Cubilin-amnionless in terminal ileum endocytoses the complex","B12 enters blood bound to transcobalamin"],"caption":"Absorption needs stomach, pancreas, and terminal ileum, so B12 deficiency can start at any of the three."},{"type":"table","title":"Absorption sites and the deficiencies that follow their loss","cols":["Nutrient","Main site","Key apical step","Result of loss"],"rows":[["Iron","Duodenum","Fe3+ reduced to Fe2+; DMT1 with H+","Microcytic, hypochromic anemia with low ferritin"],["Calcium (active)","Duodenum","TRPV6, induced by calcitriol","Low bone mineral, secondary hyperparathyroidism"],["Folate","Duodenum and proximal jejunum","Deconjugation, then proton-coupled folate transporter","Megaloblastic anemia; high homocysteine, normal methylmalonic acid"],["Vitamin B12","Terminal ileum","Intrinsic factor-B12 taken up by cubilin-amnionless","Megaloblastic anemia; high homocysteine and methylmalonic acid; neuropathy"],["Bile acids","Terminal ileum","ASBT with Na+","Bile acid diarrhea or steatorrhea; fat-soluble vitamin loss (C05)"]],"caption":"Proximal disease costs iron, calcium, and folate; ileal disease costs B12 and bile acids."},{"type":"p","text":"Duodenal disease or bypass, as in celiac disease (most severe proximally) or a gastric bypass that diverts food past it, causes iron deficiency first, often with folate and calcium deficiency. Terminal ileal disease or resection, as in Crohn ileitis, causes B12 deficiency and bile acid loss but spares iron and folate. The two megaloblastic anemias separate biochemically: both deficiencies raise homocysteine, because both vitamins are needed to remethylate it, but only B12 deficiency raises methylmalonic acid, because B12 is the cofactor of methylmalonyl-CoA mutase. When resection removes the ileocecal valve, colonic bacteria can colonize the small bowel, consume B12, and make folate, so serum folate may be normal or high while B12 is low."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Proximal celiac disease or a 100 cm terminal ileal resection: which causes iron deficiency, and which causes B12 deficiency?","explain":"Celiac disease causes iron (and often folate) deficiency because DMT1 and folate uptake are proximal. Resection causes B12 deficiency because cubilin-amnionless is only in the terminal ileum.","check":"The absorption site table and the final paragraph."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-07-01-C05","obj":"GI-7.5","title":"Enterohepatic circulation of bile acids and the effects of ileal loss","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hepatocytes make the primary bile acids, cholic acid and chenodeoxycholic acid, from cholesterol; the rate-limiting enzyme is cholesterol 7-alpha-hydroxylase (CYP7A1). They are then conjugated with glycine or taurine. Conjugation lowers the pKa, so the bile acids stay ionized at intestinal pH and diffuse back across the jejunal membrane only poorly. That keeps them in the lumen above the critical micellar concentration while fat is absorbed, so their return needs a dedicated carrier in the terminal ileum."},{"type":"p","text":"The apical sodium-dependent bile acid transporter (ASBT) on terminal ileal enterocytes takes up conjugated bile acids with Na+. They cross the cell and leave through the basolateral organic solute transporter (OST alpha and beta) into portal blood. Hepatocytes extract most of them on first pass with another Na+-coupled carrier, NTCP, and secrete them into bile again. Colonic bacteria convert the fraction that escapes to the secondary bile acids deoxycholic and lithocholic acid, some of which is absorbed passively. Overall about 95% of secreted bile acid is reclaimed. The pool is only about 2 to 4 g but cycles several times a day, so the gut sees far more bile acid than the liver makes, and the roughly 0.5 g lost in stool each day is replaced by new synthesis."},{"type":"cycle","title":"Enterohepatic circulation of bile acids","steps":["Liver makes bile acids from cholesterol and conjugates them","Bile acids secreted in bile form micelles in the jejunum","ASBT reabsorbs about 95% in the terminal ileum","Portal blood returns bile acids to the liver","NTCP takes them up; FGF19 signal limits new synthesis"],"caption":"The pool cycles several times daily; synthesis replaces only stool losses."},{"type":"p","text":"The returning bile acids also regulate synthesis. In ileal enterocytes they activate the nuclear receptor FXR, which induces FGF19; FGF19 reaches the liver in portal blood and represses CYP7A1, and bile acids taken up by hepatocytes suppress CYP7A1 through hepatic FXR as well. When ileal return falls, this brake is released and synthesis rises, but hepatic capacity can increase only a few-fold, so it can make up for moderate losses but not large ones."},{"type":"p","text":"The effect of ileal disease therefore depends on the amount lost. Excess bile acids entering the colon, especially the dihydroxy bile acids chenodeoxycholic and deoxycholic acid, stimulate colonocyte Cl- and water secretion and speed transit, causing watery diarrhea. Once fecal loss exceeds maximal synthesis, luminal bile acid falls below the critical micellar concentration and micelles fail."},{"type":"table","title":"Limited versus extensive terminal ileal loss","cols":["Feature","Limited (less than about 100 cm)","Extensive (more than about 100 cm)"],"rows":[["Bile acid pool","Maintained by increased synthesis","Depleted; losses exceed maximal synthesis"],["Micelle formation","Adequate","Inadequate"],["Main stool problem","Watery bile acid diarrhea","Steatorrhea"],["Fat-soluble vitamins","Usually adequate","A, D, E, and K deficiency"]],"caption":"The 100 cm boundary is approximate: where synthesis can no longer match fecal loss."},{"type":"p","text":"Gallstones follow from the same shrinking pool. Bile holds cholesterol in solution only within mixed micelles and vesicles of bile acid and phosphatidylcholine. With less bile acid secreted per unit of cholesterol, bile becomes supersaturated, cholesterol crystals nucleate, and cholesterol gallstones form, more readily when gallbladder emptying is slow. With an intact colon, fat malabsorption also causes calcium oxalate kidney stones: unabsorbed fatty acids bind luminal calcium, leaving oxalate free for colonic absorption."},{"type":"example","title":"Predicting the result of two ileal resections","text":"Patient A loses 50 cm of terminal ileum; patient B loses 180 cm. Both lose cubilin-bearing ileum, so both risk B12 deficiency once stores run down. In A, fecal bile acid loss rises, FGF19 falls, and CYP7A1 activity increases enough to keep the pool and micelles adequate, so fat is absorbed, but excess bile acids reaching the colon cause watery diarrhea. In B, loss exceeds maximal synthesis, the pool shrinks, micelles fail, and steatorrhea appears with vitamin A, D, E, and K deficiency. B also has the higher risk of cholesterol gallstones and, with an intact colon, oxalate kidney stones."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-07-01-C06","obj":"GI-7.6","title":"Secretory, osmotic, and inflammatory diarrhea","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"About 8 to 9 L of fluid enters the small intestine each day and all but about 100 to 200 mL is absorbed, so a small shift from absorption (villus cells, colonocytes) toward secretion (crypt cells) causes diarrhea. Crypt cells secrete Cl- with three carriers: basolateral NKCC1 loads Na+, K+, and 2 Cl- into the cell, basolateral K+ channels recycle K+ and keep the cell negative, and the apical CFTR channel releases Cl- into the lumen when protein kinase A opens it. Na+ follows through the paracellular pathway and water follows the NaCl."},{"type":"p","text":"Secretory diarrhea comes from turning this secretion on (and absorption off) by a second messenger inside the epithelial cell. Cholera toxin is the model (figure); it also inhibits villus NaCl absorption but spares SGLT1 (C01). Enterotoxigenic E. coli heat-labile toxin acts the same way; its heat-stable toxin raises cGMP through guanylyl cyclase C, and VIP from a VIPoma raises cAMP. Because the stimulus is inside the cell, not in the food, secretion continues during fasting and electrolytes account for nearly all stool osmolality."},{"type":"flow","title":"Cholera toxin to secretory diarrhea","steps":["B subunit binds GM1 ganglioside on enterocytes","A subunit ADP-ribosylates Gs alpha, blocking its GTPase","Adenylyl cyclase stays active and cAMP rises","Protein kinase A opens CFTR in crypt cells","Cl- secreted; Na+ and water follow","Large-volume isotonic stool and volume depletion"],"caption":"Persistent Gs activation drives Cl- secretion; Na+-glucose cotransport is spared."},{"type":"p","text":"Osmotic diarrhea comes from a poorly absorbed solute that holds water in the lumen. In lactase deficiency, undigested lactose draws water into the small bowel, and colonic bacteria ferment it to short-chain fatty acids, hydrogen, and carbon dioxide, giving acidic stool (pH below about 6), bloating, and hydrogen in breath. Magnesium salts and sorbitol act the same way. Because the solute comes from what is eaten, the diarrhea stops with fasting, and stool Na+ and K+ are low because the unabsorbed solute, not electrolyte, fills stool osmolality."},{"type":"p","text":"Inflammatory diarrhea comes from mucosal injury by invasive or cytotoxin-producing organisms (Shigella, Campylobacter, Salmonella, Clostridioides difficile, Entamoeba histolytica) or by inflammatory bowel disease. Damaged epithelium absorbs less, exudes protein, blood, and leukocytes into the lumen, and releases prostaglandins and cytokines that stimulate secretion. Stools contain blood and leukocytes (or raised fecal calprotectin), with fever and tenesmus. Its osmotic gap varies with the mix of mechanisms."},{"type":"p","text":"The stool osmotic gap separates the first two. As stool passes through the colon, its osmolality equilibrates with plasma at about 290 mOsm/kg. Measured stool osmolality rises after collection as bacteria ferment, so 290 is used. Doubling stool Na+ and K+ accounts for their anions, and the gap is 290 minus 2 times (stool Na+ plus stool K+). A gap below about 50 mOsm/kg means electrolytes explain the stool, which is secretory; a gap above about 100 to 125 mOsm/kg means an unmeasured solute is present, which is osmotic."},{"type":"table","title":"Distinguishing the three types of diarrhea","cols":["Feature","Secretory","Osmotic","Inflammatory"],"rows":[["Classic causes","Cholera, ETEC, VIPoma","Lactase deficiency, magnesium, sorbitol","Shigella, Campylobacter, C. difficile, IBD"],["Stool osmotic gap","Below about 50 mOsm/kg","Above about 100 to 125 mOsm/kg","Variable"],["Effect of fasting","Continues","Stops","Often continues"],["Stool features","Large volume, watery, high Na+","Watery, low Na+; acidic with lactose","Blood, leukocytes, mucus; fever"]],"caption":"Gap and fasting separate secretory from osmotic; blood and leukocytes mark inflammatory."},{"type":"example","title":"Calculating the stool osmotic gap","text":"Stool 1 has Na+ 110 mmol/L and K+ 30 mmol/L. The sum is 140, doubled is 280, and 290 minus 280 gives a gap of 10 mOsm/kg. Electrolytes explain almost all stool osmolality, so the diarrhea is secretory, as in cholera, and will persist during a fast. Stool 2 has Na+ 25 mmol/L and K+ 35 mmol/L. The sum is 60, doubled is 120, and the gap is 170 mOsm/kg, an unmeasured solute such as lactose or magnesium, so the diarrhea is osmotic and should stop with fasting."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and histology explain where things happen: villus height, the brush border, and segment-specific carriers place iron and folate uptake proximally and B12 and bile acid uptake in the terminal ileum, and lacteals give long-chain fat a lymphatic route that bypasses the liver. Biochemistry explains why carriers matter: the Na+/K+-ATPase powers nearly every apical uptake step directly or through NHE3, mRNA editing makes apoB-48, and tryptophan feeds niacin synthesis, which is why Hartnup disease looks like pellagra. The same carriers in the proximal tubule explain why Hartnup disease and cystinuria show up in urine."},{"type":"p","text":"Microbiology and pathology show what happens when the system is pushed. Toxins turn on crypt secretion through cAMP or cGMP, invasive organisms and inflammatory bowel disease destroy absorptive surface, and resection or Crohn disease of the ileum breaks the enterohepatic circulation, leading to diarrhea, steatorrhea, gallstones, and oxalate stones. The limits of these rules matter: the 100 cm resection boundary and the osmotic gap cutoffs are approximate, inflammatory and mixed diarrheas do not fit the gap neatly, and the remaining bowel adapts over months, so findings change with time after injury."}],"summary":["SGLT1 moves glucose and galactose in with 2 Na+ (secondary active), GLUT5 lets fructose in by facilitated diffusion, and GLUT2 releases all three to portal blood.","Oral rehydration works because cholera toxin spares SGLT1, so Na+-coupled glucose uptake restores Na+ and water absorption.","Free amino acids enter mostly on Na+-coupled class-specific carriers; dipeptides and tripeptides enter on H+-coupled PepT1.","Hartnup disease is loss of the neutral amino acid carrier B0AT1 in gut and kidney, giving neutral aminoaciduria and pellagra-like findings from low tryptophan-derived niacin.","Long-chain fat needs micelles, re-esterification, MTP, and apoB-48 to leave the enterocyte as chylomicrons into lacteals; medium-chain fatty acids go directly to portal blood.","Abetalipoproteinemia (MTP loss) causes steatorrhea, absent apoB lipoproteins, acanthocytes, and vitamin E deficiency with ataxia and retinopathy.","Iron and active calcium are absorbed in the duodenum, folate in the duodenum and proximal jejunum, and B12 and bile acids only in the terminal ileum.","About 95% of bile acids are reclaimed by ileal ASBT; limited ileal loss causes bile acid diarrhea, extensive loss causes steatorrhea, and both raise the risk of cholesterol gallstones.","Secretory diarrhea has a low stool osmotic gap and continues during fasting; osmotic diarrhea has a high gap and stops with fasting; inflammatory diarrhea has blood, leukocytes, and fever."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Digestion and Absorption in the Gastrointestinal Tract"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Nutrient digestion and absorption; intestinal fluid and electrolyte movement"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Metabolism of dietary lipids; digestion of dietary proteins; niacin and Hartnup disease"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"The Gastrointestinal Tract, sections on malabsorption and diarrhea"},{"book":"Jawetz, Melnick and Adelberg's Medical Microbiology","where":"Vibrio cholerae and cholera toxin"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Gastrointestinal Disease, sections on diarrhea and malabsorption"}]},"GI-8":{"unit":"GI-8","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-08","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-08-01","title":"Digestive Biochemistry and Dietary Requirements","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter covers what the body must obtain from food and what happens when intake, absorption, or storage fails. It begins with essential amino acids, essential fatty acids, and the two forms of severe undernutrition, then follows the fat-soluble vitamins, vitamin B12, the water-soluble cofactor vitamins, and the minerals iron, zinc, and copper, and closes with vitamin A and D toxicity. A section on fed and fasting metabolism explains how the liver keeps fuel flowing between meals. Board questions usually describe a deficiency picture and ask for the missing nutrient, the enzyme it serves, or the laboratory value that separates two look-alikes. Linking each finding to the enzyme or protein that fails is the skill that answers them.","prerequisites":["Glycolysis, the TCA cycle, and oxidative phosphorylation","Lipid digestion: pancreatic lipase, bile salt micelles, and chylomicrons (GI-6, GI-7)","Gastric parietal cell secretion and pancreatic exocrine enzymes (GI-6)","Insulin and glucagon signaling through receptor tyrosine kinase and cAMP pathways","Collagen structure and the steps of collagen synthesis"],"connections":[{"to":"Products of digestion (GI-7)","how":"Micelle formation and chylomicron assembly are the steps that, when lost, take vitamins A, D, E, and K down together."},{"to":"Liver functions (GI-9)","how":"The liver stores vitamins A and B12, makes albumin, clotting factors, and transport proteins, and runs gluconeogenesis and ketogenesis."},{"to":"Deficiency and malabsorption conditions (GI-13-E)","how":"Celiac disease, pancreatic insufficiency, and ileal disease produce the nutrient deficiencies explained here."},{"to":"Hematology (HE-3, HE-9-A)","how":"Iron, copper, B6, B12, and folate deficiencies are the major nutritional causes of microcytic, sideroblastic, and megaloblastic anemia."},{"to":"Neurology (NE-14, NE-20-D)","how":"Thiamine, B12, vitamin E, and copper deficiencies damage the brain, spinal cord, and peripheral nerves through defined enzyme failures."}],"terms":[{"term":"Essential nutrient","def":"A nutrient the body cannot synthesize in adequate amounts and must obtain from the diet."},{"term":"Coenzyme","def":"An organic molecule, often derived from a vitamin, that binds an enzyme and carries electrons or chemical groups during the reaction."},{"term":"Kwashiorkor","def":"Severe malnutrition from inadequate protein relative to energy, marked by edema, fatty liver, and skin and hair changes."},{"term":"Marasmus","def":"Severe malnutrition from inadequate total energy, marked by wasting of muscle and fat without edema."},{"term":"Micelle","def":"A small aggregate of bile salts, fatty acids, and monoglycerides that carries lipids and fat-soluble vitamins to the enterocyte surface."},{"term":"Intrinsic factor","def":"A glycoprotein secreted by gastric parietal cells that binds vitamin B12 and allows its uptake in the terminal ileum."},{"term":"Methylmalonic acid (MMA)","def":"An organic acid that accumulates when vitamin B12-dependent methylmalonyl-CoA mutase cannot convert methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA."},{"term":"Gluconeogenesis","def":"Synthesis of new glucose from noncarbohydrate precursors such as lactate, alanine, and glycerol, mainly in the liver and to a lesser extent the kidney."},{"term":"Ketone bodies","def":"Acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate, water-soluble fuels made from fatty acid-derived acetyl-CoA in liver mitochondria."},{"term":"Metallothionein","def":"A cysteine-rich intracellular protein that binds zinc and copper; induced by zinc, it traps copper in enterocytes."},{"term":"Gamma-carboxylation","def":"Vitamin K-dependent addition of a carboxyl group to glutamate residues, which lets clotting factors bind Ca2+ and phospholipid."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-08-01-C01","obj":"GI-8.1","title":"Essential amino acids, essential fatty acids, and protein-energy malnutrition","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"An amino acid is essential when humans cannot build its carbon skeleton at all, or not fast enough to meet need. Nine are essential in adults: phenylalanine, valine, threonine, tryptophan, isoleucine, methionine, histidine, leucine, and lysine. Others are conditionally essential. Tyrosine is made from phenylalanine, so it becomes essential in phenylketonuria; cysteine needs sulfur from methionine; and arginine synthesis may not keep up with need during growth or severe stress. Leucine and lysine are purely ketogenic, so their carbon skeletons cannot be used to make glucose during a fast."},{"type":"p","text":"Humans can desaturate fatty acids only up to carbon 9 from the carboxyl end and lack the enzymes that insert double bonds nearer the methyl end. They therefore cannot make linoleic acid (an omega-6 fatty acid, 18 carbons, two double bonds) or alpha-linolenic acid (an omega-3 fatty acid, 18 carbons, three double bonds), and both must come from food. Linoleic acid is elongated and desaturated to arachidonic acid, the precursor of prostaglandins, thromboxanes, and leukotrienes, and it is a component of the ceramides that seal the epidermal water barrier. Alpha-linolenic acid is converted, inefficiently, to eicosapentaenoic and docosahexaenoic acids, which are concentrated in retina and brain. Deficiency, seen with long-term fat-free parenteral feeding or severe fat malabsorption, causes scaly dermatitis, hair loss, and poor wound healing."},{"type":"table","title":"Essential dietary building blocks","cols":["Nutrient","Why it is essential","Key product or role"],"rows":[["Nine essential amino acids","Carbon skeleton cannot be synthesized","Protein synthesis; tryptophan also feeds niacin and serotonin"],["Linoleic acid (omega-6)","No desaturase acts beyond carbon 9","Arachidonic acid, eicosanoids, epidermal barrier lipids"],["Alpha-linolenic acid (omega-3)","Same desaturase limit","EPA and DHA for retina, brain, and membranes"]],"caption":"Essentiality reflects missing synthetic enzymes, not importance; many nonessential nutrients are equally vital."},{"type":"h","text":"Kwashiorkor and marasmus"},{"type":"p","text":"Protein-energy malnutrition has two classic poles. In marasmus, total energy intake is too low. The body adapts by burning fat and then muscle, so the child is severely wasted, but the amino acids released from muscle keep hepatic protein synthesis going, serum albumin stays near normal, and there is no edema. In kwashiorkor, energy intake may be adequate but protein is insufficient, often after weaning onto a starchy diet or during an infection. The liver lacks amino acids to make albumin and apolipoprotein B-100. Low albumin lowers plasma oncotic pressure, so fluid leaves capillaries as pitting edema and ascites. Without enough apolipoprotein B-100, triglyceride cannot be packaged into VLDL, so it accumulates in hepatocytes as fatty liver."},{"type":"flow","title":"Why kwashiorkor causes edema and fatty liver","steps":["Dietary protein falls while carbohydrate intake continues","Hepatic amino acid supply limits protein synthesis","Albumin falls, lowering plasma oncotic pressure","Fluid filters into tissues: edema and ascites","Apolipoprotein B-100 falls, so VLDL export fails","Triglyceride accumulates in hepatocytes: fatty liver"],"caption":"Both hallmark findings of kwashiorkor trace to reduced hepatic protein synthesis."},{"type":"table","title":"Kwashiorkor compared with marasmus","cols":["Feature","Kwashiorkor","Marasmus"],"rows":[["Main deficit","Protein, with energy relatively preserved","Total energy"],["Edema and ascites","Present, from hypoalbuminemia","Absent"],["Serum albumin","Low","Normal or mildly low"],["Liver","Enlarged, fatty","Usually normal"],["Muscle and fat","Muscle loss masked by edema; some fat kept","Severe wasting of both"],["Skin and hair","Flaky, peeling dermatosis; thin, depigmented hair","Loose, wrinkled skin"]],"caption":"Edema is the single most useful discriminator between the two."},{"type":"p","text":"Children with either form have impaired cell-mediated immunity, thymic atrophy, and frequent infections, and many have mixed features. Oxidative stress and infection may also contribute to kwashiorkor, but the protein-albumin-edema mechanism remains the standard explanation."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-08-01-C02","obj":"GI-8.2","title":"Fat malabsorption and deficiency of vitamins A, D, E, and K","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Vitamins A, D, E, and K are hydrophobic, so they travel the same route as dietary fat. Pancreatic lipase and colipase digest triglyceride, bile salts gather the products and the vitamins into micelles, the micelles deliver them to the jejunal enterocyte, and the enterocyte packages them into chylomicrons that enter lymph. A defect at any step lowers all four vitamins together, along with steatorrhea (bulky, pale, foul-smelling stool with excess fat)."},{"type":"flow","title":"Three routes to fat-soluble vitamin deficiency","steps":["Pancreatic insufficiency: too little lipase to digest triglyceride","Cholestasis: too little bile salt to form micelles","Celiac disease: villous atrophy reduces absorptive surface","Micelle uptake or chylomicron formation fails","Steatorrhea with low vitamins A, D, E, and K"],"caption":"Different lesions converge on the same outcome because all four vitamins depend on intact fat absorption."},{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin A (retinol) is converted to 11-cis-retinal, the chromophore of rhodopsin in rods, and to retinoic acid, which binds nuclear receptors that maintain specialized epithelia. Rods fail first because they work in dim light, so night blindness is the earliest sign. Loss of retinoic acid signaling causes squamous metaplasia: a dry, keratinized conjunctiva (xerophthalmia), Bitot spots, follicular hyperkeratosis of skin, and weakened epithelial defenses against infection."},{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin D is hydroxylated in the liver to 25-hydroxyvitamin D and in the kidney to calcitriol, which raises intestinal calcium and phosphate absorption. When it is deficient, a slight fall in ionized calcium raises PTH, which restores calcium by resorbing bone and wasting phosphate in urine. The low calcium-phosphate product leaves newly formed osteoid unmineralized: osteomalacia in adults (bone pain, pseudofractures) and rickets in children."},{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin E (alpha-tocopherol) is a lipid-soluble antioxidant that stops chain reactions of lipid peroxidation in membranes rich in polyunsaturated fatty acids. Red cell membranes then break down under oxidant stress (hemolytic anemia), and long sensory axons degenerate, giving dorsal column loss, spinocerebellar ataxia, and peripheral neuropathy. This resembles B12 deficiency but without megaloblastic anemia or raised MMA."},{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin K in its reduced form is the cofactor for gamma-glutamyl carboxylase in the liver. Uncarboxylated factors II, VII, IX, and X and proteins C and S cannot bind Ca2+ and phospholipid, so they are secreted but nonfunctional. Factor VII has the shortest half-life (about 4 to 6 hours), so the prothrombin time (PT) prolongs before the partial thromboplastin time. Factor V is not vitamin K dependent: it is normal in vitamin K deficiency but low in liver failure, which separates the two causes of a long PT."},{"type":"table","title":"Fat-soluble vitamin deficiencies","cols":["Vitamin","Active form and role","Deficiency findings","Mechanism of findings"],"rows":[["A","11-cis-retinal in rhodopsin; retinoic acid for epithelial differentiation","Night blindness, xerophthalmia, Bitot spots, follicular hyperkeratosis, infections","Rod pigment cannot regenerate; squamous metaplasia"],["D","Calcitriol increases gut Ca2+ and phosphate absorption","Osteomalacia, rickets; low or normal Ca2+, low phosphate, high PTH and alkaline phosphatase","Secondary hyperparathyroidism wastes phosphate; osteoid stays unmineralized"],["E","Chain-breaking antioxidant in membranes","Hemolytic anemia, dorsal column loss, ataxia, neuropathy","Peroxidation of red cell and axonal membranes"],["K","Cofactor for gamma-carboxylation of II, VII, IX, X, C, S","Bruising, bleeding, prolonged PT","Factors cannot bind Ca2+ and phospholipid"]],"caption":"All four fall together in steatorrhea; the clinical picture depends on which deficit dominates."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient with chronic cholestasis has a prolonged PT. What would a factor V level show, and why?","explain":"Factor V is made by the liver but is not gamma-carboxylated, so it stays normal when the long PT is caused by vitamin K malabsorption and falls only if hepatocyte synthetic function itself fails.","check":"Compare with the vitamin K paragraph and the table row for K."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-08-01-C03","obj":"GI-8.3","title":"Vitamin B12 absorption and the distinction from folate deficiency","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) is made only by microorganisms and reaches humans mainly in animal foods, where it is bound to protein. Its absorption requires a relay of binding proteins, each made in a different organ, and failure at any step causes deficiency. Body stores of about 2 to 5 mg, mostly in the liver, cover several years of loss, so deficiency appears long after absorption fails."},{"type":"flow","title":"Vitamin B12 from food to tissue","steps":["Gastric acid and pepsin release B12 from food protein","Free B12 binds haptocorrin (R binder) from saliva and stomach","In the duodenum, pancreatic proteases digest haptocorrin","Released B12 binds intrinsic factor from parietal cells","Cubilin receptors in the terminal ileum take up IF-B12","Transcobalamin II carries B12 in plasma to tissues and liver"],"caption":"Each step depends on a different organ: stomach, pancreas, and terminal ileum."},{"type":"p","text":"Haptocorrin protects B12 in the acid stomach, where B12 binds it more avidly than intrinsic factor; only after trypsin and other pancreatic proteases degrade haptocorrin in the alkaline duodenum can intrinsic factor take over. This is why pancreatic insufficiency can lower B12 absorption even though the stomach is normal."},{"type":"table","title":"Where B12 absorption fails","cols":["Step","Cause of failure"],"rows":[["Release from food","Atrophic gastritis, gastrectomy, long-term acid suppression"],["Intrinsic factor","Pernicious anemia (autoantibodies to parietal cells and intrinsic factor), gastrectomy"],["Haptocorrin cleavage","Pancreatic insufficiency"],["Ileal uptake","Crohn disease of the ileum, ileal resection"],["Competition in the lumen","Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth, fish tapeworm (Diphyllobothrium latum)"],["Intake","Strict vegan diet without supplementation"]],"caption":"Locating the failed step guides the search for the underlying disease."},{"type":"h","text":"Two enzymes, two metabolites"},{"type":"p","text":"B12 serves only two human enzymes. Methionine synthase, in the cytoplasm, uses methylcobalamin to move a methyl group from N5-methyltetrahydrofolate to homocysteine, forming methionine and regenerating tetrahydrofolate (THF). Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase, in mitochondria, uses adenosylcobalamin to convert methylmalonyl-CoA, derived from odd-chain fatty acids and some amino acids, to succinyl-CoA."},{"type":"p","text":"Folate is needed only for the first reaction. A lack of either vitamin therefore raises homocysteine, but only B12 lack raises MMA. In B12 deficiency, folate also becomes trapped as N5-methyl-THF, which cannot be converted back to other forms (the methyl-folate trap). Both deficiencies therefore starve thymidylate synthesis, slowing DNA replication while RNA and cytoplasm keep growing, which produces megaloblastic anemia with hypersegmented neutrophils. B12 deficiency alone causes subacute combined degeneration of the dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts and peripheral neuropathy, attributed to impaired myelin methylation and abnormal fatty acids built from excess methylmalonyl-CoA. Folate stores last only months, so folate deficiency appears sooner, in alcohol use disorder, pregnancy, and poor diets."},{"type":"table","title":"Laboratory distinction of B12 and folate deficiency","cols":["Finding","B12 deficiency","Folate deficiency"],"rows":[["Megaloblastic anemia","Yes","Yes"],["Homocysteine","High","High"],["Methylmalonic acid","High","Normal"],["Neurologic disease","Subacute combined degeneration, neuropathy","Absent"],["Body stores","Several years","About 3 to 4 months"]],"caption":"MMA is the deciding test because only B12 serves methylmalonyl-CoA mutase."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: reading homocysteine and MMA","text":"A 62-year-old with macrocytic anemia has a borderline serum B12, high homocysteine, and high MMA. Step 1: high homocysteine means methionine synthase is failing, which fits B12 or folate deficiency. Step 2: high MMA means methylmalonyl-CoA mutase is also failing, and only B12 serves that enzyme. Step 3: conclude B12 deficiency; the metabolites show intracellular function better than the serum level. With normal MMA, folate deficiency would be the answer. Kidney failure raises both metabolites, so renal function must be known first."},{"type":"stop","predict":"B12 and folate deficiency both cause megaloblastic anemia. Why does only B12 deficiency damage the spinal cord?","explain":"Both vitamins are needed for methionine synthase and thymidylate supply, so both impair DNA synthesis. Only B12 serves methylmalonyl-CoA mutase, and its failure, together with impaired myelin methylation, is linked to demyelination of the dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts.","check":"Compare with the paragraph on the two enzymes and the methyl-folate trap."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-08-01-C04","obj":"GI-8.4","title":"Thiamine, niacin, pyridoxine, and vitamin C as cofactors","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Water-soluble vitamins are stored in small amounts and turned over quickly, so deficiency develops within weeks to months of poor intake or absorption. Each deficiency syndrome follows from the reactions its coenzyme supports: the tissues that depend most on those reactions fail first."},{"type":"p","text":"Thiamine (B1) is converted to thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP), the cofactor for oxidative decarboxylation of alpha-keto acids by pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, and branched-chain alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenase, and for transketolase in the pentose phosphate pathway. Without TPP, pyruvate cannot enter the TCA cycle and the cycle itself slows, so ATP production falls and lactate accumulates. Neurons, which rely on glucose oxidation, are injured first, especially in the mammillary bodies, medial thalamus, and periaqueductal gray, giving Wernicke encephalopathy (confusion, ophthalmoplegia, ataxia). A glucose load in a thiamine-depleted person increases flux toward the blocked enzymes and can precipitate it. Dry beriberi is a symmetric peripheral neuropathy; wet beriberi is high-output heart failure from peripheral vasodilation and impaired myocardial energy production. Alcohol use disorder is the usual setting, because intake, absorption, and storage are all reduced."},{"type":"p","text":"Niacin (B3) forms NAD+ and NADP+, which carry electrons in hundreds of redox reactions. Some niacin is made from tryptophan, a pathway that needs B6 and riboflavin. Tissues with high turnover and energy demand fail first, giving pellagra: photosensitive dermatitis on sun-exposed skin (including a collar around the neck), diarrhea, and dementia. Pellagra follows diets based on untreated maize, Hartnup disease (loss of neutral amino acids including tryptophan through a defective intestinal and renal transporter), carcinoid syndrome (tryptophan diverted to serotonin), and isoniazid exposure, which depletes B6 and so blocks niacin synthesis."},{"type":"p","text":"Pyridoxine (B6) forms pyridoxal phosphate (PLP), the cofactor for transamination, many decarboxylations, and the first step of heme synthesis. Erythroid ALA synthase needs PLP, so heme production falls while iron keeps arriving; iron accumulates in mitochondria around the nucleus, forming ring sideroblasts, and the anemia is microcytic. Glutamate decarboxylase needs PLP to make GABA, so deficiency lowers inhibitory tone and causes seizures, most often in infants. Peripheral neuropathy, glossitis, and cheilosis also occur."},{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin C (ascorbate) keeps the iron in prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases in the reduced Fe2+ state. These enzymes hydroxylate procollagen in the rough endoplasmic reticulum; hydroxyproline stabilizes the triple helix by hydrogen bonding, and hydroxylysine allows glycosylation and cross-linking. Without them, collagen is unstable and is degraded or secreted in weak form, so blood vessel walls, gums, and healing wounds fail. Scurvy presents with perifollicular hemorrhages, corkscrew hairs, swollen bleeding gums, easy bruising, poor wound healing, and subperiosteal or joint bleeding. Ascorbate also reduces dietary Fe3+ to absorbable Fe2+ and is a cofactor for dopamine beta-hydroxylase."},{"type":"table","title":"Cofactor roles and deficiency findings","cols":["Vitamin","Coenzyme","Key enzymes","Deficiency findings"],"rows":[["B1, thiamine","TPP","Pyruvate, alpha-ketoglutarate, and branched-chain ketoacid dehydrogenases; transketolase","Wernicke encephalopathy, Korsakoff amnesia, dry and wet beriberi"],["B3, niacin","NAD+, NADP+","Dehydrogenases and other redox enzymes","Pellagra: dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia"],["B6, pyridoxine","PLP","ALA synthase, glutamate decarboxylase, transaminases, kynureninase","Sideroblastic anemia, seizures, neuropathy, cheilosis"],["C, ascorbate","Ascorbate (reducing agent)","Prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases, dopamine beta-hydroxylase","Scurvy: bleeding gums, perifollicular hemorrhage, poor healing"]],"caption":"Kynureninase in the tryptophan-to-niacin pathway explains why B6 deficiency can cause pellagra."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-08-01-C05","obj":"GI-8.5","title":"Fuel supply in the fed and fasting states","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Blood glucose must stay near about 70 to 100 mg/dL (3.9 to 5.6 mmol/L) between meals because red cells use only glucose and the brain uses mainly glucose. The liver manages this supply, switching between storage and release under the control of the insulin-to-glucagon ratio."},{"type":"p","text":"In the fed state, insulin dominates. Hepatocytes take up glucose through GLUT2, trap it with glucokinase, and store it as glycogen. Insulin-activated phosphatases switch on glycogen synthase and raise fructose-2,6-bisphosphate, which stimulates glycolysis, so excess carbon becomes fatty acid and leaves the liver as VLDL."},{"type":"p","text":"A few hours after a meal, glucagon dominates. It raises cAMP and activates protein kinase A, which phosphorylates the same enzymes in the opposite direction: glycogen phosphorylase is activated, glycogen synthase is inhibited, and fructose-2,6-bisphosphate falls, so gluconeogenesis is favored over glycolysis. The liver has glucose-6-phosphatase and can release free glucose; muscle lacks it, so its larger glycogen store serves only muscle. Liver glycogen is largely exhausted after about 24 hours."},{"type":"p","text":"Gluconeogenesis then becomes the main source. It reverses glycolysis using four bypass enzymes: pyruvate carboxylase (biotin dependent, activated by acetyl-CoA), PEP carboxykinase, fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase, and glucose-6-phosphatase. Lactate from red cells and muscle returns to the liver and is converted to glucose (Cori cycle). Alanine from muscle protein breakdown is transaminated to pyruvate, and its nitrogen goes to urea (glucose-alanine cycle). Glycerol from adipose lipolysis enters as dihydroxyacetone phosphate. Even-chain fatty acids cannot supply net glucose, because pyruvate dehydrogenase is irreversible and the TCA cycle releases two carbons as CO2 for each acetyl-CoA that enters; only the propionyl-CoA from odd-chain fatty acids is glucogenic."},{"type":"table","title":"Fuel sources across a fast","cols":["Phase","Dominant hormones","Main source of blood glucose or fuel"],"rows":[["Fed (0 to about 4 hours)","Insulin","Dietary glucose; liver stores glycogen and makes fat"],["Postabsorptive (about 4 to 24 hours)","Glucagon","Hepatic glycogenolysis, with rising gluconeogenesis"],["Early fast (about 1 to 3 days)","Glucagon, cortisol","Gluconeogenesis from alanine, lactate, and glycerol; fatty acids for muscle"],["Prolonged fast (days to weeks)","Glucagon, low insulin","Ketone bodies for brain; gluconeogenesis and muscle protein breakdown decline"]],"caption":"Time boundaries are approximate and vary with prior diet and activity."},{"type":"h","text":"Ketogenesis and why the liver cannot use ketones"},{"type":"p","text":"With low insulin, falling malonyl-CoA releases carnitine palmitoyltransferase I, so fatty acids from adipose lipolysis enter liver mitochondria for beta-oxidation. Because oxaloacetate is drawn off for gluconeogenesis, the acetyl-CoA cannot all enter the TCA cycle and is converted by mitochondrial HMG-CoA synthase (rate limiting) to acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate. Extrahepatic tissues reactivate acetoacetate with succinyl-CoA:3-ketoacid CoA transferase (thiophorase). The liver lacks this enzyme, so its ketones are exported rather than burned, and red cells, lacking mitochondria, cannot use them. After a week or more, ketones supply up to about two thirds of the brain's energy, lowering its glucose need and so the muscle protein broken down to make glucose."},{"type":"flow","title":"Ketone production and use in prolonged fasting","steps":["Low insulin and high glucagon activate adipose lipolysis","Beta-oxidation produces acetyl-CoA faster than the TCA cycle uses it","HMG-CoA synthase forms acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate","Liver lacks thiophorase, so ketones enter the blood","Brain, heart, and muscle convert ketones back to acetyl-CoA"],"caption":"Ketogenesis exports fat-derived fuel from the liver to tissues that cannot use fatty acids directly."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: why fat cannot make glucose","text":"Why does muscle protein break down during a five-day fast when fat stores are large? Step 1: red cells and brain still need glucose. Step 2: acetyl-CoA from fatty acids joins oxaloacetate, and two carbons leave as CO2 before oxaloacetate is regenerated, so no net oxaloacetate or glucose is gained. Step 3: only glycerol, a small part of each triglyceride, is glucogenic. Step 4: the rest must come from amino acids such as alanine, until ketones lower the brain's glucose need. Fatty acid oxidation still helps by supplying the ATP that gluconeogenesis consumes and the acetyl-CoA that activates pyruvate carboxylase."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-08-01-C06","obj":"GI-8.6","title":"Iron, zinc, and copper deficiency","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Iron, zinc, and copper are absorbed mainly in the duodenum and proximal small intestine, so they share risk from celiac disease, gastric surgery, and bypass of the proximal bowel. They also interact: copper enzymes are needed to move iron, and zinc can block copper absorption."},{"type":"p","text":"Dietary nonheme iron is reduced from Fe3+ to Fe2+ by duodenal cytochrome b on the brush border, a step helped by gastric acid and ascorbate, and enters the enterocyte through divalent metal transporter 1 (DMT1). Heme iron is absorbed separately and more efficiently. Iron leaves the cell through ferroportin, is oxidized to Fe3+ by the copper-containing ferroxidase hephaestin, and binds transferrin. Hepcidin from the liver degrades ferroportin and so limits absorption. The body has no regulated route for iron excretion, so balance is set at absorption."},{"type":"p","text":"In iron deficiency, stores are lost first (low ferritin), then transport iron falls (low serum iron and transferrin saturation, with high total iron-binding capacity because the liver makes more transferrin), and only then does anemia appear. Developing red cells keep dividing until their hemoglobin concentration reaches a threshold, so too little heme produces extra divisions and small, pale (microcytic, hypochromic) cells. In adults, iron deficiency usually reflects chronic blood loss, from menstruation in premenopausal women and most often from the gastrointestinal tract in men and postmenopausal women."},{"type":"p","text":"Zinc is a structural or catalytic cofactor for hundreds of enzymes (including alkaline phosphatase, carbonic anhydrase, and matrix metalloproteinases) and for zinc-finger transcription factors. Deficiency therefore strikes rapidly dividing tissues: poor wound healing, dermatitis around the mouth and anus and on the hands and feet (acrodermatitis), alopecia, diarrhea, impaired T cell immunity, dysgeusia, hypogonadism, and growth failure in children. Acrodermatitis enteropathica is an inherited defect of the intestinal zinc transporter ZIP4; acquired deficiency follows alcohol use disorder, malabsorption, and diets high in phytate."},{"type":"p","text":"Copper is a cofactor for ceruloplasmin and hephaestin (ferroxidases that allow iron export), cytochrome c oxidase, superoxide dismutase, lysyl oxidase, and dopamine beta-hydroxylase. Deficiency causes anemia, because iron is trapped in enterocytes and macrophages, and neutropenia. It also causes a myelopathy of the dorsal columns with sensory ataxia that closely mimics B12 subacute combined degeneration, probably from failed mitochondrial and antioxidant copper enzymes. Copper deficiency follows gastric bypass, which removes the proximal absorptive surface, and excess zinc intake."},{"type":"flow","title":"How excess zinc causes copper deficiency","steps":["High zinc intake from supplements or zinc-containing products","Zinc induces metallothionein in enterocytes","Metallothionein binds copper more tightly than zinc","Copper is trapped and lost as enterocytes are shed","Low copper: anemia, neutropenia, myelopathy"],"caption":"Zinc excess causes copper deficiency by sequestering copper inside the intestinal lining."},{"type":"table","title":"Mineral deficiencies","cols":["Mineral","Key proteins or roles","Deficiency findings","Characteristic laboratory clues"],"rows":[["Iron","Hemoglobin, myoglobin, cytochromes","Microcytic hypochromic anemia, fatigue, pica, koilonychia","Low ferritin, high TIBC, low transferrin saturation"],["Zinc","Zinc enzymes and zinc-finger proteins","Poor wound healing, dysgeusia, acrodermatitis, alopecia, hypogonadism","Low plasma zinc; low alkaline phosphatase may accompany"],["Copper","Ceruloplasmin, hephaestin, cytochrome c oxidase","Anemia, neutropenia, dorsal column myelopathy","Low serum copper and ceruloplasmin; normal B12 and MMA"]],"caption":"Normal B12 and MMA with a cord syndrome and neutropenia point to copper rather than B12."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient two years after gastric bypass has anemia, neutropenia, and a dorsal column sensory ataxia with normal B12 and MMA. What is the likely deficiency, and why are the blood counts low?","explain":"Copper deficiency; without copper ferroxidases, iron cannot leave enterocytes and macrophages to reach marrow, and copper-dependent enzymes in marrow precursors fail, lowering red and white cell production.","check":"Compare with the copper paragraph and the table row for copper."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-08-01-C07","obj":"GI-8.7","title":"Vitamin A and vitamin D toxicity","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Fat-soluble vitamins are stored in liver and adipose tissue and are not excreted readily in urine, so excess intake accumulates. Toxicity follows high-dose supplements, retinoid drugs, or, for vitamin A, eating very large amounts of liver."},{"type":"p","text":"The liver stores vitamin A as retinyl esters in hepatic stellate (Ito) cells. When intake exceeds storage capacity, the stellate cells become engorged and activated, laying down collagen in the space of Disse; this causes hepatomegaly, perisinusoidal fibrosis, and in severe cases noncirrhotic portal hypertension or cirrhosis. Excess retinoid also raises intracranial pressure, thought to reflect impaired CSF absorption, giving headache, vomiting, papilledema, and in infants a bulging fontanelle (pseudotumor cerebri). Chronic excess also causes dry, peeling skin, alopecia, bone and joint pain, and increased bone resorption with fracture risk and sometimes hypercalcemia. Retinoic acid controls HOX gene expression along the embryonic axis, so excess early in pregnancy disrupts cranial neural crest development: craniofacial and ear malformations, conotruncal heart defects, and central nervous system defects. Isotretinoin, a synthetic retinoid, carries the same teratogenic risk, which illustrates the receptor mechanism."},{"type":"p","text":"Vitamin D toxicity comes from very high intake, which raises 25-hydroxyvitamin D. At high concentrations, 25-hydroxyvitamin D is thought to activate the vitamin D receptor directly and to displace calcitriol from vitamin D-binding protein, raising free calcitriol. Intestinal calcium and phosphate absorption and osteoclastic bone resorption both rise, producing hypercalcemia and sometimes hyperphosphatemia. The high calcium acts on the parathyroid calcium-sensing receptor to suppress PTH, so renal calcium reabsorption falls and filtered calcium spills into urine (hypercalciuria). This causes calcium stones, nephrocalcinosis, and polyuria, because hypercalcemia blunts the collecting duct response to ADH (nephrogenic diabetes insipidus). Hypercalcemia also causes constipation, nausea, confusion, a shortened QT interval, and calcification of soft tissues when the calcium-phosphate product is high."},{"type":"p","text":"Laboratory values separate the causes of hypercalcemia. Vitamin D toxicity gives high 25-hydroxyvitamin D and low PTH. Granulomatous diseases such as sarcoidosis also give low PTH, but macrophages convert 25-hydroxyvitamin D to calcitriol without feedback control, so calcitriol is high while 25-hydroxyvitamin D is normal. Primary hyperparathyroidism gives high PTH with low phosphate."},{"type":"table","title":"Vitamin A and D toxicity compared","cols":["Feature","Vitamin A toxicity","Vitamin D toxicity"],"rows":[["Main storage site","Hepatic stellate cells","Adipose tissue and liver"],["Nervous system","Raised intracranial pressure, papilledema","Confusion, lethargy from hypercalcemia"],["Liver","Stellate cell activation, fibrosis, portal hypertension","Not a feature"],["Kidney","Not a main feature","Hypercalciuria, stones, nephrocalcinosis, polyuria"],["Calcium and PTH","Calcium sometimes high from bone resorption","High calcium, low PTH, high 25-hydroxyvitamin D"],["Pregnancy","Strong teratogen","Hypercalcemia risk"]],"caption":"Vitamin A toxicity centers on brain, liver, skin, and embryo; vitamin D toxicity centers on calcium."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: a hypercalcemic patient with low PTH","text":"A 48-year-old taking high-dose supplements has serum calcium 12.8 mg/dL (reference about 8.5 to 10.5), low PTH, and kidney stones. Step 1: low PTH means the parathyroids are responding normally, so the calcium source lies elsewhere. Step 2: check vitamin D metabolites. A very high 25-hydroxyvitamin D with only modestly raised calcitriol points to exogenous vitamin D. Step 3: if 25-hydroxyvitamin D were normal and calcitriol high, unregulated 1-alpha-hydroxylase in granulomas would be more likely. Step 4: stones follow because low PTH and a high filtered load both increase urinary calcium."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"The deficiencies in this chapter cluster by anatomy. Gastric disease removes acid, pepsin, and intrinsic factor and impairs iron and B12 absorption; pancreatic and biliary disease remove lipase and bile salts and take down vitamins A, D, E, and K; duodenal and proximal jejunal disease or bypass lowers iron, zinc, copper, and folate; and terminal ileal disease removes B12 and bile salt recycling. Reading a patient's surgical history or gut disease therefore predicts which nutrients will run short. Biochemistry then explains the findings: each vitamin or mineral serves specific enzymes, and the tissues most dependent on those enzymes fail first."},{"type":"p","text":"Several deficiencies imitate one another, and metabolites or companion tests separate them. B12, copper, and vitamin E deficiency all damage the dorsal columns, but only B12 raises MMA and only copper lowers ceruloplasmin; iron and B6 deficiency both produce microcytosis, but only B6 deficiency leaves iron stores full with ring sideroblasts. Serum levels of vitamins and minerals can mislead, because ferritin and zinc shift with inflammation and serum B12 may stay borderline, so functional markers and the whole clinical pattern carry more weight than any single value."}],"summary":["Essential nutrients are those whose synthesis humans lack: nine amino acids and the omega-6 and omega-3 fatty acids linoleic and alpha-linolenic acid.","Kwashiorkor is protein lack with low albumin (edema) and failed apolipoprotein B-100 export (fatty liver); marasmus is energy lack with wasting and no edema.","Fat malabsorption from pancreatic, biliary, or mucosal disease lowers vitamins A, D, E, and K together: night blindness, osteomalacia, hemolysis with neuropathy, and a prolonged PT.","B12 absorption needs acid and pepsin, haptocorrin, pancreatic proteases, intrinsic factor, and the terminal ileum; homocysteine rises in B12 or folate deficiency, MMA only in B12 deficiency.","Thiamine (TPP) serves alpha-ketoacid dehydrogenases, niacin forms NAD+, B6 (PLP) serves ALA synthase and GABA synthesis, and vitamin C keeps collagen hydroxylases active.","Glycogenolysis supports glucose for about a day, then gluconeogenesis from lactate, alanine, and glycerol; even-chain fatty acids cannot make net glucose.","The liver makes ketones from fatty acid acetyl-CoA but lacks thiophorase, so it exports them to brain, heart, and muscle.","Iron deficiency gives microcytic anemia with low ferritin and high TIBC; zinc deficiency gives poor healing, dysgeusia, and acrodermatitis; copper deficiency gives anemia, neutropenia, and myelopathy.","Excess zinc induces metallothionein that traps copper; gastric bypass removes the proximal surface that absorbs iron and copper.","Vitamin A excess causes intracranial hypertension, stellate cell liver injury, and birth defects; vitamin D excess causes hypercalcemia with low PTH, hypercalciuria, and stones."],"sources":[{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Vitamins; Minerals; Metabolic effects of insulin and glucagon; The feed-fast cycle"},{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Dietary balances, regulation of feeding, obesity and starvation, vitamins and minerals"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Nutrient digestion and absorption; energy metabolism"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Environmental and Nutritional Diseases"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Disorders of the exocrine pancreas; gastrointestinal disease"}]},"GI-9":{"unit":"GI-9","chapter_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-09","topic_id":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-09-01","title":"Liver Detoxification, Bilirubin, and Gland Functions","subject":"Gastrointestinal System (GAST)","version":"1.0","author":"Dr. Sharilyn Rennie","brand":"MedMasters Collaborative","overview":{"intro":"This chapter follows the work the liver and digestive glands do beyond digestion itself. It traces bilirubin from heme to stool and uses that pathway to read jaundice panels, explains how hepatocytes transform drugs and toxins and how that same chemistry injures zone 3 in acetaminophen and carbon tetrachloride poisoning, and links the loss of hepatic synthesis and ammonia disposal to the findings of liver failure. It closes with the protective chemistry of saliva and the way the gallbladder concentrates and releases bile. Board questions usually give a lab panel, an exposure, or a failing organ and ask which step broke; each section is built so you can work back to that step.","prerequisites":["Hepatic lobule and acinus, including zones 1 to 3 and portal blood flow (GI anatomy units)","Red cell life span and splenic removal of senescent red cells","Enzyme cofactors NADPH, UDP-glucuronic acid, and glutathione","Starling forces and plasma oncotic pressure","Composition of bile and the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts"],"connections":[{"to":"Hematology (hemolytic anemias)","how":"Hemolysis raises bilirubin production, which explains unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia, high urine urobilinogen, and black pigment gallstones."},{"to":"Pathology (cell injury)","how":"Acetaminophen and carbon tetrachloride are the standard models of bioactivation, glutathione depletion, and free radical membrane injury."},{"to":"Biochemistry (nitrogen metabolism)","how":"The urea cycle in periportal hepatocytes disposes of ammonia, so its failure explains hyperammonemia and hepatic encephalopathy."},{"to":"Hemostasis","how":"Hepatic synthesis and vitamin K-dependent carboxylation of clotting factors explain why the prothrombin time is the earliest marker of synthetic failure."},{"to":"Immunology and oral health","how":"Secretory IgA, lysozyme, and lactoferrin in saliva are mucosal defenses whose loss in Sjogren syndrome leads to rampant caries."}],"terms":[{"term":"Unconjugated bilirubin","def":"The water-insoluble product of heme breakdown that travels in plasma bound to albumin and is reported as indirect bilirubin."},{"term":"Conjugated bilirubin","def":"Bilirubin linked to glucuronic acid by UGT1A1, which makes it water-soluble, excretable in bile, and filterable into urine; it is reported approximately as direct bilirubin."},{"term":"Urobilinogen","def":"Colorless products formed when gut bacteria deconjugate and reduce bilirubin; a small reabsorbed fraction appears in urine."},{"term":"Biotransformation","def":"Enzymatic conversion of a lipophilic drug or toxin into a more polar product that can be excreted in urine or bile."},{"term":"Cytochrome P450 (CYP)","def":"A family of heme-containing monooxygenases in the smooth endoplasmic reticulum that use NADPH and O2 to carry out most phase I oxidations."},{"term":"Bioactivation","def":"Metabolism of a relatively harmless compound into a reactive, toxic product, such as NAPQI from acetaminophen or the trichloromethyl radical from carbon tetrachloride."},{"term":"Glutathione","def":"A tripeptide of glutamate, cysteine, and glycine whose thiol group conjugates electrophiles and reduces peroxides inside cells."},{"term":"Lipid peroxidation","def":"A free radical chain reaction that oxidizes polyunsaturated fatty acids in membrane phospholipids and destroys membrane integrity."},{"term":"Centrilobular (zone 3) necrosis","def":"Death of hepatocytes around the central vein, the region with the most CYP2E1 and the least oxygen."},{"term":"Hepatic encephalopathy","def":"Reversible brain dysfunction caused mainly by ammonia and other gut-derived toxins that bypass or overwhelm a failing liver."},{"term":"Xerostomia","def":"Dry mouth from reduced salivary flow, which removes buffering, remineralization, and antimicrobial protection of the teeth."},{"term":"Cholecystokinin (CCK)","def":"A hormone released by duodenal and jejunal I cells in response to fat and protein that contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi."}]},"sections":[{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-09-01-C01","obj":"GI-9.1","title":"The bilirubin pathway from heme to stool and urine","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Bilirubin is the end product of heme breakdown. An adult makes about 250 to 350 mg per day, roughly 80% from senescent red cells removed by macrophages of the spleen, liver, and bone marrow, and the rest from ineffective erythropoiesis and other heme proteins such as myoglobin and cytochromes. Each step of the pathway changes the solubility of the pigment, and solubility decides where bilirubin can travel and where it appears when a step fails."},{"type":"h","text":"Formation in the macrophage"},{"type":"p","text":"Heme oxygenase, a microsomal enzyme that uses O2 and NADPH, opens the porphyrin ring of heme in the rate-limiting step of heme breakdown, releasing green biliverdin, ferrous iron that is stored in ferritin or returned to transferrin, and carbon monoxide, which is exhaled. Biliverdin reductase then uses NADPH to reduce biliverdin to yellow unconjugated bilirubin. Internal hydrogen bonds fold this molecule so that its polar groups are hidden, making it nearly insoluble in water. It therefore travels in plasma tightly bound to albumin, which keeps it out of the glomerular filtrate and, normally, out of the brain."},{"type":"h","text":"Uptake, conjugation, and excretion in the hepatocyte"},{"type":"p","text":"At the sinusoid, bilirubin dissociates from albumin and enters the hepatocyte by facilitated transport (the carriers are not fully defined), then binds cytosolic ligandin, a glutathione S-transferase that limits its escape back to blood. In the smooth endoplasmic reticulum, UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 1A1 (UGT1A1) transfers glucuronic acid from UDP-glucuronic acid, forming mainly bilirubin diglucuronide. Conjugation disrupts the internal hydrogen bonds and makes bilirubin water-soluble. Multidrug resistance-associated protein 2 (MRP2), an ATP-dependent pump on the canalicular membrane, then exports it into bile. Export is the rate-limiting step of hepatic bilirubin handling, which is why diffuse hepatocyte injury raises conjugated bilirubin: the injured cell can still conjugate but cannot excrete, and conjugated pigment refluxes into blood."},{"type":"h","text":"Fate in the intestine"},{"type":"p","text":"Conjugated bilirubin is not absorbed by the small intestine. In the terminal ileum and colon, bacterial beta-glucuronidases remove the glucuronides and bacterial reductases convert bilirubin to colorless urobilinogen. Most urobilinogen is oxidized to stercobilin, the brown pigment of stool. A small fraction (about 10 to 20%) is reabsorbed into portal blood; the liver re-excretes most of it, and the remainder is filtered by the kidney and oxidized in urine to yellow urobilin. This small enterohepatic loop is the basis of the urine urobilinogen test."},{"type":"flow","title":"Bilirubin from heme to stool and urine","steps":["Macrophage heme oxygenase: heme to biliverdin, Fe2+, and CO","Biliverdin reductase: biliverdin to unconjugated bilirubin","Albumin carries unconjugated bilirubin to the liver","Hepatocyte uptake; ligandin binding","UGT1A1 adds glucuronic acid (conjugated bilirubin)","MRP2 pumps conjugated bilirubin into bile (rate-limiting)","Gut bacteria deconjugate and reduce it to urobilinogen","Most oxidized to stercobilin in stool; some reabsorbed","Small fraction excreted in urine as urobilin"],"caption":"Solubility changes at conjugation: before UGT1A1 bilirubin needs albumin and cannot enter urine; after it, bilirubin is water-soluble and can be filtered if it backs up into blood."},{"type":"p","text":"The newborn is the main physiologic exception. A high red cell mass with a shorter life span, low UGT1A1 activity, and a sterile gut in which mucosal beta-glucuronidase deconjugates bilirubin for reabsorption instead of conversion to urobilinogen together raise unconjugated bilirubin, typically peaking about day 3 to 5. If free unconjugated bilirubin exceeds albumin binding, or a drug such as a sulfonamide displaces it from albumin, the lipid-soluble pigment crosses the blood-brain barrier and stains the basal ganglia (kernicterus)."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A large bruise resolves over two weeks. Predict its color changes and which serum bilirubin fraction, if any, rises.","explain":"Tissue macrophages degrade the heme in place, making green biliverdin and then yellow bilirubin, so the bruise turns from purple-blue to green to yellow. Pigment made there is unconjugated, so any serum rise is in the unconjugated (indirect) fraction.","check":"Formation in the macrophage and the albumin transport step."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-09-01-C02","obj":"GI-9.2","title":"Reading jaundice panels: hemolytic, hepatocellular, and obstructive","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Serum bilirubin is reported as total and direct. Direct bilirubin reacts with the diazo reagent without an accelerator and approximates the conjugated fraction; indirect bilirubin (total minus direct) approximates the unconjugated fraction. Typical adult values are a total of about 0.3 to 1.2 mg/dL with a direct fraction under about 0.3 mg/dL, and jaundice becomes visible, first in the sclerae, above about 2 to 3 mg/dL."},{"type":"p","text":"Two urine tests complete the panel. Urine bilirubin appears only when conjugated bilirubin is raised, because unconjugated bilirubin is bound to albumin and not filtered; dark, tea-colored urine with a positive dipstick places the problem in the liver or biliary tree. Urine urobilinogen reflects how much bilirubin reaches the gut and how well the liver reclaims what is reabsorbed. It rises when more bilirubin is made or when injured hepatocytes fail to re-extract it, and it falls toward zero when bile cannot reach the intestine."},{"type":"p","text":"In hemolytic (prehepatic) jaundice, production outruns hepatic uptake and conjugation, so unconjugated bilirubin rises, urine bilirubin is absent (acholuric jaundice), and urine urobilinogen rises because more conjugated bilirubin enters the gut. Because a normal liver conjugates a large load, hemolysis alone seldom raises total bilirubin above about 4 to 5 mg/dL. In hepatocellular jaundice, uptake, conjugation, and especially excretion fail together, so both fractions rise and aminotransferases are markedly raised. In obstructive (posthepatic) jaundice, conjugated bilirubin refluxes into blood, little or none reaches the gut, and retained bile induces canalicular and bile duct cells to make and release alkaline phosphatase (ALP) and gamma-glutamyl transferase (GGT)."},{"type":"table","title":"Jaundice by mechanism","cols":["Pattern","Serum bilirubin","Urine bilirubin","Urine urobilinogen","Stool","Enzymes"],"rows":[["Hemolytic (prehepatic)","Unconjugated high","Absent","Increased","Normal or dark","Normal liver enzymes; LDH up, haptoglobin down"],["Hepatocellular","Both fractions high","Present","Normal or increased","Normal or pale","ALT and AST markedly high"],["Obstructive (posthepatic)","Conjugated high","Present, dark urine","Decreased or absent","Pale, clay-colored","ALP and GGT markedly high"]],"caption":"Urine bilirubin tells you whether the conjugated fraction is up; urine urobilinogen tells you whether bilirubin is reaching the gut."},{"type":"p","text":"Prolonged conjugated hyperbilirubinemia explains a common puzzle: some conjugated bilirubin binds covalently to albumin (delta bilirubin), measures as direct, is not filtered, and clears with the roughly 3-week half-life of albumin, so after an obstruction is relieved the urine clears quickly while scleral icterus fades slowly. Inherited disorders act at single steps and leave other liver tests normal."},{"type":"table","title":"Inherited hyperbilirubinemias","cols":["Disorder","Defective step","Fraction raised","Distinguishing feature"],"rows":[["Gilbert syndrome","Reduced UGT1A1 expression (promoter variant)","Unconjugated","Mild jaundice with fasting, illness, or exertion; common and benign"],["Crigler-Najjar type I","Absent UGT1A1 activity","Unconjugated","Severe from birth; kernicterus"],["Crigler-Najjar type II","Markedly reduced, inducible UGT1A1","Unconjugated","Milder; kernicterus uncommon"],["Dubin-Johnson syndrome","MRP2 canalicular export","Conjugated","Black liver from retained pigment"],["Rotor syndrome","OATP1B1 and OATP1B3 reuptake","Conjugated","Normal liver color; milder"]],"caption":"Defects before conjugation raise unconjugated bilirubin; defects after conjugation raise conjugated bilirubin."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: localizing jaundice from the panel","text":"A 58-year-old has jaundice, total bilirubin 9 mg/dL with direct 7 mg/dL, dark urine positive for bilirubin, absent urine urobilinogen, clay-colored stools, ALP four times normal, and ALT mildly raised. Step 1: direct makes up most of the total, so the liver is conjugating and the problem lies at or after excretion. Step 2: positive urine bilirubin confirms a raised water-soluble conjugated fraction. Step 3: absent urobilinogen and pale stool mean bilirubin is not reaching the gut, so the flow of bile is blocked. Step 4: high ALP with modest ALT points to bile duct injury rather than hepatocyte necrosis. Conclusion: obstructive jaundice, as from a common bile duct stone or a tumor of the pancreatic head. Hemolysis would instead show indirect predominance, no urine bilirubin, and high urobilinogen."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-09-01-C03","obj":"GI-9.3","title":"Phase I and phase II biotransformation and acetaminophen toxicity","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Lipophilic drugs and toxins cross membranes easily, so the kidney would filter and then reabsorb them almost indefinitely. The liver converts them into polar products that stay in urine or bile. This biotransformation takes place mainly in the smooth endoplasmic reticulum and cytosol of hepatocytes and runs in two phases."},{"type":"p","text":"Phase I reactions (oxidation, reduction, and hydrolysis) introduce or expose a functional group such as hydroxyl, amine, or thiol. Most oxidations are done by cytochrome P450 monooxygenases, which receive electrons from NADPH through NADPH-cytochrome P450 reductase and insert one oxygen atom from O2 into the substrate while reducing the other to water. CYP activity can be induced (CYP2E1 by chronic ethanol) or inhibited, which changes how much of any metabolite forms. Phase I products are often only slightly more polar and sometimes more reactive than the parent compound, which is the basis of bioactivation."},{"type":"p","text":"Phase II reactions attach a large polar donor group, which usually inactivates the molecule and makes it water-soluble. Glucuronidation has high capacity, sulfation has high affinity but limited capacity and saturates first, and glutathione conjugation captures electrophiles. Glutathione, a tripeptide of glutamate, cysteine, and glycine, is made in the hepatocyte at a rate limited by cysteine supply. Conjugates leave through canalicular and basolateral transporters such as MRP2."},{"type":"table","title":"Phase I and phase II biotransformation","cols":["Feature","Phase I","Phase II"],"rows":[["Reactions","Oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis","Glucuronidation, sulfation, glutathione conjugation, acetylation, methylation"],["Main enzymes","Cytochrome P450 monooxygenases","UGTs, sulfotransferases, glutathione S-transferases, N-acetyltransferases"],["Cofactor or donor","NADPH and O2","UDP-glucuronic acid, PAPS, glutathione, acetyl-CoA"],["Location","Smooth endoplasmic reticulum","Mostly cytosol; UGTs in endoplasmic reticulum"],["Effect on product","Small rise in polarity; may become reactive","Large rise in polarity; usually inactive"]],"caption":"Phase I can create a toxic metabolite; phase II usually removes it."},{"type":"p","text":"Acetaminophen shows how the two phases balance. At therapeutic doses, most is glucuronidated and sulfated, and only about 5 to 10% is oxidized, mainly by CYP2E1 with contributions from CYP3A4 and CYP1A2, to N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine (NAPQI), an electrophile that glutathione neutralizes at once. In overdose, sulfation and then glucuronidation saturate, more drug flows through CYP oxidation, and glutathione is consumed faster than it is made. When hepatic glutathione falls to roughly 30% of normal, free NAPQI binds cysteine residues on cellular proteins, especially mitochondrial proteins, producing oxidative stress, ATP depletion, and necrosis."},{"type":"flow","title":"Acetaminophen overdose to zone 3 necrosis","steps":["Overdose saturates sulfation, then glucuronidation","More drug is oxidized by CYP2E1 to NAPQI","Glutathione conjugates NAPQI until stores are depleted","Free NAPQI binds cysteine groups on mitochondrial proteins","Oxidative stress and ATP failure cause hepatocyte necrosis","Necrosis concentrates in zone 3 around the central vein"],"caption":"The toxic step is not the drug itself but the reactive metabolite left over once glutathione is exhausted."},{"type":"p","text":"Necrosis is centrilobular (zone 3) because hepatocytes around the central vein hold the most CYP2E1, receive the least oxygenated blood, and have smaller glutathione reserves than periportal cells. Aminotransferases often climb into the thousands of units per liter and peak about 3 to 4 days after ingestion. Chronic alcohol use (CYP2E1 induction and low glutathione) and fasting or malnutrition (low glutathione) lower the toxic threshold. N-acetylcysteine protects because it supplies cysteine for new glutathione, which confirms that glutathione depletion is the critical step."},{"type":"stop","predict":"Two people take the same large acetaminophen dose. One drinks heavily every day; the other has been fasting for several days. Who is at greater risk, and through which step?","explain":"Both are at higher risk than a healthy person. Chronic drinking induces CYP2E1, so more NAPQI forms, and also lowers glutathione; fasting lowers glutathione and the sulfate and glucuronide donors, so less NAPQI can be neutralized. Either change tips the balance toward free NAPQI.","check":"The acetaminophen flow and the paragraph on zone 3 and risk factors."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-09-01-C04","obj":"GI-9.4","title":"Carbon tetrachloride: free radical injury in the liver","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Carbon tetrachloride (CCl4), a former dry-cleaning solvent, is the classic model of free radical injury. CCl4 itself is relatively inert. In the smooth endoplasmic reticulum, CYP2E1 transfers an electron to it, releasing a chloride ion and forming the trichloromethyl free radical (CCl3•). A free radical has an unpaired electron in its outer orbital, which makes it highly reactive and able to start chain reactions."},{"type":"p","text":"CCl3• reacts with O2 to form the trichloromethyl peroxyl radical (CCl3OO•). These radicals pull hydrogen atoms from polyunsaturated fatty acids in membrane phospholipids, starting lipid peroxidation. The lipid radicals that result react with O2 to form lipid peroxyl radicals, which attack neighboring fatty acids, so damage spreads autocatalytically through the endoplasmic reticulum membrane until vitamin E or glutathione peroxidase ends the chain. Peroxidation fragments fatty acids into reactive aldehydes, such as malondialdehyde and 4-hydroxynonenal, that cross-link proteins. The radicals also inactivate the cytochrome P450 that produced them."},{"type":"p","text":"Fatty change comes first. Within hours, the injured endoplasmic reticulum swells, ribosomes detach, and protein synthesis falls, including synthesis of apolipoproteins. Without apolipoprotein, the hepatocyte cannot package triglyceride into very low density lipoprotein (VLDL) for export, so lipid collects as cytoplasmic vacuoles. Injury then spreads to the plasma membrane and mitochondria. Ca2+ enters through the damaged membrane and activates phospholipases, proteases, and endonucleases, and mitochondrial injury stops ATP production, so the cells swell, lose membrane integrity, and die by necrosis."},{"type":"p","text":"The necrosis is centrilobular for the same reason as in acetaminophen injury: CYP2E1 is concentrated in zone 3, where oxygen is lowest. Chronic ethanol use, by inducing CYP2E1, increases CCl4 toxicity, and repeated exposure causes fibrosis, which is why CCl4 is used experimentally to produce cirrhosis. The two toxins differ in the reactive species and in what protects against them."},{"type":"flow","title":"Carbon tetrachloride to centrilobular necrosis","steps":["CYP2E1 reduces CCl4 to the CCl3• radical","CCl3• plus O2 forms the CCl3OO• radical","Radicals start lipid peroxidation in endoplasmic reticulum membranes","Ribosomes detach; apolipoprotein synthesis falls","Triglyceride cannot leave as VLDL: fatty change","Membrane and mitochondrial injury; Ca2+ influx, ATP loss","Zone 3 necrosis; fibrosis with repeated exposure"],"caption":"A single enzymatic step turns an inert solvent into a radical whose chain reaction does the damage."},{"type":"table","title":"Acetaminophen and carbon tetrachloride compared","cols":["Feature","Acetaminophen overdose","Carbon tetrachloride"],"rows":[["Activating enzyme","CYP2E1 (also CYP3A4, CYP1A2)","CYP2E1"],["Toxic species","NAPQI, an electrophile","CCl3• and CCl3OO• free radicals"],["Primary target","Cysteine groups on cell proteins","Polyunsaturated lipids in membranes"],["Key protection","Glutathione conjugation","Antioxidants that end radical chains"],["Early morphology","Necrosis","Fatty change, then necrosis"],["Zone injured","Zone 3","Zone 3"]],"caption":"Both are bioactivation injuries in zone 3; they differ in the chemistry of the toxic product."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-09-01-C05","obj":"GI-9.5","title":"Hepatic synthesis, ammonia disposal, and the findings of liver failure","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"Hepatocytes make most plasma proteins, and portal blood passes through the liver before reaching the systemic circulation, so liver failure produces too little of what the liver makes and too much of what it should remove."},{"type":"h","text":"Albumin and clotting factors"},{"type":"p","text":"Albumin is the most abundant plasma protein (about 3.5 to 5.0 g/dL). The liver makes about 10 to 15 g per day, and its half-life is about 20 days. Albumin supplies roughly 75 to 80% of plasma oncotic pressure, so a low level favors filtration, causing edema and, with portal hypertension and renal Na+ retention, ascites. Because of its long half-life, albumin reflects chronic rather than acute synthetic failure."},{"type":"p","text":"The liver makes fibrinogen, prothrombin (factor II), factors V, VII, IX, X, XI, XII, and XIII, the anticoagulants protein C, protein S, and antithrombin, and plasminogen. Factors II, VII, IX, and X and proteins C and S need vitamin K-dependent gamma-carboxylation of glutamate residues to bind Ca2+ and phospholipid. The prothrombin time (PT, about 11 to 13.5 s; INR about 1.0) measures factor VII and the common pathway (X, V, II, fibrinogen). Factor VII has the shortest half-life, about 4 to 6 hours, so the PT lengthens within a day of acute failure and is the earliest marker of synthetic function. In cholestasis the PT is long from vitamin K malabsorption and corrects with parenteral vitamin K; in hepatocellular failure it does not, because synthesis itself has failed. Factor VIII, made mainly by endothelial cells, stays normal or high in liver disease but is consumed in disseminated intravascular coagulation."},{"type":"h","text":"Ammonia and the urea cycle"},{"type":"p","text":"Ammonia comes from colonic bacterial urease, enterocyte glutamine breakdown, and amino acid catabolism. Periportal (zone 1) hepatocytes convert it to urea, and perivenous (zone 3) hepatocytes trap what remains as glutamine. In mitochondria, carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I (CPS I), activated by N-acetylglutamate, combines NH3 and HCO3- into carbamoyl phosphate, the committed step; ornithine transcarbamylase forms citrulline, aspartate adds the second nitrogen in the cytosol, and arginase releases urea for renal excretion. Urea cycle failure therefore lowers BUN while raising ammonia."},{"type":"p","text":"When hepatocyte mass falls or portosystemic shunts carry portal blood around the liver, ammonia reaches the brain. Astrocytes lack a urea cycle and detoxify it with glutamine synthetase; glutamine accumulates, draws in water, and swells the astrocytes, and neurotransmission is disturbed. Alkalosis shifts NH4+ to freely diffusible NH3, hypokalemia increases renal ammonia production, and gastrointestinal bleeding adds a protein load, so each can precipitate encephalopathy."},{"type":"flow","title":"Hepatic encephalopathy","steps":["Gut bacteria and amino acid breakdown make ammonia","Portal blood carries ammonia toward the liver","Low urea cycle capacity or portosystemic shunts let it pass","Ammonia crosses the blood-brain barrier","Astrocytes make glutamine from glutamate and ammonia","Astrocyte swelling and disturbed neurotransmission","Confusion, asterixis, then stupor and coma"],"caption":"Asterixis is a flapping tremor of the outstretched hands caused by brief lapses in postural tone."},{"type":"table","title":"Findings of liver failure and the function lost","cols":["Finding","Lost function"],"rows":[["Prolonged PT, bleeding (factor VII falls first)","Clotting factor synthesis"],["Hypoalbuminemia, edema, ascites","Albumin synthesis"],["Hyperammonemia, low BUN, encephalopathy","Urea cycle"],["Jaundice","Bilirubin uptake, conjugation, and excretion"],["Hypoglycemia","Glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis"],["Gynecomastia, spider angiomas","Hormone inactivation"]],"caption":"Each finding traces to a specific hepatic function."},{"type":"example","title":"Worked example: timing of PT and albumin","text":"Two days after an acetaminophen overdose, a patient has a PT of 28 s and an albumin of 4.1 g/dL. Step 1: factor VII (half-life about 4 to 6 hours) is nearly gone after two days without synthesis, so the PT lengthens sharply. Step 2: albumin (half-life about 20 days) falls well under 10% in two days, so it stays normal. Step 3: a normal albumin does not mean the liver is working. In long-standing cirrhosis both are abnormal, because months of reduced synthesis have lowered albumin."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-09-01-C06","obj":"GI-9.6","title":"Protective functions of saliva and caries when flow falls","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The salivary glands secrete about 1 to 1.5 L per day. The parotid makes serous saliva rich in alpha-amylase, the submandibular a mixed secretion, and the sublingual mostly mucus. Secretion is driven chiefly by parasympathetic fibers (facial nerve to the submandibular and sublingual glands, glossopharyngeal nerve through the otic ganglion to the parotid) acting on M3 muscarinic receptors; sympathetic stimulation adds a smaller, protein-rich flow. Acinar cells secrete an isotonic, plasma-like fluid, and the ducts reabsorb Na+ and Cl- and secrete K+ and HCO3-, so resting saliva is hypotonic. Stimulated flow carries more HCO3-, so buffering capacity is greatest during meals, when acid challenge is highest."},{"type":"p","text":"Enamel is mostly hydroxyapatite, a calcium phosphate crystal. After sugar intake, plaque bacteria such as Streptococcus mutans ferment carbohydrate to lactic acid, and plaque pH falls within minutes. Below a critical pH of about 5.5, the fluid around the enamel is undersaturated with hydroxyapatite and mineral dissolves. Saliva reverses this three ways: its flow clears sugar and acid, its bicarbonate (with phosphate and proteins) buffers plaque acid back toward neutral, and because it is supersaturated with calcium and phosphate, mineral redeposits in early lesions once pH recovers (remineralization). Proteins such as statherin keep calcium phosphate in solution without letting it precipitate, and fluoride taken up during remineralization forms fluorapatite, which resists acid better."},{"type":"table","title":"Antimicrobial and protective components of saliva","cols":["Component","Action"],"rows":[["Secretory IgA","Dimeric IgA from local plasma cells, carried across the epithelium by the polymeric immunoglobulin receptor; blocks bacterial adherence"],["Lysozyme","Hydrolyzes the bond between N-acetylmuramic acid and N-acetylglucosamine in peptidoglycan"],["Lactoferrin","Binds iron, depriving bacteria of an essential nutrient"],["Lactoperoxidase","Oxidizes thiocyanate to hypothiocyanite, which inhibits bacterial metabolism"],["Histatins","Antifungal peptides active against Candida"],["Mucins","Lubricate and coat the mucosa and aggregate bacteria for swallowing"]],"caption":"These defenses act together with flow, buffering, and remineralization."},{"type":"p","text":"Caries form when demineralization outpaces remineralization over time. When flow falls (xerostomia from Sjogren syndrome, radiation to the head and neck, dehydration, or drugs with anticholinergic action that block M3 receptors), sugar and acid stay on the teeth longer, buffering and remineralization fail, and antimicrobial proteins are lost. Caries then progress rapidly and appear at sites that saliva normally protects well, such as the cervical margins at the gumline, exposed root surfaces, and incisal edges. Loss of these defenses also favors oral candidiasis and, with slow flow through the ducts, ascending bacterial parotitis."}]},{"comp":"NPLEX-P1-GAST-09-01-C07","obj":"GI-9.7","title":"Gallbladder concentration, contraction, and gallstone formation","blocks":[{"type":"p","text":"The liver secretes about 600 to 1,000 mL of bile per day, but the gallbladder holds only about 30 to 60 mL. Between meals the contracted sphincter of Oddi diverts hepatic bile into the gallbladder, which stores it and concentrates it, usually about 5-fold and up to about 12 to 20-fold."},{"type":"p","text":"Concentration is driven by active Na+ transport. Na+ enters the apical membrane in exchange for H+, Cl- enters in exchange for HCO3-, and the basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase pumps Na+ into the lateral intercellular spaces, with Cl- following. The local osmotic gradient draws water across the epithelium, so absorption is nearly isotonic. Bile salts, cholesterol, phospholipid, and bilirubin are large or held in micelles and are not absorbed, so they become concentrated, and the exchange of Na+ for H+ acidifies gallbladder bile, which keeps calcium salts more soluble."},{"type":"p","text":"Fatty acids and amino acids in the duodenum stimulate I cells of the duodenal and jejunal mucosa to release cholecystokinin (CCK). CCK acts on CCK1 receptors on gallbladder smooth muscle, and through vagal pathways, to contract the gallbladder, and it relaxes the sphincter of Oddi, so concentrated bile arrives when there is fat to emulsify. Vagal acetylcholine causes modest contraction in the cephalic phase, and the gallbladder partly empties with the migrating motor complex between meals."},{"type":"p","text":"Cholesterol is insoluble in water and stays in solution only in mixed micelles with bile salts and phosphatidylcholine. Bile becomes supersaturated when the liver secretes too much cholesterol (obesity, estrogen, aging) or too little bile salt (ileal disease or resection that breaks the enterohepatic circulation). Supersaturation alone is not enough: cholesterol monohydrate crystals must nucleate, a step promoted by mucin from the gallbladder wall, and they must remain long enough to grow. Concentration raises the levels of cholesterol and calcium, and stasis provides the time. Stasis follows fasting and total parenteral nutrition (no fat in the duodenum, so little CCK), pregnancy (progesterone relaxes smooth muscle), rapid weight loss, and spinal cord injury."},{"type":"flow","title":"Cholesterol gallstone formation","steps":["Excess cholesterol or too little bile salt reaches bile","Micelles cannot hold all the cholesterol: supersaturation","Gallbladder absorbs water, concentrating cholesterol and calcium","Low CCK or impaired motility leaves bile stagnant","Mucin promotes nucleation of cholesterol crystals","Crystals aggregate into sludge, then stones"],"caption":"Stones need supersaturation, nucleation, and stasis together."},{"type":"p","text":"Pigment stones follow the same logic for bilirubin. In chronic hemolysis more bilirubin enters bile, some is deconjugated, and unconjugated bilirubin precipitates with calcium as black calcium bilirubinate stones in the gallbladder. In biliary infection, bacterial beta-glucuronidase deconjugates bilirubin in the ducts, forming soft brown stones."},{"type":"stop","predict":"A patient on total parenteral nutrition for six weeks develops gallbladder sludge. Which step of normal gallbladder function has been lost, and why does that matter?","explain":"With nothing entering the duodenum, I cells release little CCK, so the gallbladder rarely contracts. Bile keeps being concentrated and stays put, giving cholesterol crystals and calcium salts time to nucleate within mucin and form sludge.","check":"The CCK paragraph and the gallstone flow."}]}],"integration":[{"type":"p","text":"Anatomy and biochemistry explain where injury lands. Blood enters the acinus at the portal tract and leaves at the central vein, so zone 1 hepatocytes receive the most oxygen and do most urea synthesis, while zone 3 cells, low in oxygen and rich in CYP2E1, take the brunt of bioactivated toxins and of ischemia. The same solubility chemistry links hematology to the liver and gallbladder: hemolysis raises unconjugated bilirubin and urine urobilinogen and can form black pigment stones, while obstruction of the duct that drains a stone-filled gallbladder causes conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with pale stool."},{"type":"p","text":"Physiology and pathology explain the lab pattern. Each finding of liver failure traces to a lost function, and the time course follows protein half-lives, so the PT reports acute failure while albumin reports chronic disease. The limits matter too: direct bilirubin only approximates conjugated bilirubin, albumin also falls in malnutrition, nephrotic syndrome, and inflammation, serum ammonia correlates only loosely with the depth of encephalopathy, and reference ranges vary by laboratory, so values are read together rather than one at a time."}],"summary":["Heme oxygenase makes biliverdin, iron, and CO; biliverdin reductase makes unconjugated bilirubin, which rides on albumin and cannot enter urine.","UGT1A1 conjugates bilirubin with glucuronic acid; MRP2 export into bile is rate-limiting; gut bacteria make urobilinogen and stercobilin.","Urine bilirubin means conjugated hyperbilirubinemia; urine urobilinogen rises in hemolysis and disappears in complete obstruction.","Hemolytic jaundice is unconjugated and acholuric; hepatocellular raises both fractions and ALT; obstructive is conjugated with pale stool and high ALP.","Phase I (CYP450, NADPH, O2) adds a reactive group and can bioactivate; phase II conjugation makes products polar and usually inactive.","Acetaminophen overdose saturates conjugation, NAPQI depletes glutathione and binds proteins, and necrosis is centrilobular (zone 3).","CYP2E1 converts CCl4 to the trichloromethyl radical, which drives lipid peroxidation, fatty change from failed VLDL export, and zone 3 necrosis.","The PT (factor VII, half-life about 4 to 6 hours) is the earliest marker of synthetic failure; albumin (about 20 days) reflects chronic disease.","Ammonia normally becomes urea in zone 1; in liver failure it reaches astrocytes, forms glutamine, and causes encephalopathy with asterixis.","Saliva buffers acid, remineralizes enamel, and kills bacteria; xerostomia causes rapid cervical and root caries. The gallbladder concentrates bile by Na+ absorption, contracts with CCK, and forms stones from supersaturation, nucleation, and stasis."],"sources":[{"book":"Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology","where":"Secretory Functions of the Alimentary Tract; The Liver"},{"book":"Boron and Boulpaep Medical Physiology","where":"Hepatobiliary function, bilirubin metabolism, and salivary secretion"},{"book":"Lippincott Illustrated Reviews: Biochemistry","where":"Heme degradation and bilirubin; Urea cycle and ammonia metabolism"},{"book":"Robbins and Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease","where":"Cell injury, chemical (toxic) injury and free radicals; Liver and Gallbladder"},{"book":"Junqueira's Basic Histology","where":"Organs Associated with the Digestive Tract"},{"book":"Lange Pathophysiology of Disease","where":"Liver Disease"}]}});
